5.0,":fresh: Every year there's one or two films that blow me away.  This is the one.  And that's tough... cause this has been a great year for movies.  But man, oh man... this is the PERFECT FILM.  And let me say this... (in case you didn't already know) I didn't like Mystic River.  Thought it was so overhyped and not directed well at all.  The screenplay was crap.  
But this isn't about Mystic River.  Let's talk Million Dollar Baby.  Actually, let's not.  Cause I don't wanna give anything away.  Just see it now.  Ever Hillary Swank was brilliant (And I don't like Hillary Swank at all).  DAMN THIS MOVIE IS FUCKING GOOD.","",False,False,False,False,5.0,"2004-12-16","","RT","900747796"
5.0,"I'd planned on writing more for at least [i]Million Dollar Baby[/i], but now's the only time I have to write, and I'm dead tired.  A short crappy review as usual shall suffice, I suppose.

[i][b]Million Dollar Baby[/i][/b] -  I liked [i]Mystic River[/i] but not nearly as much as most people did.   So I was expecting the same type of thing for [i]Million Dollar Baby[/i].  Instead, I was completely blown away.  Eveything about the film is just amazing.  The story is awesome, every step of the way.  All the acting in the film is perfect, all worthy of an Oscar nominations.

The story is about Maggie, and her journey becoming a professional boxer.  But more than that, it's a story about family, and her relationship with her trainer, played by Clint Eastwood, as well as her own family.  

The fight scenes are thrilling, and then the story turns, and it's like a slap in the face.  And it beats you to the ground.  And it haunts you, long after the film ends.  I couldn't stop thinking about it.  It's a true masterpiece, and the best film to come out of America this year, at least as far as I've seen, and I don't think it'll be topped.  This is one film truly deserving of all the praise it's getting.  

[i][b]The Phantom of the Opera[/i][/b] - Let me first off say I've never seen any other version of this- not a theatrical version, film version, nor written version.  I'd heard some of the songs, which I'd enjoyed beforehand.  So going into this, I was really a [i]Phantom of the Opera[/i] virgin, as well as a Joel Schumacher hater.  He's one of the few directors I honestly hate, despite a recent decent effort ([i]Phonebooth[/i]).

So, with that, I have very mixed feelings about the film.  There are lots of things I do like about it, but lots of things I don't like much.

The acting/singing talent for the most part is good.  Emmy Rossum has a very beautiful voice, but I just didn't get her character.  Or rather, her struggle with love.  There's this love triangle between her, the phantom, and her childhood friend who recently appeared, and she just didn't seem to like either of them.  The songs were hit and miss with me.  I really enjoyed about half of them, the other half I didn't care for.  And I didn't much care for going in and out of songs and dialogue.  It didn't work.

The sets could have been much better.  Some of them were okay, but they seriously could have done a lot more.  And the film itself looked so boring.  There seemed to be more of an emphasis on what's infront of the camera instead of using the camera itself as part of the aesthtics, and that could have really made the difference between greatness and mediocrity.  

I liked the very basic premise of story, and I guess that just about covers everything.  So while I do like more than I dislike, Schumacher could have made this film great.  He could have made it Oscar worthy.  

[i][b]Kicking and Screaming[/i][/b] - This is a Will Ferrell movie slated in the same summer slot as [i]Daddy Day Care[/i].  They're the same type of film.  Same calibur as well.  Pretty bad stuff.  The movie is about a dad, Ferrell, who end up coaching is son's soccer team, and Ferrell's father is the opposing coach.  They end up going head to head at the end for the championship.  One funny thing though, is Mike Ditka plays himself and helps Ferrell coach the team.

Anyway, it's a sad comedy, and will probably make a ton of unnecessary money.  This isn't in the ratings system yet.  I'll add it when it gets updated, but I'm giving the movie a 4.","",False,False,False,False,5.0,"2004-12-21","","RT","900626599"
4.0,"[b]Million Dollar Baby[/b]
 
[img]http://us.movies1.yimg.com/movies.yahoo.com/images/hv/photo/movie_pix/warner_brothers/million_dollar_baby/_group_photos/clint_eastwood6.jpg[/img]
 
Superficially, this film appears very standard and even a little bland, but there's a poignancy brewing underneath that only reaches its explosive effect when the movie is considered in its entirety. A second viewing would be interesting, not so much for a new sense of foreboding, but for the completely new light the ending shines on the seemingly generic opening. All the acting is fine, but only Hilary Swank deserves recognition. Clint Eastwood has done something odd here, creating a truly thoughtful movie that left my stomach in knots and my head spinning, but which also feels forced. When I thought [b]Spanglish[/b] and [b]The Aviator[/b] were balancing acts, I had no idea what was coming. Million Dollar Baby is a hybrid of a hammy, ridiculous, failed melodrama and of a perfect, heart-wrenching, fully functional gut-buster, so much so, in fact, I practically had to remind myself to breathe. Something has to click inside of you or this movie will fall flat on its face and you'll never trust the critics again, for me, it worked. 
 
I don't normally do this, but I want to say something that may venture into spoiler territory for some, so blackness ahoy:
 
[spoiler]Specifically, the idea that a life cut short is not necessarily wholly tragic had me thinking and offers a wonderful new spin on the Cinderella story, which I would argue this movie is still a version of. I think this movie takes this position and I think Eastwood has given us something new to consider under the guise of this tragic, formulaic story.[/spoiler]
 
[b]The Life Aquatic with Steve Zissou[/b]
 
[img]http://us.movies1.yimg.com/movies.yahoo.com/images/hv/photo/movie_pix/touchstone_pictures/the_life_aquatic_with_steve_zissou/bill_murray/orca.jpg[/img]
 
While the hate doesn't completely baffle me (high expectations + a majority view that this is a sub par movie = bad film, and I'm referring to people I know in real life, not just the RT populace), I found The Life Aquatic to be enormously entertaining with nary a deal-breaking misstep. Sure, some drama near the end doesn't work so well and is lost in an abundance of plot, but the film is original, well-acted and filled with fun-to-study characters. When considering Zissou's eye-rollingly over-the-top escape from pirates, I'd offer that it's no accident that the movie we're watching and the movie-within-the-movie share the same title. Both The Life Aquatics are showboaty and deliciously pompous.  
 
[b]Meet the Fockers[/b]
 
[img]http://us.movies1.yimg.com/movies.yahoo.com/images/hv/photo/movie_pix/universal_pictures/meet_the_fockers/_group_photos/barbra_streisand22.jpg[/img]
 
The Fockers themselves are rather interesting, but the film is always stupid and only sometimes funny; also... whatever, you get the idea.","",False,False,False,False,4.0,"2004-12-28","","RT","900606293"
4.0,"These are some of the last movements I will make before 2004 cuts itself off from the future. This must be the time of the year when you arch your pen just above the paper to make that final punctuation mark. It's a quick motion, relatively speaking of course. How fresh off our final written letter were we? Our last word? Are we satisfied with this last sentence? Chapter? Is the pen descending back to the page now? How close are we to going back down? How close?
 
*holds index finger & thumb mere milliimeters apart*
 
[i]That[/i] close, baby.
 
 
I wish you all a safe & marvelous New Year's! :) God Bless.
 
 
 
