4.0,"Clint Eastwood's follow up to 'Mystic River' is a magnificent, a brilliant tale of a female boxer (Hilary Swank), who is driven by an old fight trainer (Clint Eastwood), and an ex-boxer (Morgan Freeman). This film, and The Aviator will probably be the head runners for best picture this year even though I thought Sideways was better than both of them, but still Million Dollar Baby is a true masterpiece, and Hilary Swank is amazing, she would be my choice for best actress, she pushes the charater, and builds a great one at that, and of course, Clint gives extraordinary directing, and makes all of his actors give top knock performances. Freeman's charater is the narrator of the story, which oddly seems like just his voice added alot to the film, there is a part in the film, where Freeman has a very emotional part, and it makes you want to cheer. Without a doubt one of the year's best.","aa071439-c35f-46b4-a76e-89d913e8ffec",False,False,False,False,4.0,"2004-12-31","","RT","900707985"
4.5,"December 15, 2004
 
[font=Verdana][b][size=5][color=#990000]C[/color][/size][/b]lint Eastwood's ""Million Dollar Baby"" is the kind of film that resonates with viewers long after they see it. It's not the kind of film you walk out of and start talking about with your friends or signficant other. It makes each person think about something unique and personal. It's poetically manipulative, solemn and tender. One thing it's not is uplifting. There's a valuable lesson within it - that life's most selfless acts, no matter how thoughtful, do not always lead to redemption and happiness.[/font]
 
If you haven't seen the previews or read about the film's already amazing reviews, then it might seem baffling that ""Million Dollar Baby"" shows itself off as a boxing movie. Eastwood stars as Frankie Dunn, a captious boxing trainer whose motives for his job remain hidden. Frankie's divorced and has a disaffected daughter for whom he incessantly prays for and writes to. Before bed, he performs what is obviously a nightly routine, saying, ""Lord, you already know what it is that I want, so there's no use in me saying it."" 
 
Frankie owns a gym where he trains Big Willie Little (Mike Colter), a determined heavyweight yearning to fight in a title match, but Frankie doesn't believe he's quite ready. Frankie's close friend, Eddie Scrap-Iron Dupris (Morgan Freeman), believes Frankie has a hard time believing in people. Eddie acts as Frankie's walking conscience, advising him on what to do but leaving Frankie to make his own decisions. He's kind of a part of the gym with his own living space set up in the back. He and Frankie have a history that goes back to when Eddie was a fighter and lost an eye, a story that has emotional meaning of its own.
 
One night, a poor white woman named Maggie Fitzgerald (Hilary Swank) runs up to Frankie and asks her to be his trainer. ""I don't train girls,"" he says. But Maggie puts in countless hours at the gym anyway. Night after night she stands punching a bag, earning the admiration of Eddie and the curiosity of Frankie. 
 
When Big Willie leaves Frankie in pursuit of a quicker root to a title fight, Frankie decides to take a chance with Maggie. Is he impressed by her persistence and heart or is he dependent on her for a new fighter? As the story progresses, we begin to realize it's both. On her own, working as a waitress and living in a dump far away from her ""white trash"" family, Maggie says that boxing is the only thing she ever grew to love, which gives Frankie the motive to like her. ""You'll never argue with me or ask me questions,"" Frankie demands.
 
Keeping sports movie conventions in mind, we expect Maggie to take a few beatings before she discovers her hidden talent to fight. But Eastwood would never be that easy on us. Maggie, plain and simple, is an exceptional fighter, knocking her opponents out in the first round. 
 
Now you might be asking what's so special about this movie? The way I'm describing it makes it sound like a generic sports film. But I should remind you that Eastwood's ""Unforgiven"" also seemed like a run-of-the-mill western and ""Mystic River"" a typical murder mystery. Again, he goes against the traditional and shows a deep understanding of the human element in his stories. 
 
In ""Million Dollar Baby,"" Eddie narrates and we're reminded of Morgan Freeman's performance as Red in ""The Shawshank Redemption."" He says, ""Boxing is an unnatural sport - you swing with your right arm while relying on your left foot."" Perhaps the same can be said of Eastwood's talent as a director. As an actor, he's still the toughest guy there is - gruff, deep-voiced with a scowl atop his forehead. As a filmmaker, he's the opposite - sensitive, thoughtful and driven by characterizations. As in ""Unforgiven"" and ""Mystic River,"" we feel we could write 1,000-page essays on the characters in these movies based on what they say, where they work and how they act around certain people. Amazingly, though, they defy our expectations based on what we've seen in other films of the same genre.
 
What happens in ""Million Dollar Baby"" doesn't happen because of a plot. We see these characters grow and experience situations we'd never want to find ourselves in but would watch others so that we can learn from them. Throughout the first two-thirds of the film, Eastwood's cinematographer, Tom Stern, flushes the film with low-key lighting. Only one side of the characters' faces is lit, and yet we still discover things about them. Towards the end, when their whole faces can be seen, not only do we know more, but so do they.
 
Eastwood, Swank and Freeman deliver performances that are so good that after we see the film we can't imagine other actors in their roles. They play characters with suppressed feelings who don't necessarily learn how to express them, which makes their roles more complicated and mysterious.
 
""Million Dollar Baby"" is a film that deserves study. Eastwood directs it with such confidence in the craft of filmmaking that we realize he's not willing to compromise his instincts that stay true to the story and the people in it. We may not walk away from the theater feeling sublime, but we leave feeling touched and later on - days, months, years - we still find ourselves in deep thought over what we've seen.","",False,False,False,False,4.5,"2005-01-02","Matthew H","RT","45716fb3-1420-4208-ab91-8817576305ad"
