5.0,"[b][u][font=Courier New][size=2]MILLION DOLLAR BABY * * * *[/size][/font][/u][/b]
 
[font=Courier New]Starring Clint Eastwood, Hilary Swank, Morgan Freeman[/font]
 
[font=Courier New]Directed by Clint Eastwood[/font]
 
[font=Courier New]Running time: 2 hours and 17 minutes[/font]
 
[font=Courier New]Rated PG-13[/font]
 
 
 
[font=Courier New][img]http://img.photobucket.com/albums/v294/devontate/million1.jpg[/img][/font]
 
 
 
[font=Courier New]Before Million Dollar Baby began playing at my local theater, there was much talk about the film being the years best picture. Similarly, the same thing happened when I had made plans to see Mystic River last year. I didnt find Mystic River to be the masterpiece everyone was making it out to be and perhaps it was due to the hype it received before I saw it. When hype comes before the film, expectations rise and generally, are let down, even if just a little because the film was already rested on a foundation of praise and high hopes. A classic example of this would be 1998s Godzilla, although it didnt receive any pre-praise, it certainly made you think it would be possible for post-praise with all the advertisements and whatnot. In this case, Million Dollar Baby is an extraordinary exception, in that I went into the theater expecting greatness and came out having received greatness of a greater measure.[/font]
 
 
 
[font=Courier New][img]http://img.photobucket.com/albums/v294/devontate/million2.jpg[/img] [/font]
 
 
 
[font=Courier New]Million Dollar Baby is the story of Maggie Fitzgerald (Hilary Swank), a thirty-one year old waitress, who wants desperately, or rather determinedly, to become a boxer. She looks to Frankie Dunn (Clint Eastwood), a boxing manager/trainer/gym owner, for guidance, only the old man isnt looking to guide her anywhere. Shes a girl, Frankie doesnt train girls, and, on top of that, shes too old. Eventually, due to a visibly relentless drive to become a boxer and word-of-mouth from Frankies long-time friend/gym co-owner, Eddie Dupris (Morgan Freeman), the old-timer decides to take her on. The two, three including Freeman, develop a sort of father/daughter relationship that strengthens the longer theyre together, making the journey to victory (and there is plenty of it) all the more risky and endearing. [/font]
 
 
 
[font=Courier New]*SPOILER*[/font]
 
 
 
[font=Courier New]The formula boxing movie ends here. By the time we reach the films final act, the characters are so well fleshed out and so well and believably performed by their actors, that any emotional blows they begin to encounter move us, leave us winded from the knock out of emotion. This occurs when Maggie is on the verge of claiming victory of a match and is suddenly, and unexpectedly punched in the face by a dirty-fighting contender. Maggie falls to the floor, her neck landing on a stool. She becomes paralyzed and bed ridden for the rest of the movie. [/font]
 
 
 
[font=Courier New]From there on out, the film is not just about the fights that take place within a boxing ring, but also the fights that occur outside of it, and sometimes, within ourselves. Spending the rest of her life as an immobile has no meaning for Maggie. Being a boxer was all about movement, physical activity, and the triumph of beating someone or something. For Frankie, his fights are all internal. He has a failed (and unexplained) relationship with his own daughter who returns every one of his letters and is now caring after a woman who hes treated like a daughter, a woman he feels as if hes ruined completely by training her and taking her to the top and to this tragic point. As for Eddie, he watches Frankie go through this, watches it all with his one good eye (the other taken when staying in a ring too long against Frankies advise).[/font]
 
 
 
[img]http://img.photobucket.com/albums/v294/devontate/million3.jpg[/img]
 
 
 
[font=Courier New]At one point, Maggie asks Frankie to end her life. The way she asks him, it gives Frankie no choice because already he feels responsible for her current condition and to end it, would be to end her suffering, a favor redeeming him of his alleged faults. Of course, Frankie takes a day to arrive at his answer, but whether he goes through with it or not, Ill leave that open. One of the most powerful scenes in the film is when Frankie confronts a priest about this dilemma and is told by the priest to leave it to God. Frankie responds, She didnt ask for Gods help, she asked for mine.[/font]
 
 
 
[font=Courier New]Million Dollar Baby is an extraordinary film about the human spirit, the limits of friendship, redemption, and the fights we cant always win the way we want to. Clint Eastwood and Morgan Freeman provide the film with light-hearted humor and believable chemistry. Clint Eastwood and Hilary Swank are kinetic. Freeman and Swank are good together as well. Each of the three deserves nominations, but this is Eastwoods creation. This is his best performance and best-directed feature since Unforgiven. [/font]
 
 
 
[font=Courier New][img]http://img.photobucket.com/albums/v294/devontate/million4.jpg[/img][/font]
 
 
 
[font=Courier New]Still, Finding Neverland is my choice for the best picture of the year, only because it is a more enjoyable film. Million Dollar Baby is not a film you should enjoy, but one that should make you think and admire. The film is dark through and through and is hardly ever uplifting. This isnt a flaw, but an excellent detail in realism, something that Finding Neverland isnt, yet is undoubtedly more appealing and enjoyable. Million Dollar Baby is my choice for the second best film of the year. [/font]","",False,False,False,False,5.0,"2005-01-09","","RT","900669084"
5.0,"[color=darkslateblue]  Throughout a long career of directing movies, Clint Eastwood has a long history of movies that were range from mediocre to good.  With one exception - the great Unforgiven.  Now, he has directed his second great film - Million Dollar Baby.[/color]
[color=#483d8b]  The premise of Million Dollar Baby is simplicity itself - a 31 year old waitress played by Hilary Swank(she is fabulous and there is nobody else who could have played this role.) seeks out a trainer/cut man played by Clint Eastwood(never better) to train her how to be a boxer.  Eastwood's character owns a run down gym called the Hit Pit.  He is seeking to atone for unmentioned wrongs against his estranged daughter.  And I haven't even mentioned Morgan Freeman as a one-eyed former boxer who performs around the gym.  Forgive me, he is excellent, as well.[/color]
[color=#483d8b]  Million Dollar Baby is an emotionally powerful movie about taking chances and trying to improve one's life.  It also defines boxing as having to do more with heart, then with simple punishment.  This is an important distinction.  Even more importantly, it does not flinch away from anything unpleasant, especially broken noses.[/color]
[color=#483d8b]  And I never thought Clint Eastwood had it in him to make another great movie, especially this one.[/color]","",False,True,False,False,5.0,"2005-01-09","walter m","RT","c0ccc6f5-3009-4812-bc50-f4fdb3941276"
