3.5,"MOOD: Excited, Ill
  
 So, it turns out that all my worrying about being sick is actually true. I visited a kind man known as a ""doctor"" (I'm hardly ever sick) and he told me that I have a sinus infection. So, I'm sitting here with a stuffy nose, presumably clogged with bacteria and snot. And I still can hardly hear out of my right ear. When I talk, my voice is distorted, and when I don't, there is an odd soft ringing noise resonating throughout my head. However, I've been told I can't complain because I won't take Advil or Tylenol. I just don't like the idea of popping pills to solve all problems. Let everything work out naturally. I have little helpers called ANTIBODIES. The doctor gave me a prescribed medicine though, so I have to take them. I mainly just dislike the casual use of Tylenol and Advil. I don't get headaches often, so I never really have to worry about frequent use, however. And even if I feel like I have a slight headache, I always think I'm just making it up, especially when considering certian circumstances. I am a fake hypochondriac.
  
 My friend was trying to tell a story yesterday (and another friend attempted to today) and I was really having trouble making out the sentences and then molding them together to form a coherent story. I feel completely detached. I felt like alienating myself, so I went to see [i]Million Dollar Baby[/i] yesterday morning...alone. Surprisingly, I could hear the film perfectly well. And if I couldn't it was the actors fault (must Morgan Freeman always speak with that wise old man cadence which mostly consists of soft mumbling). And then there was Eastwood's raspy and stubborn voice. Oh well, I got through the film despite the distractingly implausible tones of these two characters.
  
  I am surprised at my slight apathy towards [b][i]Million Dollar Baby[/i][/b]. What begins as a conventional tale of an underdog slowly unfolds to become a daring and poignant revelation. However, this thematic film concerning dreams, life's journey and redemption doesn't have an ending as powerful and cathartic as it seems to believe it does. I never really thought the religious material in the film was completely necessary other than to add some ""deeper emotions"", however unwarranted they may be at the time. This extremely well-acted film, which has its fair share of clichés, is held together by an emotional core, which is not as strong as I had expected it to be. And I've also realized that I don't much like Clint Eastwood, the actor. His squinty, stubborn was of portraying characters can be downright distracting at times. The tenacious Hilary Swank portrays her character with resilience and power. The film seems to have a nearly unnecessary voiceover which causes a somewhat ambivalent and confused narrative. It seems as if two stories are being told, and if that was the point, it should have been a bit more polished.
  
  The second story in [b][i]Chungking Express[/i][/b]was quite superior to the preceding tale. The first had its charming bits, but the second included the adorable Faye Wong and The Mamas and the Papas' lamenting yet hopeful ""California Dreamin'"" which perfectly correlates with the relationship of the two main characters. Now that is tough to beat -- no wonder why I thought the first section was inferior. Regardless, [i]Chungking [/i]is a quick-paced, mesmerizing, energetic, if a bit fidgety two-parter. I can only expect it's rating to rise upon repeat viewings. And I find myself very eager to rewatch it. Odd for a film I just watched. Wong Kar Wai is a cool guy, but I'm sure you already knew that.
  
  [b][i]The Life Aquatic[/i][/b]...I really don't know what to say. I'm somewhat disappointed in Wes Anderson, but I expected to be. I enjoyed moments, but the characters were nearly contemptuous and the supporting characters were all very poorly written and rather forgettable. Considering the film and his love for Bowie (which is very evident in the film), I think this film should have been titled, ""Ocean Oddity"". 
  
    So, what is the deal with this genre-beding romatic zombie comedy, [b][i]Shaun of the Dead[/i][/b]? Does it deserve to be placed in all three of those genres? Not really. Does it care? Not really. The nonchalant [i]Shaun of the Dead[/i] is the most enjoyable when it is making you giggle by it's jocular humor or overembellishing the ""routine"" life of many ""living"" individuals who are hardly...well, living. [i]Shaun [/i]claims to be a hybrid of the three formally mentioned genres, and for the most part it only fits into one nicely: comedy, stupid! It's not exactly ever frightening, or romantic, but that doesn't matter very much, it just accounts for a few ridiculous (obviously intended, but still unbearably silly) moments which made me groan like a zombie. I didn't think the foreshadowing humor was very funny, but the filmmakers must have, since they used it quite often throughout the film. I have a few more complaints about the characters, situations, lack of answers and motivational flaws, but I'm sure most were presented deliberately. However, I do not appreciate how certain films which pass themselves off as farces scapegoat the originals for intentional flaws. I believe that intentionally placed flaws should be played up a bit more (not overtly so, just not extremely subtle).
  
  So, that's about it. I have an illness, I watch too many films (4 in two days!) and I'm Boston bound tomorrow.
  
 I feel some sort of weird obligation to post this entry, even though I should be packing and making preparations. If I forget anything or get lost on the trip, I'm blaming every single one of you on my friends list.
  
  Anyway, ciao.","",False,False,False,False,3.5,"2005-01-12","","RT","900612375"
4.5,"MILLION DOLLAR BABY (2005)
         
       CLINT EASTWOOD, HILARY SWANK, MORGAN FREEMAN
       
           Drama Quick Rating (out of 5)
                Memorable One Liners: 4
           Acting: 5
           Cinematography: 4.5
           Direction: 5
         Heart-tugging action: 5
         Fight Sequences: 4.5
         Script:5
                Pace: 3 (Its not a bad thing, though)
        
 Yeah, so what, Clint? Another boxing movie? We''ve all seen Rocky, and there is hundreds more of the same. If you think your going to get around that with throwing in a female, wasn't [i]Against the Ropes[/i] just made not even a year ago? Why the heck was everyone raving about M[i]illion Dollar Baby[/i]? I wasn't quite sure--but I had to find out. Being that the biggest, baddest movie theater in the world (Muvico Palace 20) is 5 mintues away and was showing [i]Baby [/i]before its wide release, I had no choice but to succumb to my curiousity. (The rest of you all can see this the 28th). Was I impressed? You bet. Storywise, Eastwood keeps it simple (on the surface), but that is part of its beauty. We follow an old boxing trainer, Frankie (Eastwood), who owns a rustic (to say the least) gym. Nothing seems to be going right for him; for one thing, he is estranged from his family. Other not-so-zesty events take place, but I wont ruin them for you. Along comes Maggie (Swank), a fiery southern girl with dreams of one day becoming a famous boxer. Though Frankie originally rejects her, she shows up at his gym. She works and works, and works some more, and eventually, Frankie agrees to train her. As I said before, it seems simple if your an outsider ""looking down"" if you will. It even seems that [i]Baby [/i]is the usual, cliched, underdog-takes-it-all-though-the-odds-are-severely-against-him type movie. Is this the case? Yes. And no. I cant explain that much further without giving too much away. While the scenes in the ring are brutal and awesome, [i]Million Dollar Baby[/i] isn't just about boxing-- it's goes surprisingly deeper. Frankie's family doesn't want a thing to do with him, and Maggie's family cares only for themselves and doesn't respect anything she does; including making it big in boxing. The most beautiful aspect of [i]Baby [/i]is the way it handles Frankie and Maggie's relationship-- though they originally clash-- they learn they need each other for more than just boxing. Yes, Frankie learns to accept Maggie's love and respect for him, much like a daughter should. Frankie becomes the father Maggie never had; he looks out for her and supports her. Words can only say so much; the intricacy of the whole relationship is best on film. Most movies choose a certain emotion-- victory, overcoming the odds, coping... but Eastwood's masterpiece displays a ""range"" rarely seen in cinema today. We see small, yet touching moments of over-coming the odds. We see pain, despair, joy, risk, reward, life, death, victory, tragedy, error, redemption, greed, sacrifice, patience, and anger...and that is only the tip of the iceberg. But enough about the story. The direction by Eastwood is excellent. He doesn't attempt to be brilliant, and that is why he is so good. He has a niche for creating atmosphere and feeling. His unrushed pace lets his movie unfold naturally, which in turn makes it more ""real"" and ""powerful."" He picked excellent actors, himself included, which give such human performances one often forgets their watching a film. Eastwood plays Frankie, an old man with deep hurts who can't forigve himself. He covers all that with his gruff personality and leathery looks, and Eastwood is perfect. Freeman plays on of Frankie's old boxers who now works at the Hit Pit Gym alongside the old trainer. His charcter had a very Shawshan-ic voice over and was Frankie's personal ""Jiminey Cricket."" Swank plays Maggie, the 31 year old ""Who grew up knowing only one thing...she was trash"" (To steal from Freeman's Voiceover). Swank is, in one word, phenominal. As she vehemently fights turning into what she was destined to be (trailer trash), Maggie's determination and joy (and good looks, i might add) turn a not-so-likable character into a truly lovable one. She works in a diner in the day, saving the scraps of food for herself. At night, she eats the scraps and counts the change she worked for. Then she heads to the gym, practicing for countless hours, even in the dark. Even when the unexpected happens, you can't but help to feel for and love her. [i]Million Dollar Baby[/i] is a rather risky move for such an old man. Many people will be disapointed with its rather unexpected turn and slower pacing. But that doesn't hinder the movie; in my opinion, it acctually makes it what it is-- a brilliantly crafted, perfectly acted, and expertly directed film. But the real reason this film is so good? It's human handling of many emotions.It is sad as it is joyful, tragic as it is triumphant, and heartfelt as it is devastating. Full of amazing boxing matches, uplifting moments of human spirit, great acting and direction, and a heartfelt/tragic story about love, acceptance, dreams, and what family truly is (and even some humor), Eastwood's [i]Baby [/i]is rare gem of a film that will stay with you many hours, even days, after watching it.","",False,False,False,False,4.5,"2005-01-12","","RT","900682740"
4.0,"i originally rated this one lower, but had a change of heart after seeing it on cable. many of the reasons for my low rating are still valid in my mind- no character ambiguity, many cliches, contrived storyline, etc. however, maybe it was the mood i was in at the time, but it became clear to me what an emotional wallop this one packs. for all it's flaws, the performances create some truly unforgettable scenes (including one of the most heart-wrenching scenes i have ever seen- not too difficult to guess which one).","",False,False,False,False,4.0,"2005-01-13","","RT","900697836"
