 There are things about this film I didn't like, and there are things about it's point of view that I disagree with.  There are things that I might have handled differently.  That said, this is, without a doubt, one of the most powerful movies I've seen...ever, quite possibly.
 
 This film masqurades as 'Rocky', at first, but is really something more along the lines of 'The Deer Hunter'.  It's a movie that, if I saw it without knowing what time it was made in, I might have expected was made in the 70's.  It's use of music, extensive lighting, 'pretty' scenery, and heightened stylistic choices, are all minimal, as were many such films in the 70's. Also, it's performances, and the story are likewise minimal.  It's a story only a director like Eastwood, and there are few in his class, could tell, because without it being explained to them by a wise old bear, few would understand how to tell it.  
 
 This film is simple.  So simple, in fact, that some of the least sophisticated, and some psudo-sophisticated filmgoers might get bored.  But that's OK becaue the average movie fan, and the true sophisticated filmgoer will become captivated by it's simple, straightforward honesty in the way it communicates.  They may be on different wavelenghts, perhaps, but both will still be engaged.  
 
 This film is really about what you want out of life.  What is success, and what role does family/friends play in it?  At least that's what I got out of it, among other things.  While it has the 'Rocky' like triumph over difficult circumstances, it has, more importantly, I think, a 'Deer Hunter' like fatalism to it, that makes you really question what this film, and thusly life, are about, what is important?
 
 This is where I have some problems, personally.  I'm not quite sure I agree with the conclusions they seem come to, at least from my point of view.  Of course, that's not saying anything about the film's quality, just something that it personally meant to me.  It's just that I 
 
 ([b]DO NOT [/b]read if you haven't seen the movie, trust me, you will regret it later if you do) 
 
 [Spoiler] have never given too much creedence to the 'Heroic Suicide'.  I can see suicide as being beautiful in a tragically human sort of way.  But I've always had trouble with it being shown as something heroic, even given horrible circumstances, and I felt this film did, even given the fact it was still obviously being shown as tragic.  
 
 When Maggie says that she can't go on, not after having people cheer her name, after being in magazines, hitting the top, as it were, and Freeman talking about how she at least had her shot, when so many people didn't ever get a shot...and somehow that was the sum total of her life...well, I've just never been able to buy that.
 
 Perhaps that's not what it was getting at.  I realize that success, when it is real, isn't about money and isn't about status, but about the drive inside you, about how the hard work changes you personally, etc.  and that's all well and good, and perhaps had it played that up a bit more, I would have been more in tone with the film.  But she talked about the crowds cheering, being on magazines etc.  Perhaps it was just her way of talking about more important things, a mask if you were, for how it had changed her and how it gave her her only friend in Franky.  It just seems to me that if that were the case, then she would stay alive for those more important things, the things that had become part of her character, and because of Franky, who was her only real family, her only real blood, and she, his only real family, his only real blood. [/spoiler]
 
 And I felt that this is a film that is asking the observant viewer so deeply personal questions, and, at least seeming to me, to draw some conclusions, that things of this nature, and what they mean to the viewer personally, were part of the film experience.  I wasn't sure I liked what I got, but perhaps that speaks to the films power.  
 
 The more I think about this film, the more I like it.  I could nitpic on details here or there, I thought some of the early fights were horribly choreographed, for example, but anything I would point out wouldn't really have anything to do with the impact of the movie.  It could become one of my favorite films of all time, or it could slide a little as time goes on.  All I will tell you for now, is that the film affected me like few have, and given what I have already told you, I'll leave it at that.  
 
 
