1.5,"This movie is what i like to call an ""Oscar Sweep Film"". It just comes out, like, a month before the oscars and takes all the awards because it takes all good actors and fornicates with them to make an oscar-bound love child. Or in this case, a ""Million Dollar Baby"". Unfortunatley for the oscars, in order to become and ""Oscar Sweep Film"", the movie must be the most boring film you've ever seen. Where are all the Oscars for movies like ""Hero"", and ""Eternal Sunshine""? Doesn't anyone think Bill Murray deserves an Oscar after all this time? What about ""Maria Full of Grace""? Do people even know what good movies are anymore? Just because Clint Eastwood is pumping his body full of preservation chemicals just enough to make another movie in which, get this, he's an angry veteran of something, doesn't mean it deserves and oscar or eight. Morgan Freeman being the only saving grace to Clint Eastwood's consistently ornery mood, and Hillary Swank's horrible, horrible southern accent gets this movie a three.","",False,False,False,False,1.5,"2005-01-20","","RT","900762340"
5.0,"Modern cinema rarely moves you. It rarely grabs you with a story and says - ""CARE"" - Million Dollar Baby is incredibly refined and artistic movie making, a story full of cliches, but yet so moving and so powerful it reduces an audience to tears for the final 40 minutes.
 
Eastwood has the confidence in his and the actors skill to leave the camera on them, allowing them to act and fill the screen. The audience encounters long lingering scenes of peoples reactions, anguish and love. He makes you love the characters and yet tells you so little about their past. He never allows the camera to intrude - it stays still when you need to focus and it pans only to tell a story. No gimmicks, no jump cuts, no CGI. Remember when all films were made this way? People confuse Eastwoods style with economy of effort - wrong. Eastwood wants the audience to focus on his story. To focus on the actors and the words they say. There is nothing in this film that you don't need to see. The pacing is extraodinary, it creates an ever mounting sense of melancholy until it delivers it's sucker punch, despite the supposed euphoric element of story up to that point.
 
Eastwood achieves this quality by ensuring the script is tight, witty and natural - but most importantly he surronds himself with phenominal talent. It is without question Eastwoods finest acting performance since Unforgiven and possibly ever. Yet he and Freeman, as good as they are, are pummelled by weight and depth of Swanks performance. Eastwood will be done out of a best actor oscar because Swank is so good. People will compare his performance to hers and not to other leading men.
 
In my humble opinion this is one of the finest examples of film making in the last 20 years - it is extraordinary in its simplistic beauty. No orks, no cgi and no real life stars to caricature - this is not an impression of a great movie it is a great movie.
 
Best actor male and femal, Best Film, Best Director, Best Adapted screenplay and best cinemaphotographer really should head it's way....but I doubt they will.","",False,False,False,False,5.0,"2005-01-20","","RT","900762841"
4.5,"[b]Summary:
[/b]Million Dollar Baby is a boxing movie about Maggie (Hilary Swank) a middle aged woman hell bent on becoming a boxing champion. Her determination is evident with her constant training and hounding of Frankie (Clint Eastwood) who is currently training a male boxer headed towards a title. When Frankies boxer decides to switch to a new manager, Frankie gives in and begins training Maggie. The two develop a father/daughter relationship that fills a hole in Frankies life left by his daughters estrangement.
 
[b]Why You Should See This Movie:[/b]
Million Dollar Baby is a truly remarkable film created by the incredibly talented Clint Eastwood. The acting is primarily from Swank, Eastwood and Morgan Freeman who plays a washed up boxer in Frankies gym. The three are all equally impressive in their portrayals of interesting and intriguing characters. The direction by Eastwood is fabulous, bringing a fabulous story to the screen artistically and with skill, however, not quite up to his monumental achievement last year with Mystic River. The film is very entertaining and is broken down into three distinct pieces that are put together very well. The story is incredible with depth and power that takes a while to fully comprehend. The twist 2/3rds of the way through the film was surprising and transitioned well into the third piece of the film.
 
[b]Why You Shouldn't See This Movie:[/b]
If you like movies, this film is worth watching. I cant think of much reason to not see this film.
 
[b]Conclusion:[/b]
Million Dollar Baby is a sports movie that explores themes well beyond sports that will leave you thinking well after the credits roll.","",False,False,False,False,4.5,"2005-01-21","","RT","900762176"
