5.0,"[font=Garamond][font=Garamond]Until about a week ago, I didnt think there would be a single film to contend with ""The Aviator"" for Best Picture.  ""Closer"" was well-crafted but too nihilistic, ""Finding Neverland"" was too sentimental, and ""Sideways,"" as fantastic as it is, was too small to go against Martin Scorseses juggernaut.  After seeing Clint Eastwoods latest film ""Million Dollar Baby,"" I became convinced that the awards season is going to be a little more interesting. [/font][/font]
[font=Garamond][font=Garamond]
Eastwood kept a low profile on the making and marketing of this movie; so low, in fact, that until two months ago I had no idea that he had directed a new film.  Yet Eastwood did more than just direct; he produced, acted, and wrote the musical score for this film, and ""Million Dollar Baby"" is a triumph in all regards.  It continues the direction toward a more personal, direct form of storytelling Eastwood began with 1992s ""Unforgiven.""

Eastwood plays Frankie Dunn, a crusty and rough but wise boxing trainer and manager whose encyclopedic knowledge of every aspect of the sport has brought many young fighters under his wing to be trained, but whose inability to take chances when necessary has cost him those very same fighters to other trainers and managers who are willing to give them shots at bigger money and bigger fights.

Into his life walks a female boxer named Maggie Fitzgerald (Hilary Swank), who wants Frankie to train her.  Frankie flat-out refuses; he thinks women have no business boxing, Maggie has no natural talent, and shes too old to fight even if she did have talent.  Unwavered, Maggie shows up at Frankies gym every day to practice and progresses without Frankies assistance.  Soon after, Frankie agrees to train her and to everyones surprise, Maggie becomes a rising star in the world of female boxing.

With just that rudimentary plot description, it would seem that ""Million Dollar Baby"" is one sports-movie cliché piled on top of another.  Yet about halfway through, the film takes a left turn that even I never saw coming, and it is what gives the film all of its emotional weight.  Yet it is not a ""plot twist"" by any means; it flows naturally with the story arc and never feels jarring.

There arent many films that could truly be said to have been perfectly directed, but ""Million Dollar Baby"" is a perfectly directed piece of cinema.  Every frame of it is observant, painting the portrait of the lives of young boxers and those who train them: the dingy gyms, long hours spent training, the visits to the hospital, and the jobs on the side where pennies are scraped together to keep the whole works running.  Not a single shot is wasted or unnecessary; there is not a single ounce of fat on this film.

Eastwood gives the performance of his career.  Previous characters of his like the Man With No Name and Dirty Harry Callahan were one-dimensional parts in excellent films, but Eastwoods portrayal of Frankie Dunn seems to be an echo of that machismo of youth.  Here we have an Eastwood questioning life decisions, carrying guilt, and pondering his motives for actions past and present.  His performance reflects the style of the film: observant, but short, sweet and simple.  In an early scene where Maggie asks to be trained, and pitches herself as ""pretty tough,"" Eastwood replies, ""Girlie, tough aint enough.""

As for Swank, her performance as Maggie is also gutsy.  It has been scooping up the most awards and praise because hers requires more range; she is extroverted in one scene and introverted the next.  Yet her role is not as introspective as Eastwoods.  While shes a virtual lock to win the Oscar, the film moves along just as much on Eastwoods acting as it does on Swanks.

Then there is Morgan Freeman, an extraordinarily underrated performer who narrates the film and plays a supporting role as Frankies friend Eddie Dupris.  He is solid, like he is in most of his work, but this is the best film and best performance hes been a part of since ""Seven.""  The quality of the material elevates Freemans part from what is usually a minor role in an ordinary sports film to one that has some emotional substance behind it.

I see that Ive used the phrase I desperately wanted to avoid, and that would be ""sports film.""  Its easy to lump ""Million Dollar Baby"" in with other sports films, but at its very core it is not a sports film.  Like ""Raging Bull,"" ""Million Dollar Baby"" is not a movie about boxing, but a movie whose lead character happens to be a boxer.  Ultimately, it is a touching tale of the bond formed between two people in the ring.
 
And that's my two cents.
