2.5,"(Theatre) (First Viewing, 2nd Eastwood film)

In a cinematic landscape increasingly marked by CGI and technical razzle-dazzle, many seem to find solace in the muted, immaculately constructed films of Clint Eastwood.  While I do applaud Mr. Eastwood's ability to craft highly personal and distinctive films within the confines of the traditional Hollywood studio, I find very little that warrants the attention and acclaim his last two films have received.  Yes, his plots and characters are solidly constructed- but I find them solid to the point of utter banality.  

MILLION DOLLAR BABY is quite good for its first half- the character dynamics are engaging, the fight scenes tense, the script brings up a number of interesting issues regarding loyalty, guilt, and redemption.  But all of this is discarded when the film abruptly veers into [i]Lifetime[/i] drama territory for the film's final half.  Yes, it's done better than most, but it's never able to transcend its tired material.  And that's the problem of the movie.  

As well-done as it is, MILLION DOLLAR BABY gives us nothing we haven't seen before, rendering it completely inconsequential.","",False,False,False,False,2.5,"2005-01-26","","RT","900614406"
5.0,"This movie doesn't take a single mis-step. Not a single one! Everything about it is perfect: the pacing, the acting, the directing, the editting, even the chips I was eating. Eastwood knows his shit!
 
What put the movie over-the-top was that there were just about every opportunity to give into a classic Hollywood-cliché, but Eastwood does not give in. He takes what could be a cliché and turns it into something spectacular. Even if he did give in, the movie would have been amazing, but not 10/10.
 
I am now in a position of having seen all movies nominated for Best Picture. This hasn't happened to me before. And the winner is......MILLION DOLLAR BABY! (in my opinion at least). For the hell of it, this is how I rank them:
 
1- Million Dollar Baby (oh baby!)
2- The Aviator (and I'm a pilot)
3- Sideways (Swingers à-la better)
4- Finding Neverland (the pan man)
5- Ray (I still don't like it)
 
MDB definately makes it as one of my all-time favorites.","",False,False,False,False,5.0,"2005-01-28","","RT","900603489"
