5.0,":'( these freakin' movies making me cry..... *tears*
 
Love, friendship, passion, determination, compassion, more love.
 
This is one of the best films I've ever seen, yet I don't think I'll be able to ever watch it again. Like [i]A Very Long Engagement[/i], Clint Eastwood's film is, putting it lightly, emotionally taxing. I can't say much about it without ruining the third act of the film, but I can say this- [i]Million Dollar Baby[/i] deserves every award it's nominated for. Clint is amazing- how great he was with that priest...""Can you spare a few minutes for the immaculate conception?"". I mean, he made the guy drop an f-bomb. At church. Priceless. And then Eastwood turns around and breaks your heart with those crinkly eyes, that young man's grin on an old man's face, that pure and real emotion. Maybe he doesn't deserve an award for acting. Jamie Foxx? He acted. Clint Eastwood just....was.
 
Remember how I sensed an ambiguous relationship between Eastwood and Swank from the trailer (not to mention after her Globes speech I think Hilary is totally in love with Clint- and I really can't blame her)? Well I think I was right, to a point. It seems that Eastwood, with his paternal instincts, protectiveness, and [i]mo chuisl-[/i]bestowing, thought of Maggie as his daughter, his companion, and maybe a little more. There was a point in the story, however, where I believe Maggie's admiration and daughterly love became something much more. I think that Frankie became everything to her, including a great love...and the complicated yet so simple relationship between the two made me love them both even more. It made you want them to be together, forever, in that little log cabin. 
 
 
[i]SPOILERS BELOW[/i]
 
 
Love, friendship, passion, determination, more love. I came out of the theatre with those emotions conveyed to me thoroughly. The fact that Frankie loved Maggie so much that he would help her die, that he would do the last thing in the world he wanted, which was to let her go...that hit me hard. That's what you do when you love somebody. The priest was wrong- all of the sins, all of the things Frankie was guilty for up until that point, they would have paled in comparison to the guilt he would feel if he had LET HER LIVE...NOT if he helped her die. Typical backwards Catholic thinking. 
 
I cried my eyes out...not when Maggie asks Frankie to help her die, not when she does, not when he tells her that she is his darling, his blood...I cried when I saw the Diner. I don't cry at sad, I cry at bittersweet...and seeing him through the window, eating his lemon merengue, at Ira's Diner, finally broke me down.
 
Incredible, incredible film. Too bad I can never watch it again. It took its toll on my heart, my soul, my mind...and yet [b]it made me believe in love[/b]. It also conveyed a hope for happiness, contentment- that even if everything turns out wrong, or even if it doesn't- that if you have your moment, like Morgan Freeman says, you can leave the world knowing that ""I did alright."".","",False,False,False,False,5.0,"2005-01-28","","RT","900693994"
4.0,"[i]Finally[/i] got to see this and I was not disappointed. Million Dollar Baby is a strong and emotional story that doesn't rush to its conclussions, it treats each plot point carefully allowing for a stronger payoff. Traditional underdog sport movie cliches have new life and dimension breathed into them by the terrific script by Paul Haggis. And cliche is too strong of a word to use because Million Dollar Baby is not a movie about boxing, it just happens to have boxing in it. 

On the same note, Million Dollar Baby is not about traditional underdog sports movie charactures, it's about the characters that inhabit them. The strength of this movie is in getting to know and consequently being affected by the rich personalities portrayed by Swank, Eastwood and Freeman. (Each one deserves the recognition the Academy has bestowed them.) When the powerful final act lands its blow, you are defenseless against it.

A-

The only negative to the movie was the jarring performance by the kid playing 'Danger' Barch. It was a bit over-the-top and distracting. Although, his character sets up a wonderful pay off that allows me to forgive it a bit.","",False,False,False,False,4.0,"2005-01-28","","RT","900630014"
5.0,"Dammit! I finally cried in a movie! Haven't done that since I saw the movie Paulie 7 years ago (that damn bird just had to leave the little girl), and now I finally cry again. Million Dollar Baby should be the definition of a perfect film. It's a movie you compare others with to see which one deserves a 10/10. This one does! Great acting, and great script. It sucked me in to this world, and brought tears to my eyes. I recommend this movie to everyone. If you only go see one movie this year, see this movie! It'll blow you away!","",False,False,False,False,5.0,"2005-01-28","","RT","900706519"
