5.0,"This is by far the best boxing movie ever made and there has been some good boxing movies!!  Clint Eastwood turns in a bravo performance as not only actor-director but also composer.  Hilary Swank perfectly cast as the 30 something female boxer he reluctantly trains.  Morgan Freeman should finally win an Oscar for his portrayal of the ex boxing cleaning man of the gym that Eastwood's character owns.  The film has more depth than at least 3/4 of the movies I have seen this year combined.  Eastwood creates a masterpeice that will endure forever.  It has been  a long time since I felt this much emotion from a film.  As I walked out of the theater I was stunned.  This is going on my top ten.  Maybe I have not seen many films up for best picture but there can't be one that is more human, raw, poignant than this Million Dollar Baby.[QUOTE][/QUOTE]","",False,False,False,False,5.0,"2005-01-29","","RT","900739645"
4.5,"Just like it took Maggie (Hilary Swank) a while to grow on Frankie (Clint Eastwood), it took a while for [i]Million Dollar Baby [/i]to grow on me. Certainly, my expectations were high, and when things were playing out in an all too average way, I was beginning to wonder what all the hype was about. 
 
But as the film continues (I don't want to elaborate too much), it takes an emotional turn and it's not hard to see why the movie has been raved about. Indeed, Hilary Swank does give one of the best leading female performances this year. Her ability to take a character that views herself as ""trash"", and not turn it into some annoying role, is impressive. She's completely loveable in a different kind of way. Freeman did a nice job, but nothing award worthy in my opinion.
 
Eastwood, on the other hand...I think his performance actually brought the film down a little bit. I don't know that many will agree with that, but it just seemed that he played his role in such a stereotypical way. When he was throwing punches on the sideline to ""will Maggie along"", I just rolled my eyes. Don't get me wrong, I think his direction was solid, but his performance just bothered me. It was Clint on screen and nothing more.
 
[i]Million Dollar Baby[/i] should do well at the Oscars, and rightfully so. Though, of the nominees (I've seen all now except for [i]Ray[/i]), I'd still like to see [i]Sideways[/i] win it all. 
 
A-:fresh:","",False,False,False,False,4.5,"2005-01-29","","RT","900674723"
3.0,"[center]~/~[/center]
EDIT: [i]I was fooled by this film, as the following paragraphs show. It is manipulative cliche retread. It's oscar quality retread but retread all the same. Eastwood paints by the numbers here, and offers a mediocre sports movie made worse because it tries to be about something else. (02/28/05)[/i]
[center]~/~[/center]
What I offer here is not a review but a few thoughts on the use of narration in Clint Eastwood's [i]Million Dollar Baby.[/i] It was after watching [i]Apocalypse Now[/i] that I became attuned to the way an effective narrator can do two things: drive the plot and develop the characters. In that film, Martin Sheen's character does so much to construct the myth of the Brando character through careful narration that when Brando finally appears on screen he is more than just a character, he is a legend.
 
In [i]Million Dollar Baby[/i] the narration is not so prominent but it does a great deal toward advancing the film toward its point, which as I see it is about the importance of making the most out of our time. The narration is done by Morgan Freeman's character, a one-eyed janitor at Eastwood's boxing facility. This narration adds cohesion and narrative focus, and much more.
 
The contribution to the tone of this work given by Freeman's raspy and sage delivery is second only to Eastwood's visual style (ie poor lighting). There is humor, remorse, nostalgia, and insight. These adjectives descibe Freeman's voice work and thus the film.
 
He introduces us to all the characters and connects the dots between the scenes. Freeman tells about these characters that inhabit this boxing world, tells us their hopes and dreams and life stories. This is the type of background and depth that can only be done by a narrator or in flashback. 
 
I used to prefer flashback because it seemed more organic and cinematic than someone reading to me. But here, the reason for the narration is worked in nicely. And more importantly it not only develops the other characters, it also develops Freeman's. He reveals much about himself through how he describes others, how he tells the story. Mostly he reveals his humanity. Simply put, Freeman's narration is crucial to the emotional and thematic impact of this film.","",False,False,False,False,3.0,"2005-01-29","","RT","900630960"
5.0,"Yesterday I closed the door for film viewings of 2004 with my trip to see the highly anticipated [i]Million Dollar Baby[/i]. This is Clint Eastwood's 25th time in the director's chair, and the end result has proven that great filmmakers only get better with age. This is the most impressive, devastating, and real motion picture of 2004. 
 
Eastwood produces, scores, directs and stars as the stubborn and aging boxing trainer Frankie Dunn in a beautifully played performance. I don't remember him ever being this amazingly deep as an actor as he goes places he's never been by the time this movie reaches its final act. The screenplay written by Paul Haggis is the key to making the film what it is - a story about the courage to make something happen and find yourself. The three main actors all deserve the nominations they have received, especially the brilliant Hilary Swank who gives everything to the part of the determined waitress who has nothing unless she has boxing. Veterans Eastwood and Freeman were unbelievably great together in [i]Unforgiven[/i] over a decade ago. They took home high honors back then, and it's once again deserved all around in this film. 
 
I haven't seen a better film since last year's [i]Lost in Translation. [/i]It knows no boundaries and has such a knowledgeable director's hand behind the camera that it cannot take a wrong step. This is an emotional rollercoaster of a film, as I found myself laughing, gasping, crying, then weeping as it played out. In the last three years Eastwood has offered the world of cinema three very different films. In 2002 it was [i]Blood Work[/i], a terrific mystery that had such a great plot. Last year it was the masterpiece [i]Mystic River[/i], a film of gigantic power that I thought he wouldn't be able to top. Now, just one year later he has constructed a film that seems like it has been labored over for 10 years for perfection. That's what [i]Million Dollar Baby [/i]is, perfection. In a trailer for the film one of the reviews states Eastwood as being ""a true artist"". I couldn't agree more.","",False,False,False,False,5.0,"2005-01-29","","RT","900670196"
5.0,"[table cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0 width=""100%"" border=0][tbody][tr][td valign=top width=""100%""][font=Verdana][color=#ff9900][font=Tahoma]I have gone through the entire year changing my favorite picture. At first it was [i]Friday Night Lights, [/i]and then I saw [i]Ray. [/i]As the year came to a finish, I thought that I had it set; [i]Ray[/i] seemed to be the best picture of the year for me. However, two films came at me like lightning that completely blew away the competition. First, I saw [i]The Aviator[/i], the biopic on the life and times of Howard Hughes. I left the theater thinking that I had seen the year's best film. Once again, I spoke too soon. Later that week, I had the great pleasure of seeing a film that came from nowhere, punching its way through all other films with its subtlety and knocking them down for the count with its extraordinary performances, the best I've seen in years. This movie, of course, is [i]Million Dollar Baby.[/i]

Being a HUGE fan of [/font][/color][i][color=#ff9900][font=Tahoma]Mystic[/font][/color][/i][i][color=#ff9900][font=Tahoma] [/font][/color][/i][i][color=#ff9900][font=Tahoma]River[/font][/color][/i][/font][font=Verdana][color=#ff9900][font=Tahoma], I came into the theater with great expectations. I left the theater quite disappointed; I was disappointed that the two hour movie wasn't two hours longer. It is a film that cannot be described in words. The best way that I can is by calling it a timeless masterpiece, a movie that will live on forever. Martin Scorsese once said that the plot of a film is what keeps a good movie going, but it is the film's story that keeps a classic living; the key to making a great movie is by creating great characters. Years later, you will not remember the end of [i]The Grudge [/i]or [i]Shrek 2, [/i]but you will always remember the way that Clint Eastwood, Hilary Swank, and Morgan Freeman swept us off our feet.

I usually give a very detailed synopsis of each film, but I am not going to get into that very much with this film. The movie is about a retired boxer and trainer, Frankie Dunn, who runs the Hit Pit Gym, a boxing gym. One day, a poor waitress, Maggie Fitzgerald, walks in and asks him to train her, without taking no for an answer. When Frankie says that he doesn't train girlies, she comes back by buying a membership to his gym, staying there from morning to closing. Frankie's assistant named Eddie ""Scrap"" Dupris, a one-eyed ex-boxer who takes the phrase ""living at the gym"" seriously, shows her the basics when Frankie's back is turned, and before long, Maggie is being trained by Frankie.

That is all that I am going to say because the rest is something that you will have to see.

If there is any justice in this world, Hilary Swank and Morgan Freeman will win in their categories. Hilary Swank is seldom seen in movies and that is probably because she has very high standards. Each film that she has done, [i]Boys Don't Cry, Insomnia, [/i]and now [i]Million Dollar Baby,[/i] there is either an Oscar to show for it, or at least Oscar talk. However, in this film, she has outdone herself. Morgan Freeman has also turned in a performance for the ages; his portrayal of Scrap is one that is up to par with his role as Red in my favorite film of all time, [i]The Shawshank Redemption.  [/i] And with his narrative voice, he drives the film. It is a true crime that [/font][/color][color=#ff9900][font=Tahoma]Cher[/font][/color][color=#ff9900][font=Tahoma] has an Oscar on her shelf, while Freeman's is empty. Let's hope that with this film, he will need to make some room.[/font][/color][/font]

[color=#ff9900][font=Tahoma]
[font=Verdana]Though I always root for Martin Scorsese to win Best Director because he has been so wrongfully shafted by the Academy, I would like Clint Eastwood to win this year. Though Scorsese did a wonderful job with [i]The Aviator[/i], the best directed film of the year is, without a doubt, [i]Million Dollar Baby.[/i] There is no way to describe how the film's shadowing and camera shots set an underlying darkness and self-emptiness to each frame.

Last year, I put [/font][/font][/color][font=Verdana][i][color=#ff9900][font=Tahoma]Mystic[/font][/color][/i][i][color=#ff9900][font=Tahoma] [/font][/color][/i][i][color=#ff9900][font=Tahoma]River[/font][/color][/i][color=#ff9900][font=Tahoma] as number three on my favorite films of all time. I never thought that a movie would come along and steal that title. But what other movie to take Eastwood's drama about death and dying, than Eastwood's film about life and living. This is, without a doubt, one of the best films that I have ever seen.[/font][/color][/font]

[/td][/tr][/tbody][/table]","",False,False,False,False,5.0,"2005-01-29","","RT","900652549"
5.0,"An awesome movie that stays with you after you leave the theater.","",False,False,False,False,5.0,"2005-01-29","","RT","900763909"
5.0,"I don't quite know what to say about [i]Million Dollar Baby[/i]. I saw it last night and I can't get it out of my brain. I thoroughly enjoyed it and immediately considered it a brilliant piece of cinema, but its staying power is stunning. Although I have not yet seen [i]The Aviator[/i], I believe that [i]Million Dollar Baby[/i] is the best film of the year, an unparalleled masterpiece. I want to see it again sometime very soon.
 
Clint Eastwood directs and stars in this semi-brutal, fully engrossing boxing drama. Hilary Swank is a 31-year-old white trash waitress who wants to learn to box, but Eastwood, a grizzled, moderately-grumpy trainer/""cut man"" (an expert at sealing spouting cuts so that the fight can continue) refuses to coach her on the basis of her sex. But Swank's character is persistent and soon enough she is learning to box in his gym under his gruff but patient tutelage. Morgan Freeman is an ex-boxer and friend of Eastwood's who also works at the gym, albeit in more of a janitorial capacity.
 
The boxing scenes are bare and violent (very violent for a PG-13 film), but uniformly excellent. Swank gets her nose broken midway through the film, and to watch Eastwood patch her up (""that nose is going to be gushing blood all over the front row in about twenty seconds, so you'd better finish her off quick"", he says) is simultaneously harrowing and fascinating, as the entire endeavor seems steeped in accuracy. Suffice it to say, the scene made my stomach hurt, and it was just a broken nose.
 
As Swank achieves greater and greater success in the boxing ring, Clint, always a cautious trainer (his eternal mantra is ""always protect yourself"") is hesitant to move her up in class or give her a title bout. Swank learns that she would see fights with bigger purses if she ditched Eastwood for a less protective manager, but her dedication to her trainer is obvious, and she's understandably reluctant to make the change.
 
Swank (who obviously trained heavily for the film) does well at progressing gradually from a clueless, stumbling excercise in patience to an aggressive, indimidating fighter. Her acting is stellar, but she truly achieves thespianic greatness in the final third of the film. 
 
Swank said in a recent interview that the script was so perfect, they were essentially shooting a first draft; no changes were necessary. It's true that Eastwood is working from an excellent screenplay, but I can't imagine that a film with a different director and a different cast could have approached the sheer excellence of [i]Million Dollar Baby[/i]. Morgan Freeman and Eastwood create a highly amusing chemistry that seems entirely effortless. In fact, the entire film seems effortless, from the muted lighting to his textbook ""master shot"" technique, which I mean as a compliment. 
 
After [i]Unforgiven[/i], [i]Mystic River[/i], even [i]Bridges of Madison County[/i], and now [i]Million Dollar Baby[/i], can anyone really question Eastwood's ability to direct films with a strong, sincere emotional baseline? The film takes a turn partway through (don't let anyone spoil this plot point for you), a turn that could have ruined the movie under the hands of a less experienced or emotionally stagnant director. Eastwood has built up such a strong portfolio of character investment, the audience will follow these characters anywhere, no matter what his approach as a director. But its in the final third that the movie finds and embraces its heavy-hearted soul.","",False,False,False,False,5.0,"2005-01-29","","RT","900645110"
