4.0,"[center][img]http://imagecache2.allposters.com/images/153/1033881.jpg[/img]
  
  [left]While not as good as I wanted it to be, Million Dollar Baby is definitely a great movie and worthy of the nominations it has recieved at the Oscars this year. It is a showcase for great acting, and although I didn't think Eastwood did really good as an actor here, he more than makes up for it in his directing. 
  
 I don't hesitate to call this the best boxing movie I have seen since the first Rocky. It has a lot of great dialogue, an excellent story and some really good performances from Hillary Swank and Morgan Freeman. It has a couple of very unexpected twists, and it has some great fights also. 
  
 The only thing that brought the movie down for me is the unexpected second half of the movie. I really did not see this coming when I walked into the theater, and I don't want to ruin it for anybody. It is very realistic, and very moving. It also shows that this is not really a movie about boxing, but a movie about a girl who just happens to be a pretty good boxer. I thought the second half, while very unexpected, and very different from other movies like this, really brought the whole experience down overall. Another thing I didn't like too much was the over-voiced narration of Morgan Freeman. It's not anything HE did wrong really, I just don't agree with having a narration like that. It's too easy and feels like somebody is holding our hand and guiding us through the story, which is really unnecessary, but was pulled off great in some movies (like Forrest Gump).
  
 Overall, I loved the movie. I'd love to see it again, and I'd recommend it to everybody. Don't go in expecting a movie about boxing though. Go in expecting a great drama with a few great boxing matches thrown in also.
 
 This is now my pick for Oscar winner for: 
 Best Supporting Actor for Morgan Freeman
 Best Director for Clint Eastwood
 Best Adapted Screenplay for Paul Haggis
 
  [/left]
  [/center]","",False,False,False,False,4.0,"2005-01-30","","RT","900686585"
4.0,"First off, congratulations are in order for Clint Eastwood today. Earning the prestigious Director's Guild of America award for his ""Million Dollar Baby,"" he pretty much cements his chances at an Oscar win come later in February.
 
Or does he?
 
The DGA has correctly predicted the winner of the Best Director Academy Award all but six years since its inception in 1949. But consider the fact that three of those occasions have happened in just the past ten years -- once in 1995, again in 2001 and yet again in 2003. Why does the margin seem to be narrowing? Given the fact that members of the DGA are essentially the same people who vote for the Directing award at the Oscars, the split is often perplexing. But perhaps the recent weakness of tradition also hints that today's voting body thinks on more levels than just honoring the biggest achievement.
 
The most recent case came in 2003, when Rob Marshall won the DGA for ""Chicago"" but Roman Polanski walked away with the Oscar for ""The Pianist."" Marshall's adaptation of the Bob Fosse musical was the critical and commercial favorite, and had been the obvious frontrunner through the entire awards season. But ""The Pianist"" had obscure support in other corners of the Academy: 1) because it dealt with the kind of harsh subject matter that voters eat up; and 2) because Polanski has been in exile from the United States for several years, and many doubted he could ever reach that level of acclaim again. His surprise victory with the critics and the audience, needless to say, was strong enough to weaken Marshall's support later in awards campaigning, and that is why he walked away with the big prize.
 
Now take that shell of reasoning and bring into the 2005 competition. Clint Eastwood is a favorite among voters and celebrities, and everyone loves his ""Million Dollar Baby."" But they also love ""The Aviator"" -- which is the most nominated of all films at the Oscars this year -- and its director, Martin Scorsese, is considered a master of his craft. But despite all his success, he has also never actually won an Academy Award himself (his most recent nomination was, oddly enough, in the same year that Polanski took the surprise win). Many see this as his last big opportunity at finally walking away with the gold. Furthermore, Clint Eastwood may have two points working against him this year: 1) he won the same award in 1992 for ""Unforgiven""; and 2) he is also nominated in the Best Actor category, which he has thus far been ignored in. Could all these factors play significant enough a role in how the voters fill in their ballots this time?
 
How this all turns out is anyone's guess at this point, but one thing is certain -- the DGA win does not easily benefit Eastwood this time around. Until the envelope is opened and the victor is announced, Scorsese should still be considered a viable challenger... and, indeed, one who has the opportunity to sneak through radar as easily as Polanski did two years before.","",False,False,False,False,4.0,"2005-01-30","","RT","900588518"
4.0,"[list][*][b]Cool Hand Luke:[/b] Wow. Just wow. This movie is excellent. Paul Newman's performance as a man who never planned a damn thing in his life who's just trying to pass the time is by turns humerous and and heart wrenching. (Yay! for alliteration) This is a must-see movie particularly if you want to trade iconic movie quotes with people with flair.[*][b]The Apartment:[/b] Billy Wilder rocks. The story, the acting, and the cinematography all combined to affect my heart. It was also a pleasant surprise to see a number of iconic images/scenes in the movie that I'd seen referenced in various other things. See this! It's laugh out loud funny![*][b]Sunset Blvd.:[/b] I don't know what to say here that hasn't been said before so I guess I won't try. This movie is a class-act all the way, tight acting, tight writing, the whole shebang. The way the story is told is also really cool (but I won't spoil it for you). I particularly enjoyed the detail work like having Buster Keaten playing cards with our sorry leading lady Norma Desmond.[*][b]In Good Company:[/b] This movie tried really hard and I did enjoy it in the end. I do question the authenticity (as well as the believability) of much of the movie. This is perhaps a question of casting rather more than a question of the story however. I don't want my five bucks back but I'd class this into the ""rainy day $1 rental"" category of movies.[*][b]Million Dollar Baby:[/b] Well Clint still has the right stuff, both his performance and direction are very good and they combine to make a well-made, heart wrenching movie. Upon reflection, I must confess that I was not as impressed overall as I probably should have been because it just feels like the movie was trying too hard to be Oscar material and the pacing needed a little tightening. I felt manipulated and I resented that even as I admired the fine performances and the grittiness of the story. Nonetheless, I do recommend the story of Maggie Fitzgerald and Frankie Dunn as something worth seeing.[/list]","",False,False,False,False,4.0,"2005-01-30","","RT","900742280"
4.5,"[i]Bubba Ho-Tep[/i]
 
 I laughed at loud after reading the premise for this movie. Elvis Presley and an old black man who believes he's JFK fight off a soul-sucking mummy in a retirement home. However, it's much more than that. It works well as a tribute to Elvis, to the legacy that he left behind. It's about an old man realizing that he's not useless, and finding the spirit to go on. While there are definitely hilarious moments, the quiet, meditative ones with the Elvis character are quite sombre. His character finds redemption in the end, however. The mummy aspect is probably the part of the story that works the least, in fact, but it's not really about destroying a mummy in the first place.
 
 [i]Million Dollar Baby[/i]
 
 I think that I gave this an unfair shake the first time I saw it. I was pressed for time, and with the long denouement, I wanted to get out as soon as I could. Seeing it a second time, I enjoyed it [i]much[/i] more. It's not about boxing, it's not about euthanasia. It's about two people who've been let down and abandoned by others, one to the point where's he's gotten seemably irreparably calloused (Frankie), finding mutual acceptance and love. It's about the extent to which Frankie is willing to extend that love, to make himself vulnerable to the point of breaking for someone else. It's about the spirit of determination, as seen in both Maggie and Danger. The long final act was needed for Frankie and Maggie's character arcs, reinforcing that they only had each other to turn to. The revelation in the last few seconds of the film with Eddie was a great final touch.","",False,False,False,False,4.5,"2005-01-31","","RT","900750332"
5.0,"Good Lord.  I don't even know what to say here.  This movie was beyond perfect.  I honestly can't review without gushing and being incoherent, but needless to say I was floored.  Luckily, I saw it by myself, and felt no need to hold back the tears which flowed (and I think this is an honest estimate cause I thought about it for a bit) consistently for the last 30 minutes and a few times prior to that as well.  Beautiful film.  Moving, brilliantly simplistic.  I was blown away.  Aviator WAS my choice for Best Picture.  Not anymore.  I can see why people are turning to this film and finding it easy to forget the sentimental favorite for the Oscar: Scorcese.  When it comes to Best Picture, M$B would get my vote.  When it comes to Best Director, I may just abstain.
 
 
Oh, and Tony Almeida is back on 24.
 
Can I get a ""Hell Yeah!""?  
 
Seriously, what a great day.  I rocked my Exam (88%), sat at Panera for hours chatting with a friend, saw M$B, gushed over it for hours to anyone who would listen, watched 24, and cast some people in the LOST thread.
 
Great night.","",False,False,False,False,5.0,"2005-01-31","","RT","900624510"
4.5,"Um okay I am very tired but I really love this movie so I am just going to explain why I have given it a 9 out of 10 and thus why I want this movie to win two oscars!
The movie has the best cast I could ever wish for: Morgan Freeman, Hilary Swank and Clint Eastwood. At first glance this Oscar contender has the trappings of a Driving Miss Daisy or a film that is guaranteed an Oscar based on an esoteric attribute that most run of the mill movies lack.
But the movie is simplistic and stunning. The direction is tailored down to the point of a hollywood formula that puts the director in the back seat and allows the audience to watch the movie without interruption. Usually in my crowd, here at the University studying film, that is a very very bad thing to do. At least we are taught to respect these conventions and aspire to them if we intend to become commercial. Films are afterall not artwork in that they are expected to successfully sell themselves...which means that the audience must be catered not the artist.
So Clint did a great job. I loved the small performances of each character per scene...usually you can watch an entire movie where you can say that there were a few if any good scenes. Here the actors become invisible and the characters are living...breathing and bleeding before your very eyes.
I was pained to see this movie because of all of the hype surrounding it but I was thankfully not disappointed.
The movie was reminiscent of Unforgiven in a few ways, for me that is I'm not taking a consensus, I grew to love the characters and they broke me heart. It is worth wathcing.
 
But now that Eastwood has unloaded this godawful masterpiece...I have to watch the Oscars. :mad: That is just wrong!
 
*sigh* :( 
There are worser things...:rolleyes:","",False,False,False,False,4.5,"2005-01-31","","RT","900745988"
4.0,"Ahh, it feels really good when you watch a really good movie and clear one of the films in your to-watch list to boot. Watching a good movie is like savouring good wine or eating a good meal- simply a delight! With that, i present you 2 movies that come highly reccommended from me.
  
  [center][size=4]Million Dollar Baby (2004)
  [img]http://imagecache2.allposters.com/images/153/1033881.jpg[/img]
  [/size] [left]
 This little publicised film came out of nowhere and scored a knockout, being one of the top contenders of this year's Oscars with 7 nominations. And deservedly so, as it forms the tripartite of good films with [b]The Aviator[/b] and [b]Sideways[/b] as [i]the[/i] films to watch this season.
  
    [b]Million Dollar Baby[/b] is not a film about boxing; its a film about boxers. Unlike other famous boxing flicks, like [b]Rocky[/b] or [b]Raging Bull[/b], boxing is not the main point of the film. Going on to watch it anticipating something like [b]Karate Kid[/b] meets [b]Rocky[/b] is going to be a big mistakes. 
  
 The acting here is simply brilliant. We get not one, but three stellar performances in Morgan Freeman, Clint Eastwood and Hillary Swank. Swank, for her part proves that here Oscar win [b]Boys Don't Cry[/b] was no fluke as she delivers a nuanced and powerfully touching performance as Maggie Fitzgerald. She gives an energetic and physical performance in the ring and a deeply layered performance out of the ring. While i still think that the lead actress for [b]Maria Full of Grace[/b] is better, Swank is almost certainly a hot fav to win this years Best Actress Oscar. Eastwood, being the veteran that he is, delivers his assured and memorable potrayal as Frankie Dunn, a boxing manager who has seen better days. Eastwood's gradual emotional attachment to his protege, as well as his experienced acting add greater resonance to this pupil-teacher relationship. And i would be remiss to not add Morgan Freeman, who provides the voice over in a role that reminds me of his performance in [b]The Shawshank Redemption[/b]. Freeman, being the consummate actor that he is, is always a pleasure to watch. 
  
    In the director's seat, Eastwood, hot off the heels of [b]Mystic River[/b] directs the film with a steady assured hand, as if he knew what the audience wanted. He seems to get better each time as a director, and this time its no exception. I especially like the dialogue and the director's use of shadow in much of the film. 
  
 Now it's impossible to mention the film without the TWIST that happens about 3/4 into the film. Only in my opinion, its not so much a twist as a suddent and somewhat abrupt change in mood which is one of the main factors that helps it transcend its cliched premise into greatness. Now the less you know of the revelation, the better. While i did not think that the relevation made it strictly better than it could have been, it certainly adds a layer of resonance to the film and makes you think and debate about the issues raised in the film. Now you may not always agree with what the film or people's action potrays, it is undeniable that the film is good. Already, i have babbled on too much about the TWIST. Just go in and enjoy a good movie. 
  
     Now, as much as i've praised it, it is however not the best film of this year- that honor still stays with [b]ONE NITE IN MONGKOK[/b], for now. The film is obviously manipulative, and while it wisely does not saturate with sappiness, as a lesser director would have done, it is still however not quite a flawless masterpiece. 
  
  [center][size=4]The People Vs Larry Flynt (1996)
  [img]http://imagecache2.allposters.com/images/DMS/841200.jpg[/img]
  [/size] [left]
    Milos Foreman might be better known as the director of Oscar winning [b]Amadeus[/b], or the classic [b]One Flew Over the Cuckoo's Nest[/b], but it is with his superlative [b]The People Vs Larry Flynt[/b] (TPVLF) that really impressed me. Very much a biopic of Larry Flynt's life and brush with the law, the film may seem just a superficial take on the owner of Hustler magazine. In truth, Flynt is a truly intruiging and controversial character. Now he may be derided as a purveyour of smut and a lowlife pornographer, but he is also the bastion for free speech and the lightning rod for the moral conservatives. 
  
 Beyond the superficial, TPVLF is also a sharp social commentary about the compromise between the First Admendment and the vanguard of morality. In addition, it also serves a a poignant potrayal of one man's life, his fall into debauchery and the many low points of his life.
  
 In this, Woody Harrelson is uncanny in his potrayal of Flynt. He is literally the character of Flynt and brings out the charcater through his many phases of life, from his brief religious conversion, to his paralysis. But it is Courtney Love better known for her singing than acting, that provides the breakthrough performance. In a daring and provocative performance as Flynt's wife, she puts in a virtuoso performance. Also notable is Edward Norton as Flynt's attorney. While his screen time is short, he does deliver one of the best courtroom monologues i've seen in a long time, and is likewise remarkable. 
  
 The weakness of the film is evidently how given Flynt's life, which is colorful to say the least, many of the important milestones of his life is just touch and go for the director. Its like the movie is moving at too fast a pace and thus, doesn't really delve deep inside the psyche of the man. 
  
 Yet, that is merely a quibble for this wellcrafted film. The debate, especially in the end vs Falwell is brilliant in its juxtaposition between the concern of the religious conservative and the freedom of expression. While one may think that Hustler is junk, one should not impose one's opinions on others, and this is certainly a message i can live with. While evidently slightly sympathetic to Flynt's character, the movie is not afraid to show Flynt for what he is, warts and all. Flynt certiainly is a sonofabitch at times and his crazy antics and craving for media attention show his misogynic and irrational side. But he is one helluva determined man and is not afraid to stand up for what he believes and be unpolitically correct. He reflects the working class ideals and his rags to riches story is not one to be sniffed at. When he so aptly puts it:
 
  ""[color=DarkRed][i]If the First Admendment will protect a scumbag like me, it will protect all of you[/i]"" 
 [/color]
 Its hard to argue against this kind of logic. 
  [/left]
  [/center]
  [/left]
  [/center]","",False,False,False,False,4.0,"2005-01-31","","RT","900665496"
