Did you get any of that? I didn't and I wrote it.","",False,False,False,False,4.5,"2005-02-01","","RT","900645581"
3.0,"This marks my return to the movie theatre after a month of film-free gallivanting about. ""Million Dollar Baby"" opened to rave reviews just before I left town and has since been nominated for a ""Best Picture"" Oscar, so it seemed like an obvious first choice. Perhaps I would've liked it more if the cumulative effect of several weeks of Larium-induced sleeplessness hadn't turned me into such a curmudgeonly zombie. Or maybe even in my normally chipper state of bee-positiveness I'd still find this film to be overly long, plodding and manipulative. 
 
""Million Dollar Baby"" is a essentially just a boxing movie, with the big new millennium twist being that the boxer is a girl. Maggie Fitzgerald (Hilary Swank) shows up one day at the sweaty, spitty, L.A. gym owned by grizzled old manager Frankie Dunn (Clint Eastwood). A wise old ex-boxer pal of Frankie's named Scrap (Morgan Freeman) works there too, as a janitor. Scrap is blind in one eye as a result of over-pummeling in what turned out to be his last fight. Freeman wears a blue contact lens in the ""blind"" eye, for an effect that makes him look kind of like a Siberian Husky.
 
Frankie is all stereotypically Irish-American Catholic. He studies Gaelic in his spare time. He goes to church every morning and badgers the priest with pressing questions of faith, such as whether the concept of three gods rolled into one is analogous to Snap, Crackle, and Pop living in one Rice Krispies box. Frankly, I find this analogy quite weak, as simply sharing a cereal box is not at all the same as embodying one being. For me, the Certs, ""two...two...two mints in one"" concept always seemed the quintessential illustration of the Catholic god paradigm, except that it leaves out the Holy Spirit (formerly known as the Holy Ghost). But let's be honest...compared to the other two guys, the old Holy Spirit has always been an also-ran. Perhaps he lives in that tiny drop of Retsyn. 
 
But I digress.
 
So Maggie shows up from Tennessee or West Virginia or some backwoodsy state where people have bad accents and she wants to be a boxer and she wants Frankie to train her. The reason Maggie gives for the former is that boxing is the only thing that's ever made her feel good, which makes me wonder what else she's been doing all her life. You see, I took boxing class for about six months, and though practicing footwork and throwing jabs at focus pads was kind of fun, getting hit in the face was decidedly un-fun and certainly didn't make me feel good.
 
In any event, Frankie doesn't want to train Maggie because she's a girl, but she still keeps after him every day, always addressing him as ""Boss"", which both he and I find annoying. She works out hard on her own, sweating in that unnatural pattern favored by movie producers armed with big cans of spray-on sweat. Eventually, old Scrap takes pity on her and gives her a few pointers (such as the always useful ""move your feet"") and lends her a speed bag.
 
I found this early set-up portion of the movie excruciatingly painful to watch and was dreading having to slog through over two hours of this tripe. But somewhere along the line, after Frankie has finally agreed to train Maggie and somewhat of a relationship forms between them, I got sucked into caring a little bit about the characters and actually enjoyed the movie for a while. As Maggie starts winning fight after fight, I found myself rooting for a plot twist to save this film from turning out to be yet another cliched sports movie, a hillbilly chick remake of ""Rocky"".
 
And I got my wish, sort of. I don't want to give anything away plot-wise, so all I'll say is that suddenly the film morphed into a completely different genre and then limped along for the last half-hour or so in an attempt to wring every last bit of emotion out of its material. As I'm immune to non-canine movie pathos, this attempt was completely wasted on me and I found the whole last act to be annoyingly draggy.
 
So in summary, I hated the beginning of this film, enjoyed the middle, and felt manipulated by the end. Hilary Swank does an excellent yokel impersonation and bulked up to scary proportions for added credibility as a boxer. Eastwood and Freeman are also good, though no special transformation was needed for them to credibly depict grizzled old men. 
 
This is the sort of film I'd recommend to my father, or anyone's father really. It's an inoffensive sports movie starring two guys around his age. It contains no sex or drugs, no blaring rock and roll soundtrack, and none of those pesky subtitles. It's just good, wholesome, PG-13 entertainment, if by ""entertainment"" you mean ""harmless diversion"". As big-budget, mainstream sports films go, it doesn't completely suck.","",False,False,False,False,3.0,"2005-02-01","","RT","900683813"
