4.0,"I always bitch and moan to my friends that Hollywood fails to create any good movies anymore.  Remember the Indiana Jones series?  The Dark Crystal?  Star Wars, etc?  Legend?  Think early Spielberg.  He was unstoppable.  Well, this movie shut me up, at least for awhile.  Hilary Swank was unbelievable.  The story was great, the casting was great.  The rhythm of the movie was steady.  I'll even forgive Eastwood the predictable earth-shattering injury sustained by the main character.  The only thing I might have changed was to fill-out the subplot involving Danger and Morgan Freeman's character.  Eastwood's use of the irritated priest delicately introduced the religious undertones to the film without bashing you in the head with them.  I even saw a glimpse of acting ability I never knew Eastwood had during the scene in the church right before he makes his big decision.  Morgan Freeman was, once again, type-cast, but who cares, he's a delight to watch.  I'd like my very own Morgan Freeman, in fact.  There are days where I need a good laugh and a tidbit of wisdom from an older man.  Watch this movie.","",False,False,False,False,4.0,"2005-02-01","","RT","900764448"
4.5,"Clint Eastwood delivers a flawless gem this time out. Unlike some of his recent past efforts characterized by overblown acting (sorry, I thought Sean Penn and Tim Robbins were idiotic in Mystic River), this ensemble of Eastwood, Hilary Swank and Morgan Freeman are nothing short of brilliant.  Oscars would be well deserved by all of them.  As far as the direction, Eastwood brings in a style reminiscent of Coppola with courageous cinematography that uses shadows and llight to create moods and to develop character.
