This film inspired an argument between my wife and I over it's merits and over the merits of ""serious"" films in general. That fact, and the ensuing discussion, are enough to make the film worthy of a decent ranking, but it provided more than enough fodder to make it worth a high billing in my book. First of all, our argument was over the meaning such a depressing and, in many ways, cynical, film could have. I held that it portrayed the darkness of the sport in question, the battle of the protagonist, the fragility of human life, and the overarching importance of achieving one's life dream. My wife did not see as much possible symbolism or metaphors in the work, but rather a well-told, moving story and little more. Our argument essentially broke down over simple semantics, but the point was important just the same. For me, a relative cinema rookie, I attempt to guard against bringing outside opinions to a film (hence the research indications) as well as placing too much credence in the work itself. In other words, I tend to give directors too much credit from time to time and place meanings and complexities in a film where only flaws and missteps truly exist. However, I don't think that is the case here. An all-star cast takes a simple story, one that has been done many times before in various ways and breathes life and meaning into it. The film captures not only the essence of the sport of boxing, but of sport's elevated stature in our culture. Swank perfectly portrays poverty being given a chance through mastery of a brutal and, in many ways, pointless art, but even more than that she allows the film to tackle the issue of the subjectivity of success. Is life based on reaching our ultimate goals and anything thereafter is unnecessary? Was that goal her achievement of greatness in the ring or her completion of the quest to find love and acceptance in other human being? This film seamlessly weaves difficult life questions into a captivating sports drama, and the result really moved me.","",False,False,False,False,4.5,"2005-02-01","","RT","900652595"
5.0,"I had read some brief reviews of Clint Eastwood's new film, ""Million Dollar Baby."" Just enough to know generally what it was about and that the performances were supposed to be really good. Of course, I knew I would go see the movie from the start because I'll watch anything Eastwood is involved with. He's one of the few filmmakers out there these days with something to say.
 
I sat down yesterday and watched this movie, and was blown away all over again. I thought ""Mystic River"" was exemplary, but this movie blows it away in almost all respects. I have to admit I haven't seen ""The Aviator"" yet, but if it's half as good as ""Million Dollar Baby,"" I'd be surprised. This flick should sweep the Oscars based on what I've seen so far from 2004's movies.","",False,False,False,False,5.0,"2005-02-02","","RT","900667958"
5.0,"[img]http://imagecache2.allposters.com/images/153/1033881.jpg[/img]
[font=Franklin Gothic Medium][size=3]****[/size][/font]
[font=Franklin Gothic Medium][size=3]Eastwood returns one year removed from the lauded [i]Mystic River[/i], only to create a film far better than his last entry. [/size][/font]
 
[font=Franklin Gothic Medium][size=3]With all the Oscar buzz that this film is getting, it's hard not to have heard what this film is about. Boxing cutman and trainer Frankie (Eastwood) and one-eyed retired boxer Scrap (Freeman) run a disheveled boxing gym. When a middle aged waitress named Maggie (Swank) enters their gym, Frankie tells her that he doesn't train girls. However, due to her determination, Frankie breaks down and agrees to train her. [/size][/font]
 
[font=Franklin Gothic Medium][size=3]Without a doubt, [i]Million Dollar Baby[/i] is easily as good as Eastwood's best work, if not the best he's ever done. The direction is genius, with subtle tones and colors as well as dimly lit environments. Eastwood achieves a simplicity that aids the credibility of the story as well as deconstructs and reinvents cliches that invaribly arise within the boxing genre. The best achievement of this film is that it conveys a real and intensly personal tone. [/size][/font]
 
[font=Franklin Gothic Medium][size=3]Expert use of voice over by Freeman works well with the material. Eastwood, Freeman, and Swank are all at the top of their acting games, and all deserve any awards they receive for their roles. The fight scenes are highly believable and realistic. Eastwood's easy going style indelibly marks the film, and in the hands of any other director, the movie would have most likely been boring and cliched. The pacing is briliantly realized on screen and keeps the audience involved with the characters and the story. The script by Paul Haggis is excellent and well delivered by the thespians. The music, composed by Eastwood, is perfectly blended with the visuals. Overall, my personal favorite from last year. Go see it if you can.[/size][/font]","",False,False,False,False,5.0,"2005-02-02","","RT","900603367"
4.0,"[size=3][color=#000000][font=Times New Roman]Million Dollar Baby (2004)[/font][/color][/size]

[font=Times New Roman][size=3][color=#000000] [/color][/size][/font]

[size=3][color=#000000][font=Times New Roman]Boxing Drama[/font][/color][/size]

[size=3][color=#000000][font=Times New Roman]Released 12/15/04[/font][/color][/size]

[size=3][color=#000000][font=Times New Roman]Reviewed 2/1/05[/font][/color][/size]

[size=3][color=#000000][font=Times New Roman]Starring Clint Eastwood, Hilary Swank, Morgan Freeman, Jay Baruchel, Mike Colter, Brian F. OByrne, Anthony Mackey, Lucia Rijker, Margo Martindale, Riki Lindhome, Micheal Pena, Bruce MacVittie, Benito Martinez[/font][/color][/size]

[size=3][color=#000000][font=Times New Roman]Based on Rope Burns by F.X. Toole[/font][/color][/size]

[size=3][color=#000000][font=Times New Roman]Screenplay written by Paul Haggis[/font][/color][/size]

[size=3][color=#000000][font=Times New Roman]Directed by Clint Eastwood[/font][/color][/size]

[size=3][color=#000000][font=Times New Roman]Rated PG-13/PG for Violence, Some Disturbing Images, Thematic Material and Language[/font][/color][/size]

[size=3][color=#000000][font=Times New Roman]Nominated for a Best Picture Academy Award[/font][/color][/size]

[font=Times New Roman][size=3][color=#000000] [/color][/size][/font]

[size=3][color=#000000][font=Times New Roman]I realized after the Academy Awards were announced that I hadnt seen a single one of the Best Picture noms. Granted, Ray and The Aviator were the only ones that ever played at my theatre and I almost say both of them, but never did. When Million Dollar Baby came out in a late release here, I said Why the hell not. I didnt go all January, this and Hide & Seek on the weekend would help make up for it. This was probably the one I would have liked to see the most afterall, besides maybe Sideways. Why? It has the same director as my personal choice for last year, Mystic River. Not to mention it is on a million critics lists. So is this really the contender for the Oscar, or is it just Rocky with Swank instead of Stallone?[/font][/color][/size]

[font=Times New Roman][size=3][color=#000000] [/color][/size][/font]

[size=3][color=#000000][font=Times New Roman]Note that there are some mild spoilers in the Plot and Entertainment sections, but nothing I would fret about.[/font][/color][/size]

[font=Times New Roman][size=3][color=#000000] [/color][/size][/font]

[size=3][color=#000000][font=Times New Roman]Plot: [b]22/25[/b]: Frankie Dunn (Eastwood) is a boxing trainer who has taught many boxers how to get to the top, only to be thrown aside once they get a shot at the title. He does his work at the gym belonging to his old friend, half-blind ex-boxer Eddie Scrap-Iron Dupris (Freeman). When trashy Maggie Fitzgerald (Swank) asks for him to train her, he initially says no because he doesnt train girls. When she starts training at Eddies gym constantly, he eventually caves and says hell train her, but wont manage her. Skip along a little, Frankie, of course, becomes her trainer/manager. She has become an excellent fighter, knocking out most competitors in the first round. But when she gets her shot at the title, something happens that shifts the entire mood of the movie.[/font][/color][/size]

[font=Times New Roman][size=3][color=#000000] [/color][/size][/font]

[size=3][color=#000000][font=Times New Roman]Essentially, this is two different movies in one. The first half is the very entertaining and surprisingly funny training part, while the second half, which I will not reveal at all, is the half that is nominated for the Academy Award. There are some problems, though. The plot seems a little stretched, having many worthless scenes that dont accomplish anything but make the film 2 hours and 17 minutes. There is also a big loose end involving Frankies daughter that is never even close to tied up. In all, the plot is good. If you dont like one half, youll probably like the other.[/font][/color][/size]

[font=Times New Roman][size=3][color=#000000] [/color][/size][/font]

[size=3][color=#000000][font=Times New Roman]Boxing Action: [b]21/25[/b]: While there arent any fights that quite match Rocky vs. Apollo Creed in the first Rocky, we do get treated to many more than Rocky ever gave us. Granted, most are very short, as Maggie has, like I said before, a tendency towards finishing in one round. All the fights are entertaining, often funny, so if you want a boxing movie, while this isnt really about the boxing, I still recommend it for you.[/font][/color][/size]

[font=Times New Roman][size=3][color=#000000] [/color][/size][/font]

[size=3][color=#000000][font=Times New Roman]Acting: [b]25/25[/b]: The acting for this has been getting a lot of accolades, so I had high expectations, especially since the three leads all had Oscar noms. Eastwood (Dirty Harry) and Freeman (The Shawshank Redemption) are both very good in their roles, though, to be honest, it wasnt too much of stretch for them. The roles they played were similar to others theyve had. Swank (Boys Dont Cry), on the other hand, is extremely good here, and out of the noms for Best Actress, I am rooting for her to take the gold for a second time. The rest of the cast is well suited, and as I have no complaints, Ill give it a perfect score here.[/font][/color][/size]

[font=Times New Roman][size=3][color=#000000] [/color][/size][/font]

[size=3][color=#000000][font=Times New Roman]Entertainment: [b]20/25[/b]: As I stated before, this movie changes halfway. The first half is very entertaining to just about anyone, for the most part, but the second half drags a little bit, with some scenes that seem unnecessary, as I stated in the Plot section. The whole boxing theme may throw off some more mainstream moviegoers and actually divert more serious moviegoers from seeing it.[/font][/color][/size]

[font=Times New Roman][size=3][color=#000000] [/color][/size][/font]

[size=3][color=#000000][font=Times New Roman]Total: [b]88%[/b]: Million Dollar Baby is a great movie. Is it Oscar worthy? Maybe. Is it better than Rocky? Hard to say. Is it an easy recommendation? Yes. This a movie I can recommend to anyone who likes boxing, anyone who likes dramas, and almost everyone else too. It carries on here and there, running about 20 minutes longer than it should have and carries a couple loose ends, but it ultimately leaves you thinking about it the day after.[/font][/color][/size]
[font=Times New Roman][size=3][color=#000000][/color][/size][/font] 
[font=Times New Roman][size=3][color=#000000][url=""http://www.jimd73.greatnow.com/milliondollarbaby.html""]http://www.jimd73.greatnow.com/milliondollarbaby.html[/url] [/color][/size][/font]","",False,False,False,False,4.0,"2005-02-02","","RT","900733528"
4.5,"Great film. Only problem is some very unrealistic boxing and referee scenes.","",False,False,False,False,4.5,"2005-02-02","","RT","900763958"
4.0,"[color=darkgreen]TO VIEW THE ""SPOILER"" INFORMATION BELOW, USE YOUR MOUSE CURSOR TO HIGHLIGHT THE ""BLANK"" SECTIONS OF TEXT.
 
Yes, [i]Million Dollar Baby[/i] is a very good movie, but don't be fooled by the previews and early reviews. I read a review that said MDB is ""not a boxing movie"". No? Well, there are several bloody, violent boxing sequences that convinced me it IS a boxing movie. If you HATE boxing violence, don't be fooled by reviews that say this is not a movie about boxing. In addition, the previews do not give you a full picture of what this picture is about. [i]Rocky[/i] it ain't!
 
SPOILER TEXT:[color=white] T[color=white]he first half of MDB LOOKS and FEELS like [i]Rocky[/i], with a lot more raw reality and interest than Stallone's fantasy. But beware: Mr. Eastwood is setting you up with a few soft jabs so he can level you with a devastating uppercut to the jaw in the final reels! You will NOT leave the theater humming any triumphant anthems like ""Eye of the Tiger"". You will, rather, walk out shaken, quiet and thoughtful. It is not easy to watch, and I have to question the marketing campaign for the movie. If they were honest about how the story ends, of course, it would put a severe crimp in the box office totals. So, they market it as a ""feel good"" movie. If you ""feel good"" at the end of MDB, get yourself a therapist, quick! This marketing approach is really unfair to moviegoers, and most reviewers have concealed the spoiler information that would warn audiences.
 
Yep, it is great movie-making. Yep, the acting is spectacular. But the story is stunningly sad, shockingly violent. You can admire the artist who creates an intentionally ugly painting, but that doesn't mean you have to love the result. So, 8 for overall quality, more like 3 for the nature of the story that Eastwood tells.[/color] [/color][color=white]
[/color]
Precise Knight Rating 8.0
 
(Viewed at Spectrum 8 Cinema)
[/color]","",False,False,False,False,4.0,"2005-02-02","","RT","900670833"
4.5,"Not much to say about this movie that isn't already known (or without revealing too much)-- Hilary Swank, Morgan Freeman, and Clint Eastwood all do superb. In eighteen words or less, if you like intense drama and talented acting, make every effort to see this film -- it won't disappoint.","",False,False,False,False,4.5,"2005-02-02","","RT","900762708"
3.5,"[font='Comic Sans MS'][color=white]A quick update as I try to see all of the films nominated for Best Picture this year.  I've seen two.  Whoopee.  [/color][/font]

[font='Comic Sans MS'][color=white] [/color][/font]

[color=white][i][font='Comic Sans MS']Million Dollar Baby.[/font][/i][font='Comic Sans MS']  I had high expectations, but I really don't know why.  Critics have said it was terrific to the max and was the best of the film of the year and such, but I knew that a story about boxing would hardly hold my interest.  And it barely did.  There were many good points to this film, but I ultimately found that the entire thing was too drawn out and long to have a place in a Best of 2004 list.[/font][/color]

[font='Comic Sans MS'][color=white] [/color][/font]

[font='Comic Sans MS'][color=white]I have absolutely no idea why Clint Eastwood is considered a good actor, or why he has been nominated for Best Actor.  I thought he was terrible.  Sure talking in one tone is 'Clint's way' but does that make it good acting. No it does not.  I think he is a very good director, judging by the fantastic Mystic River, but he dragged this film down with his ""I hate the world and have a gruff voice"" attitude.  Not there was much to drag down, as the film is incredibly depressing and really doesn't give a clear moral or message.[/color][/font]

[font='Comic Sans MS'][color=white] [/color][/font]

[font='Comic Sans MS'][color=white]Hilary Swank and Morgan Freeman, do commendable jobs in their roles, and I must admit that the chemistry between the three of the main characters adds a little more of a human feel to the film, enough to make it serviceable. The writing was fairly well done, with only a few winded rants that I've become all too used to when watching films.  Only these ones seemed irrelevant and added nothing to the story, of which there is very little to begin with.[/color][/font]

[font='Comic Sans MS'][color=white] [/color][/font]

[font='Comic Sans MS'][color=white]The movie is filmed in kind of a grainy look, making it hard on the eyes most of the time.  Several elements, such as characters standing half in the light and half in the dark are used many times, but were pulled off rather well thanks to the acting ability of Morgan Freeman.  Clint just...I won't talk about Clint.  The sound in the film was well done, the fight scenes felt sickeningly real as every blow reverberated through the theatre.  The music, however was an interesting note.  I listened and noticed that the music was almost identical in parts to the score of Mystic River.  I thought that Clint must have hired the same guy to do both, but it turned out that he himself does the music in both films.  So I guess I can add another tick to the list of things Mystic River did much better.[/color][/font]

[font='Comic Sans MS'][color=white] [/color][/font]

[font='Comic Sans MS'][color=white]I would like to say that I had a different experience with [i]Million Dollar Baby[/i] than I was expecting but honestly, I cannot.  The critical praise made me want to see it, as well as the nominations for various awards but somewhere deep down, I knew it wasn't going to be the movie for me.  It's not really a sports movie even. It's like a specialty film that applies to one in a million people.  It just seems that all those people are critics.  I still held hope while watching the film, having heard from a local newspaper article that there was a twist that had to be seen to be believed.  I waited and waited for said twist and wasn't surprised in the least.  But my overall biggest complaint about [i]Million Dollar Baby[/i] was that it did nothing to me.  All of my favorite films have given me something.  Whether it be advice or a life lesson or just a real fun time, I watched those films and something struck me.  Not so with this one.  I felt no sympathy for any of these characters nor could I empathize with the situations.  The film had some good performances and that's what saves it, in my books, from being just your average film.[/color][/font]

[color=white][/color]
[color=white][/color]","",False,False,False,False,3.5,"2005-02-02","","RT","900749041"
4.0,"[i][size=3][font=Times New Roman]THREE TERRIFIC ACTORS THAT TOTALLY DRAW YOU IN. FROM THE MAGICAL NARRATION THAT COMES FROM MORGAN FREEMAN, JUST AS IN ""THE SHAWSHANK REMDEMPTION'' TO THE DRIVEN SPIRIT OF HILARY SWANK AND THE NO B.S. APPROACH CLINT EASTWOOD POSSESSES, YOU KNOW THAT THERE IS A GRIPPING STORY TO TELL. A GREAT JOB CLINT EASTWOOD DOES WITH AND ALL THE HATS HE WORE IN GETTING THIS MOVIE OUT THERE. HILARY SWANK WAS SO DEDICATED IN HER TRAINING TO MAKE HER CHARACTER AS BELIEVABLE AS IF SHE WERE THE REAL THING.  LAST BUT NOT LEAST,  MORGAN FREEMAN'S DELIVERY IS AS COMFORTABLE AS YOUR FAVORITE PAIR OF OLD SLIPPERS.  A MILLION KUDOS TO A SUPERB MOVIE EXPERIENCE. MAKE SURE YOU HAVE TISSUE FOR THE EMOTIONS THIS STORY BRINGS TO YOU[/font].[/size][/i]","bda2a5a2-05b8-4ac5-9bd3-bbce559fe38f",False,False,False,False,4.0,"2005-02-03","","RT","900762968"
4.5,"Million Dollar Baby is not as good as everyone's making it out to be. It's good, but not as good as Mystic River. 
 
My beef with it is how it handles white poor people. 
 
Early on in the film, Morgan Freeman, in his voice-over narration makes the following statement about the one thing Hilary Swank's character ""knows"" about herself.
 
""There was only one thing she did know; she was trash.""
 
On top of that, Swank's character has a family composed of welfare-cheating, southern-accented, ungrateful individuals. One more movie to add to the heap of films that reinforce these stereotypes. Oh, almost forgot, the only village idiot in the movie is of course, a Texan.
 
The relationship between Eastwood's character and Freeman's is really interesting and part of what makes the film as good as it is.
 
The mise-en-scene has a generally washed out look and doesn't make the film one that you really need to see on the big screen.
 
The characters are what make the movie and the acting. Freeman, Eastwood, and Swank are all superb. I found Freeman's character to be particularly interesting. We may even learn the most about him. It was nice to see Eastwood play a more vulnerable, complex character than he's ever taken on.","",False,False,False,False,4.5,"2005-02-03","","RT","900731593"
4.5,"[u][i]Million Dollar Baby[/i] (Clint Eastwood, 2004)[/u]
 Situated on a cinematic fault line somewhere between drama and sports fairy tale, gritty realism and teary melodrama, [b]Million Dollar Baby[/b] (directed by, scored by, and starring Clint Eastwood) is kind of miraculous for achieving a transcendent emotional power with conventional elements. The multitalented film craftsman, who has solidified his position as a Renaissance man with this film and last year's stunning [i]Mystic River[/i], appears here as the bottled-up, grizzled Frankie Dunn, the owner of a run-down L.A. gym, a cautious, protective fight trainer, and a former ""cut man"" (the man who attempts to patch up the bleeding faces of fighters ringside with cotton swabs and peroxide before sending them off for more beatings). When a scrappy but inexperienced ""trailer trash"" waitress named Maggie (Hilary Swank, in a performance of fierce determination and softness) comes to Frankie for training, he gruffly brushes her off, partly because of his prejudices (his own daughter no longer speaks to him), his weariness, and because of the tragedies that have befallen previous fighters under his watch... such as Eddie ""Scrap Iron"" Dupris (Morgan Freeman, who also provides elegant, melodious narration), a former fighter who lost an eye in the ring, and is now Frankie's loyal friend and right-hand man around the gym. But Eddie seems to believe in Maggie, and through their pestering of Frankie and his intuition about (maybe even grudging affection for) her, he begins to train her, and she rises to fame on the wings of sheer heart, strength of will, and determination. 
  
 This section of the film is an expertly crafted example of a traditional ""rise of the underdog"" boxing story, filled with potentially too-ordinary characters that have been transformed into living, breathing humans by the talented actors Eastwood has assembled. The fight scenes have been staged with simple but violent and rousing skill. The various rough-and-tumble people and locations filling the film show Eastwood's talent at creating a palpable sense of place, environment, and atmosphere with seemingly little effort. And the scenes of friendly sparring between Frankie and Eddie, or of tenderness between Frankie and Maggie, just exude an old-school charm. 
  
 But then the movie turns dark, and the potential for overheated melodrama grows as Maggie is injured by a mishap in the ring, and all the characters are faced with extremely tough choices. What keeps all the churning emotions at bay are Eastwood's carefulness as a director, the fine performances, and the smart, tough ruminations Eastwood makes here about love, faith, family, friendship, our choices in crisis, and sometimes confounding human behaviour. Even more incredibly, he takes on religion with the intelligence and seriousness it deserves by featuring a smart, realistic, good-hearted priest in the picture and by having Frankie's faith and beliefs factor heavily into his actions. 
  
 There are some manipulations here: certainly the arrival of Maggie's lazy, insensitive, money-grubbing family into the picture is meant to rile up both Frankie and the audience, and it does indeed pack a punch. But Eastwood understates as much as he overstates: He is reserved in his performance, his narrative structure is simple, unflashy, and beautifully old-fashioned, the talks with the priest and Eddie seem realistic and not overdone. (The final image of a shadowy Eastwood eating lemon meringue pie in an old-timey diner may be the most emotional of the year.) What is expected here are the familiar characters, the environment, and plotline (most of it, anyway); what's not expected are the simple but elegant craftsmanship, the fine performances that breathe such life into the characters, the smart, complex statements made about a variety of tough topics, and the ability to tackle organized religion with seriousness and respect and without coming off as false. With all these unexpected but striking elements leavening any sappiness in the movie, Eastwood's [i]Million Dollar Baby[/i] becomes an acute, powerful experience; it's an intelligent, superbly controlled emotional knockout. [b]A[/b]","",False,False,False,False,4.5,"2005-02-04","","RT","900691990"
4.5,"[font=Century Gothic][color=yellow]If Morgan Freeman doesn't win Best Supporting Actor at the Oscars, I think I may cry. [/color][/font]
[color=yellow] [/color]
[color=yellow]I am fresh from seeing [i]Million Dollar Baby [/i]at the cinemas, and I must say that as much as I loved [i]Finding Neverland[/i], I will be pulling for this movie come February 27th. The funny thing is, I am not a huge Clint Eastwood fan. I liked [i]Mystic River [/i]for the preformances, [i]Midnight in the Garden of Good and Evil [/i]was a fine film--but his opus [i]Unforgiven [/i]and all of the spagetti westerns he did were lost on me. My dad and Grandpa loved westerns, Clint Eastwood and John Wayne so much--I think I was over-saturated at an early age.[/color]
[color=yellow] [/color]
[color=yellow]However, I won't include my childhood favorites [i]Every Which Way But Loose, Any Which Way You Can [/i]and [i]Paint Your Wagon [/i]because the previous two have him starring opposite an orangutan and the latter is a musical.[/color]
[color=yellow] [/color]
[color=yellow]The relationships in [i]Million Dollar Baby [/i]are not overacted and are very palpable and believable. The relationship between the emotionally hardened Frankie (Clint Eastwood) and Maggie (Hilary Swank) builds over the course of the movie and you feel the connection that they make--nothing is implied or hinted at--it is there, growing in front of the audience. [/color]
[color=yellow] [/color]
[color=yellow]The relationship between Frankie and Scrap (Morgan Freeman) is strong and honest. They have a wonderful on-screen rapport and you can tell that neither of them would take any shit from the other.[/color]
[color=yellow] [/color]
[color=yellow]The thing I liked about Maggie is that she was tough inside and out. She hounded Frankie into training her, despite what he said about training girl boxers and how he told her that she was too old at 31 to throw a serious punch. She may had been poor white trash from ""somewhere in between nowhere and goodbye""--but she didn't let anyone get the better of her. She was as sharp as a tack. Like Scrap says, ""She always liked to knock them out in the first round.""[/color]
[color=yellow] [/color]
[color=yellow]As much as I would love to see Kate Winslet win Best Actress for [i]Eternal Sunshine [/i](she deserves an Oscar for almost everything she has done), I really loved and respected Hilary Swank's character in this film. She worked her ass off and it shows. Also, as much as I love Clive Owen and thought he did an awesome job as a real asshole in [i]Closer[/i]; I am really, really rooting for Morgan Freeman.[/color]
[color=yellow] [/color]
[color=yellow]Let's face it. The man is long overdue for Oscar. He should've won for [i]Driving Miss Daisy [/i]and [i]The Shawshank Redemption! [/i]I personally have loved him since I watched [i]The Electric Company [/i]as Easy Reader and in [i]Robin Hood: Prince of Thieves[/i]. However, he should not win because he is the sentimental favorite--he should win because he kicked ass in this movie. [/color]
[color=yellow] [/color]
[color=yellow]Scrap plays narrator and acts as a confidant for both Frankie and Maggie. He takes a scrawny, challenged wannabe named Danger under his wing. The one scene alone where Scrap gets to fight his 110th battle is worth the vote to get the little, bald, gold guy.[/color]
[color=yellow] [/color]
[color=yellow]Run, don't walk to see this movie. And bring plenty of tissues--myself and most others in the theater cried for 45 minutes.[/color]
[color=yellow] [/color]
[color=yellow]Hilary Swank for Best Actress![/color]
[color=yellow]Morgan Freeman for Best Supporting Actor![/color]
[color=yellow]Clint Eastwood for Best Director![/color]
[color=yellow] [/color]
[color=yellow]I am refraining from making my bets on Best Picture--I still have one more of the five nominated movies to see. Hopefully, I will see [i]Sideways [/i]soon. Then I'll let you know. [/color]
[color=yellow] [/color]
[color=yellow]Laters![/color]
[color=yellow]Courtney[/color]","",False,False,False,False,4.5,"2005-02-04","","RT","900725718"
5.0,"I think I just found this year's oscar winner for best picture. Having seen all five contenders I have no doubt that [u]Million Dollar Baby[/u] will win. If it does lose I would not be devastated as all the films nominated this year deserve the title.
 
This movie was by far a cinematic masterpiece. Clint Eastwood (although I am not a big fan of his) really brought this film together both onscreen and off. Every aspect was pure brilliance, and missing a moment would be a crime against cinema.
 
The story is about finding out who you are inside. We have a boxing trainer -- who is estrange from his family -- going against his misguided code of ethics to train an eager girl wanting to learn the sport. As the story progresses we learn more about our characters and their background. Eventually, the inevitable downfall happens, and we discover that they are going to have to make the ultimate choice.
 
Hilary Swank, gives one of the best performances of her career. She made me believe her character was real, and I wanted to be there to comfort her when her character has her accident.
 
Morgan Freeman, although a superb actor, just didn't give the omf I usually expect from him. Although he is the omnicient narrator of the movie, I felt that his performance was the only real downturn to the movie.
 
This film will be a tremendous success despite the outcome of the academy awards. I personally believe that this will become required viewing for any film buff.","",False,False,False,False,5.0,"2005-02-04","","RT","900716260"
4.5,"[center][img]http://www.themoviebox.net/movies/2004/STUVWXYZ/Woodsman,The/images/main-page.jpg[/img]
   [left][font=Book Antiqua]I always feared this one would turn out to be a movie that only attempts to shock the viewer. Luckily it's not as the entire thing is rather restrained. 
 Director Nicole Kassel lets the camera flow naturally but never forgets it's about the character. It's all skilfully done, not over the top luckily. 
 There's also the supporting cast: Sedgwick, Mos Def and Eve. All three are really good, I especially loved Mos Def as the cop who mistrusts Bacon.
   
   It really all comes down to Bacon. Here he plays a pedophile who gets released after 12 years. 
 It's a hard role, one that could've easily have been screwed up. Not by Bacon though, who once again proves he's one terribly underrated actor. This is a subtle performance and he handles it perfectly. At times he comes across as a frightened boy while at other times as a creep. Just watch that scene in the park, perhaps one of the best of the year.
   
 The subject matter was a hard one to handle but The Woodsman never glorifies its main character. It just treats him like a human being and tells us his story. It also asks us if society really gives sex offenders a second chance. Gripping stuff. Better than last year's Mystic River.
   [/font] [center][font=Book Antiqua][b]8.8/10
   --------------------
   [img]http://www.affichescinema.com/insc_d/dreamers.jpg[/img]
   [/b][/font] [left][font=Book Antiqua]Bertolluci obviously likes cinema, as he frequently pays homage to several scenes from more artsy fare such as Bande à Part. It all starts of rather interesting with an American getting caught up in the relationship between French twins of the opposite sex. 
 Their relationship starts off rather interesting, but after a while I couldn't shake the feeling that this one felt like a travel brochure with movies instead of countries. And really, wouldn't everyone rather be in the country than read about it?
   [/font] [center][font=Arial][b][font=Book Antiqua]6.9/10[/font]
   [/b][/font][font=Book Antiqua][b]--------------------
   [img]http://us.movies1.yimg.com/movies.yahoo.com/images/hv/photo/movie_pix/warner_brothers/million_dollar_baby/_group_photos/clint_eastwood14.jpg[/img]
   [/b][/font] [left][font=Book Antiqua]It's hard to come up with a fresh angle for a sports movie these days. Imagine my surprise when Million Dollar Baby succeeded.
 I really don't want to give too much of the plot away, so let me just say that this is a movie with scenes ranging from exhilarating to poignant. The relationship between Eastwood and Swank grows more interesting as the movie continues.
   Great acting all around (Freeman in a good movie again!) with Swank being the real stand-out.
   
   In short, a movie that deserves its Oscar nominations.
