5.0,"I saw this movie because of it's Oscar nominations, and I wasn't disappointed in the least. The movie is fantastic. The performances of Hilary Swank and Clint Eastwood are phenomenal; the chemistry between them is fantastic and really suggests a familial bond. Maggie Fitzgerald (Swank) is an up-and-coming boxer who is in desperate need of a trainer. She finds the perfect one in Frankie Dunn (Eastwood), but he is reluctant to train a female. He's quickly worn down by her persuasion, and trains her to be a fantastic fighter. She quickly knocks out all her opponents in the first round, but Frankie's not eager to put her in a championship title bout so quickly. The boxing scenes are really well choreographed and Swank really pulls off Maggie's confidence and her desperation as well, since Maggie feels that boxing is the only thing that can save her from being white trash like her family. Her family is unbelievably selfish, and it's amazing that Maggie could fight her way out and become successful. There are somewhat graphic scenes in the ring, where Maggie gets her nose broken and several gashes as well. Frankie begins to act more like a father-figure to Maggie, and this is truly moving as his own daughter's letters keep getting sent back to him as ""return to sender"". The last about 1/2 hour to 45 minutes become somewhat harrowing, and I don't want to reveal what happens, but it is truly emotional. This is close to the best picture of 2004.","",False,False,False,False,5.0,"2005-02-04","","RT","900680712"
