million dollar baby: bleh. why does this movie have 93% on RT? i honestly thought that more critics would have been turned off by the ego of this movie. are we all sucking up to the great clint eastwood? whose neck is starting to look like a vagina? (HAHA. i don't mean any offense by that cuz i do like clint eastwood and some of his other movies, and i even find him cute. just trying to unsuccessfully make my journal more entertaining! haha -_-).
but yeah... i know this movie doesn't employ the happy-ending that most sports movies do, but to me it felt cliche as fuck. and the narration was NOT profound but rather made me feel like i was listening to my grandpa. the fights were good, but the characters were surprisingly flat and the movie ultimately disappointing. it seemed to cater too much to the best picture category and i got this feeling that it was pretty smug in its complacency as well.","",False,False,False,False,2.5,"2005-02-05","","RT","900745809"
3.5,"Sideways, Million Dollar Baby and Closer. I am in the moviegeek equivalent to Disneyland. Im going to do some quick reviews for Sideways and MDB because what I really want to talk about is the revelation that I had after watching Closer. 
 
So heres the quick part:
 
 
 
[center][img]http://us.ent4.yimg.com/movies.yahoo.com/images/hv/photo/movie_pix/fox_searchlight/sideways/_group_photos/paul_giamatti2.jpg[/img][/center]
[center] [/center]
[center] [/center]

Sideways is a very witty comedy that goes from being absolutely hilarious to quite insightful in some parts. The thing I love the most about it, besides the spot-on naturalistic acting, is the absence of a Hollywood ending. The story isnt particularly happy, the characters are not always likeable and the man who deserves to go live in a ditch for the rest of his life due to the amount of appalling things he does (Sorry, Mr Church) gets away with everything. Its fantastic because its so real. Great cast, great story, great film. Oscar worthy? Yes.
 
 
 
[center][img]http://us.movies1.yimg.com/movies.yahoo.com/images/hv/photo/movie_pix/warner_brothers/million_dollar_baby/_group_photos/clint_eastwood6.jpg[/img][/center]
[center] [/center]
[center] [/center]
 
Million Dollar Baby is a film that has been gaining a lot of critical acclaim, particularly for its director, Clint Eastwood. The story starts off at a cracking pace setting up scenes and developing characters beautifully. Then, when the film is at its peak, something unexpected happens the narrative slowly begins to drift into a dangerous territory. I found this film really difficult to enjoy because its so emotionally harrowing in some points. Its wonderfully acted, superbly directed, expertly told but its not easy going. I had the same problem with Mr Eastwoods previous offering, Mystic River. The film is brilliant but I dont think I could ever sit through it again. Oscar worthy? Hillary Swank is. She is totally in her element and hopefully will be recognised for it.
 
[center][img]http://us.movies1.yimg.com/movies.yahoo.com/images/hv/photo/movie_pix/columbia_pictures/closer/_group_photos/clive_owen5.jpg[/img][/center]
 
 
Alright, heres the not so quick part. 
 
Sometimes there are movies worth getting excited about. When I heard there was a film that had the involvement of Jude Law, Julia Roberts, Clive Owen and Natalie Portman, I knew it was going to merit the price of a cinema ticket, if only so I could stare at Jude and Clive. Closer is one of those rare gems that come along every so often and completely take your breath away. This film is beyond excellent.
 
Let me begin by stating one simple fact. Natalie Portman is the epitome of perfection. She is beautiful, she is intelligent and she is one of the most talented performers I have ever seen. She is luminous as Alice in a role that could have ended up being a bland stereotype of white trash. Clive Owen is also outstanding in his role. Only he could utter the line ""Now, go fuck off and die"" yet still remain fascinatingly likeable. 
 
The story is ulimately about relationships, sex and desire and how they help to influence and reflect human behaviour. The story unfolds using a series of flash forwards which is actually a very interesting technique because you are always kept guessing about the direction in which the narrative is progressing. Just when you think youve got it all figured out, something changes. People change.
 
I mentioned before that I had a bit of a revelation while watching Closer. I realised that this is the type of film I ultimately want to produce. A film that requires little action but maintains and captivates interest through dialogue driven drama. A film that is intimate and real without having to play the tear-jerker card. An instant classic because it is so ahead of its time, yet uses techniques that have been around since the dawn of cinema. It is clever without being smug. Funny without being obvious. Heartfelt without being overtly emotional. 
 
Closer is a thing of terrible beauty. One of the best films Ive seen in many, many years. Oscar worthy? Absolutely.
 
Hello, Stranger.","",False,False,False,False,3.5,"2005-02-05","","RT","900721775"
5.0,"Simply the best movie of the year. A must see.","",False,False,False,False,5.0,"2005-02-05","","RT","900765304"
4.5,"When you leave a movie theater still wiping your eyes, sniffing, and thinking about the main characters that grabbed your attention for the past 2 hours, this is a movie that was worth every penny and thoroughly enjoyable!
 
I'm not a fan of sports movies, but having the triple threat of it being a Clint Eastwood movie, co-starring Morgan Freeman, and Oscar winner Hillary Swank as the female boxer, I just had to see it.
 
Hillary's character make you sympathize with her plight from growing up in a good for nothing family to wanting to be someone whom people respected. She was the type of girl a lot of us are - independent, stubborn, but still very sweet. I forsaw the father/daughter type relationship that would develop between Hillary and Clint early on with his faux lack of interest in training a girl, and that's when Morgan comes in and lives through Maggie by pushing her to train with the best and be all that she can be.
 
I cannot believe the pre-Oscar anti-MDB campaign that is raging on now, all due to a decision that is made near the end of the movie that was made after a lot of thinking and soul searching and crying.
 
This is a fantastic movie and I will be shocked if it doesn't win Best Picture and at least Best Actress for Hillary. I want Morgan to win too for Best Supporting Actor... but now seeing this, I think Clint is most deserving of winning Best Director too (sorry Marty, you didn't act in and direct The Aviator).
 
Go see Million Dollar Baby, but have some tissue during the last half-hour. :fresh:","",False,False,False,False,4.5,"2005-02-05","","RT","900668267"
