5.0,"[img]http://us.movies1.yimg.com/movies.yahoo.com/images/hv/photo/movie_pix/warner_brothers/million_dollar_baby/milliondollarbaby_poster.jpg[/img]
 
[font=Times New Roman][size=3]Million Dollar Baby is a character study of three defeated souls, all of them past their prime, all lacking true family, all of them damaged goods. It is not an uplifting or pleasing film; Clint Eastwood, as a director, gives it to us straight, he does not try to fool the audience or parade clichéd, improbable characters across the screen. Likewise, the actors were cast to fit the parts, both physically and mentally; Swank is no model, and is immediately fit to play the somewhat masculine part. Eastwood brilliantly directs himself as a shredded soul, a worn out Irish Catholic man with a lead chip hanging from his shoulder; guilt etched into his face and neck. A man that has attended Mass like clockwork for twenty-three years is searching for a forgiveness that his priest cannot impart upon him. Unlike many directors starring in their own films, he has a masterful grace with his acting; he does not stick out or seem contrived. He plays the part of Frankie Dunn, the owner of The Hit Pit gym and, in his mind, a semi-retired boxing trainer. Even though he has spent most of his life by the ring, and is obviously devoted to his role in the sport, he has no misconceptions of the backwards affair; the corruption, the savagery, the masochism that one endures to make it to the top. Eastwood is understated and restrained. This is by far one of his most dramatic and internal roles, and he not only pulls it off perfectly, but begins to become the character, and we see not only a broken father estranged from his daughter, but a great American actor who has seemingly fallen off of the mountain of machismo and leery-eyed roles that he built and once controlled. [/size][/font]
 
 
 
[font=Times New Roman][size=3]This goes for Morgan Freeman and Hilary Swank, as well. Freeman plays a part comparable to his superb performance in The Shawshank Redemption. He is the voice of reason; the low, calm narrator always a few steps away in a grey corner. He plays Scrap, the only friend of Frankie, and the only person he shows sympathy to and the only one who knows about his inner guilt. Dunn has only one prospective title-contender, and when the fighter leaves him for a less protective manager, is left with a withering career and a premier position at the end of the road. That is, until, Maggie Fitzgerald (Swank) refuses to be brushed off to another manager. Dunn sees passion, determination in her, and though he hates the thought of training a woman, eventually agrees partly out of his own severed relationship with his daughter. Maggie is a splintered woman of 31 years, who comes from a trailer park upbringing, and who only wants to box. She knows full and well that she has nothing of value in her life, and doesnt mean much to anybody, especially her family. Swank is brilliant, and portrays Fitzgerald with devotion and honesty. We, as viewers, can feel only admiration; Maggie has nothing, therefore every cut, every broken bone and streak of blood across her face is her fighting the odds. She is not coming from wealth or trying to win it; she is fighting to make an impression on her world, so she can say I got somewhere, I did alright.[/size][/font]
 
 
 
[font=Times New Roman][size=3]The lighting, throughout the entire film, creates a grain that lends harshness to the story. The use of shadows and bright lights emphasize the action and dialogue and the camera is constantly behind Dunn during the fights. We are not watching from the stands; we are in the ring being hammered and then quickly strafing around the action. Eastwood never takes the easy way out; there are no fancy special effects, unnecessary romance scenes, or stylized camera sweeps. He employs the camera as a constant onlooker; a third party that sees everything but can not intervene. The score, composed by Eastwood, greatly compliments the overall tone of the film. Lonely guitars and wandering pianos never overwhelm or obscure, they simply magnify the mood. One of the other elements rounding out the story is the presence of a subplot. The gym in which Scrap, Frankie, and Maggie so frequently appear is a living community. There are hopefuls, and hopeless kids with nowhere else to go. These lesser plots add substance and puts Maggie and Frankies story into context.[/size][/font]
 
 
 
[font=Times New Roman][size=3]I cannot stress how impressive and overpowering this film is. It will stick with you; it will follow you out of the theater and will influence all the films you see thereafter. What makes this such a great, character-driven film is the use of boxing as its springboard. It is not a boxing story, just as Mystic River was not a police procedural crime movie. It is a story about human desperation and devotion. It is neither kind nor forgiving on its characters; you will not find any Hollywood happy endings here. But I urge anyone, despite your previous opinions of Clint Eastwood movies or boxing in general; this is a film that you must see. Is it an Oscar contender along side The Aviator? I think not. Million Dollar Baby deserves the [i]win[/i] for picture, director, actor, actress, and supporting actor.[/size][/font]
 
-DP","",False,False,False,False,5.0,"2005-02-07","","RT","900764495"
