 I could totally sit here and pick apart the flaws of this film until the cows came home, but I ain't gonna do it because I don't have any cows. I live in a city. But if you'd like to know what some of them are, I will tell you. Let's just say the film as a whole left me stunned and virtually nailed to my seat. An incredibly powerful and moving drama that avoids sentimentality and contains a trio of flawless performances. The surrogate family theme was extremely well-handled.
 
 [img]http://www.filmforum.com/films/born/bornintobrothelssm.jpg[/img]
 
 One of the best documentaries I've ever seen.  [b]Born into Brothels[/b] is about a photo-journalist working in the red-light district of Calcutta who not only provides the children of prostitutes with cameras and urges them to document their surroundings, she instills them with hope and opportunity in a place where there is virtually none.  Yes, it's sad and disturbing to see these people living in such squalor and filth, but the film is simulataneously uplifting and always entertaining.  Sometimes all it takes is a charismatic smile, I guess.  Zana Briski, the co-director and reluctant protagonist is some sort of modern-day saint, and I felt so galvanized by her cause (www.kids-with-cameras.org) that I already donated $50 and might purchase one of the prints.  These are extremely talented and articulate children, but moreso than that, they are so damn lovable you just want to shake your fist at the invisible antagonist known as ""unfair social structure"".  The music is also really cool.
 
