 What can I say, I'm a sucker for sports movies.  I guess I was really taken back by how important high-school football is in some parts of the country, and how an entire town can pin it's hopes on a team.   The film doesn't endorse this mind-set, by the way, just tells it like it is; how these kids will most likely never leave their small town and how this is the only opportunity they'll have to create happy memories.  Win, or else.  That's pressure for ya!  Not knowing anything about the real-life story going into it, the ending really shocked me.","",False,False,False,False,4.5,"2005-02-09","","RT","900622420"
4.0,"Hey ho, lets go.
  
  [b]Million Dollar Baby[/b] - Clint's follow-up to Mystic River proves to be more touchy-feely (not in the really demeaning way) and a bit less layered. Yes, there are layers to it, but its very cryptic and hard to dissern what's being said about these people's past. I like how Eastwood doesn't cop out for a feel-good ""we did it!"" ending, and takes a more unseen route down a darker path; however, my main concern is that critics (especially Ebert) are blowing this out of proportion. Yes, its a good, solid film, but doesn't achieve the emotions or feelings that a Best Picture should (IMO).
  [b]
  Manhattan[/b] - Woody Allen has made some classics in his life, this being one of them, and how gorgeous it is. The choice for B&W was a wonderful one, and this turns out to be his most visually developed film. Great visuals and music (great New York music) on top of Allen's trademark neurotic dialogue makes for the strongest film of his I've seen. Sadly, romantic comedies don't thrill me, but Allen usually can break this barrier for me.
  [b]
  Schindler's List [/b]- Often regarded as one of the best films of the past decade, not mentioning of all time, this is a moving and beautiful film. This is probably the most visually stunning B&W film I've ever seen, thats right, better than Citizen Kane. Spielberg utilizes lights in such a genius manner that it makes you just gasp in beauty. This feeling is a dynamic one, for our story is a dark and depressing one. He shows us the way the Nazis didn't even think that a Jews life was a real one, and that they're just animals. And the acting---Liam Neeson gives the performance of a lifetime with Oskar Schindler, a business man whose just in it for the profit. Through the film, we're given insight into his character and the way his world is crumbling because he actually starts to care for these massacred people (shown wonderfully in the ""Girl with a Pink Jacket"" scene). He also makes us fear in every moment a Jew hides or that a Nazi lurks around with his pistol. I just can't say enough about this film to make you understand how great of a film it is.
  
  [b]Hotel Rwanda [/b]- Now called ""The Schindler's List of Africa,"" this film has met its aspirations. It shows us the nastiness of brainwashing & propaganda, the fear of genocide, and the fear of being torn apart from those we hold most dearly. It reminded me of (sorry for the possibly-considered insensitivity) Night of the Living Dead in that these people are in this small area trying to keep the outside forces from coming in, but this isn't the strong point of the film, for it lies in the evokations of emotions. This film is one of very few that gets me close to crying, and for most people, it will be near-impossible not to. 
  
  ...Ray??
  
  [b]Day of the Dead[/b] - George A. Romero's follow-up to the genius and classic [i]Dawn of the Dead[/i] is a far weaker film with upped special effects and watered-down story. Some of the ideas it presents are interesting, but once thought through, don't make as much sense as they should. I liked the scientific approach to explaining the zombies opposed to the discussed (and superior) religious approach of [i]Dawn of the Dead[/i] (""When there's no more room in Hell, the Dead will walk the Earth"").  The movie isn't nearly as much fun as [i]Dawn of the Dead [/i]was or as inventive as the original [i]Night of the Living Dead, [/i]and the only thing that pushes it over the Rotten/Fresh edge is the amazing special effects from Tom Savini (most noteably the first zombie we see and the one on the table with his torso cut open and empty).","",False,False,False,False,4.0,"2005-02-09","","RT","900704927"
5.0,"[img]http://images.rottentomatoes.com/images/movie/gallery/1139338/photo_29.jpg[/img]
 
 There is one flaw, and one flaw only with [i]Million Dollar Baby[/i]: the title.  Why they switched it from ""Rope Burns"" I'll never know.  Aside from that error, the rest of the film was perfection.
 
 I'm not the biggest fan of Mystic River.  There were some problems with the script and some of the characters (particularly Laura Linney) that I just couldn't ignore.  Most people disagreed with me and thought that film was incredible as well.  Due to this (and a cliche-looking trailer), I wasn't all that excited for this one.  Even as the rave reviews were pouring in I remained skeptical.  In all honesty, I think my expectations could have been through the roof and I still would have been more than satisfied with this film.
 
 I've heard a lot mentioned about how Clint Eastwood didn't deserve the acting nom he received.  That couldn't be further from the truth.  I'm not the worlds biggest Clint fan either.  I think he was great in westerns when he didn't have any lines.  Away from that, I generally don't like his work.  This was an exception, and he deserves the accolades.
 
 [img]http://images.rottentomatoes.com/images/movie/gallery/1139338/photo_24.jpg[/img]
 
 And to think he played second fiddle to Swank and Freeman.  They both should walk away with gold.  Swank is a puzzle to me.  To listen to her talk in interviews and such, I'd never guess she's this talented.  But every role I see her in since Boys Don't Cry, I've been impressed.  It's good to see Freeman on track again too.  He's a phenomenal actor who has chosen some pretty shotty roles lately, but he hit the bullseye here.
 
 This also had, hands down, the best cinematography of the year.  It's seamless shift in tone from the bright lights and vibrant life of the ring to the dark shadows and the mystery and doubt cast in the lockers and homes of its key characters creates a living, breathing atmosphere.  It's almost a separate character.  Tom Stern is proving to be an emmense talent, one worth remembering.  I guess that's what happens when you work with the likes of Conrad Hall.
 
 [img]http://images.rottentomatoes.com/images/movie/gallery/1139338/photo_03.jpg[/img]
 
 This film is not a sports film.  It's not a typical film.  It's not a film for a particular person.  This film has enough depth, enough true heart and emotion for anyone out there.  It has such a wide range of feeling, and never goes where you expect it to.  Thanks to a great script, it has great characters to take you through the elation as well as the misery.
 
 A lot has been made about the reveal in the third act as well, and it deserves its attention.  The end of the film is very powerful and is what took it from a top ten film to the #1 film of the year for me.  It manages to bring everything full circle and take the film to a new level.
 
 It's very rare that a film lives up to such positive buzz, but this is one of those rare cases.  This film is not only as good as everyone has said, it's even better.  It completely transcends any plot contrivances and plays as a very emotionally honest film.  Eastwood has created a masterpiece here, and unless Marty worked magic with [i]The Aviator[/i], the statue should go home with Clint.","",False,False,False,False,5.0,"2005-02-09","","RT","900628396"
