4.5,"Following 2003's critical darling [i]Mystic River[/i], the insanely talented Clint Eastwood switched his entire production team immediately over to the cause of filming [b]Million Dollar Baby[/b], a tender family drama that's nominally a boxing movie but really is all about redemption, love and determination. Sounds a bit cheesy? Don't worry; it's anything but. Eastwood himself plays the gruff Frankie Dunn, a small-time trainer and patch-up man whose star fighters always grow out of him. He runs a small, broken-down gym with the help of his friend and former boxing champ Eddie 'Scrap-Iron' Dupris (Morgan Freeman), into which one day wanders stubborn trailer trash Maggie Fitzgerald (Hilary Swank), who's bent on becoming a boxing sensation to drag herself out of a world she knows she'll never escape otherwise. The taciturn, rude Frankie at first refuses to take Maggie on, but when she proves to him that she can give as good as she gets, yelling at him that boxing is the only thing that's ever made her feel worthwhile, he gives in. Following a bout of training, Maggie's winning streak begins, as she heads ever further in search of a million-dollar championship title.
 
Every review you read is going to tell you: if you think you know where this movie is going (all triumph of the spirit and glorious sporting victories), you don't. This review is no different. The whole last third of this movie is the equivalent of a knockout punch: just as painful and stinging, with a sheer hard-hitting power that no one will forget in a hurry. When Frankie finally reveals to Maggie the meaning of the Gaelic slogan 'mo-cuish-le', I felt the audience in the cinema break down alongside me. It's not a comfortable or easy theme to be dealing with, but Eastwood handles it with such care and respect that it all feeds beautifully into the movie's central themes of love and hope: yes, hope. In spite of his appearance as a broken, cynical man, Frankie is, after all, hiding a reservoir of hope as large as the ocean: his priest, frustrated by Frankie's daily tongue-in-cheek interrogations, thinks he lies, but every day, Frankie dutifully writes to his estranged daughter and waits for the letter that doesn't hit his doorstep marked 'return to sender'. To Eastwood's credit, he doesn't pound this into the audience the way a less confident film-maker would; he allows his viewers to come to this understanding themselves, which makes Frankie's attachment to Maggie, and actions at the end on her behalf, so much more heartbreaking.
 
That's not to say that the preceding two-thirds was shit; far from it. Eastwood directs with an assured, confident hand and eye, skilfully developing narrative, characters and relationships with a profound subtlety and a deep undercurrent of respect. It never feels like filler when Frankie bickers with Scrap about holes in the latter's socks, or about using the expensive bleach when it all just smells like bleach anyways. Frankie's banter with surrogate daughter Maggie is also charming: she mouths off to him, flirts with him the way a little girl does with her daddy, and always proves to him just how much of her own person she is. She wins his respect the hard way, and it's this delightfully detailed development of a relationship between the two main characters that gives the movie its enormous heart and soul. In a dark car-ride home, after visiting her horrifically cold-hearted and ungrateful mother, Maggie informs Frankie that he's all she's got. In any other movie, this scene would have been cringeworthy at best; here, it steals up on you and sneaks right into your gut. 
 
As an accomplished actor and legend in his own right, Eastwood knows just how to handle his actors, drawing great performances from all of them, even bit players like Jay Baruchel (whose giddily optimistic Danger Barch provides levity and pathos in equal measure). Freeman has no problem establishing himself as trusty sidekick and story narrator, but gracefully and knowingly steps aside for the two leads. Swank delivers one of the female performances of the year: her character was no doubt written with the word 'feisty' in mind, and Swank gives Maggie that and more. She's been rightfully lauded and name-checked by the biggest award ceremonies around. But, as Swank herself admits, this is Eastwood's movie, and it's a riveting performance of incredible complexity and depth from a man and acting legend who really has nothing left to prove but goes ahead and does it anyway. Eastwood pours just the right amount of his own movie-star charm into the character, keeping Frankie credibly ordinary but also engaging despite his surface rudeness. A lesser actor would not have made us enjoy Frankie's chauvinistic attitude towards Maggie at the beginning, or his cheeky pestering of his priest everyday after Mass. (It certainly helps that, at 74, Eastwood still has the astoundingly handsome chiselled face of decades past, except it's now weather-beaten into maturity and wisdom.) But Eastwood is stunning and powerfully affecting in a scene towards the end where he breaks down before his priest: the raw pain he expresses is so real and, most importantly, believable in spite of Frankie's established emotional reticence. (The character papers over emotion with wisecracks: at one point, when he is reading to Maggie, he tells her that he liked the room so much he'd come back and read even if she wasn't there.)
 
I never really bought into all that [i]Mystic River[/i] hype. I felt the movie produced was slightly off-kilter (if not full-out strange) and not as emotionally resonant as all the critics were making it out to be. Sign me up for the Eastwood love-train with MDB though: that Eastwood not only acts in, but also directs, produces and composed for this film is astounding. That he does a phenomenal job in each capacity is even more amazing. The script is punchy and clever, though the denouement not for everyone, and the acting powerhouse. What's a shame, I find, is that however gushy this review has been so far, it still hasn't managed to capture the feeling of sheer enjoyment that the movie gave me while I was watching it. It's been far too long since I've seen a film that flowed as beautifully from scene to scene as this one: it's like Clint Eastwood's internal sense of poetry and timing has been captured onscreen, and we're all invited to a front-row seat. A movie that is all at once sublimely simple and deeply complex in its treatment of the universal themes of love and sacrifice and forgiveness--it's been a privilege, Clint.","",False,False,False,False,4.5,"2005-02-12","","RT","900685673"
5.0,"It's been a long time since a movie made me cry. I saw ""Million Dollar Baby"" today. Tears were flowing out of me by the end.
      I have no interest in ""The Aviator"" and ""Sideways"" cannot possibly be better than this picture, so ""Million Dollay Baby"" is hands down the best film of last year foir me. Just thinking about it makes me emotional. Really it's not so much a film about boxing as a film about love, the love you search for when the people who are supposed to love you don't and the love for another person that overcomes all sense of right and wrong. Screw the ""Scorsese is overdue"" crap. Clint Eastwood richly deserves the Best Director Oscar and though Best Actor is going to Jamie Foxx by acclamation, Eastwood's performance here shouldn't be overlooked. It's the best acting I've ever seen from him. And Hilary Swank...my God. Everytime I think of her trussed up in that hospital bed I want to cry again. That young lady is about to win her second Best Actress Oscar. How bloody good do you have to be to turn out movies like this and ""Mystic River"" back to back in your 70's?
 
   Last night I looked at most of ""The Human Stain"". I couldn't take any more after an hour. The movie tried to cover too much ground too quickly and never felt real for a minute. The casting didn't help either. A dead sexy Nicole Kidman as an abused wife and Anthony Hopkins as a light-skinned black man? Right, and I'm Mister Ed.
 
    I also looked at the Popeye cartoon collection I rented. It wasn't quite as bad as I expected. Some of the cartoons were brain dead and unfunny but some had a few laughs. One thing for sure, I didn't realize before how cheap and empty looking 60's television cartoons were. People owe Hanna-Barbera something of an apology. They were not the only ones doing repetitive limited animation back then. 
    Actually five different groups produced the Popeye cartoons. One group, led by director Seymour Kneitel, was the one closest to the style of the 50's Popeye theatrical work, just cheaper and more far fetched. (In one of these, Popeye and Olive are menaced by an evil wheel of cheese. I am not making this up.) Another group was done by Larry Harmon who was far more famous for his Bozo the Clown cartoons and his stuff had Popeye acting like Bozo. Gene Deitch's work, at least the two shown here, were more adventure cartoons and nothing really special. The one sample from Bob Bemiller was negligible.
     The good stuff came from Jack Kinney's unit. These cartoons were occasionally pretty funny, if sometimes odd. They had situations like Wimpy blown up to giant size to sell to a circus, and a jungle cartoon with Olive shooing awway a friendly tiger and Brutus trapped by an amorous flower. They also used all the pop culture signifiers of the time like television commercials, the suburbs, beatniks, abstract art and a few were fairy tale parodies in the vein of Jay Ward's sainted ""Fractured Fairy Tales"". The main problem with this group was that the plots could be really random and the cartoons often faded out with no real ending. Sometimes they didn't even let Popeye take his spinach.","",False,False,False,False,5.0,"2005-02-12","","RT","900691892"
