4.5,"""Valentine's Day is a holiday invented by greeting card companies to make people feel like crap"" - Jim Carrey in what I believe is the opening line to [i]Eternal Sunshine of the Spotless Mind[/i].
 
Valentine's day will be different for me this year than I've been used to spending it in recent years. It's the first one in 4 years in which I don't really have any plans. 3 years ago today I was hanging out with Shauna a lot, and was about a week away from when our relationship really began. Try as I might, I cannot remember what we did two years ago. A year ago was our last Valentine's Day together, we ate at the Black Eyed Pea, which was her favorite restaurant at the time and may still be, then we watched [i]Miracle[/i], which was the last movie we watched while we were together. 
 
We've been broken up for 10 months now and she's engaged for all intents and purposes to somebody who two and a half months ago I believed she'd never consider dating. I'm trying to move on with it because they'll be probably be married by the end of the year, but it's not an easy thing for me to do, as the two regular readers of this journal have probably figured out by now. 
 
I have a mock interview at the career services center at my old school today. I'll basically be interviewed by a representative of an area company, who will then tell me what I did right and wrong in the interview and what employers are looking for when they interview applicants. An hour after that's supposed to be over, I have a real interview with a company in Irving. I'm not exactly sure what the job is and I don't rightly remember applying to this company, but the description of the work, not to mention the starting pay ($12.50/hour!!!), sounded pretty interesting. I think the job involves looking over resumés for a staffing company and combing them over to see whether applicants are qualified and whether or not they have the proper paperwork submitted, etc. We'll see how that goes. I also have an interview for tomorrow, this one with a company that trains background investigators, which I think I'd be good at since I've always been good at getting information on stuff and on people. I'm kind of wishing the first job, but I'll have to go through those interviews and meet the people in charge first to see if I like the companies involved.
 
I might do something crazy today after that interview. I called Regan earlier today while at an area mall (more on that momentarily) and asked her how she was doing and how her job interview last week went. It was a normal Regan phone conversation, which means I asked her a lot of questions about how she was and what was going on, and she answered sounding like someone who hated talking on the phone. I asked her if I could see her after the interview, using the lame excuse that I didn't want to walk around all day in my suit and needed a place to change clothes. I've used dumber excuses to see a girl before. Anyway, she said she wouldn't mind if I came over, and the tone in her voice seemed to say, ""that would make my day if you came to see me, but since I don't like revealing my true feelings about anything I would never actually say that to you outright."" I burned her a cd of songs that I know she likes or probably will like anyway, and I looked over the Valentine's day aisle at Wal-Mart, before deciding that getting her a Russel Stover or Whitman's Sampler-type chocolate box was probably a bad idea since we really weren't at that stage and likely wouldn't be if I dared to give her something that nice just like that. So I went low-key and got a small heart-shaped box with small Hershey's mini-bars. Probably still a bad idea but I thought it was a better one than the more expensive box. I know she loves chocolate, something I can't say for Shauna, who would never let me get her chocolate out of weight concerns or something like that. I got Regan a giant size Hershey's dark chocolate bar for a half-birthday present last October, becoming the first person to ever do that for someone they've known just over a month. I may be in a very good mood this time tomorrow, or I'll likely be incredibly depressed, which I was for most of December and January anyway (see previous entries). We'll see how that goes.
 
Now, I saw [i]Million Dollar Baby[/i] finally on Saturday afternoon. Very good movie, though not quite the masterpiece critics are making it out to be IMO. I had been out of theater for about 30 seconds after it ended when my sister called me and said, ""I know you just watched a movie, but would you like to watch another one?"" I guessed that one of her guy friends at school wanted to see a movie in town and she wanted me to go along with them so it didn't look like a date, which is pretty much how I saw [i]Coach Carter[/i]. It turned out that some of her friends from church were planning to go to the Parks Mall in Arlington (nearly 50 miles from our house) to watch [i]Hitch[/i] and she wanted me to drive her there. I've been to the Parks' theater dozens of times and knew perfectly well that with all the crowds that go there, it would be virtually impossible to go there for a movie that was in its second day of release, the weekend before Valentine's Day, on a Saturday night, at the Parks Mall, show up 15 minutes before it was supposed to start, and have any realistic expectations of getting a ticket. They wanted to try anyway and so I was a nice big brother and I drove Sarah there. We had expected her friends to beat us there, buy our tickets for us, then meet us outside the entrance so we could all sit together. However we were the first ones there, and immediately saw that all showings of Hitch for the next two hours were sold out. The others got there late because one friend had car trouble (read: drove her car into a ditch on her way home and had trouble getting it out). So instead of going to the movie, they wandered around the mall for the hour left before closing time. Somewhere between the time we sat down for dinner and the time Sarah tried to pay for her food at Sonic (which is where we all went after leaving the theater), Sarah lost her wallet. Where and when is anybody's guess. She had it because she payed for her food at the mall with money out of it. She thought she might have dropped it under the table we sat at, but didn't remember seeing it under there when she got up to leave. She thought she might have dropped it at the one store where she tried on a prom dress, but we went back today and the employees told us no wallet had been found or turned in there. The mall's customer service desk also said no wallet had been turned in to them. So Sarah was understandably upset for most of today. But we went to the mall with her friends and saw the movie anyway after church. 
 
On the drive home she was still mad but felt like doing something wild all of a sudden. She said she felt like going clubbing (this is a 17 year old talking), then said she wanted to get her ears pierced again, then said she wanted to dye her hair. Girls can be so impulsive sometimes. I would understand if all this had happened during her period or something but it didn't. So after we got home I took her and a friend to Wal-Mart (where I also bought Regan's chocolates) and they picked out a hair dye and colored her hair. It's naturally a sort of dark blonde, but I think she dyed it brown or almost a reddish color. I'll have to see it in the daylight to tell. If only I hadn't been so nice and driven her on an ill-advised trip to a movie there's no way they could have gotten a ticket for... her hair would still be blonde, I wouldn't have driven a good 150 miles out of my way between Saturday and Sunday, and she would still have her wallet, which happened to contain, among other things, $20-30 in cash, some unused gift cards she got for Christmas, her school ID, her driver's permit, her social security card, and her key to our house. That last part had mom and dad a bit worried, although I'm sure it's unlikely someone would steal her wallet and then drive 50 miles on the off chance that the key in it was our house key, wait for us to all leave, then rob us. If they're very worried we might end up changing our locks. I'm still hoping that whoever found it (and I know someone had to because a wallet doesn't just lie around for very long at a place like that) will try and contact her or mail it to the address on her permit. The cynic in me says that quite unlikely, but I can always hope. 
 
So that's pretty much my weekend. Hopefully my next entry will bring better tidings.","",False,False,False,False,4.5,"2005-02-14","","RT","900589922"
