4.0,"My guy and I made the rounds at the Portland International Film Festival this weekend. (And saw a couple movies that weren't part of the festival.) Here's what we saw.
 
[b][i]Millions.[/i][/b] This is Danny Boyle's new film, who's known for his previous films [i]Trainspotting[/i] (haven't seen it) and [i]28 Days Later[/i] (saw it and liked it). This new film of his was a disappointment though and reminded me of the craptastic [i]Pay It Forward[/i], a movie I hated despite Kevin Spacey being in it. It's cute (in a bad way), saccharine, and cliched. Barf. Next!
 
[i][b]The Ballad of Jack and Rose.[/b][/i] I liked this one more, but I'm a sucker for coming-of-age stories, especially when it involves a young woman's sexual awakening and a stepfamily situation, as this film does. I did notice a major goof in the film though: the movie supposedly takes place on an island of the east coast of the U.S. So how come when Jack and Rose are driving through town on the mainland, they pass a Canadian Tire store? Did anyone else notice that?
 
[i][b]Dutch Light.[/b][/i] This movie wasn't in the RT database. It's a documentary from the Netherlands about the phenomenon of Dutch light and how it inspired and influenced legions of artists, from Rembrandt to Monet. It's a visually stunning film, of course, and I LOVED the part where they talked about Van Gogh. I wasn't expecting the film to touch on him because even though he was Dutch, he painted most of his works in France. But they did and they talked about how different the light and colors were in the south of France versus Holland, how it caused Van Gogh to lighten his palette, etc. Awesome. I was in art geek heaven.
 
[i][b]Million Dollar Baby.[/b][/i] I went into this movie expecting to hate it because of all the critical praise and Oscar nominations it's received and what I thought would be a boring, cliched storyline. (i.e. Underdog gets training from coach, wins the big prize, etc. Zzzz . . .) Imagine my surprise when I found myself caring about the characters . . . and when I found myself teary-eyed at the end. It's not really a boxing movie at all; instead it's a movie about people, about (surrogate) fathers and (surrogate) daughters. And Hilary Swank, who I usually hate, is very good in it. I hate to say it, but she almost deserves the Oscar nom (her second). If she wins, I won't be totally disappointed.
 
[i][b]Head On.[/b][/i] I loved this and it was my favorite out of all the movies I saw over the weekend. ([i]Million Dollar Baby[/i] is second.) It's a film about two Turkish-German immigrants who meet each other in the loony bin, enter a marriage of convenience, and eventually fall in love, but weirdly enough, don't wind up together. (I really wanted them to though.) I loved the female lead character, Sibel, and really felt for her, coming as she did from an abusive Turkish family who blamed everything on her. What a bunch of sexist assholes.
 
[i][b]Office Space.[/b][/i] I've seen this movie several times and think it's a classic, especially for anyone who's had a mindless, soul-killing office job. Who hasn't dealt with an insufferable boss, a piece of shit printer/copier, the indignity of being told by several different people that you fucked up something small, like leaving the cover of a TPS report, or being told by the uber-perky secretary that you ""have a case of the Mondays""? Work sucks and this movie shows how much it does -- and how to deal with it. If you don't mind being fired or sent to jail, that is.","",False,False,False,False,4.0,"2005-02-14","","RT","900754693"
