5.0,"There are many reasons why Clint Eastwood is a Hollywood icon, films like this are one of them.
 
[img]http://movie-reviews.colossus.net/2004images/million1.JPG[/img]
 
Eastwood produced, directed and stars in the story of an almost middle-aged hayseed woman named Maggie, played to perfection by Hillary Swank who goes from waiting tables and dreaming about her shot at glory to standing in the same ring with the champion of the world as fans scream her name. 
 
Guiding Maggie along the way is aged trainer and gym owner Frankie Dunn, played by Clint and his close friend, retired boxer Eddie Dupris, played brilliantly by Morgan Freeman, who also narrates the story with much of the same dry inflections and smart ass humor as in [i]The Shawshank Redemption.[/i]
 
After a fighter he's trained for eight years ditches him right before a title fight, Eastwood is left hanging and eventually after the prodding of Freeman and the thick-headedness of Swank agrees to train her. Initially blowing her off because he doesn't ""train girls"" Eastwood finally agrees and Maggie is trained and put in the ring. Eastwood's worried tough guy exterior ponders the fate of her sweet-natured good looks; indeed, after a handful of unadventurously staged fights, Swank's nose should've looked like Owen Wilson's. In any case, Maggie displays the heart and guts with a right hook that could stop a freight train dead in its tracks and goes on to bigger and better fights, eventually earning a title shot. Maggie's journey with Frankie eventually develops into a great father-daughter dynamic which the final moments of the film center around. (Eastwood writes to his real life daughter, whom he hasn't seen in years every week only to have his letters returned to sender, one after the other.)
 
The film's pace seems unrushed and yet so much is packed into a little over two hours. The cinematography is so smooth here, moving from the shadows of the gym to the bright lights of the ring and outside world and then back again without the slightest blip on the screen.
 
The final act of the film is touching and heartbreaking and with true Eastwood style, not overloaded with cliches and overt manipulation like most filmmakers today resort to. Anyone who doesn't tear up a little has no heart beating in thier chest. 
 
Eastwood has again created a masterpiece. This film again afirms his place as my favorite actor and filmmaker. The wonderful script and outstanding performances by Eastwood, Swank and particularly Freeman are all deserving of any and every award Hollywood has to offer.
 
[i]Million Dollar Baby [/i]is a film to be contemplated long after it is viewed. James Berardinelli's review asks a great question Clint lobs at us in the end; what shows more heart - to help a loved one to die with dignity, or to offer support to ease the pain of what they view as a valueless existence? Clint delivers the difficulties of this choice with one hell of an emotional wollup.
 
This is by far the best film I have seen in months and anyone would be a fool to miss it. This is a film that leaves you sitting in the theater and quietly draws you into the three character's who you begin to care about very deeply.","",False,False,False,False,5.0,"2005-02-15","","RT","900702756"
