5.0,"[img]http://us.movies1.yimg.com/movies.yahoo.com/images/hv/photo/movie_pix/warner_brothers/million_dollar_baby/milliondollarbabyposterbig.jpg[/img]
 
Plot: A female fighter with little chance of becoming famous meets a trainer who doesn't train women.
 
[img]http://us.movies1.yimg.com/movies.yahoo.com/images/hv/photo/movie_pix/warner_brothers/million_dollar_baby/_group_photos/clint_eastwood11.jpg[/img]
 
With so much hype behind this movie I was afraid it couldn't live up to it. Well this movie lived up to all the hype and then some this movie is definately one of Clint Eastwoods better films. Now his best work but this movie holds its own in a way most filmmakers seem to ignore these days. He uses character development and often forgotten the idea of hope. He always displays here that people need hope no matter what is going wrong in their life and the story here is told beautifully. I couldn't imagine Clint Eastwood doing this film any other way and thats why it gets a 10.
 
The entire film, the dialog, story, action, acting, is all so well done that it manages to involve you in the film. It does have several stereotypes, such as family members and fight scenes, but the movie pushes on with powerful acting and never manages to sway from what it knows it can do. To me this is by far Clint Eastwoods most emotional, if not his best, acting role ever and Hilary Swank holds her own ver nicely. Morgan Freeman is much less scene but every scene he is in, he steals it with just being himself.
 
The dialog in this movie is fairly predictable but the film displays it in a way that really gets you involved in the film. This movie does have some violence and fight scenes but even then you get a feel for the characters. Basic actions they make and things they do only enhance your thought on them. Even the more secendary actors give a good feal to the way life is. Some of them have a harsh reality to face and some have to just cool down but ultimately they all show character.
 
[img]http://us.movies1.yimg.com/movies.yahoo.com/images/hv/photo/movie_pix/warner_brothers/million_dollar_baby/_group_photos/clint_eastwood6.jpg[/img]
 
Though definately a drama this movie is not one you will soon forget. I highly recommend this movie to anyone that loves to watch characters.","",False,False,False,False,5.0,"2005-02-18","","RT","900667747"
3.0,"[img]http://images.rottentomatoes.com/images/movie/custom/96/10004696.jpg[/img]
 
I've been at this long enough that I feel like I can call it like I see it.
 
It was about a year ago that I saw Mystic River. I must've been journaling over my lame-ass GeoCities page at that time and was still new to this Internet writing thing and trying to put into words how I felt about film.
 
So probably I wrote some shit about how great Mystic River was even though I didn't really like it.
 
Somehow, it's easier to say I didn't really like Million Dollar Baby, either, though I definitely enjoyed it more than Mystic River.
 
The first two acts of Million Dollar Baby are full of energy, with lots of comic energy between Clint and Morgan Freeman - a great team if there ever was one. Pure gold between those two. Also enjoyed Clint's rejoinders with his priest.
 
Frank: ""What is He? A demigod?""
Priest: ""There are no demigods you fucking pagan!""
 
And more comic relief from Danger Dan.
 
Hillary Swank looks great. I could relate to her character, fighting to break the cycle of trailer-park-trash hell and poverty.
 
As long as there is boxing going on, the movie was happening for me - right up there with Rocky and Raging Bull, maybe even When We Were Kings.
 
Then the movie takes a dive and doesn't come back up. It was dismaying and disappointing. I understood what it was doing and why it took so long to do what it did. To do it any other way would've seemed crass and sudden, but I still wasn't ready for it. Wasn't looking.
 
[indent][i][size=1]The Boxer[/size][/i]
[i][size=1]I am just a poor boy
