4.5,"I've come to the conclusion that Dirty Harry is one darned good filmmaker.  Who honestly could have said that they would have thought that the guy who has done a billion bad-ass gunslinger movies had it in hime to be such an excellent filmaker in the serious drama genre.  Seriously.  Now after seeing a few of his movies I'm just wondering what kept him acting the same gunslinger outlaw roles over and over while these dramatic movies and directing ideas were all floating around his head.  Wish I could interview him and ask him that one.  Either way, now seeing ""Mystic River"" and ""Million Dollar Baby"" I have a lot of respect for the man's directing skills.  It can't hurt that he seems to draw in some great actors to his films, but without a good director or script the movie is bound to flop.  
 
My initial reaction to this film was that it was just a bit better than o.k. for me.  However after I get the feeling that I would like it even more after a 2nd viewing.  Just not a movie that you're gonna pull out all of the meaning from in one sitting.  It's not an epic, mind-blowing plot but just more of an interesting character study.  As one co-worker of mine put it so eloquently, ""It's a film about how people do the best they can with what they are given.""  Couldn't say it better myself.  Which also made me like the film just a bit more in hindsight.  Plus I thought the ending was pretty well done, no ribbons and bows, no pulling the curtain back to reveal some interesting twist, but more a gradual decresendo (slow fade out).  Also another critically aclaimed, slightly masculine, performance by Hillary Swank.  I can just hear her mom threatening, ""If you keep making those Tomboy faces, Hollywood is going to make it stay that way.""
 
[color=lightblue]In other news my movie watching intake is probably going to go up dramatically.  I just moved in with two friends who have Blockbuster online.  So they try to average three DVDs a week to make it worth the cost.  Not only that but they have digital cable with HBO and Showtime, with In-demand so you can watch the movies whenever.  It's craziness.  I can't promise that I'm going to critique all of them on here, but I'll try to keep up as best I can.[/color]
[color=#add8e6][/color] 
[color=#add8e6]Oh and if you haven't heard ""Chocolate"" by a lesser known band called ""Snow Patrol"" I highly recommend that you pop on-line or duct tape a transistor radio to your head until you catch it on the alternative rock station.  It's choice.  Or if you're a music video fan and you happen to see a falling star tonight, I recommend you wish to see Beck's new video ""E-Pro"".  It's pretty funky.[/color]","",False,False,False,False,4.5,"2005-02-21","","RT","900744413"
4.5,"[center][img]http://images.rottentomatoes.com/images/movie/custom/96/10004696.jpg[/img]
 
  [left] Million Dollar Baby is an emotionally involving thrill-ride, filled with laughs, cries, and shrieks. Its a beautiful film with a beautiful story to tell.
 
 Director Clint Eastwood has taken Paul Haggis multi-layered script and crafted a near-perfect film. The emotional depth in Million Dollar Baby is beyond words. Eastwood places you under the skin of every single character and lets their feelings sink into your brain.
 
 There are so many moments that Eastwood could have gone the over-sentimental route and made this an average film about the endurance of one woman, but instead, he squeezes raw emotions out of each actor.
 
 The cinematography is gorgeous. Eastwoods uses lighting and unique angles to capture the gritty realities of life. The editing is also nearly flawless, with every jump done with meticulous fingers.
 
 Paul Haggis adapted the F. X. Tooles novel, but I cant say much about the adaptation since I havent read the book. However, the raw emotions of all the characters are portrayed with great skill through the use of dialogue and an excellent voice over.
 
 Morgan Freemans deep voice has been used to narrate several educational videos for its ability to captivate the viewer and keep him or her involved. He doesnt disappoint here either. His performance in the film is simply perfect. He deserves the Oscar as much as the other guy.
 
 Clint Eastwoods performance is remarkable. At his tender age, Eastwood still remains a powerful force both on the screen and behind. His ability to respond using facial expressions is mesmerizing. Hilary Swank took the cake playing the dedicated female boxer. With a southern accent and the charisma of a lion, Swank conveys the internal beauty of the character.
 
 The chemistry among the entire ensemble is the bond for this unique drama. Scene after scene, the actors work together as if they had known each other their entire lives. The joy of working with Eastwood bleeds off the screen in an unimaginable form, a form of beauty.
 
 The controversial ending, which I wont go into, is possibly the only flaw for the entire film. Some may argue that it makes perfect sense, but there is no sense of closure. The viewer is left with some important questions left unanswered.
  
 Watching ""Million Dollar Baby"" takes you through a whirlwind of emotions: happiness, sadness, confusion, love, and pain. Even at the most trying times, Eastwood manages to squeeze a good laugh to dry the tears. If you made a mistake by holding off for this long (like me), catch it while its still on the big screen to view it in its purest, crispest form.
 [/left]
  [/center]","",False,False,False,False,4.5,"2005-02-21","","RT","900619822"
5.0,"Well, I just join in Rotten Tomatoes, the best movie site of the net. It's great, I love the forums, always something interesting to talk about. Anyway, it's Oscar season, so let me just say that [i]The Aviator[/i] and [i]Million Dollar Baby[/i] makes for one hell of a fight next Sunday, unlike last when I knew [i]The Lord of the Rings: The Return of the King[/i] would sweep the awards. This is a tough year to predict, and I expect some sort of surprise (Jamie Foxx [i]not[/i] winning! Could be possible). I think the Best Director will be a tough matchup. Will Clint Eastwood take home his second directing Oscar, or will the long-overdue Martin Scorsese finally gain his well-deserved win? Sorry Hackford, sorry Payne, and sorry Leigh, but you guys will get it next time (especially Payne). I'll come back later in the week, and I'll give you my complete Oscar prediction. Lata, biyatch. Oh yeah, I'm currently playing three games of three different systems: [i]Metroid Prime 2: Echoes[/i] (Gamecube), [i]Final Fantasy I & II: Dawn of Souls[/i] (Game Boy Advance), and [i]Super Mario 64 DS[/i] (Nintendo DS).","",False,False,False,False,5.0,"2005-02-21","","RT","900768280"
4.5,"Quite the range of movies viewed this weekend.
 
[b]King Arthur[/b]
Oh, man, this is not a good movie. First of all, if you decide to watch this, please [u]pretend that it's not about King Arthur[/u]. It has very, very little to do with the Arthurian legend and confuses Guinevere with Bodicea, among many other inconsistencies, which I'm not going to enumerate. Just trust me. It also completely ignores the romantic triangle of Arthur, Guinevere and Lancelot (although there are some sidelong glances). 
 
The battles are hack 'n' slash - not necessarily a bad thing, but every. single. hit. is a critical one. That's just not realistic, no matter how you slice it (pun intended). Frankly, they bored me.
 
Clive Owen plays Arthur as a whiny asshole, Keira Knightly does the tough girl thing, and Ioan Gruffudd is my new crush. Hot damn, he's gorgeous...you might remember him as the lead from the A&E series ""Horatio Hornblower"" (a good rental, by the way), and he'll next be playing Reed Richards in the Fantastic Four movie out this summer. Okay, I'm there.
 
[b]Million Dollar Baby[/b]
As good as everyone says, and not just because of the ending. The main trio (Swank, Eastwood and Freeman) are so good together...you really feel the comfort they have together. The boxing scenes are electric - and I don't like boxing - and riveting to watch. Beautifully shot, tastefully scored and perfectly written (although I think the title sucks). Don't miss this one. Swank for Best Actress...although I really loved Clint in this. Freeman has one of the most satisfying scenes in the movie (#110 - you'll know when you see it).
 
[b]Dungeons and Dragons[/b]
The movie that makes RPGamers everywhere cringe. Boy, this is bad. Thora Birch (yeah, that's what happened to her) looks about 10 years old and scared stiff. Jeremy Irons is chewing scenery right and left (in his few scenes). The adventuring party is made up of a bunch of D-list actors and a Wayans (who is annoying). Considering this is based on D&D, they should have done a bit more research (whuh? mages aren't considered regular people? since when?). 
 
And jeez, ya put a Beholder in a scene and it doesn't do a damned thing? How disappointing.","",False,False,False,False,4.5,"2005-02-21","","RT","900591330"
3.0,"Well after months of playing the trailer but no sight of it ever coming to our podunk little town's theatre Million Dollar Baby finally showed up at our local movie theatre and My Pretty Lady and me went to view this movie and now I'm gonna' talk about it... COS THAT'S WHAT THIS IS ABOUT!!! R-right?
 
Million Dollar Baby is an interesting movie. If it wasn't for some very unrealistic and very Hollywood leanings this could've been a really really strong powerful film. None of the acting is like blow away IMHO but none of it is really bad either. Clint Eastwood is... Clint Eastwood. Morgan Freeman is... Morgan Freeman. Hilary Swank was nothing special. But they all were adequate enough to pull things off... if only some things were different. 
 
First off the boxing is just totally unrealistic. I know it's a movie, and Rocky set the precedent for super unrealistic boxing in boxing movies. But in real life boxing they won't allow a boxer to grab their opponent by the face trying to shove their nose into their skull, hit them with a back elbow, trick the referee into turning their back as if it was pro wrestling, and hit the opponent while they're down. If the boxer grabbed the other by the face like mentioned above.. DQ or seperation by referee with points deducted. If they hit a back elbow to the head, totally a DQ or points deduction. The referee is NOT going to get distracted by outside intereferance like in a wrestling match if a fighter goes down from dirty tricks like mentioned above or from a legitimate blow. The referee is going to stay focused on the person down. And if a boxer hit their opponent while they're down, and it has happened before, that's a DQ or time for the opponent to recover with points deduction for the attacker, or some combination etc. If anyone watches this film and think that is how boxing really is that will be a real shame. I LOVE pro wrestling but the boxing scenes here should've been realistic boxing and not pro wrestling. 
 
I think the reason they had the boxing be the way it was in the film was they didn't want Swank's character to come off looking weak when the big injury happens but I think that is silly. Boxing is a violent sport. No one wins all the time. No one goes uninjured. Boxers have legitimately died or suffered crippling injuries from just normal boxing. If they had kept it more realistic it would've had a bigger impact because then it's more like real life, anything can happen, tragedy can strike out of the blue, and the audience can relate to that more as everyone knows something about bad things happening out of the blue to some degree or another as opposed to this incredibly unrealistic set up of events that lead to tragedy. 
 
Some of the situations were kinda' very hollywood like Swanks character's family. Was not a realistic idea really. And I'm not saying movies have to be realistic, they don't, some movies need to be totally outrageous and defiant of realism to reach their potentials. But I'm saying for this movie, if things had been maybe a little less Hollywood and more realistic then the emotional payoffs would've been bigger because we would've felt more  connection to what is going on. Because under the Hollywood-isms is a touching story with the potential to really move some emotions. 
 
One last gripe and that's about the priest character and his relationship with Eastwood's character. He was the most unrealistic, impatient, foul mouthed, unhelpful Catholic priest there can be. Being Catholic I know something about this to some degree and maybe it's just my Catholic-ness feeling defensive but this character was totally pointless and Eastwood's big talk with him about his pivotal decision towards the movies controversial climax doesn't resonate cos the relationship between the two isn't established to come off that way. They're established as antagonizers towards each other and then suddenly their consultants and soul searchers together just cos the priest character is needed as a plot device. Just sorta' useless fat that should've been trimmed from the film.
 
I sorta' enjoyed the film but I just wish it had been handled differently. As it is I understand it's nomination for best picture but of the 4 nominees I've seen so far for Best Picture I think Sideways is the best and unless Ray which I haven't seen is some blow away super duper epic I think Sideways should take the duke here.","",False,False,False,False,3.0,"2005-02-22","","RT","900764106"
4.0,"[size=3][color=lime]I gotta say [url=""http://www.imdb.com/title/tt0405159/""][color=#0000ff]Million Dollar Baby[/color][/url] is a pretty solid film with great performances all around. [url=""http://www.imdb.com/name/nm0005476/""][color=#0000ff]Hilary Swank[/color][/url] might even nab another best actress oscar, she defintely got in shape for the role. [u][color=#0000ff]Eastwood[/color][/u] was in top form as well although my bet is that [url=""http://www.imdb.com/name/nm0004937/""][color=#0000ff]Jamie Foxx[/color][/url] will win the best actor catagory and [u][color=#0000ff]Scorsese[/color][/u] will take home best director ([url=""http://www.imdb.com/title/tt0338751/""][color=#800080]The Aviator[/color][/url]) seeing how he's never been honored with it before. As for [url=""http://www.imdb.com/name/nm0000151/""][color=#0000ff]Morgan Freeman[/color][/url], he did a wonderfull job as always, playing the dignified sidekick full of gravitas, and has a great chance to win due to a fairly low profile, unknown competition (Foxx will win for best actor thus negating him for supporting actor). As for the the film's weaknesses: the boxing scenes looked totally unrealistic to me, quite cheesy and very ""Rocky""esque. Also, some of the movie & scenes felt contrived: the nurse going on a coffee break at just the right moment; what a freakin coincidence! As for the film score, I got a little bored of Eastwood's twangy single note tunes that he did so well for Unforgiven. Finally, with the film at 137 mins it began to drag just enough to cause you to wiggle in your seat at the end second act. All in all though, a nice solid piece of acting, harsh gritty production design and a smooth story which moves nicely and definitely has a chance for best picture. Check it out! And girls ... bring some tissues.[/color][/size]","",False,False,False,False,4.0,"2005-02-22","jk a","RT","786af060-da96-4d65-a7ed-29d7493c09a3"
4.0,"It is hard to get away from the typical stereotype of the boxing movie as a Rocky movie. It is also hard for me to think of Hilary Swank as anything other than the karate kid from the fourth and last installment of that series, but I love Clint Eastwood's projects, especially 1997's Midnight in the Garden of Good and Evil. So I put my biases aside and watched his newest end of the year release, Million Dollar Baby. 
 
Maggie Fitzgerald (Hilary Swank) is a 30 year old female boxer who wants to be trained under the best trainer in the industry, mainly Frankie Dunn (Clint Eastwood). He continually refuses, until he finally realizes through the chirping conscience that is his ever-present sidekick, Eddie Dupris (Morgan Freeman), that she is worth more than some of the other men he has trained. Slowly their relationship blossoms and Maggie becomes a star on the rise until tragedy strikes, threatening their relationship and their careers. 
 
[url=""http://www.smart-popcorn.com/review/606/""]See what I think of this film at Smart-Popcorn.com[/url]","",False,False,False,False,4.0,"2005-02-22","","RT","900662483"
