4.0,"[left][img]http://us.movies1.yimg.com/movies.yahoo.com/images/hv/photo/movie_pix/warner_brothers/million_dollar_baby/_group_photos/clint_eastwood8.jpg[/img][/left]
 
Any sports film that involves it's self centrally in the actual playing of the sport is almost bound to cover the much treaded territory out of necessity and [i]Million Dollar Baby[/i] is no different. What is different and refreshing is how much it relies on and transcends those usual stereotypes. It certainly is about how it's characters make their way outside the ring, but the life inside is essential to it's triumphs. We have an underdog, a cynical trainer, and his sidekick who loves the little guy, but director Clint Eastwood understands what he wants to accomplish and the picture is a restrained film interested simply in it's characters and little beyond. It is personal, classic filmmaking packaged in a weathered box, and it's really good.
 
As stated before the film follows a cliched underdog story, here a female boxer Maggie Fitzgerald (Hilary Swank) who does her determined best to get an aged, guilt-ridden trainer Frankie (Eastwood) to take her on. He doesn't train girls, but his long time friend and gym partner ""Scrap-Iron"" Dupris (Morgan Freeman) sees himself in the scrappy fighter. 
 
Frankie's reputation is that of an expert wounder man. He can fix all, but his own. He's haunted by an unexplained daughter who is so distanced from him won't even return his letters. He fears his mending only leads to later tragedy because it seems to be his path. He let Scrap go the full 15 once with a horrible cut above his eye. The eye is gone, but the guilt lives on. His motto is ""Always Protect Yourself,"" but he takes it to it's extremes. Rather than let his fighters take a shot at the title he holds them back, only to see them leave. He fears chance, plain and simple. Eastwood's gruff self is an easy fit, but he makes the most out of it and as usual Freeman is so good and makes it looks so easy he's easy to look past. Again, characters we've seen before made better by talent and a good tale.
 
What little sun and energy the film has comes from the fighter herself. ""She grew up knowing one thing - she was trash"" the voiceover tells us and her one wish of becoming something becomes something greather than itself by the end. When his final fighter leaves Frankie, she is all he has left and his pity let's him start, but she makes sure he finishes. Swanks performance is indeed award worthy, like much of the film. The relationships among the 3 leads is given time to really develop and the film almost absolutely stays on them. One of the film's few weaknesses is that too often it's side stories and characters have one dimension, if they are much investigated at all.
 
I adore the film, but it just couldn't get over the final hurdle and really devastate me as it has others, but major credit to Eastwood, Swank, and Freeman for elevating a film treading familiar territroy into something very emotional and memorable. The last act isn't out of nowhere, but it just didn't seem to me where the picture needed to go, maybe it's just not where I wanted it to go. I hope to see it again, I hope and want the film to get me the final few yards. Still, it's quite understandable how it's really touching people, and any awards/accolades it gets are well deserved.","",False,False,False,False,4.0,"2005-02-26","","RT","900618376"
5.0,"[b]Million Dollar Baby[/b]
 
I saw Million Dollar Baby last night with friends (Thanks again Megan and Bob for a great evening!) I did not know much about the plot so I did not know what to expect, except that it was about a women becoming a professional fighter.  The last third of the movie surprised me with an upper, left hook that left me stunned on the direction the story took.  All three performances Clint Eastwood, Morgan Freeman and Hilary Swank were perfect!  This one is worth the eight bucks to see in the theater. 
 
Trivia question, what was the first movie Hilary Swack was in.  I'll give a few hints. She had a minor role. The movie is a campy classic (in my opinion), and a hit TV show spun off of it. For answer click [url=""http://www.rottentomatoes.com/m/buffy_the_vampire_slayer/""]here.[/url]    
 
[b]Love Actually[/b]
 
[i]Love is all around us. -[/i] Love Actually 
 
All you romantics out there have probably see this one already, but for you borderline sentimentalists consider it.  It is just plain fun. As all the characters are related to each other, the storyline intertwines but with one constant theme - love, in all its many forms.   I am closet romantic and I appreciate the movies that remind me that in this cynical and often cruel world love exists, you just need to look for it.","",False,False,False,False,5.0,"2005-02-26","","RT","900760409"
