4.0,"##Proviso: By not revealing a major plot event, reviewing this film is difficult. 

     Frankie Dunn, an ex-fighter runs a boxing gym , and is training an up and coming fighter to be ready for a title shot. This fighter leaves him for Mickey Mack just before his title fight, as Dunn has been avoiding setting up a title fight. The reason for this is trying to protect his fighter from serious injury. Maggie Fitzgerald meets Dunn at a fight and tries to coax him into training her. She turns up at the gym and the two begin a fiery training relationship. 

   This, like many great boxing films, is a boxing film where the main crux of character empathy and dramatic arcs do not relate to boxing. Those of you whove seen it may think to the contrary. The story (to keep us from nodding off) is a highly paced boxing story, but Eastwoods main focus is the relationships between Dunn, Scrap (a typically good performance by Morgan Freeman) and Fitzgerald. Eastwood explores Dunns guilt, and attempted redemption with great skill and some scenes are fantastically realised. The discussions Dunn has with the priest are both funny and interesting, the priest doesnt believe that he mails his estranged daughter every week (despite the fact that he does) we learn that Dunn is clearly a character who finds forgiving himself impossible, probably started by whatever led to his daughter stopping contact. 

     I would recommend that you see this film, but dont tell anyone about the contents.","",False,False,False,False,4.0,"2005-02-26","","RT","900741260"
