5.0,"[font=Franklin Gothic Medium]When [i]Million Dollar Baby[/i] had finished I knew that watched [i]The Aviator[/i]´s worse opponent. And for [i]Scorsese[/i]´s disappointment I was right.[/font]
 

[font=Franklin Gothic Medium]Using a cold and hard narrative, [i]Clint Eastwood[/i] directed ([i]Clint[/i] worked in three different fields of this movie. As director, actor and produced original score) a film that tells the meeting of two lives in the same objective: Obtain success in Boxe.[/font]
 

[font=Franklin Gothic Medium]First we meet Frankie Dunn ([i]Clint[/i] in a good perfomance as actor), an old and experienced boxe´s manager that spent his whole life training fighters and dreaming with the responsability of create a big champion. The new Frankie´s bet to reach that dream is Big Willie Little ([i]Mike Colter[/i]), but he disappoint Frankie when decide change his manager because Willie couldn´t wait more to fight for the title.[/font]
 

[font=Franklin Gothic Medium]After Frankie watched the fighter, that he spent so many time training to reach a dream, be champion with a new coach, Maggie Fitzgerald ([i]Hillary Swank[/i] in a great job) appears wanting be teach by Dunn. He´s with a deep bruise about life´s joke and extremely conservative said there were not any chance to put his time away teaching a woman.[/font]
 

[font=Franklin Gothic Medium]A brilliant and envolving history starts from now on, when [i]Morgan Freeman[/i] stars his great perfomance too, supporting and encouraging Maggie, that has to divide her time between practise and work in a poor restaurant (here we can observe one of the most beautiful scenes of that [i]Eastwood[/i]´s movie, when Maggie keeps a piece of steak to eat later saying that the food was to her dog).[/font]
 

[font=Franklin Gothic Medium]With troubles she persuade Frankie to be her coach until she meets one better. What happen after that is a realistic and magic acquanintance born in the reasons of the past. Frankie Dunn write letter to his daughter and even so doesn´t have any answer since she left, otherwise Fitzgerald miss her father´s presence a lot. Those feelings of lost can be in part complete for this relationship that, it´s important, never shift for sexual advantages.[/font]
 

[font=Franklin Gothic Medium]The time pass, Maggie is one of the best fighters around the world and one more time Dunn is afraid to put she in a decisio for the title because his bad memories. Waiting a long time for be the big champion, Maggie has the invitation to be training by Big Willie´s coach and she recuse.[/font]
 

[font=Franklin Gothic Medium]So, with that, Frankie has courage and accept decide the title. In a dishonest fight Fitzgerald has a fatal accident that finished with her career and she decided that who will make the decision of your life in the Dunn´s hands. The person who did more for she than all her family.[/font]
 

[font=Franklin Gothic Medium]If you leave the cinema with a pain in your heart, be sure you´re not the unique feeling this. With a perfect emotional approach, [i]Clint Eastwood[/i] made a movie that can transmit the boxe´s teaching for our lives in the same time that we feel be sharing and rooting for the Fitzgerald and Frankie´s dream.[/font]","",False,False,False,False,5.0,"2005-03-04","","RT","900765717"
1.5,"[b][font=Verdana]Film: [/font][/b][font=Verdana]Million Dollar Baby
  [b]Director: [/b]Clint Eastwood
  [b]Year: [/b]2004
  [b]Rating: [/b]PG-13 for violence, some disturbing images, thematic material and language
  
  Review Tagline: [i]Here's my two cents on Million Dollar Baby!
  [/i]Quality Rating: [i]**/5[/i][/font]
  
 
  [font=Verdana][b]-Introduction
  
  [/b]I've found that over the years, my respect for the Academy's choices, especially for Best Picture has declined. It seems as though any movie by a respectable director and a fairly big budget can take this coveted award. Gone are the days where a fairly conservative film could do well in the academy and instead, controversy seems to be the main component of their choices. The case is true for Million Dollar Baby.
  
 Now, this is not to say that Million Dollar Baby was complete and utter trash. It was redemptive... but only though it's acting. Million Dollar Baby is truly nothing more than a poorly (and falsely) advertised commentary of euthanasia. Ironically enough, Mr. Eastwood is a fairly by-the-book Republican and yet directs a movie like this.
  
 This is a warning. Spoilers will be given because in this case, I fell to fairly assess the film the ending and events leading up to this must be discussed. This is your final warning and with this, I shall begin the meat of my review.
  
  
  [b]-Sample Stuff 1: [/b]Million Dollar 'Bomb' was poorly advertised. Its story didn't add up either.
  
 While many will debate me on this, I have to state the fact that Million Dollar Baby deals with issues that were skirted in the previews. All you seem to see in the previews are scenes that depict a gruff Clint Eastwood helping a female Rocky-like charcater to the top. Sure, the movie has this component in it. But what the previews fail to tell you is that the whole second half of the film, you'll be watching a character who has seemingly defeated uncountable odds give up. The movie is so depressing, instead of the victory that Sylvester Stallone brought us, you'll feel sorrowful, invaded, and in all other senses, cheated. 
  
 Eastwood's 'Baby' is all about Frankie Dunn (Eastwood), a washed up boxing coach and owner of a gym. He's down on his luck and manages the gym with a staff of one, Eddie Dupris (Morgan Freeman) who was a boxer coached by Dunn in his day but lost his eye due to a hard fight. After being stiffed by a boxer with amazing potential, Dunn meets Maggie Fitzgerald (Hilary Swank). 
  
 After some convincing, he decides to train her and help her become a great boxer. A first match proves that Maggie has extraordinary potential. She keeps going higher and higher on the charts until she decides to challenge the world champion for a chance at the title. She appears to be winning, when the fierce champion hits her so hard, Maggie falls over and snaps her neck on a chair, paralyzing her.
  
 The rest of the movie goes downhill as we watch an already depressed Eastwood shrink into lower levels of desperation after his friend and trainee is told she will never be able to fight again. After a request by Maggie, Dunn assists her in suicide.[/font]
 
     [font=Verdana]
 Whoa! Is that what you thought would happen? I sure didn't. Now, no matter what your political or moral beliefs on euthanasia are, we can all concur that the direction the film took, after raising us to victory, was totally inappropriate. 
  
 What makes even less sense is that Maggie (Swank) would take the pain of hits and punches, and then just give up living because of paralysis. I'll admit, her condition is a hard one but a person with that much will power to just give up is ludicrous!
  
 So be sure you know, 'Baby' is not at all what it advertises. Depression is ridden all through it. If you're still interested however, read on.
  
  
  [b]-Sample Stuff 2: [/b]The acting was the only aspect that deserved any kind of award.
  
 I am not so narrow-minded as to not admit a movie has good acting. 'Baby' is full of stars that have proven to be great at what they do. Swank plays the role of Maggie well. Despite the obvious character flaws of Maggie, Swank delivers a performance that gives the movie at least some gold.
  
 Eastwood, as always, does his thing as a rough and solitary person. It's nothing we haven't seen before but that's not to say he doesn't do well. 
  
 Freeman however, stands out from both of them. Up until this point, Mr. Freeman had never won an Academy Award despite how well he's done and so I felt he truly deserved this. He provides some comic relief as Eddie and has some very deep, thought-provoking quotes.
  
 Unfortunately, he was written into the script as though he was narrating the whole thing. Monologues never seem to impress me (truly the only flaw with the wonderful Platoon) and in this case, they only continue to make you question the film. 
  
 However, all of the actors deserved the nominations they got. I suppose on an acting level, this film succeeds but to expect less of such a cast would be crazy.
  
  
  [b]-Sample Stuff 3: [/b]Million Dollar Baby pushes an R-Rating.
  
 Million Dollar Baby is one of those dramas that will make you question the MPAA (rating system). Not only is the movie ridden with obscene language but the violence is over-the-top.
  
 I understand this is a boxing film but Rocky, a prime example (one that also won best Picture) showed that a boxing movie can be tastefully done without excess language, gore, or depression.
  
 For instance, Maggie, who really only has control of her neck up, bites her tongue in an effort to kill herself since at this point, Dunn is unable to comply. The scene is gory and makes you feel angry at this girl. Her death is so selfish in that she's not willing to fight.
  
 People like Lance Armstrong, who have been diagnosed with cancer in almost every area of their body, come back. In this case, Maggie probably wouldn't be able to but what kind of message is it when a hero is shown as a low-road taker? Personally, I'd have rather seen Maggie fight for life and not be so quick as to just give up. Unfortunately, that is the message that this film is portraying.
  
  ""If you're hurt enough, giving up is okay and selling yourself short is fine!""
  
 It is for this reason that I suggest anyone who can't see the untruth of this message stay away from this film. Also, because of the violence, gore, and overall message, anyone not mature enough to handle and discuss these ideas should avoid this film.
  
  
  [b]-Conclusion
  
  [/b]Eastwood's Million Dollar Baby is anything but the masterpiece that Hollywood is trying to sell people. Instead of an uplifting, victory-ridden film, it condones practices such as euthanasia and teaches that giving up to death is okay (note: Maggie was not in any terminal state).
  
 While the acting is superior too much of what is produced today, a depressing script and overall unenjoyable picture is shot down due to story issues.
  
  And what is a ""Million Dollar Baby""? Certainly not this film.[/font]","",False,False,False,False,1.5,"2005-03-06","","RT","900769611"
