5.0,"[center][img]http://imagecache2.allposters.com/images/153/1112880.jpg[/img][/center]
[center] [/center]

[left][i]""If there's magic in boxing it's the magic of fighting battles beyond endurance, beyond cracked ribs, ruptured kidneys and detached retinas. It's the magic of risking everything for a dream that nobody sees but you.""[/i][/left]
[left] [/left]

[left]It was said during the Golden Globes that[i] Million Dollar Baby[/i] is a movie about our hopes and dreams. As cliche as that sounds, it's very true. The characters in the film go to boxing to get away from their mediocre lives, whether it be unfulfilled or when eating 3 times a day becomes a challenge. Maggie Fitzgerald is a fighter, before and after she became a professional boxer. Her unsupportive family and dead-end job waiting tables all day long somewhat keeps her from achieving her goal, but she never gave up. Frankie, after losing his player, decides to train Maggie, and off they go to easily win fights until Maggie's very last one. Cliches are thankfully avoided in the film, where things can easily feel forced and trite. It's such a tragic story full of victories, and what an emotionally-draining film. [/left]
[left] [/left]

[left]The movie wouldn't be anything that remarkable without the performances. What a fine group of actors to work in one project, offering their best in the film. Eastwood, with yet again the ambitious task of directing, producing and starring in the film, offers another fine performance as Frankie. And Morgan Freeman, one of the coolest actors working today, gives one of my favorite performances of 2004. But Hilary Swank is obviously the star of the film, giving her best performance so far and probably her first great movie role ever since [i]Boys Don't Cry[/i]. The way she got into character was very convincing, gaining nearly 20 pounds of muscle for the role of Maggie Fitzgerald. Perhaps the film would feel empty without these fine bunch of actors theat easily make the entire film. [/left]
[left] [/left]

[left]The cinematography is very, very good. I loved how the gym was lighted at night, and how every place always seems so depressing, which is absolutely perfect for the film. And no Hollywood cliches can be found here, like people hugging in slow-mo or dramatic music playing in every scene that handles emotion. Eastwood's very simple score, which isn't used very often in the film, is simply right for every dramatic moment. They never felt out of place or forced, which could've destroyed the film. [/left]
[left] [/left]

[left]I have absolutely no interest in boxing. It often strikes me as boring and as senseless as wrestling. But of course, the film makes everything about boxing fascinating and seem harder than what we would expect. But it isn't all about boxing. It's mainly about these 3 characters trying to achieve their dreams and helping each other out. All of them are playing the same game. Some fail and some gain hope. It's a simple story surrounded by background noise that helps develop these great characters, and it managed to keep me interested all throughout the entire film. It's a very effective film that certainly wouldn't make you feel happy in the end, or, during the entire film for that matter. No scenes ever felt forced or too long, and Eastwood obviously knows how to handle emotional scenes (which I didn't see in [i]Mystic River[/i]), which makes the film utterly honest and all the more tragic in the end. [/left]
[left] [/left]

