5.0,"I think I should stop watching so many great films in a row....... don't you?
 
On a sidenote, I finished reading [i]Lolita[/i] (so lyrical and ironic!) and [i]The Great Gatsby[/i] (so restrained and subtle!) in the past week, and loved the both; two days ago I started reading Capote's [i]In Cold Blood[/i].
 
And I'm currently listening to stuff by The Who (I dig 'em!) Right now, however, I'm listening to Bob Dylan's inimitable [i]Tangled Up In Blue.[/i]
 
[u]Movies:[/u]
 
[i]His Girl Friday[/i]
 
[img]http://www.filmnight.org/images/hisgirlphoto.jpg[/img]
 
A highly enjoyable and witty screwball comedy starring the inimitable Cary Grant, characterized by its frenzied pace and the timing of the visual/aural gags. Predictable at times, and I can't say I laughed that much, but it's a very noteworthy film, narrated with brio and skill by none other than the great Howard Hawks. The chemistry between the two leads is electrifying, and the[i] double-entendres[/i] give way to some very fine comic moments. Highly reccomended, especially for fans of the subgenre.
 
[i]Million Dollar Baby[/i]
 
[img]http://us.movies1.yimg.com/movies.yahoo.com/images/hv/photo/movie_pix/warner_brothers/million_dollar_baby/_group_photos/clint_eastwood6.jpg[/img]
 
Simply sublime. Eastwood has achieved an honest and mature film, containing some topics that are very much worth talking about and, in addition, a high level of cinematic artistry. Eastwood takes his time in developing his characters and themes, he feels no need for rushing; the key words here are ""restraint"" and ""sincerity"". The performances of the main trio are all excellent and the film accomplishes what it sets out to do; to punch the viewer in the gut, almost literally, when he least expects it, thus making the overall experience even more emotionally immersing. [i]M$B[/i] deserves every award it receives, and given that nowadays a film full of so much humanity seems to be a rarity, Eastwood's latest (some call it his finest, but I'm afraid this is his only directorial effort that I've seen) is praise-worthy for that aspect alone. 
 
[i]The Apartment[/i]
 
[img]http://cnnstudentnews.cnn.com/interactive/entertainment/0203/wilder.films/apartment.jpg[/img]
 
The perfect embodiment of the tragicomedy, and the second Billy Wilder I've seen thus far. It's impossible not to feel sorry for Jack Lemmon in this film, and the overall melancholy tone that the director achieves is great. In addition, its closing line is unforgettable: ""Shut up and deal!"" One of the greatest American films of the '60s. 
 
 
[i]Dogville[/i]
 
[img]http://us.movies1.yimg.com/movies.yahoo.com/images/hv/photo/movie_pix/lions_gate_films/dogville/nicole_kidman/dogville2.jpg[/img]
 
I'm tempted to call this one of the best films of the last ten years. I can see why some would hate it, but I fell in love with it from the very start. [i]Dogville[/i] is a testament to von Trier's storytelling and visual abilities, and what's more, it contains what is probably Nicole Kidman's finest performance yet. Von Trier lays everything bare by taking a wholly minimalistic approach, and begins the film with the utmost restraint only to completely convey his message in an excessive -- but ever-effective -- fashion at the end. [i]Dogville[/i] contains some moments of real beauty (the opening of the curtains, the overhead shots of the small town, the see-through scene of the picture above), but at its core it is a tragedy about the human condition which speaks in many levels; and whilst the film may seem to be completely against America, it's obvious that it's talking about the whole world. Though-provoking, daring and brilliant; [i]Dogville [/i]is controversial and evidently divides viewers' opinions, but I was constantly astonished by it all.
 
[i]Annie Hall[/i]
 
[img]http://www.culturevulture.net/Movies/anniehall3b.jpg[/img]
 
Woody Allen's film is a self-aware, quirky, ironic and sincere look at the complex, absurd world of love relationships. A masterpiece? I'm inclined to say ""yes""; it's a very thorough, multi-layered and complete film, filled to the brim with memorable and funny scenes, and with an inventiveness that it seems to have in spades (you just know this when Woody Allen looks and talks directly to the camera and includes split-screens, voiceovers, subtitles that voice the characters' feelings, etc). Allen's ever-criticizing eye gives way to an audacious and penetrating film in many facets, proving to be a film as memorable as its title character. It's on the same level of [i]Manhattan[/i], which just about speaks for itself, and its combination of comedy and drama is a triumph.
 
......................................
 
