4.5,"[i]Million Dollar Baby (2004)[/i]
Clint Eastwood, Hilary Swank, Morgan Freeman
Directed by Clint Eastwood
 
What can be said about boxing? It's certainly only for the toughest, the strongest, and the bravest. Images come to mind of strong fighters, blood and spit flying through the air, bruises and black eyes commonplace.
 
So, one would assume that a drama about boxing couldn't possibly be a sensitive movie about life and love? Well, one would be wrong. Million Dollar Baby is that movie that treads the controversial ground of a love that isn't romantic. A father/daughter love shared by two people not related at all.
 
M$B's script, based on the ""Rope Burns"" stories, is one of the most moving, emotional, and to be quite honest, ""perfect"" scripts in years. Everything in the movie has a double meaning, and by the end, everything seemingly worthless or out of place falls right in. A daring film, by any standard, M$B leaves the viewer feeling shattered and broken, but also with a sense that he or she was able to view a truly powerful human experience.
 
Much controversy, and therefore publicity, has surrounded the end of the film. Of course, I won't go into it here, but in reality that ""ending"" really happens only half way through the film (maybe a little more), dividing it into two entirely different halves. Many people who haven't seen it have overheard or been told the ending. To those people, I encourage you see this film anyway.
 
Which, brings me to my next point. The direction of this movie is fantastic. Clint portrays this hard tale with such realism and grace, it's hard to believe it never happened. He put so much detail and ""humanity"" into all the characters and situations, that it really felt ""real."" Artistically, this film is nothing short of dazzling. It really casts a whole new light on boxing. He really captured sports on film unlike anyone else has been able to for a long time.
 
The actors, also, are outstanding. Hilary Swank provides us with the greatest performance by an actress in, quite possibly, the last ten years. That may seem overdramatic, but the things that Hilary had to portray seem almost impossible, and her portrayal of them is perfect. Clint Eastwood isn't so hot for some of the film, but he does have his redeeming moments. Morgan Freeman does is job well, providing that sturdy backdrop for the other characters to revolve around. That's not to say he doesn't have his own issues, however. His situations are a nice break, emotionally and humorously, from the main plot.
 
Million Dollar Baby is a powerful, moving film. It will emotionally affect you unlike any movie you have seen in a long time. In the end, however, you will be glad you witnessed this nearly perfect masterpiece of the human spirit.
 
9 out of 10","",False,False,False,False,4.5,"2005-03-16","","RT","900772620"
4.5,"Wow. I don't know what to say about this movie that has not already been said before. This movie is about an old boxing manager that is completely estranged from his daughter, goes to mass everyday and has done something in his past that he cannot forgive himself for. It is about a 31 year-old waitress, who although is considered too old and ""girly"", still has the heart and enthusiasm to take up boxing. It is about a former boxer who lost one of his eyes due to a bout in the ring and although past his prime, still runs a gym and can still fend for himself. The movie centers itself around these three characters and a pivotal event that makes it the excellent movie that it is.
 
Great acting: Hilary Swank's best performance since Boy's Don't Cry (heh, I actually wouldn't know, as I've never seen that movie, but this is what I've heard, even from the critics who didn't like this movie), 
Clint Eastwood delivering some emotion (I have seen Clint Eastwood before, but I can't remember him ever crying) and Morgan Freeman in his usual, sage-like role (also probably one of the best performances I have seen him give). Good drama: the plot 'twist' for lack of a better word and the acting basically give this characteristic to the movie and a nice spin on the old underdog sports movie type plot. I loved it","",False,False,False,False,4.5,"2005-03-16","","RT","900707668"
4.0,"** SPOILERS **
 
 
Finally saw what all the fuss was about. I wouldn't say it was the best movie of 2004, but it wasn't bad. Not bad at all. It certainly took an interesting turn of events in the second half. I figured something was going to happen to our determined, dumb-luck hero Maggie Fitzgerald. I just didn't know it would involve such potentially firecracker issues. It's been a few days and I'm still not sure exactly how I feel about the conclusion. I haven't seen 'The Sea Inside', but I wonder if it treats the whole euthanasia theme in a similar way. But I'll save the heavy-handed stuff for a time when I'm more awake. All in all, great performances from the three main players. Hilary Swank broke my heart and Morgan Freeman was lovely as always. I think I need to sit on this one a bit more though, or see it again. 
 
[img]http://home.bawue.de/~jtesch/pics/wolves.jpg[/img]
 
Alright, second film I finally saw after a few years of wanting to, 'The Company of Wolves', a 1984 ""horror"" film based on some Angela Carter stories. I say ""horror"" because, though some scenes were horrifying, this really isn't the right classification for this unique fairy tale allegory. For those unfamiliar with the plot ... it's difficult to pin down. Basically it's an extended study of the 'Little Red Riding Hood' story. I think other than the truly disturbing, gruesome werewolf transformations (think 'American Werewolf in London' was scary? You ain't seen nothing til you see a man tear off his skin, literally becoming the werewolf inside-out), horror fans will be disappointed. If you ask me, this film is really a feminist allegory of the innocence lost and sexual awakening found when girls ""become women"". Red Hood, you get it now? If you're interested, check out it's boards on IMDB, apparently several people see odd similarities between mensturation and werewolves. Anyway, it's utterly bizarre but totally wonderful. Anyone willing to venture into unknown territory shouldn't be disappointed.","",False,False,False,False,4.0,"2005-03-17","","RT","900773149"
