2.0,"I was ready to put a fight against this movie. For some reason, I disliked it even before I watched it. Which is, of course, really unfair, but just because I am a great guy, I went and gave it a chance (Note to MR. Eastwood, Pm me and I´ll send you my personal address, just in case you want to send flowers, or something for Xmas. You´re wellcome)
 
But the, something happened. As the movie progressed, I let my guard down. I was liking it. It wasn´t a total KO, but surely It had me against the ropes...
 
And then, something else happened. Namely, Maggie´s family.
 
It is not just that they are stupid. And evil. And then, more stupid. And more evil. And that they are stupid or evil even if It goes clearly against them - They hate their loving, beautiful and sweet to the point of vomit child so much, they even despise her when she buys them a house or It is about to sign a will that will leave them all her money. Yep.
 
The problem with all of this is, apart from the cheap shots of the script, and the fact that I have seen more complex, subtle characters playing villians in a Powerpuff girls episode, is: It degrades Maggie. She needs to be slapped in the face with her family evilness so many times, she just doesn´t get it to the point of thinking that she is stupid. Stupid ""don´t-you-see-what-everybody-else-sees"" stupid, like a character in a ""Friday 13th"" film who is so oblivious to what´s going on that you don´t care is she dies. Actually, you want Jason to stab her twenty times, just to check out is she now gets it.
 
So I stopped caring about her. And her fate. And therefore, the movie. And its old-fashioned idea of saints and demons -don´t get me started on the evil black boxer and the Rocky-like female opponent. Of course, she wins the fight by playing dirty. And the guy beats up a mentally handicapped guy. Just cause ""He has no heart"". Messagge of the movie: there is bad people and good people, and the good people normally lose cause the baddies play dirty and are careless. That is some moving shit,right?. And It makes ""The champ"" restrained by comparison.
 
It is not classic movie-making. It is cheap, and easy... - and also well directed and acted. At the end, I felt like I has been punched far too many times. Or maybe It´s just that my intelligence had been insulted too much.","",False,False,False,False,2.0,"2005-03-18","","RT","900606265"
3.0,"I was one of the people driven to see ""Million Dollar Baby"" by the number of Academy Awards it won. I'm glad they thought this movie was awesome and deserving of Best Atress and Best Director Oscars, aming others. I thought it was ok. Sure, Hillary Swank was very convincing and all, but should she really get another Oscar for making herself as unfeminine as possible? Don't even get me started on Clint Eastwood's award. My vote was not for him but nobody cares because I am not in the Academy. Whatever.
 
I tried to like this picture because a die-hard James Bond fan told me it sucked. I did not want to be in the same league as someone who thinks that if the cast is not singing or spying the movie sucks. I managed to avoid being grouped with the James Bond fan group because I thought ""Million Dollar Baby"" made an interesting point about euthanasia. They took too long to make it. They took way too long to make it. I wouldn't say it was a well made point. But at least they had a point.
 
Bottomline, if you like really long, really dark movies that win Oscars for stuff they shouldn't this is going to be your favorite film. If you don't want to share your movie taste with the MI2 fan club, you'll appreciate it.","",False,False,False,False,3.0,"2005-03-19","","RT","900723790"
3.5,"[SIZE=3][SIZE=4]In an ideal universe, [I]Million Dollar Baby[/I] would be the bare minimum that an audience would expect from a film.  But we do not live in an ideal universe, and so it was hailed as one of the best movies of 2004.  Without ever once straying into the realm of artistic inventiveness or originality, [I]Million Dollar Baby[/I] meets the modest criteria of telling a reasonably entertaining story with fairly memorable characters who are brought to life by talented actors.  The film has definite flaws, and if it is indeed a profound work of art (as many seem to believe), then its profundity escapes me.  Yet I enjoyed it to some degree, in the way that I enjoy short stories by O. Henry: I knew where the story was going at every moment, but was willing to embark on the predictable journey.  The film tells the story of a boxing trainer named Frankie Dunn (Clint Eastwood), a grizzled veteran who understands how to teach boxing but often loses his young prospects to other trainers because of his unwilligness to let them take big chances.  Frankie runs a reasonably successful gym, where his lifelong friend Eddie (Morgan Freeman) works as a janitor.  Eddie was once a boxer himself, but lost an eye in a fight that Frankie (Eddie's trainer at the time) allowed to go on for too long.  Into their world comes Maggie (Hilary Swank), a plucky waitress who is determined to forge a boxing career and persistently attempts to persuade Frankie to train her.  Old-fashioned and comfortable with his routine, Frankie initially rejects the idea of training a female boxer, but after he recognizes that Maggie has determination (and more importantly, skill), he agrees to take her on.  The first three-quarters of [I]Million Dollar Baby[/I] are quite formulaic and predictable, yet they are nevertheless entertaining simply because Swank's energetic performance provides a humourous counterpoint to Eastwood's trademark world-weariness.  However, even at its most engrossing, [I]Million Dollar Baby[/I] never really offers us anything that seems fresh, surprising, or true-to-life.  A monologue given by Eddie explaining how he lost his eye is well-delivered by Morgan Freeman, but the scene might as well come with a banner above it reading ""Character Development"".  There are other scenes like that in the film, and so the overall effect is that we are made consciously aware of the machinations of the drama unfolding before us.  When Maggie tells Frankie the story of a dog she had as a little girl, it is foreshadowing of the most obvious kind, thus the emotional impact of the story is lost.  The obviousness of the film's script is forgiveable though, simply because the actors succeed at making us care about what happens to them.  The film eventually takes a turn into much darker territory, but the closing sequences in [I]Million Dollar Baby[/I] do not explore the mysteries of life and death (as some have claimed)...rather, they are the stuff of old-fashioned melodrama.  The melodrama is quite successful, and I know there are many people who have wept at the end, but I found myself somewhat emotionally removed from the movie (perhaps because I was so aware of its melodramatic roots) and so I was not devastated by its conclusion.  The film's conclusion hinges on an issue that remains controversial (and by now, I suspect that most people already know what that issue is...but I won't get into specifics).  However, even though it became a center of controversy, [I]Million Dollar Baby[/I] is really not a daring film...even the closing scenes are always playing it safe.  There is one sequence that is very powerful, in which Frankie consults his Catholic priest and asks for advice on a difficult decision he must make.  This scene is one of the finest that Eastwood has ever done as an actor: we sense the character's conflict between his feelings of love and his feelings of obligation to do what he believes is right.  Yet if [I]Million Dollar Baby[/I] is a triumph for Eastwood the actor, I am not entirely convinced that it is one for Eastwood the director.  There are some who believe that Eastwood is one of the most intelligent and profound directors working today, but I am not sold on that notion.  [I]Unforgiven[/I] is a great Western, but [I]Mystic River[/I] struck me as occasionally hokey, over-acted and ill-conceived.  Likewise, [I]Million Dollar Baby[/I] contains nagging flaws (both in conception and execution) that undermine the film's power.  The movie is narrated by Morgan Freeman's character, and that storytelling decision produces mixed results, since the narration often comes across as pretentious and imbued with faux wisdom.  But that flaw is forgiveable compared to the film's major shortcoming, which is the way many of the secondary characters are treated.  Eastwood is a filmmaker who seems to enjoy using archetypes...but that strategy can backfire immensely when the archetypes turn into stereotypes.  Such is the case with some of the supporting players in [I]Million Dollar Baby[/I].  One character, a mentally retarded would-be boxer named Danger (Jay Baruchel), is completely unnecessary and occasionally becomes downright annoying.  A rival boxer who is notorious for delivering cheap shots is shot like a villain in a silent film (and without being a p.c. bore, I'll just quickly point out that the character is black, something that Eastwood takes a little too much relish in emphasizing).  Most offensive is the portrayal of Maggie's family, who are presented as a cliche flock of trailer trash.  Not only is Maggie's mother a walking cliche, she is also unreasonably evil toward Maggie (as is the rest of the family).  How can such egregious stereotypes be explained?  Is it possible that Eastwood, who is notoriously conservative, is simply expressing his disdain for what he perceives as the welfare-dependent, Disneyland-attending lower-classes?  No, I don't believe so.  I think the explanation is merely that Eastwood is a fairly uninventive filmmaker who must rely on stereotypes to manipulate the audience.  There is nothing wrong with stereotypes in the service of melodrama, but a film should at least have the sense of humour to acknowledge them.  There is a scene in the film where another cliche secondary character, a jive-talking hothead boxer, begins bullying the retarded boxer Danger, until Eddie steps in and knocks the hothead out.  At the theater where I saw the film, a member of the audience applauded this scene, and I couldn't help but feel depressed that someone could be so gullible as to be taken in by such pandering.  It is moments like this that prevent Eastwood from being a complete success as a director.  He receives praise for his ""professionalism"" and ""low-key style"", for keeping his presence as a director to a minimum, but in fact Eastwood's hands are on the puppet strings at all times...he just doesn't want to acknowledge that.  Make no mistake, [I]Million Dollar Baby[/I] is a solid, entertaining film, and one driven by the consummate skill of its experienced cast and director.  The performances by Swank, Freeman and especially Eastwood are successful in bringing the three main characters to life.  And by the end of the movie, we have been placed in a situation where we are left to wonder what we would do given the choices faced by a certain character.  It is not an easy feat to accomplish these things, and Eastwood does deserve some praise for the film.  If I am critical of [I]Million Dollar Baby[/I] (and I am), it is not because I feel the movie is dreadful or incompetent.  It is only because I am wary of accepting something that never once seems spontaneous or fresh as a masterpiece.  We can do better.[/SIZE][/SIZE]","",False,False,False,False,3.5,"2005-03-20","","RT","900665601"
