5.0,"Starring: Clint Eastwood, Hilary Swank, and Morgan Freeman.
   Directed by Clint Eastwood.
   Written by Paul Haggis.
   Rated [b]PG-13[/b] (for violence, some disturbing images, thematic material and language).
   Running time approximately 2 hours 12 minutes.
   
   Two in a row is a rarity, but this man has achieved that feat. After the almost unbelievable success of [i]Mystic River[/i] in 2003, [i]Million Dollar Baby[/i] astonishes a year later, yet another masterpiece from director Clint Eastwood. Not only is it perfectly flawless on every single level of its production, but the performances are so damned good that you feel as if you're watching a documentary. I think it's finally time for me to compile my Top 10 of 2004, and I'm prepared to suffer the consequences of many of my choices, but for this one, I am very proud.[b] **** (out of ****) A+[/b]","",False,False,False,False,5.0,"2005-03-21","","RT","900731877"
5.0,"Set in the wider context of boxing, [b]Million Dollar Baby[/b], is a beautifully crafted, emotional and thought provoking film that, in my opinion, everyone should see. I find it detrimental to the film to pigeon-hole it as a ""boxing"" movie while, yes, it has boxing in it, boxing really serves as the platform for the development of the father-daughter love story between Eastwood and Swank and the emotional development of the main characters.

Eastwood stars as Frankie Dunn, the owner of a run-down gym in downtown LA. A mildly successful trainer, Frankie lets future glory slip through his fingers when he loses one of his fighters to a rival sponsor. Wary of this happening again, and over-protective of his boxers because of a previous accident involving Freeman's character, he at first refuses to train Maggie Fitzgerald (Swank) a tenacious new boxer who walks into the gym one day wanting to make it as a professional boxer. However, seeing her commitment and drive Frankie overlooks issues with her gender and age and agrees to train Maggie.

The performances delivered by Eastwood, Swank and Freeman are brilliant and really merited the awarding of their Oscars. Whilst at times in Eastwood's career he has fallen into the ""john wayne syndrome"" he delivers a powerfully understated performance in this film. The chemistry between Eastwood and Freeman makes their scenes together a delight to watch and Hillary Swank delivers, in my opinion, the performance of her career. She is at the moment probably the most talented actress in Hollywood and I look forward to see what she does next.

The powerful and steady directing of Eastwood beautifully aids the denouement of the plot and the cinematography really captures the type of lives these characters live. The low-level lighting and blue-grey colours makes for an edgy, gritty film that whilst visually harsh, is a touching and emotional story.

Other thoughts: Hilary Swank is just brilliant in this film. She's such a beautiful and talented actress. I'm really excited to see her next project The Black Dahlia.","",False,False,False,False,5.0,"2005-03-21","","RT","900773422"
4.5,"[color=White][img]http://imagecache3.allposters.com/images/153/1112880.jpg[/img]
  
      I almost wept for this film. Lucky I have had many many practises in the art of Tear-holding-in
  
 Great Directing: Not Marty of course, but that was some nice use of shadow there. Never seen that many shadows before in a film (provided I don't see many films). Very nice touches, with slight use of humour (appropriate), easy on the violence (even more appropriate) and a little heavy on the emotional part (not so appropriate). But it was a good job.
  
 Excellent Acting: Does Morgan Freeman KICKASS! Ok... ...I just like him. Hilary really stole the show. She was so good and so deserving (although I was more of a Kate Winslet Inclination than a Hilary one) of that Oscar. She's definitely going to be around for a long time! Clint is good, but not good enough for the strong category this year. Morgan Freeman KICKSASS...wait a minute, Ok, already mentioned...
  
 Great Score: Simple, single theme, but so beautiful! When the guitar starts plucking and Morgan starts writing, your heart does break.
  
      Excellent Plot: I know some people were a little unnerved by the [/color]          [color=Navy][color=White][color=Navy]euthanasia[/color] [/color][color=Black][color=White]in the film, but I thought it was a really touching ending. A beautiful, tear-inducing movie. Big Weepie...something I'd never think of as I assumed it to be some hard boxing film. Guess I was wrong.
  
