4.5,"Sigamos con los Oscar, ahora con la gran triunfadora. Clint Eastwood, con más años que el café migao, gana los premios al mejor director y a la mejor película y les pone en bandeja los suyos a Hilary Swank y Morgan Freeman. Todo esto después de incontables problemas para financiar la película. Tampoco era de extrañar: ¿quién quiere arriesgar la pasta para que un director septuagenario haga lo que parece en principio un [i]Rocky[/i] femenino? ¿Nadie? Lo suponía. Pero vayamos a la plácida oscuridad de la sala, dejando aparte la insondable, alquitranada negrura del mundo de los estudios.
 
LADO BUENO: ¡Toma puñetazo rápido! ¡Hostia fina! Señores, en pie. Esta es la obra de un maestro, de una leyenda, de un sabio. Esta es una película hecha por un tío con dos pares, protagonizada por dos tíos con dos pares cada uno y una tía con varios pares ella sola, y dirigida directamente a los pares de los espectadores. Eso sí, con sutileza. Con relajo. Como quien no quiere la cosa, el tito Clint nos deja noqueados con una película que parece peso pluma pero se va espesando y ganando músculo hasta tener los brazos como las piernas de otro. Una obra maestra, comparable a [i]Sin Perdón[/i]. Y punto.
 
LADO MALO: Poniéndonos tiquismiquis, el chaval Jay Baruchel (el aspirante a boxeador, canijo y medio lelo pero entrañable) no me acaba de convencer.
 
EN TRES (3) PALABRAS: K.O. sin peros.","",False,False,False,False,4.5,"2005-04-04","","RT","900775598"
5.0,"I didnt cry during [u]Million Dollar Baby[/u].

My girlfriend, and every other woman in the audience, bawled. But with the exception of Morgan Freemans character's speech to Frankie, to help him make the difficult decision in which he points out how much Frankie has done for Magge, I didnt even tear up. Why?

Because [u]Million Dollar Baby[/u] is a triumph.

Its lead character, played by Hilary Swank, wants to be a boxer. For that she enlists the proverbial down and out trainer, Frankie, played by Clint Eastwood, at Morgan Freemans characters gym. Frankie is a great trainer  his last fighter, before Magge, Swanks character, went on to  become world champion. But he could have done that while Frankies protégé  Frankie is just terrified of watching his fighters lose.

Frankie also has no relationship with his biological daughter. So when Magge begs to be trained, we can almost understand his resistance. Hes not good with girls, and hes obviously not good at keeping fighters either. But eventually, Magges spirit and Freemans characters urging wins him over, and he makes Magge into maybe the best female boxer ever.

And what happens next is impossible to describe.

The absolute best thing, to me, about [u]Million Dollar Baby[/u], is how simple it is. The story is straight-forward. There are no gimmicks, no tricks, and while many may tell you theres a big surprise at the end, you can almost guess what it is, as it flows naturally from everything that comes before it. Its directed like a small film  very clean, very straight-forward, and very realistic. And as a result, its beautifully executed.

This is high drama played out on a microscopic scale. Its not big, like [u]the Aviator[/u]. It doesnt have tons of special effects, and though it has three huge stars, its a pretty small film. But its so well acted, so well written, and so well directed, that it pays off, big time. When you see a movie like this youre reminded that its really not hard to make a good film, or rather, theres not much to it. Good acting, good story, and directing that matches the material. [u]Million Dollar Baby[/u] shows that it doesnt take money to achieve greatness, but talent. Clint Eastwood and crew have this in spades.

And it shows.","",False,False,False,False,5.0,"2005-04-05","","RT","900752177"
4.5,"For once, the critics got it right. This is a great movie, and should be considered in the same league as the great sports movies. I really dont like Hillary Swank, I really think Clint Eastwood should have gracefully retired after Mystic River, and I REALLY dont care about women's boxing. Somehow, all of these combined to create a great movie, deserved best picture, Swank and Freeman were both Oscar worthy. Unfortunately, I still think Eastwood did the second best directing job this year, right after Martin for the Aviator.","",False,False,False,False,4.5,"2005-04-07","","RT","900775734"
3.5,"Overall, the movie is great.","",False,False,False,False,3.5,"2005-04-09","","RT","900777373"
2.5,"The Academy Awards have bugged me for years. I lost faith in them way back in 1998, when [i]Saving Private Ryan[/i] lost the Best Picture award to [i]Shakespeare in Love[/i] (seven years later and which one do numerous film scholars say has had the most impact on the modern war film? [i]Shakespeare in Love[/i] of course). Yet, for some reason, I keep watching them every year; and every year, I end up yelling at the TV like a sports fan who's team is getting stomped bad. This year was no exception, and a lot of that was due to [i]Million Dollar Baby[/i].
    
 Let's start with the basics of what I didn't like about this film: for starters, it's another boxing movie. Yeah, there's an original concept. And just to keep up with the predictability, let's throw in an elderly, sexist coach who misses his own former glory and doesn't think this new boxer has what it takes. Of course the coach doesn't want to train this new boxer because (*gasp*) it's a girl! But the girl could never possibly get any better with another, less biased coach, so she keeps pestering him, until he finally gives in and starts training her. From there, she quickly rises to the top of the female boxing circuit, proving that she had what it takes all along. 
    
 And that's all the plot points I guessed before I even saw the movie, which turned out to be entirely true. I garnered all this just from knowing it was about a male coach training a female boxer. Add a quick ten-minute scene in the beginning and a cameo by Sylvester Stallone, and this film could've been titled [i]Rocky VI: The Sex Change[/i]. This movie didn't even need to be about boxing and I could've seen most of the story coming. If the sport of choice were break-dancing, I could've said the exact same thing. I will give it credit though for the surprise ending, which came out of nowhere and was probably the only part of the story that wasn't a tired movie cliché, which this film is ripe with. A person struggling to enter a part of society dominated by the opposite gender? Check. A harsh mentor who serves as a father-like figure? Check. Hours of voice-over to tell the audience details it doesnt even need to know? Check.
    
 Then there's the acting: Hilary Swank did not deserve that Oscar either. I'm not saying she's a bad actress, but I know she can do better than this. She was only really good near the end. Other than that, she had a grand total of three facial expressions: determined, naive, and blank stare. My pick for Best Actress would've been Catalina Sandino Moreno in [i]Maria Full of Grace[/i]. She can convey in one daunting look what Hilary Swank needs a voice-over to tell. I'm not going to knock Clint Eastwood's acting (which was actually pretty good), because, well, he's freakin' CLint Eastwood, and he's proven himself enough in his life that if all he did now was a sitcom on the WB, he'd still be remembered as a badass. 
    
         I expected [i]Million Dollar Baby[/i] to be popular at the Oscars, but I never expected such a tepid piece of unoriginality to [i]sweep[/i] the damn thing. It got seven nominations, yet [i]Eternal Sunshine of the Spotless Mind[/i], a film dripping with originality and a surprisingly well-executed presentation, received a paltry two. [i]Million Dollar Baby[/i] exemplifies why, in my opinion, the Oscars a film receives are not a true measure of its worth. They are doled out more on the basis of popularity than actual innovation in film.
   
  ---------------------------------------------------------------------
  
   UPDATE: Want to see a [i]good[/i] boxing movie? Check out [i]Cinderella Man[/i]. Awesome fight scenes, great character development (so I actually cared what happens to these people), and much [i]much [/i]better acting.
 
 UPDATE AGAIN: Or check out [i]Girlfight[/i]. Much more believable.","",False,False,False,False,2.5,"2005-04-10","","RT","900777393"
