4.0,"[font=Comic Sans MS][color=navy]A poet I'm not, but ""poetic"" describes the feel I got from both movies. A poetic sense of justice and despair, yet of justice and triumph as well. I am not a huge boxing or comic book fan, so I'm sure I don't appreciate these movies as much as they deserve to be. While SIN CITY was violent to the core, it helped that the dismembered body parts were not in full color; the arm sockets weren't graphically red or sinewy, they were in black and white. I enjoyed the stories involved and how they were acted out, especially by Mickey Rourke. Good to see him again. MILLION DOLLAR BABY I appreciated more because of the stellar acting by Eastwood, Swank, and especially Morgan Freeman. Swank was a female Sugar Ray Leonard, and watching her kick ass was...well, enjoyably impressive and surprising, but not as surprising as the end of the movie. She totally impressed me, as always. I recommend both movies.[/color][/font]","",False,False,False,False,4.0,"2005-04-12","","RT","900730016"
4.5,"Clint Eastwood has outdone himself in this movie. I thought Mystic River was bloody good but this one takes the cake. The dark colourings , snappy fight sequences and then the sudden turnaround halfway to slow , long scenes bringing the pace of the movie almost to a halt epitomises the tragedy of the story so well.","",False,False,False,False,4.5,"2005-04-13","","RT","900777947"
2.5,"The movie wasn't something I would have been interested in seeing other than the fact that it won an Oscar -- that being said, I saw it with high expectations and was SORELY disappointed. I thought the film had excellent intentions but its heroistic premise has managed to fool a lot of people.
 
EASTWOOD manages to continue to make fun of himself in his dramatic choices -- it would be exciting to see Clint not play a Clint impersonator playing Clint. Act, Clint, act.
 
FREEMAN and SWANK are incredibly gifted actors, but their wonderful performances couldn't make up for what was a truly shoddy script by Oscar-winner standards. The ""twist"" ending was simply contrived and felt bizarre; SWANK's familial relations were UNBELIEVABLE, and if we are to believe that SWANK's family IS what it is presented to be, it demeans SWANK's character's integrity and intelligence. You begin to think that the boxing champ is every bit as stupid and misguided as her mother says she is.
 
And, as always, EASTWOOD's less than fabulous score (pardon, I'm a composer-lyricist) is offensive ... to receive a ""Music by"" credit when all he did was plunk out a ditty on his pianah and then have a skilled musician, arrange, orchestrate and completely produce it ... well, that's just sad.
 
Million Dollar Baby: C-.","",False,False,False,False,2.5,"2005-04-15","","RT","900766280"
4.5,"Though it's been a couple of weeks since I've seen ""Million Dollar Baby,"" I thought I'd give it a little review in light of its Oscar success. ""Baby"" is filmmaking at its best. It's sparse. It's poetic. It's devastating. It's brilliant. 

We may have come to love Clint Eastwood (""Blood Work"") from his hardnosed characters such as The Man With No Name and Dirty Harry. But through the years he's proven that he is more than a grizzled face; he's a darn fine director. ""Unforgiven"" and ""Mystic River"" pop into my mind as examples of this statement (he first won a Best Director Academy Award in 1992 for ""Unforgiven."") And ""Million Dollar Baby"" sealed the deal on his icon status with his second Oscar win for directing. 

An actor's director, Eastwood leads his characters through a shadowy landscape of loneliness and brings them to some of the greatest performances of their careers. We see each of their empty lives and then we see them come together in the love of boxing. 

Eastwood stars as boxing trainer Frankie Dunn, a former cut-man, whose fighter leaves him for a flashier manager. While he's dealing with this blow, a green but eager girl shows up at his gym, begging for him to train her. Hilary Swank (""Iron Jawed Angels"") gives her second Oscar-winning performance as Maggie, a girl from the trailer park who's really too old to start boxing, but refuses to give up on her dream. 

Morgan Freeman, who won the Best Supporting Actor award, (""The Big Bounce"") brings the story into focus through voice over and the keen observation of a man blind in one eye, Eddie. A former boxer, he provides maintenance and janitorial service for the gym, as well as living in a small room off to the side. While Frankie is rebuffing Maggie, Eddie watches her and begins to take her under his wing. 

Eventually, Frankie breaks his mantra of ""I don't train girls,"" and begins to work with Maggie. She proves to be a natural and triumphs during her first few fights. 

And here's where the Disney story ends. I can't say more for fear of spoiling the surprise. But the movie devastated me. I cried. A lot. Images of the film still haunt me. That's what an Acedemy Award winning Best Picture movie should do. Move you. Make you think about it long after it's over. Eastwood has proven to be a master of this. 

See it. See it now.","",False,False,False,False,4.5,"2005-04-15","","RT","900777916"
5.0,"[center][/center]

[color=#000000][size=3][font=Times New Roman]When a young boxer is against the ropes and their opponent is ready for the knockout punch, there is little else the trainer can do for their cherished fighter. The drama that unfolds in the boxing ring is a gripping spectacle. Even more astounding is the amount of physical and mental pain a boxer in training must endure to reach the pinnacle of boxing success. The defeat, glory, and agony of the sport are flawlessly portrayed in the best American made movie of 2004, [i]Million Dollar Baby[/i]. [/font][/size][/color]

 
[font=Times New Roman][size=3][color=#000000]Celebrated actor/director Clint Eastwood brings to life the story of a young female boxer who seeks the assistance of a trainer to bolster her career. This is a simple story at first glance, but once the layers of this film are uncovered, it is brutal and tragic. [/color][/size][/font]
 
[font=Times New Roman][size=3][color=#000000]The most impressive part of this movie is the stellar acting performances delivered by Hilary Swank, Morgan Freeman, and Clint Eastwood. Most people are familiar with on-screen duos, but in this movie, it is a surprising on-screen trio that makes this film so incredible. Hilary proves that she can immerse herself in a role and take on all the difficulties in portraying it, as she trained for months to get in perfect physical shape. Morgan Freeman delivers a similar narrative performance as in [i]The[/i] [i]Shawshank Redemption, [/i]giving a surreal meaning to much of the dialogue in the movie. Clint plays his normal gritty, harsh character, but also delivers an emotionally charged performance as well. The acting ensemble is astoundingly perfect. I hardly ever feel real emotion with any characters, as I did with the ones in this movie. Aside from the three main characters, much of the supporting cast also adds depth to the story of this movie. [/color][/size][/font]

 
[color=#000000][size=3][font=Times New Roman]Much of this film centers on Eastwoods character not being able to cope with the torn relationship between himself and his daughter. The inner turmoil that he faces on a regular basis causes him to save his fighters from harm, much as he always wanted to do for his own daughter. Yet, by trying to save them from getting injured in the ring by not scheduling them in many fights, he effectively ends his relationship with them, perpetuating the very cycle he was trying to break out of. Why would someone stay with a trainer that was holding them back from winning in the ring? Perhaps the most involving part of this story is the relationship that Eastwoods character develops with Swanks character, which is a mimicking connection to the one he once had with his daughter. [/font][/size][/color]
 
[color=#000000][size=3][font=Times New Roman]As the movie unfolds near the end, there is much more sorrow to this simple story than anyone could have expected. The film takes the audience in a direction rarely approached in modern filmmaking. It can be described as highly controversial. I will save this portion of the movie solely for your uncovering, but regardless of personal opinion, it amounts to nothing short of brilliant film-making. The audience is exposed to a viciously matter-of-fact situation that will forever change them. I cannot think of a better movie than one that can floor me with such honest realism. [/font][/size][/color]
 

 
