[b][color=red][size=3][/size][/color][/b]
[b][color=red][size=3][font=Times New Roman]Entertainment Value: Buy It[/font][/size][/color][/b]","",False,False,False,False,5.0,"2005-04-16","","RT","900760322"
4.5,"Well, it's been a long time since I last wrote on here.  A damn long time and since then I've seen plenty of good films.  I couldn't even fit all the films I wanted to, so this will be a double-entry (HA!).  
 
 I'm not really in the mood to talk about each film in detail so I'll give a breakdown.  
 
 [i]Once Upon a Time in the West[/i], the forgotten cousin of Leone's more famous [i]Man with No Name[/i] trilogy of films.  Leone's trilogy of films with Clint Eastwood redefined the western genre.  It ushered in the sixties revisionist style that is commonly associated with the spaghetti western.  However, following these three films, Leone filmed [i]Once Upon a Time in the West[/i].  An homage to the classic western film.  A huge bomb at the time, it is now regarded as a classic and rightly so.  Leone directs with such precision and his pacing is beautifully rhythmic and soothing.  Also watch out for Charles Bronson's performance.  Oh, before I forget, Henry Fonda plays a villain.  Must be seen to believe.
 
 [i]Picinic at Hanging Rock[/i] is simply a haunting film but beautifully crafted.  I fell asleep and missed about half an hour and was still completely engaged.  Based on a novel of the same name, [i]Picnic at Hanging Rock[/i] details the psychological fallout from the disappearance of three female students and their headmaster in the physically imposing hanging rock.  What seems to set up as a whodunnit flick, the film is transformed into an emotional whirlwind of those affected by the mystery.
 
 Well, now we get into some recent films.  Firstly, I must say, Jim Carrey was robbed.  The least the Academy could have done was given him a nomination.  [i]Eternal Sunshine of the Spotless Mind[/i], should have garnered Carrey a nomination but apparently the Academy is still upset over Carrey talking out of his ass in a previous show.  Regardless, he was robbed.  It's been awhile since I've seen this film and I'm trying to describe it the best I can from memory.  Basically, Carrey and the brilliant Kate Winslet try to forget each other by having a new procedure done to their minds where someone can be completely removed from their memory.  From the eccentric but brilliant mind of Charlie Kauffman.
 
 [i]Sideways[/i] was this year's big surprise.  Paul Giamatti takes his friend Thomas Haden Church on a week long stag across different California vineyards, sampling some of the golden state's finest vino.  Church is looking for one last lay before he settles down, Giamatti is just looking for acceptance from both the literary world and from women (be it his ex-wife or new flames).  In the end, Church and Giamatti are a duo who need each other more than anything.  Surprisingly funny too.  Church steals this film with some impeccable comic timing.  Definately deserved his best supporting actor nomination.
 
 [i]Million Dollar Baby[/i] is a quintessential American film.  Clint Eastwood is one of America's finest cinema stars.  With [i]Million Dollar Baby[/i], Eastwood has gone back to what filmmakers like John Ford and Howard Hawks did with American cinema, which is to tell an American story in a fashion that is poetic, theatric and rhythmic.  There is one scene in particular that I'm thinking of that better explains what I'm trying to say.  It is near the opening sequences of the film when the boxing gym owned by Eastwood is first revealed.  I say no more, must be seen, an incredible shot.  As for the film itself, I'm not too interested in delving into it; I plain loved it and worthy of all it's Oscar accolades.  If you haven't seen this film, DO NOT READ any online articles on it.  A lot of reviews and criticism reveal too much of the film.  Just go check it out.  
 
 Finally I would like to comment on the Academy Awards.  I'm a huge Chris Rock fan.  I have a couple of his DVDs and several of his performaces on my computer.  I think he is one of the most talented stand-up comics in the biz.  In comedy central's top 100 stand-ups he came in fifth place behind Woody Allen, Lenny Bruce, George Carlin and Richard Pryor.  The Academy's decision to employ Rock as host was certainly a bold move.  While Rock was enjoyable, he just did not have the edge that makes his stand-up bold.  He was of course held back by censors and what not (bleedin' facists FCC!!!!).  However, he made the best of it and I certainly enjoyed monologue.   I certainly would not mind seeing him do it again.  [i]Million Dollar Baby [/i]dominated the major awards, which was good, after the joke that was last year's Academy ([i]Lord of the Rings[/i]???  Give me a break, enjoyable but not best picture material), it was nice to see classic filmmaking and storytelling recognized this year.  
 
 Alright, I'm done writing for now.  I said there was going to be a double entry but I'm tired of typing.  So all three of you are going have to wait.","",False,False,False,False,4.5,"2005-04-17","","RT","900737715"
5.0,"Definitely a jaw-dropper.","",False,False,False,False,5.0,"2005-04-19","","RT","900778610"
5.0,"[b]I can say this is the finest movie I have ever seen...an overwhelming story of relationships and triumph of the human spirit...all against a backdrop of the most simplistic environment. This is not a boxing story, in fact, sports is a minor component - Maggie could've been selling pencils on the corner...and Frankie could've been her toughest customer. I saw this as a story of a woman fighting life's uphill battle as all of us do, but striving to realize a dream that would instill pride and filling a void that eluded her all her life.  This desire was channeled through the only thing she knew best to make a mark in her life as well as winning the approval of her clueless family...and it just happened to be boxing.  She couldn't have picked a tougher road to hoe and this determination brought her everything she had dream about - friendship and love. She went out on her own terms and finally found the peace to let go...we should all be so lucky to write our own chapter and leave a winner.[/b]","",False,False,False,False,5.0,"2005-04-30","","RT","900780545"
4.5,"I cannot begin to say how much I liked this movie. I've been wanting to see it for months now and finally got a chance. Well, I've actually had a lot of chances. It seems that every time I'm in Veliko Turnovo I go watch at least one movie at the Internet Club. Yes, it is in Bulgaria where the true copy right infridgements are taking place. And as part of my integration into Bulgarian society that is essential to being a Peace Corps volunteer, I've jumped right into it. That's really not true. It's really out of a desire to, from time to time, feel like I'm in the States again. It's not a great excuse but I try to make up for it by going to the actual movie theater every time I'm in Sofia. And you'd be impressed by the size of my own DVD and VHS collection.
 
Alright, enough rationalizing. I finally was able to watch Million Dollar Baby the other weekend because I went to the Internet Club by myself instead of going with Chris Lillie, who is the one person who is more guilty than I am for taking advantage of the bootlegged movies and seems to have seen almost every movie at the Internet Club. That is why many times I end up seeing movies like ""Tears of the Sun"" and ""Intolerable Cruelty"" instead of movies that I've really been wanting to see. I might give write ups of some of these other movies at another time, but don't hold your breath.
 
I've always been a sucker for movies with people who find real family outside of their own screwed up families. An odd thing for me considering how great my family is. But it always gets me and this movie is no exception. I also am a huge fan of Morgan Freeman and am so impressed with his performance, as well as the performances from the rest of the cast. Speaking of Morgan Freeman, my friend Genevieve met him shortly before she came to Bulgaria. He had just shot a movie in Romania so the 2 chatted about different problems that Eastern Europe is facing. Anyway, he thanked Genevieve at the Golden Globes this year when he said ""I'd like to thank everyone I've ever met"" when accepting his best supporting actor award. haha... sorry, I just think that's really cool.
 
Another thing I really liked about this movie was the parallel between the relationships between Maggie and Frankie and Frankie and the priest. I'd really like to watch the movie again to take another look at those parallels. But no time soon because I can't handle crying that much.
 
I don't want to say much more in case there are people reading this who have not yet seen the movie. As unlikely as that is. But if you are one of them, go to the nearest video store or Internet Club and watch it now. If you watch it bootlegged, don't worry, I'll make up for it for both of us by buying this movie when I go back to the States.","",False,False,False,False,4.5,"2005-04-30","","RT","900750678"
5.0,"[left][font=Palatino Linotype]   [size=3] *As some of you already know, I have become a tad obsessed with this year's Oscars. I've always loved movies, but this year's awards have been the first in quite awhile that seem to be a little more wide-open than previous contests. That has a lot to do with the emergence of Clint Eastwood's late entry into the movie season, [i]Million Dollar Baby[/i], a story about a female boxer Maggie (played by Hilary Swank) who struggles to convince an older boxing trainer, Frankie (played by Clint Eastwood) to train and mold her into a real boxer. Once convinced, the two team together (along with Eddie Scrap-Iron Dupris played by Morgan Freeman) to create an unstoppable fighter, as Maggie quickly becomes one of the top female fighters in the world, knocking out all opponents on the way to getting a championship fight. [/size][/font][font=Palatino Linotype][size=3]
 [/size][/font][font=Palatino Linotype][size=3]   [b][u]*SPOLIER ALERT*[/u][/b] [i](If you don't want a major plot twist to be revealed - stop reading)[/i] However, during the course of the fight, a cheap shot from the Champ leads to Maggie breaking her neck on the edge of the corner stool at the end of the round, leading to the end of the fight, the end of her career, and full paralysis. Maggie cannot walk, eat, or even move any part of her body, leading to body lesions and even more medical dilemmas. After breaking ties with her white trash mother and sister, who have never supported Maggie or her dreams and are only after her money, she turns to Frankie as her last hope. [/size][/font][font=Palatino Linotype][size=3]
 [/size][/font] [/left]
 [font=Palatino Linotype][size=3]      Ultimately Maggie begins to fully realize the entire scope of the situation and concludes that she does not want to live anymore. She asks Frankie to end her life, but he initially refuses. We learn that Frankie has been estranged from his daughter for many years, and that she refuses to answer his letters. As a result, Frankie has finally opened up to Maggie and accepted her as a daughter-figure after much hesitation. However, after a failed suicide attempt on Maggie's part, and after struggling over the decision with his priest, he finally concludes that the only humane and loving thing to do is to acquiese to her request and end her suffering. 
        The paralysis of Maggie comes about two thirds into the movie, and is both jolting and raw in its visual simplicity and emotional complexity. Up until that point, the movie is very even-keeled and meticulous as Eastwood doesn't tip his hand too early in revealing the possible problems ahead. The dialogue is short but sharp, to-the-point, and unstylized. The movie itself is dark, with the classic contrast of light and darkness being prominently portrayed in several key scenes, including the scene in the boxing gym where Frankie agrees to train Maggie, and in the car on the way back from her mother's house as they being to realize what they mean to one another. 
        When Maggie does become paralyzed the movie takes a drastic left-turn as the events of before are not completely overshadowed and the issue of euthanisa takes up the last twenty to thirty minutes of the movie. Whether we agree with Eastwood's decision or not - that is besides the point. It is not one of those plot twists to stir controversy or outrage. It is a work of art, attempting to portray a very real-life situation and very real-life issues such as euthanisa, mortality, and whether or not humans can act as God. The controversy over the seemingly ""pro-euthanisa"" plot twist is ridiculous and frightening. Yet again, extreme right-wing activists and mouthpieces are attempting to sabotage the free expression of creativity and art, and this time are even going after a conservative Hollywood figure in Clint Eastwood. It won't work for two reasons. First, this is a legitimate work of art, one of the highest quality and is indiscriminate in its viewpoints or themes. It has no political slant, plain and simple. Secondly, the continuous barking and complaining from these extremist figures is getting tiresome and has long left the normal bounds of political discourse. Eastwood doesn't care, most filmgoers don't care, and quite frankly, the movie itself doesn't care. [i]M$B[/i] takes no sides, instead leaving the effects of the story take hold of its audience, leaving the final questions up to them to decide.
        The performances of Swank, Freeman, and Eastwood, along with the great direction of Eastwood himself, leave filmgoers completely engaged throughout the film and absolutely stunned by the end. The movie is emotionally raw and powerful, coming from a story that could unfairly be considered ""just another boxing story."" [i]Million Dollar Baby[/i] is anything [b]BUT [/b]just another boxing movie. Eastwood has thrown the ultimate wrench into an awards season that was expected to be dominated by [i]The Aviator[/i] and which was being swept by [i]Sideways [/i]for much of the Fall. 
        The Oscars are this Sunday, and I believe that I have made my opinions well known. Freeman, Swank, and Eastwood (as director) should all take statuettes home for their work on this film, and ultimately [i]Million Dollar Baby[/i] should (and I believe WILL) be named Best Picture. Eastwood has always been known as an ""actor's director,"" many times only shooting a scene once or twice before getting it right, filming a picture under budget and wrapping it up earlier than expected, and making as little changes to the script as possible. It is reported that [i]M$B[/i] is one of the few movies to not have ONE change in script during shooting. That is not only a testament to the quality of the story and script, but to the vision and talent of Eastwood, a director who seems to be getting better with age. This is his masterpiece. 
        [i]Million Dollar Baby[/i] is one of those rare films which remind the audience what great acting, writing, and direction can accomplish without the need for stupendous visal effects, over-the-top extreme characters, or gimmicky plot twists. It is not only a great movie, but is simply the best movie of this year. Ultimately, I believe [i]Million Dollar Baby[/i] will stand the test of time, and be recognized as [b]one of the all-time great films ever[/b].[/size] [/font]","",False,False,False,False,5.0,"2005-05-02","","RT","900781414"
