3.0,"[font=Century Gothic]I really like Clint Eastwood, I honestly do. From his acting days, ""High Plains Drifter"" being a personal favorite, to his directorial work, ""Mystic River"" was teriffic, I think Clint is one of the last bastions of tough-guy Hollywood, a man not afraid to get his hands dirty with the darkest of corners of humanity. But, sorry Clint, ""Million Dollar Baby"" isn't the instant classic that everyone seems to think it is. When I saw ""Baby"" in the theaters, well before it marched on Oscartown, set on raping and pillaging everything in sight, I never would have guessed that America would latch onto this movie like it has. I guess it's success can be attributed, like Zach Braff's groan-inducingly wacky ""Garden State"", to the public's desire to feel like an intellectual. They go into movies like ""Million Dollar Baby"" and ""The Phantom of the Opera"" and get to play ""adult make-believe"", trying desperately to convince themselves and everyone else that they're smarter than they really are. Give it up, I'm on to you fuckers.[/font]
[font=Century Gothic][/font]
[font=Century Gothic]The big problem with this movie is how dully predictable it is. If I can correctly guess the ending of a movie thirty minutes before the fact, that doesn't exactly bode well. Not that every movie has to knock your socks off with some ""Sixth Sense"" twist ending, because that leads to movies like ""Attic Expeditions"" were not one single fucking thing makes sense, but knowing the ending to a story takes the mystery out of a movie. Let me put it this way, would you watch a sporting event, doesn't matter what sport, if you already knew the outcome? Unless you're looking to make an easy buck hustling some slack-jawed idiot, probably not.[/font]
[font=Century Gothic][/font]
[font=Century Gothic]So, Clint plays a grizzled manager and Swank a gifted, if not retardedely enthusiastic, boxer. Does this not sound incredibly cliched already? It's like ""The Next Rocky"", something I hoped would never happen. Don't get me wrong, the movie has plenty of laughs and great acting, but touting the acting abilities of Morgan Freeman and Clint Eastwood as a reason to watch a movie is the same reasoning people use when they recommend every movie Steven Spielberg craps out. Yeah, the direction is going to be great, but if the plot blows, like ""The Terminal"", then Speilberg alone isn't going to save things.[/font]
[font=Century Gothic][/font]
[font=Century Gothic]Like most sport movies, Swank rises to the top of her sport and everything seems to be going her way until, BAM, tragedy strikes. Sorry, that makes it seem like Swanks misfortune is some kind of surprise. Let me rephrase: Swank rises to the top of her sport and everything seems to be going her way until, [size=1]whimper[/size][size=2], tragedy strikes. So now that we know she'll never recover, which they mention about five minutes after the accident, we're forced to wait another 45 minutes while Eastwood and Swank talk about how much her predicament sucks, and all the while I'm tapping my feet in frustration, waiting for Clint to finally pull the fucking plug and put Swank and myself out of our collective misery. [/size][/font]
[font=Century Gothic] [/font]
[font=Century Gothic]And then there's the whole priest side story which doesn't go anywhere. What could have been one of the most revealing and, frankly, interesting portions of the story never EVER gets explained. Why does he keep going to church if all he does is harrass the priest and sully the name of God? It'd be interesting if it was explored in more depth, but that never happens, just like the side story of him and his daughter. All these loose ends, if they were fully drawn out, could have pulled the story together, but they're simply ignored towards the end of the film in favor of watching Swank bite her own tongue off. Don't get me wrong, I think Hillary should do that in every movie. Think of how much better ""The Core"" would have been if Hillary had just dropped everything and bit her own tongue off. BAM, the movie would have jumped up a few notches in my book, from ""shit stain"" all they way to ""mere embarrasment"".[/font]
[font=Century Gothic] [/font]
[font=Century Gothic]So, ""Million..."" is simply a victim of inertia. One critic slept his way through the movie, looked at the cast and crew credits and determined that, even though he hadn't seen a damn thing, it must have been a revelation. Every critic then jumped under the bandwagon, taking the viewing public along with them, and were subsequently crushed. ""Baby"" is probably the most undeserving Oscar winner since ""Chicago"" took a steaming shit on the chest of the Oscar jury, but it can take solace in the fact that, at least, it's not as bad as ""Chicago"".[/font]","",False,False,False,False,3.0,"2005-05-03","","RT","900776244"
