5.0,"Yesterday I went to see Downfall, today I went and saw Million Dollar Baby (for the third time).  Million Dollar Baby has [i]the key element[/i] that was missing from Downfall: connection to the characters.  Because of how it was written, you feel like you know and love the characters you watch in MDB.  It is a fantastic story that is beautifully shot and fantastically acted.  The only thing lacking is the music, which Clint decided to do himself.  Not too impressive.  Other than that, it is perfect.  I love this movie.  It's currently playing at the cheap theater here, and I'm going to try to see it at least once more before it leaves.  It truely deserved the Oscar for best movie of the year.  Go see it now if you can, or buy it the second it comes out on DVD.","",False,False,False,False,5.0,"2005-05-10","","RT","900781152"
3.5,"Million dollar baby is a good example of how films should be done to be appraised by the critics across the board and on the Academy staff. No wonder it got the major wins in February 2005. It has all the elements essential to prize-winners: drama, point, depth, good actors and a good and reputable director (which, I suppose, was a decisive factor in the battle of David with Avia-Goliath this year). Clint Eastwood seems to have felt for the right to key to the prize door. You may dislike his films as much as you want, but you understand that it is a proper way to make films which are worth of being distinguished and remembered for some time. Maybe for years, maybe for months, or even for days. 

Frankly speaking, I didnt like it very much indeed. It must have been my fault that I dont like films where theres little action compared to the quantity of sense. In the film theres much point, its no use defying the evident fact, but it seems to be spread about the film like a little piece of butter on a too big slice of bread (thanks to Bilbo Baggins). Some scenes are really impressive, and I was really sorry that all the boxing episodes were too short to be enjoyed by the audience properly. I never favoured boxing, but after the film I came to understand that here theres something lying underneath, under the outer violent layer. Nothing is omitted here, everything necessary is emphasized and everything extra is deliberately excluded. We dont come to know much about the previous lives of the leads: what we need to know is their relevance to boxing, and the rest of their backgrounds doesnt matter at all. The boxing-related scenes are from time to time interrupted by Franks (the coach) conversations with the local priest on religious issues. As I see it Eastwood tried to show the controversial feelings overwhelming the old coach when he has to decide whether to commit a mortal sin of killing or not. Its in the under layer of the film, and its not easy to get it right at once. 

When I watched the film, I remembered Eastwoods another film  The Mystic River. Some parallels are easy to discover and Im not going to waste my time on mentioning them. What is common in the films is a sense of deep feelings creating an intolerable strain in the inner world of the characters and rending their hearts. Everything is literally soaked in emotions. Its not a pathetic love story or a success story of a woman (Maggie, Hillary Swank). Its a hard-real narrative of a woman who wants to fill her life with the only passion she has inside: everything is against her  her relatives who prefer to remain on the dole, her lack of educations which allows her only working as a waitress, and her being too old to start a new life from scratch. Shes deeply bound to her surroundings, but its not the nostalgic feeling of belonging to the place where you were born. No, it is not. Its rather a painful inability to rise beyond what is supposed to be your lot. So many of us just dont want to understand, that we are the masters of our life, and prefer to whine like pups. Neither do her relatives: her mother cares only about the younger single daughter and regards her older one as a constant and granted source of money. The moment, when they tried to seize on her financial actives at a time which was not quite proper, was the most revealing. 

Morgan Freeman acted in the film as a cameo, but he did really well. In generally, all the actors in the work are those to thank for the films success. And the ending is unexpected. I supposed it to be a female version of Rocky and it disappointed me pleasurably cause she lost some times and after all she broke her neck. Then I started to expect her full recovery and again I wasnt right. The film just defies the rules of Hollywood sugar-making. No happy-ending at all, and no comfort for the leads  its like Shakespeares tragedy where everybody dies and in the end someone pronounces wise words. Morgan Freeman is the person to fill in this position and he remains on the stage when everybody leaves. Thanks to Eastwood  you did well, sir.

Now that Im writing the lines I begin to understand that the film is too good for me to understand it at first sight. Perhaps, Ill borrow a DVD once more to enjoy it or to be depressed off my head. Perhaps","",False,False,False,False,3.5,"2005-05-12","","RT","900772520"
