2.5,"Of course, we all know the acting was fine.  Swank proved she could act-- great, we know!  But the third section of this movie dragged on and on and on: wrap it up already!  Then for the big ""right to die"" political twist at the end-- give me a break.  I don't have any desire to be taken away by a film (not that I was by this one anyway) and then get a sucker punch of an overpoliticized plot at the end.  Makes me feel yucky poo-poo!","",False,False,False,False,2.5,"2005-05-27","","RT","900784161"
4.5,"I finally had the chance to see ""Million Dollar Baby"", produced and directed by Eastwood, and starred by himself, Morgan Freeman and Hillary Swank. The award-winner presents a simple story, very much in the line of his last movies, clean and devoid of ostentation. In a very linear fashion, and framed from the very beginning by the husky off-voice of Freeman, the plot about the relationship between a female boxer and his veteran trainer unfolds unaffectedly. That is something we really appreciate here from Mr. Eastwood, in a world constantly assailed by Van Helsings and Alexanders. 
The interest in the movie is built up by the relationship between instructor and apprentice. The boxer, Maggie, moves from distant admiration for Ricky -'the boss'- to complete devotion to him as a trainer. At the end, the unavoidable symmetry with her lost father is drawn. They are bound as a family, more so when we are confronted with Maggie's redneck white-trashy real relatives (her mother is especially hateful). Eastwood wonderfully portrays the eternal solitude of the trainer that devotes himself to another being, just to be trashed out by those whom he loves. 
The trainer, tormented by a myterious past, has always been protective with his trainees. He does not want to wreck anybody's life. On the other hand, one eyed Freeman, wounded in the ring, still claims that it is better to wreck your life doing what you want than dying without giving it a try. This last thesis dominates the movie. Because this movie is about boxing, of course, but the main theme underlying it, unlike in other hollywood products, is about living, but living in the right sense of the word. Fighting and learning to find the balance between quitting (or throwing the towel) and fighting on.
We liked this message here at the inquisition. That's why we rescued the film from the fire, just in time. 
Ok, and also 'cause Hillary is hot, whattheheck:p .
 
:rotten: WEAK POINT: We are a bit pissed:mad: . It's not the movie's fault, though. Perhaps we should blame the Ministry of Culture here in Japan, not really sure about where exactly the responsibility lies. Facts: The movie was released yesterday in Japan, May 28th, 2005. More than five months after the release in the States and four months and a week after the release worldwide. I've read about it, seen bits of it and heard people talk about this movie for ages, and right when it was starting to become a thing of the past, here it is, in the movie theaters of Osaka city. Not to mention I had to pay the ludicrous quantity of 1,800 yen to watch it. What have we, the keepers of the faith in good cinema, done to deserve this misery?","",False,False,False,False,4.5,"2005-05-29","","RT","900766931"
3.5,"MILLION DOLLAR BABY: 7 out of 10
 
Million Dollar Baby is the story of a small town woman who has nothing to live for but a boxing dream who chases after that dream, hooking up with a grumpy boxing coach who is reluctant to coach a woman. Relationships grow deep as Clint Eastwood's character as the grumpy coach forms a close bond with Hillary Swank's poor, yet encouraged boxing character.
 
Million Dollar baby is one of those good ol' melowdramatic, contraversial-message oscar pics. What is it about these movies that the oscars love so much?? In Million Dollar Baby, it's this-
 
Clint Eastwood does a good job in film direction. If the mood is depressing, you feel depressed. If the mood is hopeful, you feel hopeful. If the mood is exciting, you want to stand up and cheer. Morgan Freeman plays a helper at Eastwood's gym who used to be a proffessional boxer, but got his eye injured and had to stop decades ago. Freeman is the heart in the movie. He brings in all the life applicable messages in the film, and they hit home. Another profound character in the film is Danger, a mentally challenged young man who comes to visit the gym every day, and has his own boxing dreams as well. Danger brings both laughs and tears to the film. His quick wittedness brings the laughs, but how he is treated by other members of the gym brings the tears. And then comes Morgan Freeman again, helping Danger out and bringin back the good morals the film portrays.
 
Million Dollar Baby isnt a feel good film, although one might think. It's just a good movie out there giving all of us some good morals and messages that we can take and apply. If you can get past the slowness and boringness that some of the scenes are filled with, and focus on the characters and how all of the relationships, morals, and messages come about, youll enjoy the movie. Million Dollar Baby gets a 7 out of 10.","",False,False,False,False,3.5,"2005-05-30","","RT","900785489"
