4.5,"I just finished watching the film earlier today and ""WOW""
 
 This film really moved me, the story of a womans, Maggie (Hilary Swank) courage and determination to succeed and follow her dreams. And of a man, Frankie (Eastwood) who for years has been trying to re-connect with his daughter with no success. He finds a purpose in life with this connection he has with Maggie.
 
 Excellent performances all round and brilliant directing by Eastwood. This film deserved every award it has won.
 
 This movie kept me captivated to the very sad end, it was a powerful film which moved me and i found myself weeping a little at the end (a hard thing to do)
 
 Favourite Quote: I'm 32, Mr. Dunn, and I'm here celebrating the fact that I spent another year scraping dishes and waitressing which is what I've been doing since 13, and according to you I'll be 37 before I can even throw a decent punch, which I have to admit, after working on this speed bag for a month may be the God's simple truth. Other truth is, my brother's in prison, my sister cheats on welfare by pretending one of her babies is still alive, my daddy's dead, and my momma weighs 312lbs. If I was thinking straight I'd go back home, find a used trailer, buy a deep fryer and some oreos. Problem is, this the only thing I ever felt good doing. If I'm too old for this then I got nothing. That enough truth to suit you?","",False,False,False,False,4.5,"2005-06-27","","RT","900770764"
4.5,"[font=Garamond][size=3]Now this is what movies are supposed to be. I am not a boxing fan - in fact, I hate the sport. And despite my clear obsession with movies, neither Clint Eastwood nor Hilary Swank draws my attention. So until this Best Picture Oscar-winner came to the theater where I work, I'd never bothered to see it. May I now say: ""I recommend this film to anyone, and especially to those who have been avoiding it for the same stupid reasons I had. It is a terrific and fully worthwhile film"".[/size][/font]
 [font=Garamond][size=3]Gym owner and trainer Frankie Dunn (Eastwood) is a bit past his prime. An earlier boxing accident involving his friend ""Scrap-iron"" (Morgan Freeman) has left Frankie so cautious that his fighters have a tendency to leave him in order to get a manager willing to risk it all on a title shot. When Maggie Fitzgerald (Swank), a self-described trailer-park white trash waitress with boxing ambitions, enters Frankie's gym, something inside him snaps. Though he is initially against training a girl, and such an old fighter, her determination and Scrap-iron's urgings eventually win him over. With competent training, Maggie soon rises to the top of her game, winning fight after fight and becoming known for her quick knock-outs. But finally she meets her match and Frankie is unable to protect her from the injury that ends her career and leaves her paralyzed. As her life and body waste away in a rehab center, she begs Frankie to help her die, saying that she has done far more in her life already than she ever expected to, and that she cannot continue to live this way.[/size][/font]
 [font=Garamond][size=3]The movie's ending is at once thought-provoking, emotionally moving, and satisfying. When the film first came out, its view of assisted suicide outraged some and inspired others, but for me, it provided a healthy dose of common sense.[/size][/font]
 [font=Garamond][size=3]""Million Dollar Baby"" had all the necessary stuff to make it an Academy favorite. It had a fabulous cast acting in top form, a great plot complemented by well-written dialogue, and some brilliant directing by Mr. Eastwood, who also composed the film's musical score. The characters were really the drive behind the film's excellence, with the three main characters well fleshed out during the film's 132-minute run-time and numerous other characters left as stereotypes (the bitter priest, hick mother, boxer with nothing but heart), but believable ones. [/size][/font]
 [font=Garamond][size=3]Overall, though heavy on drama and adult themes, ""Million Dollar Baby"" deserved the title of Best Picture of 2004. Heck with ""coulda been""...this film was a contender.[/size][/font]
 [font=Garamond][size=3][img]http://i567.photobucket.com/albums/ss112/medicatherine/million_dollar_baby.jpg[/img]
[/size][/font]","",False,False,False,False,4.5,"2005-06-28","","RT","900772172"
5.0,"Taxi Driver Was a rather weird movie.  Seeing Robert DeNiro acting like a crazy Vietnam Vet was just really weird and whatnot.  I don't get why the movie is so huge, but I suppose back then it may have been pretty shocking for some people.  It was very graphic and had an ok story, but it just didn't feel like something great.  It is worth watching though.
 
 Million Dollar Baby was this movie I had never heard of until I saw a poster for it at Wal-Mart.  I had no idea what it was about but it had Clint Eastwood and Morgan Freeman, which are two very fine actors.  It was a movie about a girl boxer from the south, a piece of trash that tries to do something for herself.  She does it, but she faces a lot of hardships.  The movie runs with a very nice light of positivity.  Then the end comes and well, it is really depressing.  Great movie though.
 
 Land of the Dead was great because Romero did it this time around and totally made it look and feel like the old movies.  The gore looked realistic and the story was nicely done.  This was a real zombie movie where zombies don't run as fast as cars, no they walked.  It was a very good film though and I suggest everyone go see it.","",False,False,False,False,5.0,"2005-07-03","","RT","900720899"
4.0,"[center][img]http://eltiempo.terra.com.co/cine/milliondolarbaby/IMAGEN/IMAGEN-1985987-0.jpg[/img][/center]
A sad sad movie.  That is all it seemed to be.  It seems to never really picks up pace.  I am very confused on how this movie won all the Oscar Awards.  I was way more touched by Finding Neverland.  This seemed to be a dark and sad film.
  The movie is about a passionate female boxer, Maggie Fitzgerald, who is old for a new boxer and has the heart of a lion.  Frankie Dunn(Clint Eastwood) refuses to train till he finally gives in.  He is aided by ex-boxer Eddie(Morgan Freeman).  The two take the middle-aged female's lion heart and turn her into one of the greatest boxers.  As all three of the characters learn great values along the way and grow into one family.
  The film had a very touching story.  I believed it lacked character though.  I don't believe Hilary Swank deserved best actress after seeing this film.  I don't believe this film deserved best movie.  Morgan Freeman is actually the only person who was a great actor in the film and deserved his Oscar.  I really don't know what I was expecting.  After watching more and more films and  critiquing them I am  becoming more expecting and critical.","",False,False,False,False,4.0,"2005-07-04","","RT","900752508"
4.0,"DEFINITELY ONE OF THE BEST FILMS OF 2004. I AM NOT AT ALL A FAN OF BOXING, HOWEVER, THIS ONE IS ABOUT A LOT MORE THAN BOXING. IT HAS OUTSTANDING CHARACTERIZATIONS, AND CHARACTER INTERACTION. REAL DEPTH AND MEANING. IT FOCUSES ON SELF DETERMINATION, RELATIONSHIPS, SUPPORT AND LOVE. IT EVEN TACKLES THE TOUCHY AND SENSITIVE SUBJECT OF MERCY KILLING. OUTSTANDING IN EVERY WAY.","",False,False,False,False,4.0,"2005-07-06","","RT","900705768"
5.0,"A few films, I have seen when they first came out, but just entering ratings for them.
 
[url=""http://www.rottentomatoes.com/m/i_am_sam/dvd.php?select=3""][img]http://images.rottentomatoes.com/images/movie/coverv/11/194211.jpg[/img][/url]
 
I Am Sam was sort of Dakota Fanning's breakout moment. I know there are many people who wish this film wasn't made for that reason alone. However, unlike many - I really enjoyed this film and it's generality to an issue. It made no attempt to be realistically correct but to just be a tear-jerking movie and in this it succeeds. Performances from Penn and Pfieffer (however you spell her stupid surname) are strong, with the pairing of the two a smart choice, due to the film's drama.
 
Throw Fanning into the mix and she completes the tearjerker as well as some cleverly done covers of Beatles covers which weaves the movie together. Ben Folds, Ben Harper, The Vines, Nick Cave and Sarah McLachlan are just some of the artists to appear on the soundtrack.
 
Not brilliant, but entertainment overall.
 
[url=""http://www.rottentomatoes.com/url/shop.php?k=pricegrabber-videorelease&id=113930602""][img]http://images.rottentomatoes.com/images/movie/coverv/85/242085_thumb.jpg[/img][/url]
 
The Aviator is what Gangs Of New York wasn't. Although like the latter, The Aviator is very long and stars DiCaprio. However unlike Gangs, it manages to conclude itself nicely. Instead of rush through history, Scorcese has learnt from his mistakes to produce a fantastic foray into the early aviation times of Howard Hughes and all things expensive in Hollywood.
 
Throw in the fantastic performance of Cate Blanchett as screen legend Kate Hepburn - where the actress has every characteristic of Hepburn down to the finest of details is remarkable and jaw-dropping.
 
Aside from this, the fantastic costumes, Alan Alda, the cinematography is the gorgeous period music from this era. It is a joyous revival of the Hollywood glory days. It's hysterical to see Jude Law play Erroll Flynn, but it is just a movie and the latter is dead I guess.
 
Nearly as good as Clint's piccie, but length and depressing themes made it lose out unfortunately. It isn't as loveable.
 
[url=""http://www.rottentomatoes.com/m/million_dollar_baby/dvd.php?select=2""][img]http://images.rottentomatoes.com/images/movie/coverv/34/243734.jpg[/img][/url]
 
This is without a doubt one of my favourite films of all time now. Fantastic performances from Hilary Swank, Morgan Freeman and the multi-talented Clint Eastwood see to it that this movie has 'classic' written all over it.
 
Heart achingly beautiful for even the toughest of viewers will assure the film of this status. The chemistry between the three leads and their characters are sublime to watch and accompanied by some touching, light appropriate music - the film carries itself nicely through without overstaying it's welcome.
 
Fantastic cinematography with dialogue that is basic, yet original. Morgan Freeman's commentary may be annoying to some but that is the closest one can come to finding any fault with this masterpeice.
 
[url=""http://www.rottentomatoes.com/m/bad_education/dvd.php?select=2""][img]http://images.rottentomatoes.com/images/movie/coverv/00/242100.jpg[/img][/url]
 
Another stunning, layered and daring film from Pedro Almodovar. Benal and Martinez are fantastic performers in all frames. The filming of this hotly-debated picture is exquisite. The film noir style it has is endearing only I did find it a little more difficult to follow than others did.:o","",False,False,False,False,5.0,"2005-07-09","","RT","900685302"
4.5,"[font=Book Antiqua][color=darkorange][img]http://images-eu.amazon.com/images/P/B0007MAPTW.02.LZZZZZZZ.jpg[/img][/color][/font]
 
[font=Book Antiqua][color=darkorange]On the surface Million Dollar Baby is a boxing movie, and a very good boxing movie at that. This is Clint Eastwoods humble submission to film history for this year, this makes it two masterpieces in two years, last year he directed the remarkable Mystic River. In this movie however, Clint Eastwood not only directs but also stars in Million Dollar Baby as Frankie, a worn down boxing trainer who runs a small-time gym and lives under the weight of a life of regret. Morgan Freeman plays Scrap-Iron, a former boxer who trained under Frankie, he and Frankie are best friends possibly each others only friends, and they live out their lives with conversations about boxing which are really not about boxing at all. Finally there is Maggie played by Hilary Swank, Maggie is a thirty year old waitress who has had a rough life but she has finally found a real joy in her life and that joy is boxing; all three of these actors giving stellar, Oscar worthy performances.
The first two acts of this film are about two things, first it is about Frankie reluctantly giving in and training Maggie who has some stubborn but natural talent and with some training she is quickly adding knockout after knockout to her record. The second thing that occurs during the first two acts of this film is the development of the characters themselves, in these two acts Eastwood directs with ease and patience and allows the audience to really get to know and care about these characters. The first two thirds however, is, only the surface of the film, it is the third act that reveals what this movie is really all about. Each one of these main characters have a dark past and the cinematography assists to tell this story beautifully, in the first act all three characters live in the shadows of life and their pasts, in the second act, they emerge from the shadows following Maggies success and fame. Finally though, in the third and most important act they all recede back into the shadows for different reasons and different choices.
Some critics have been spoiling the third act in their reviews; I am one of the more intelligent critics who will not spoil the experience of watching this film. As I said before Million Dollar Baby is an honest human tale, it is so honest and real that it captivates you in a way that is so very rare in film today. The audience actually has the chance to learn who these people are from top to bottom and cherish the relationships they have with each other. As a result of all this, the choices these characters make in the third act that have life-long consequences, affect us so deeply.
Clint Eastwoods Million Dollar Baby is a masterpiece because it does two things that is rare in filmmaking today. First, it develops authentic three-dimensional characters we care about. Secondly, it allows these characters to make real-life decisions that, whether we agree with them or not, makes us stop and reflect upon our own lives, our own ethics and our own beliefs. Filmmaking is not about giving the audience something they all agree with, it is art, and art makes you stop and think about your own life and what you would do in that situation. Any movie that sparks deep long conversation days after its viewing is a masterpiece because you have to go back and watch it again because to us, these were real people, people who made us laugh, made us cry, and maybe even made us angry. Million Dollar Baby is one of only a few films from this past year that provides beauty for the eyes and questions for the heart, this film will definitely be on my list of The Top Ten Movies of 2004.[/color][/font]","",False,False,False,False,4.5,"2005-07-11","","RT","900789686"
3.5,"I will start off this review by telling everyone that this movie is definatly not for everyone and some may find this movie very boring. I am one of those people. Not to say that this movie was bad because it was really good but I found it really boring. The acting was top of the line and the movie had a great plot but I just didn't dig it as much as people said I would. 
  In the wake of a painful estrangement from his daughter, boxing trainer Frankie Dunn (CLINT EASTWOOD) has been unwilling to let himself get close to anyone for a very long time - then Maggie Fitzgerald (HILARY SWANK) walks into his gym. In a life of constant struggle, Maggie's gotten herself this far on raw talent, unshakable focus and a tremendous force of will. But more than anything, she wants someone to believe in her. The last thing Frankie needs is that kind of responsibility - let alone that kind of risk - but won over by Maggie's sheer determination, he begrudgingly agrees to take her on. In turns exasperating and inspiring each other, the two come to discover that they share a common spirit that transcends the pain and loss of their pasts, and they find in each other a sense of family they lost long ago.
  The movie has a solid plot but it still is not a rocky film. I enjoyed mystic river way more than this film because of the fact that the movie provided the audience with a plot that we have never seen, yet million dollar baby uses a plot that has been used many times before.","",False,False,False,False,3.5,"2005-07-12","","RT","900789136"
