4.5,"Million Dollar Baby is a fantastic film. Though boxing is the back drop the film is not about boxing. Its about determination, dedication, hard work, and overcoming obstacles. Eastwood, Freeman and Swank all give excellent performances and you care for them. I loved the touches of humor in the screen play as well as the sub plots. The direction as well as the lighting is also well done. Eastwood is a talented director and I look forward to all his upcoming jobs. The lighting used adds quite a bit to many of the shots. With a heartfelt ending you cant but leave Million Dollar Baby satisfied.","",False,False,False,False,4.5,"2005-07-14","","RT","900704533"
5.0,"I watched Million Dollar Baby with my whole family and it was really pretty awesome. My mom, dad, little sister and I, ALL enjoyed this movie, which is pretty amazing considering our VERY different tastes. I cried and cried. This is one of the best movies I've seen in a LONG time.","",False,False,False,False,5.0,"2005-07-15","","RT","900789357"
3.5,"[u][img]http://us.movies1.yimg.com/movies.yahoo.com/images/hv/photo/movie_pix/warner_brothers/million_dollar_baby/_group_photos/clint_eastwood13.jpg[/img][/u]
 
[u]Million Dollar Baby[/u] (Eastwood, 2004)
 
Just like with [i]Mystic River[/i], Eastwood's steady direction is able to elevate his rather pedestrian script into something that feels remarkably moving and honest. Eastwood handles the emotions in the film with nuance and gives this simple story some much needed complexity. His major misstep, though, is the portrayal of Maggie's family, which comes across as eye-rollingly hokey and would have been best left excised from the film entirely. Thanks to Eastwood, [i]Million Dollar Baby [/i]is a much better film than it looks on paper, but even he can only take this material so far. 
 
[**1/2]","",False,False,False,False,3.5,"2005-07-15","","RT","900640109"
5.0,"EXCEEEEEELLLLLLLLLENT MOVIE.  I can see now why it's gotten so many awards.  Clint Eastwood is a genius.  As is Tim Burton.  But tomorrow's movie-going has yet to decide that, haha.  Not that I'm doubting ol' Tim or anything.
 
But seriously folks, Million Dollar Baby is just a beautiful movie.  I am so glad that Danger kid was in there for comedy relief or else I would have been depressed right now.  I also don't think I enjoyed the movie in its entirety because some dolt invited my away-state self (on MSN) into a chat room and drove me and my folks nuts with that crazy IM sound during the climax of the movie.  Ugh.  But I'm over that now.
 
So.  If you're up for an amazing movie, rent this one.  And be sure to get the kleenex's while you're out!","",False,False,False,False,5.0,"2005-07-15","","RT","900759405"
3.0,"I finally saw [b]Million Dollar Baby[/b] last night. I was already prepared for the last 45 minutes, because some idiot on NPR let the cat out of the ol' bag back at Oscars time. So, I don't know if I was already prepared to be disappointed by yet another spoon-fed, entirely predicatable drama about some castaway pulling himself/herself up by the bootstraps, following a dream, and only to be cut down by some inhuman bad guy with no heart, a life-threatening illness/condition, or both. Now I'm NOT surprised it won the Oscars that it did, I'm simply disappointed that the Oscar folks continue to support movies with the same plotlines being recycled and reassembled. 
   
   Don't get me wrong.  Baby did NOT suck.  I'm just tired of this stuff.
   
 I really don't like feeling so cold and unfeeling about the movie. Eastwood's performance was absolutely top-notch. His best performance since Space Cowboys. Just kidding.... since [b]Unforgiven[/b]. I was, however, not particularly thrilled with his Direction. It was.... OK. But not Best Director quality work. There were only a handful of top-notch scenes. And ESPECIALLY compared to what Scorsese did with [b]The Aviator[/b].

 The MOST redeeming quality of the movie was the interaction and chemistry between Morgan Freeman and Eastwood. Although, at times it ventured into ""Driving Miss Eastwood"" territory, the performances were still virtually perfect. 
   
 As far as Swank's performance, I hate to say it, but I really think any less-than-perfect looking good actress could have pulled it off equally as well. Again, a leading actress Oscar nod goes to the ROLE and not the performance. Unfortunately, I've only seen one of the other Supporting Actress nom's preformance, so I can't really compare Swank to the others here. But if Kate Winslet (whom I normally only regard as mediocre) had played a drug-addicted prostitute with some unappealing face prosthetic, the Oscar decision wouldn't have been so easy.
   
 OK, so I'm done being all pi$$y about this movie. It was a GOOD movie. It was not a horrible movie. I just am so ttirrrrrrred of this movie. And I'm REALLY surprised that virtually none of the critics here on RT didn't complain similarly. Wow. I never felt so much like an outcast. Perhaps someone should pull [i]my[/i] plug.","",False,False,False,False,3.0,"2005-07-16","","RT","900793435"
5.0,"[font=Arial]This movie is truly a masterpiece. From the beginning to the climax, its gloomy. Not dark or sinister, its just that Eastwood found a way to make you empathize with the characters without being heavy handed. The sadness is usually just an undercurrent, almost subliminal. You never feel manipulated. The weight of the movie comes at you from all angles in a perfect blend of cinematography, plot development, character development, dialogue, acting, and scoring. As I stated before, all of these elements are blended so subtly that it never feels manipulative or contrived. The plot and character development are perfectly timed but the slow development may turn off fans of action movies although it will appeal to movie fans for whom an actual story line is appealing.[/font]

   [font=Arial] [/font]

   [font=Arial]The genius really comes into focus when you realize that everything (I mean absolutely everything!) in the first three quarters of the movie is just preparation for the climax and resolution at which point the sadness moves from the background to the foreground in a major way and turns to pure misery. Im a 28 year old male and pride my self on not being a crybaby, but I actually teared-up during the resolution, something a movie has not done to me for over a decade. Eastwood has made some crappy movies in his day but he has truly hit his golden era.[/font]","",False,False,False,False,5.0,"2005-07-17","","RT","900790739"
