4.0,"As promised, here are a few catch-up reviews plus ""Million Dollar Baby.""
 
[b][u]Birth:[/u][/b]  In a brilliant performance, Nicole Kidman portrays a widow, Anna, haunted by a 10 year-old boy (a very mature Cameron Bright) who claims to be her dead husband, Sean.  As the boy slowly (and I mean slowly) breaks through Anna's emotional walls, the audience witnesses just how long and painful Anna's recovery of her loss has been.  With the help of Anne Heche and Lauren Bacall in noteworthy roles, Kidman and Bright bring a believability to an intriguing conceptual film.  Despite its originality, ""Birth"" seems to lag at times which in turn hinders the overall experience.
 
[b][u]Hero:[/u][/b]  After experienceing the mesmerizing ""House of Flying Daggers,"" I was excited to plop ""Hero"" into my DVD player.  Quite a few people, having seen both films, suggested I would enjoy ""Hero"" more than ""Daggers.""  Those people must not really know me!  I found ""Daggers"" to be a much more beautiful and satisfying film.  That is not to say, however, that ""Hero"" is without merit.  With admiral performances and an interesting storyline, ""Hero"" achieves decency but never truly bests ""House of Flying Daggers.""
 
[b][u]Vera Drake:[/u][/b]  Now here is a film I can get behind!  ""Vera Drake"" follows the story of a British woman in the 1930s with a heart of gold.  She works several jobs as a servant to the wealthy, and in her spare time, she looks in on the sick, destitute and lonely.  Vera's main goal in life seems to be making everyone's life a bit more pleasant if possible.  Played impeccably by Imelda Staunton (in an Oscar-nominated turn), Vera Drake also performs illegal abortions to ""help girls out"" when they have no one else to turn to.  Sadly, Vera gets caught when one of her girls becomes violently ill much to Vera's surprise and dismay.  Heart-wrenching and poignant, ""Vera Drake"" is definitely worth a viewing.
 
[b][u]Million Dollar Baby:[/u][/b]  Okay, okay.  I decided to give Clint Eastwood another chance after the dismally disappointing ""Mystic River,"" and I must say I was pleasantly surprised.  Not only did 2004's Oscar-winning Best Picture [i]almost[/i] live up to its hype, but the movie restored my faith in the ability of the aging director and star.  The story of boxer Maggie Fitzgerald (Hilary Swank) proved a touching one in which she became more than she ever dreamed she could under the harsh tutelage of Frankie Dunn.  With masterful acting from Eastwood, Oscar-winners Swank and Morgan Freeman, ""Million Dollar Baby"" packed an undeniable emotional punch.  (Sorry about the pun - I couldn't stop msyelf!)  While Freeman deserved a Golden Statue for his supporting performance, Swank's Oscar should have gone to any of the other nominees, and the Best Picture Oscar would have fit better had the award gone to ""Finding Neverland"" or the deserving (but not nominated) ""Eternal Sunshine of the Spotless Mind.""
 
Still to come: ""Troy,"" ""Sex and the City,"" ""Ocean's Twelve"" and ""Shaun of the Dead""","",False,False,False,False,4.0,"2005-09-19","","RT","900749700"
1.0,"Don't let the Academy fool you.  The movies that win Best Picture don't always deserve it, and ""Million Dollar Baby"" didn't.  This film won the top honor not for its acting (which is decent) but rather for its message.  ""Baby"" is an issues movie.  It uses a popular underdog boxer image (Rocky anyone?) as the packaging by which to present its pro-euthanasia message.
    The film centers on wannabe female boxer, Maggie Fitzgerald, (well-played by Hilary Swank) who manages to convince a grizzled trainer, Frankie Dunn (Clint Eastwood, who else?), that she has what it takes to be a professional boxer.  The story is interesting enough, but once youve seen one boxing movie, youve seen them all.  The movie has all the trimmings of an inspirational sports story and plays that role for most of the movie before pulling the rug out from under the viewers.  In a championship bout with a crazy German gal, Maggie gets sucker-punched and lands, neck first, onto her stool.  Snap.  This once dynamic gal went from living her dream to living the nightmare of a paralytic. 

   Sitting in the hospital, she is preyed on by her family who wants her money, not her company.  They care only so long as they benefit from her passing.  Every day Frankie visits her and reads to her, becoming the loving father she never had.  Rather than adjust to this new life, she says to Frankie that she has accomplished what she wanted and wants to finish a winner.  She asks him to kill her, but he refuses at first so she tries a couple of times to kill herself.  Finally, Frankie realizes that the most loving thing he could do for her is to inject her with a load of adrenalin and pull her breathing tube out.  All of this is tenderly narrated by Morgan Freeman who, with his soft words and kindly understanding, communicates that euthanasia is okay, its loving, its caring.

       To one who believes in the sanctity of all life, whether it be walking or paralyzed, this smacks of a deeper agenda.  If we allow the euthanasia of the severely disabled, where do we draw the line?  If a professional soccer player loses his kicking leg to an accident and thinks his life is over, do we smile kindly and put him down like some pack animal that has outlived its usefulness?  Euthanasia is a Pandoras Box that we dare not open. 

   Back to the film.  Swank, Eastwood and Freeman all did well, but the dull story lacked any real impact, and the euthanasia issue was a dirty ambush that annoyed me more than moved me.  No tear was shed by this dull movie, but it was okay if you want a mediocre movie with a blatant political agenda.","",False,False,False,False,1.0,"2005-09-19","","RT","900806818"
