5.0,"[center][b][i][font=Comic Sans MS][size=5][color=white]Million Doller Baby[/color][/size][/font][/i][/b][/center]
[center][b][i][font=Comic Sans MS][size=5][color=#ffffff][/color][/size][/font][/i][/b] [/center]
[size=4][color=white][b][i]In the wake of a painful estrangement from his daughter, boxing trainer Frankie Dunn has been unwilling to let himself get close to anyone for a very long time--then Maggie Fitzgerald walks into his gym. In a life of constant struggle, Maggie's gotten herself this far on raw talent, unshakable focus and a tremendous force of will. But more than anything, she wants someone to believe in her. The last thing Frankie needs is that kind of responsibility-- let alone that kind of risk--but won over by Maggie's sheer determination, he begrudgingly agrees to take her on. In turns exasperating and inspiring each other, the two come to discover that they share a common spirit that transcends the pain and loss of their pasts, and they find in each other a sense of family they lost long ago. Yet, they both face a battle that will demand more heart and courage than any they've ever known.[/i][/b][/color][/size]
[b][i][size=4][color=#ffffff][/color][/size][/i][/b] 
[b][i][font=Comic Sans MS][size=3][color=#ffffff]written by Sujit.R.Varmon to IMDB[/color][/size][/font][/i][/b]","",False,False,False,False,5.0,"2005-10-02","","RT","900809297"
3.5,"For some reason, it took me weeks to actually watch the DVD of ""Million Dollar Baby"" once it was delivered to my mailbox. This is strange mostly because the movie won so many Academy Awards. I knew it would be good, but I was reluctant to watch it for two reasons: It is a mainstream film about boxing; and I had heard that it was really, really depressing.
 
It was, however, everything the critics promised, although I still believe that American critics give too much credance to certain films because they are depressing while ignoring films without a Cinderella story that ends in tragedy. And that's what this is: a Cinderella story that ends in tragedy. It was pretty much made to win an Oscar; It's rather predictable and very depressing. 
 
However, some very well-done boxing scenes, a montage that doesn't feel like a montage, and really convincing performances by Clint Eastwood, Hillary Swank and the always-astounding Morgan Freeman made the movie worth seeing. Unfortunately, though I enjoyed her performance, I did not truly empathize with Hillary Swank's Maggie. The character with whom I felt the most connection was actually the stiffly-acted, gruff old boxing trainer Frankie, whose life is a story about other people paying for his mistakes. I think we all feel the guilt of our own misjudgments harming someone else, and that is Frankie's plight. 
 
So, despite some cheesy lines and moments (Morgan Freeman's half-blind retired boxer punching out a much younger man in defense of a nerdy kid who has been beaten to a bloody pulp) and way too much sentiment, the movie is worth renting. It goes on a little long and ends a little melodramatically, but that's an Oscar-winner for ya.","",False,False,False,False,3.5,"2005-10-05","","RT","900805308"
5.0,"[img]http://images.rottentomatoes.com/images/movie/coverv/34/243734.jpg[/img]

 
[size=3][font=Times New Roman]Maggie is a girl strait from the trailer park.  She finds herself in the big city, waiting tables making it through life by any means necessary.  Maggies family lives off welfare and Medicaid benefits; however, Maggie decided to move to the city and make an honest living waiting tables.  Maggie is only happy doing one thing, boxing.  Maggies inspiration in life was her since deceased father.  One day while watching a fight Maggie discovers the answer to her dreams, Frankie Dunn.  Dunn is an old school boxing trainer that due to previous boxing experiences is reluctant to place his fighters in the big one; he is also one of the best cut men in the business.  With Maggies resilience and Dunns experience, there is nowhere to go but strait to the top.  For one girl from the trailer park her dreams of fighting her way to the top is about to begin.  [/font][/size]

[font=Times New Roman][size=3] [/size][/font]

[font=Times New Roman][size=3]Boxing is about respect; getting it for your self and taking it from the other guy.[/size][/font]

[font=Times New Roman][size=3] [/size][/font]

[font=Times New Roman][size=3]Clint Eastwood, director of Mystic River, Blood Work, Space Cowboys, Absolute Power, Unforgiven, The Rookie, Pale Rider, and Fire Fox directs Million Dollar Baby.  Eastwood does a fantastic job of creating the characters.  Maggie, Morgan Freemans character, and his own are depicted perfectly.  However he injects characters into the film that if they did not appear would not have detracted from the film; however, with their appearance creates a masterpiece.  Examples of this type of character were Danger, the Priest, and Maggies mother/family.[/size][/font]

[font=Times New Roman][size=3] [/size][/font]

[font=Times New Roman][size=3]What the hell is Danger doing?[/size][/font]

[font=Times New Roman][size=3]Looking at another water bottle.[/size][/font]

[font=Times New Roman][size=3] [/size][/font]

[font=Times New Roman][size=3]The actual boxing matches were fantastic.  The training sequences were fun to watch, as were the people at the facility.  The story as a whole was brilliant, but watching each characters story unfold was really the glue that held the film together.  Each character had their own dramatic story that became wrapped-up or involved in Maggies.  Maggies story was obviously the most dramatic and heart wrenching, but you felt sorry for each character.[/size][/font]

[font=Times New Roman][size=3] [/size][/font]

[font=Times New Roman][size=3]Youre standing outside my church comparing god to rice crispies?[/size][/font]

[font=Times New Roman][size=3] [/size][/font]

[font=Times New Roman][size=3]It does not take the greatest movie critic in the world to see the brilliance of this film.  The Academy definitely recognized the correct film to give its awards to.  Hopefully it does the same for Crash.  The characters, story, plot, and theme are all presented masterfully.  I strongly recommend watching this film.  I do not know if I would recommend purchasing it, as this may be a film with a story-line you only need to watch once.[/size][/font]

[font=Times New Roman][size=3] [/size][/font]

[font=Times New Roman][size=3]Maggie did always like taking them out in the first round.[/size][/font]

[font=Times New Roman][size=3] [/size][/font]

[font=Times New Roman][size=3]Grade: A[/size][/font]","",False,False,False,False,5.0,"2005-10-11","","RT","900796252"
