4.0,"[IMG]http://images.amazon.com/images/P/B00005JNP1.01._SCLZZZZZZZ_.jpg[/IMG]

[COLOR=White]I avoided this movie for a long time. I knew about the ending before I watched it, and as a rule, I typically look down my nose at films with dour, depressing climaxes. Whenever  I see a movie designed solely for the sake of depressing its audience, I cant help but see the words Oscar bait subliminally flashing before my eyes. *coughcoughCold Mountaincoughcough* Million Dollar Baby is probably no exception to that rule, as it raked in a great deal of respect come Oscar season. For all of that however, it still stands out as a solidly impressive piece of film-making. 

Million Dollar Baby grabs you in the first five minutes, and holds on to you through every second of the film. Its never slow or pointless and it never talks down to its audience. All of the characters are full, and vibrant and most importantly, they are REAL. 

What I find interesting about MDB is how the focus of the movie has very little to do with Hilary Swanks character. It is more about Clint Eastwood and his personal journey as he battles the traumas of his own past, his current sense of self-worth and his own broken relationships. In this regard, I think it would fair to compare Million Dollar Baby to the Color of Money. In CoM, the audience is drawn towards the younger, slicker Tom Cruise character, when in fact the thrust of the story centers around Paul Newmans character, Fast Eddie Felson.  

In short, despite the fact that I, as a viewer, universally prefer movies with more polished, Spielbergesque climaxes, I am forced to conclude that Million Dollar Baby is every bit a triumph as fans and reviewers say it is.  
[/COLOR]","",False,False,False,False,4.0,"2005-10-17","","RT","900592556"
