5.0,"[center][color=dimgray][img]http://us.movies1.yimg.com/movies.yahoo.com/images/hv/photo/movie_pix/warner_brothers/million_dollar_baby/_group_photos/clint_eastwood10.jpg[/img] [/color][/center]
[center][font=Franklin Gothic Medium][size=1][color=dimgray]Eastwood has had so many years in the business I'm still amazed his films are as powerful and provoctative since the day he was at his prime. But at the age of 75 Clint Eastwood is still going strong at the Box office and at the award ceremonies. Million Dollar Baby knocked out all other competition at last years Oscar's winning Best Actress for Hilary Swank, Best Supporting Actor for Morgan Freeman, Best Director for Eastwood and Best Picture. Mystic River did the same in 2003 and now Million Dollar Baby in 2004, proof that Eastwood certainly hasn't lost his magic touch. It centres around an Old boxing trainer named Frankie (Clint Eastwood) who has battled demons for years ever since he allowed his old mate and trainee Scrap (Morgan Freeman) fight in a match and saw his mate Scrap lose sight in one eye. Scrap at the time was past his prime but Frankie takes responsibilty and continually blames himself. A female named Maggie (Two time Academy award winner Hilary Swank) shows up at Frankie's gym asking to be trained, but Frankie refuses as he doesn't train girls. [/color][/size][/font][/center]
[center][font=Franklin Gothic Medium][size=1][color=#696969][/color][/size][/font] [/center]

[center][font=Franklin Gothic Medium][size=1][color=dimgray]Maggie keeps on trying though with the support of Scrap and frankie tries her out in the ring where she impresses so much she knocks out the competition in seconds, as Maggie, Frankie and Scrap's friendship bonds closer a final match threatens to shape their lives forever.[/color][/size][/font][/center]

[center][color=dimgray][font=Franklin Gothic Medium][size=1]I won't go into detail what happens at the end because if I do I will spoil it for those who have yet to see it, but I will say it's emotionally heartbreaking. And well it brought me too tears as I didn't see it coming. And let me say very few movies make me cry but Million Dollar Baby did. Million Dollar Baby is one emotional punch after another, powerful, beautifully shot and wonderfully acted from all. [/size][/font][font=Franklin Gothic Medium][size=1]The performances are what makes the film so warming and heartwrenching. [/size][/font][/color][/center]
[center] [/center]

[center][color=dimgray][font=Franklin Gothic Medium][size=1]Swank is mesmorising once again, just as she was in Boy's don't cry. Her Maggie is tough, yet underneath lies a woman who's suffering and hasn't had the easiet life. [/size][/font][font=Franklin Gothic Medium][size=1]Swank is extraordinary as Maggie not just looking the part but really testing the audiences emotions and pulling at the heart strings as she did with me. Freeman is solid as always and puts in an excellent performance, the partnership and banter between him and Eastwood is amusing and realistic. And seeing these two legendary actors working together is a wonderful sight to watch. Eastwood is just Eastwood, gruff and tough, yet a man who breaks down when it all gets to him. Just like Mystic River's three leading Actors Million Dollar Baby is the same and the performances don't let the film down. All of them deserved their Oscars.I will also point out Paul Haggis's screenplay is remarkable, and I have yet to see his directional debut Crash but I will be checking it out when released on DVD. Eastwood's direction is flawless the film is just a emotional masterpiece, extraordinary.[/size][/font][/color][/center]


[font=Franklin Gothic Medium][size=6][color=dimgray]A+[/color][/size][/font]","",False,False,False,False,5.0,"2005-11-26","","RT","900786985"
5.0,"There are few movies that lead me to tears. This is one of them. Clint Eastwood has created an emotional powerhouse of a film. He is also terriffic in the lead role. Morgan Freeman also gives arguably the strongest performance of his career as Eastwood's right hand man. That being said, Hillary Swank's Maggie is the best part of this movie. She is brilliant. She creates a lovable character that is so real. Her relationship with Frankie is a beautiful. This is a terrific boxing movie and one of the most wrenching movies I have ever seen and I won't spoil any of it. Eastwood shows that he may be the best director working today. The action sequences are poetic and exciting. What a great movie.","",False,False,False,False,5.0,"2005-12-01","","RT","900811697"
4.5,"This movie took me by surprise.  You go in expecting a grit-iron boxing movie and you get this.  Hilary Swank was nothing short of charming.  Clint Eastwood was a perfectly loveable, jaded grump.  Morgan Freeman continues to impress me with his variety of roles and how he can pull off (almost) every one.  The story was gripping and involved, but not a roller coaster of emotion, which I appreciate.  It had its amusing moments, too, which was surprising.  The ending was a little too slow for me, but it got there when it got there and no one was rushing it.  This movie is worth adding to your collection for sure.","",False,False,False,False,4.5,"2005-12-02","","RT","900821839"
