5.0,"This is the heartfelt masterpiece that separates the good films from the great ones.  Eastwood has given us awesome direction and an awesome story.  Eastwood plays an old trainer who is scared to get his boxers hurt.  He is weathered and worn because of past experiences and lost relationships.  Once he grudgingly accepts the job to train a female boxer (Swank) he begins to soften.  Swank has all the qualities that Eastwood has always wanted in a daughter as well as a boxer.  As he trains her to be an incredible fighter, he loves her as she was his own.  Swank's character begins as a person who needs Eastwood to achieve her dreams.  As the film nears an end, we see that Clint is the one who is desperate.  Words can't describe this wonderfully directed Academy Award winner.   Million Dollar Baby should rank in everyone's top ten.","",False,False,False,False,5.0,"2006-01-11","","RT","900781686"
3.0,"[color=cyan]Rated PG-13 for boxing violence, some down south cussin and sex dialougin, and Clint Eastwood's powerful mad faces.[/color]
[color=cyan] [/color]
[color=cyan] [/color]
[color=cyan]If everyone's life were a movie, would they all have a sad ending? Everyone dies, and that's not uplifting!?![/color]
[color=cyan] [/color]
[color=cyan]What a way to start a review. Million Dollar Baby is about a lady who wants to be a boxing star, her trainer who's old and has raised up stars only to be left by them when they reach stardom, and a half-blind gym manager (Morgan Freeman) who narrates the story.[/color]
[color=cyan] [/color]
[color=cyan]If there's any part of the movie to watch, it's the beginning, because Freeman and Eastwood have excellent comic dialogue picking on each other. There are minor characters that are funny additions too, such as an ""all-heart"" boxer who wears tight pants and a black boxer who thinks he's hot stuff. [/color]
[color=cyan] [/color]
[color=cyan]Ebert says this movie gives the best overview of boxing he's ever seen, but why do we need to see that? We all know people get messed up over boxing injuries. The joyful uplifting first 80 minutes of the film are misplaced with the intentionally depressing ending. If you watch it, you should be prepared not to be emotionally molested when the sad part comes.[/color]
[color=cyan] [/color]
[color=cyan]My parting shot would be that I like the first 80 minutes, a heck of a lot in fact, and would watch that part anytime. Then 'click', and the movie would go 'down for the count'.[/color]
[color=cyan] [/color]
