4.5,"So classes have resumed. I'll be plowing through three political science courses for my major this semester, in addition to ""Science Fiction and Philosophy,"" which I consider to be a very well-deserved elective. Besides, also working with ""American Political Systems,"" ""Introduction to Comparative Politics"" and ""Energy Policy,"" I think I'll need something to lighten the load a little bit, and I've already plowed through the first seven chapters of ""2001: A Space Odyssey."" :D
  
  [b][i]Million Dollar Baby[/i] (2004)
  **** out of ****
  [/b]
  Had a chance to revisit thisrecently, and now more than ever it's apparent that this is going to be a [i]justified [/i]classic amongst the same ranks of [i]Casablanca [/i]and the works, expertly employing tried-and-true storytelling methods in the telling of a classic story that focuses on nothing more than the characters at its epicenter. Eastwood's Frankie is the initially reluctant trainer to Swank's boxer Maggie, who longs to fight to escape her economically strained lifestyle, their vacant family relations finding solace in each other's company. The stripped-down direction not only finds a kind of simplistic purity in the proceedings, but unearths the weathered lives and emotions of Frankie, Maggie, and Morgan Freeman's Scrap (the film's Greek chorus, if you will), communicating with unstated ease the weight of life on their collective existence. The unnecasssarily controversial third-act change of direction isn't an advocation of political status in any way, but a profound look at the power of love and sacrifice when it seems like life hasn't anything else to offer.
  
  [b][i]Brokeback Mountain[/i] (2005)
  **** out of ****
  
  [/b]I'll certainly need at least a second veiwing of this to fully reflect on and absorb what it has to offer, but let me simply state that Ang Lee's stripped-down look at social repression and the destroyed lives that arise out of such a situation is a deeply and unexpectedly heartbreaking, and a potential rival for (the as-of-yet unreviewed) [i]Munich[/i] as best movie of the year. However, as far as the whole ""gay cowboy romance"" deal, all I can say is: poppycock. So far, from what I've seen, [i]Brokeback Mountain [/i]is being praised and derided from many angles for something it is not, the stakes being driven down more into the buzz surrounding the film than the work in itself. In committing the original seven-page short story to screen, Lee employs a slightly fractured means of editing that emphasizes the randomness of life, passion, as well as the external controls of fate that largely determine the course these characters' lives take. Ledger's Ennis and Gyllenhaal Jack find their unexpected desire for each other whilst working on the titular mountain as cattle hands during the summer of 1963; well aware that their feelings and behavior would be met with violent response in society, they are forced to assume normal hetero lives as they marry and have children, their true selves relegated to only expressing their feelings during fleeting ""fishing trips"" when they can reclude themselves from man's world in the wilderness. As over twenty years pass, one feels the weight of repression and what it does to these lives with an unexpected emotional plow, the final, heartbreaking scene doubling as a reinforcement of the need to keep one's identity in the closet in such harsh climates, in addition to summarizing with incredibly ease the deep emotional pain brought about by such unaccepting veiws towards someone different than yourself.","",False,False,False,False,4.5,"2006-01-17","","RT","900830610"
4.0,"A very good film that asks very relevant questions. I had my doubts, though, for the longest time during this movie--it was all about boxing, and her rising fame, and I had very little interest in the area. It was hard to believe for me that there was something more--but there is, and its profound, and I'm glad to have stuck around to see the whole thing!","",False,False,False,False,4.0,"2006-01-17","","RT","9079284"
