2.5,"[b]Million Dollar Baby[/b], directed by Clint Eastwood, won a slew of Academy Awards, including Best Picture, Best Director and Best Actress (Hillary Swank).  It's the story of a 31 year old named Maggie Fitzgerald (Swank) determined to make it as a boxer despite little experience or formal training.  When she shows up at Frankie Dunn's gym, the grizzled Frankie (Eastwood) wants nothing to do with her.  The old school, chauvinistic Frankie doesn't believe women belong in the ring, and doesn't want anything to do with Maggie.  However Eddie ""Scrap"" Dupris (Morgan Freeman), an aging boxer Frankie once trained, takes an interest in Maggie and tries to convince Frankie to do the same after Frankie's latest protege leaves him for another trainer/manager.  Reluctantly Frankie agrees, and he takes the same slow, deliberate appraoch with Maggie he did with his younger male boxers.  Maggie begins her ascent, and Frankie slowly and begrudgingly begins to respect and admire Maggie.   Soon an unexpected twist occurs, which I won't reveal in case someone actually may read this (like anyone reads my reviews) or someone has yet to see this movie (just as unlikely), which sends the movie in an entirely different direction.
 
So what did I find so wrong about this movie that thousands of critics, millions of viewers, and even the Academy Awards did not?  Well despite the unexpected twist, this is an extremely flawed movie.  I think the allure for most was the novelty of a female boxer.  Hey I'll admit not a bad idea.  However the first part of the script is so formulaic that if this novelty didn't exist, one would have dismissed the film early on.  Put a guy in Maggie's role and nobody would have cared.  Why?  It's a pedestrian ""rags to riches"" story riddled with cliched characters.  Maggie is poor white trash.  Frankie the stubborn, grizzled trainer.  Only the Freeman character has much depth.  Plus some of the peripheral characters are just plan annoying.  The scrawny white kid who hangs around the gym annoying everyone.  The over-the-top stereotyping of Maggie's trailer park family.  I'm not seeing anything new here.  And the ""twist"", although welcomed and unexpected, ends up wallowing in it's own set of cliches.  
 
Eastwood has directed some fine films, but I stand almost alone saying this isn't one of them.  As much as I wanted to join the masses in their adoration of this film, I just couldn't.  A dissappointment.","",False,False,False,False,2.5,"2006-02-16","","RT","900789687"
