4.5,"[b]DVD [/b]First Viewing, 3 Eastwood films seen
 
[center][url=""http://www.rottentomatoes.com/m/million_dollar_baby/gallery.php?page=20&size=lores&nopop=1""][img]http://images.rottentomatoes.com/images/movie/gallery/1139338/photo_21.jpg[/img][/url][/center]
 
[b]NOTE[/b]: I do not know if I am writing this review to early after watching this, but I feel like the only way to express my feelings toward the film is to write about it.
 
Frankie Dunn (Clint Eastwood) is a veteran in training boxers. He has done this almost all of his life, and even trained Eddie Scrap (Morgan Freeman), a boxer who was once the best. These two guys are so in love with the sport, that even in their old age they can not forget boxing, until the day they die. So, they run the Hit Pit gym where they tran up-and-coming boxers. Then one day, Maggie Fitzgerald (Hilary Swank) walks in and asks Clint Eastwood to train her and he gives her a simple reply, ""I don't train girls."" Maggie will not accept this so everyday when she isn't waiting tables (which has been her main job since thirteen) she is in the gym boxing to prove to Frankie she is worth it. Any intelligent person from either watching the trailer or from just, as I said before, being inteligent would probably guess that Eastwood eventually throws in the towel and trains her. If you did guess this, then you are correct. The rest of the film chronicles Swank progressing in her sport all the way until the title match, but not without the help of Freeman and Eastwood.
 
It may seem like a basic plot and it is, but there is so much more depth to the film that meets the eye. The acting is definitely one of the two things that make the movie. Not one actor in the whole film isn't delivering their A game at all times. Hilary Swank won her second Best Actress award for her performance (she had one the award previously in 1999 for [i]Boys Don't Cry[/i]) and she completely deserved it. Although I have not seen three of the movies that had performances for Best Actress, I do not think anyone could have topped Hilary's. This is also coming from a guy who is mad at the Academy for not giving Winslet Best Actress for [i]Eternal Sunshine of the Spotless Mind[/i]. But at the end, Swank's performance is better than Winslet's. Clint Eastwood was nominated for Best Actor but lost to Jaime Foxx for RAY. It isn't that Eastwood was bad in the film, in fact he was truly wonderful, it is just no one had anything on Foxx's performance. If 2004 wasn't a year where Johnny Depp, Jaime Foxx, Leonardo DiCaprio, and Don Cheadle were all up for the award, I think Eastwood may have snagged it. Then we have Morgan Freeman who yet again, delivers a powerhouse peformance in which he won his first oscar in the Best Supporting Actor category. Freeman also narrates the film and uses a barely audible voice to show authenticity. It bugged me in the fact that I had to watch the film in subtitles to hear what he was saying, I now semi agree with Eastwood's decision to make Freeman's voice go deep, rough, and quiet to make his character feel realistic. In the end, [i]Million Dollar Baby[/i] contains the best acting that I have seen all year.
 
Clint Eastwood may just be one of the best directors in the business and BABY is another excellent example to prove it. I was taken over by him as director when I saw [i]Mystic River[/i] back in 2003. He doesn't waste camera time in his films because every scene he shoots serves a purpose. I am definitely more into his transition to directing mature films like [i]Unforgiven[/i], [i]Mystic River[/i], and [i]Million Dollar Baby[/i] than all of the westerns he loved to be in. Again, that is just some kid's opinion and some eighty year old guy may disagree. The beauty of film criticism!
 
Another aspect of the film I have yet to talk about are the chemistry between the characters and the lighting. The chemistry between Eastwood and Swank is the main part of the movie, but I also enjoyed Eastwood and Freeman's friendship. Chemistry is one of the main things people overlook in their movie-going experiences. Swank and Eastwood's relationship develops throughout the movie from being strictly business to father-daughter who love each other very much. Dunn's daughter never writes back to the weekly letters that he has been writing her for years, so Swank eventually plays the role of the daughter Eastwood never had. It is odd because by the end, you feel like you are watching a daughter talk to her father as if she has known him all of her life. It shows true talent when two actors can capture you like that. And the funny thing is, Eastwood and Freeman also captured me and made me feels as if I was watching two best friends bickering about things like real friends would do.
 
To conclude, I really think that this film deserved all of the Oscars it received (Best Picture, Best Actress, Best Director, and Best Supporting Actor) and should not be missed by anyone.","",False,False,False,False,4.5,"2006-04-15","","RT","900777435"
