3.5,"...but since there has hardly been a watchable thing released yet this year, I find myself perusing the rental lists for things I always had meant to get around to. Hmm..I have a lot of mixed feelings here...let's start with the minuses and get them out of the way, because the pluses are always more fun.  1. Morgan Freeman's performance was just as consistently great as always, but I had two problems with it, neither of which are his fault. He honestly wasnt given that much to do, in terms of emotion or conflict or character development; he's given so many more outstanding performances in better roles, it really feels like his Oscar was a giveaway one because he deserved it for earlier things, almost like Crowe's was for Gladiator. Also, I found his dry voiceover narration distracting at best, silly at worst; between Shawshank Redemption (where it was necessary first person storytelling, an unusual hook and also often profound) and the recent Penguins movie (where he carried the whole film along) it's just been done, done already. And, worse, the fact that he was a character in this film that had [i]no[/i] way of knowing much of the story he was 'explaining' to us while we were already watching it, it was unrealistic and unnecessary.  2. All the medical aspects of her post-trauma life & treatment were just completely inaccurate. Unfortunately, I had been told going in about the dramatic 'twist' in the film and, so, was probably deprived of the intensity or shock I would have felt in the theater that might have suspended disbelief enough for me not to notice, but everything from the bed she occupied to the almost immediate onset of bedsores (even though they state she has the best care possible) to the amputation of obviously non-gangrenous leg, as well as her speech not matching her breathing apparatus just kept leaping out at me.  I still was able to be deeply moved and appreciate the beautiful filmmaking and great acting until her death scene, which was just downright ridiculous.  Watching it with a person who has no medical knowledge who, after the film was over, asked ""aren't there alarms for life-support machines when theyre turned off?"" ""isn't there more than one nurse per floor?"" ""how can a visitor just stroll down the hall in the middle of the night?"" ""aren't there alarms in the nursing station when she flatlines?"" etc, etc...... made me realize it wasn't nitpicking; it was  a little sloppy. I suppose, also, all of Clint's reading lead him to know how to obtain vials of epinephrine, how to use it in her IV, the dosage to give, etc., but walking out of the room empty handed and leaving the bag with it all in there to quietly amble all the way out of the hospital? sigh.....I guess he must have also read this state-of-the-art hospital couldnt afford staff or alarms and felt confident. OK, OK, enough sniping already....now onto the good stuff and, fortunately, there's a lot.  1. I know there wasn't a lot of character development, even among the three leads, but they are all played so outstandingly and it fits the simplistic underplayed tone of the film so well, it really doesn't matter. All three of these Oscar noms was well deserved; Clint Eastwood, however, is the main one who should have taken it home. I know he probably didn't because he was awarded the others but, honestly, this has to be his best performance in I dont know how many years; maybe EVER.  2. The direction, art direction and cinematography are close to genius. Most films with a distinct ""style"" (think One Hour Photo, Natural Born Killers, Pi, etc) can be overshadowed by the direction so much it takes away from the story. Here was an understated, quiet, intelligent movie style of pacing, lighting and camera angles that engrossed you without ever detracting from the people it was supporting, but noticable all along in it's unwavering form.  3. For such a heartbreaking story of broken people all thinking there is something in each of the others to let them hang onto a thread of hope, or reason for life, that ends in failure for all, this movie never veers into gooey sentimentality, never milks you just for tears, never goes for hysteria and even spares you the devastation of Clint's fate, choosing instead to leave you alone to find the answers yourself over simple, quietly scored end credits. It is not a political piece, not a pro-euthanasia propaganda, not a pro- or anti-faith statement. It's very simply the telling of a story, and if you can just let go and go along for the fairy tale ride, it's fantastic. I don't know why that's so easy to do; usually when a movie seems patently unrealistic (at least within it's own genre or intent) it's just spoiled for me. But I find myself having no ill feelings toward this film; I actually can see myself watching it several more times and look forward to the extras and, while watching it, never had any desire for it to end and never cared about it's implausability or flaws....like I said, it's very easy to let all that go and just live with these marvelous actors' creations. That's the beauty of both the actors and the direction and that's why it winds up being a good, very good piece of work.","",False,False,False,False,3.5,"2006-05-06","","RT","900826463"
5.0,"the realest movie i have seen","",False,False,False,False,5.0,"2006-05-06","","RT","146993753"
3.0,"[font=Times New Roman][size=3][color=#000000]I didnt see it when it was in theatres because I knew what happened and I thought it was stupid. For those of you who dont know (and its been like 2 years so youre probably not that interested anyway) about 2/3 of the way through this movie theres-for lack of a better term-a SUCKER PUNCH. Up until that point, youre watching a boxing movie. Great performances. Good dialogue. Unique directing, editing, and acting from Clint Eastwood. Better, to my mind, than anything hed ever done. I was thinking up until the twist-and I still feel this way now- that the Oscar this movie should have really won was for Best Actor for Clint Eastwood. Morgan Freeman has done about a dozen things better than this (not that he was bad at all, but Morgan Freeman was stellar in freaking March of the Penguins) and he only got his out of the guilt the Academy felt at snubbing him so many times. I dont like Hillary Swank, but I thought she was excellent. I was wondering a bit at whether Morgan Freeman and Clint Eastwood were having a gravelly voice contest for most of the film as they scraped 2 octaves below C every time they opened their mouths, but their interaction was so endearing and fun that going into THE SUCKER PUNCH. I was really, really liking the movie. Stop reading the review right now if you dont want to know.[/color][/size][/font]
 

 
[font=Times New Roman][size=3][color=#000000]Hillary Swanks character has just won the title, when she gets sucker punched from behind, lands on her corner stool, and breaks her neck. Happens quicker than it took you to read that last sentence, and the rest of the movie is about her characters slow slide towards death. Her agonizing, improbable, increasingly absurd slide towards euthanasia via Clint Eastwoods character. Now my problem is not the euthanasia itself, which is weird, because thats what I thought would be my problem. I am extremely torn on the issue and I thought the film was going to be a preachy diatribe about how humane euthanasia is. Thats not the films bent. The decision to end her life isnt treated by the characters in the film as the humane thing or even the right thing, and it clearly destroys the lives of everyone involves in making the decision. Heres the problem though: in no way could it have happened. The way it was done was so absurd as to border on ludicrous. Im not a doctor, but I have dated a nurse practitioner for 4 years and I work at a nursing college so I have a little medical knowledge, but is there any medical facility (let alone the best care money could buy) in the country where Clint Eastwood could have strolled in with a bag of syringes, disconnected her breathing machine, loaded her with adrenaline, and strolled out with no one noticing. No alarms. No nurses coming. The movie doesnt seem to indicate that the police were called, which is odd because this is a famous boxer who was obviously euthanized and her manager goes missing the day it happens. Huh. Hmmm. Wonder what happened there! I just found it all so completely ridiculous that it took me out of the movie and eroded most of the good will that the film had built going into THE SUCKER PUNCH. In the end, Im still giving it a positive rating because the acting is stellar and 2/3 of the movie is a really excellent sports movie. However, that last 1/3 is melodramatic and dumb. So if you havent seen the movie, when Hillary Swank gets SUCKER PUNCHED, do yourself a favor and end the movie there.[/color][/size][/font]
