2.0,"[center][font=Tahoma][b][img]http://media.movieweb.com/galleries/2744/1899/lo/coc.jpg[/img][/b][/font][/center]
[font=Tahoma][/font] 
[font=Tahoma][b]A hardened fighter-cum-trainer works with a determined woman in her attempt to establish herself as a boxer.[/b][/font]
[b][font=Tahoma][/font][/b] 
[center][img]http://media.movieweb.com/galleries/2744/1899/lo/coh.jpg[/img][/center]
[b][font=Tahoma][/font][/b] 
[b][font=Tahoma]Clint Eastwood's newest critical and commercial achievement has generated everything from immense controversy to radical adoration. The screenplay, written by Paul Haggis, is adapted from the short stories of, real life fight manager and cutman, Jerry Boyd, who wrote under the alias of F.X. Toole. Eastwood's picture earned seven Oscar nominations, winning five that consist of ""Best Supporting Actor"" (Morgan Freeman), ""Best Actress"" (Hilary Swank), ""Best Direction"" (Clint Eastwood), and ""Best Picture"". Despite the critical applaud, Million Dollar Baby met with much debate from disability rights activists, Gaelic speaking people, and sportswriters. And while several have been bothered with its ""political questioning"" and unrealistic look at boxing, the feature has unanimously been titled ""The Best Film of 2004"". 

What can I say about Million Dollar Baby that hasn't already been said; I guess alot, since I didn't care for it. The biggest problem I have is how banal it is; keep in mind, Crash writer/director, Paul Haggis wrote the screenplay. How can we expect anything else from the man but terribly obvious clichés? Up until the very end, the picture is overflowing with stereotypical characters that receive little or no development. Even the conclusion, granted it had been spoiled for me, seemed too melodramatic and to serve as nothing more than cheap sentimentality. 

Many critics and moviegoers have agreed that the acting and cinematography improve the trite material, but it did no such thing for me. While Eastwood's dark, drab, and gritty look is effective for the feature, I found all of the performances to be above average at best. Hilary Swank's charismatic and dedicated portrayal is fine, but when compared to the other Oscar nominees, I feel they were snubbed. Eastwood's hardened yet sympathetic performance is actually fairly impressive, and Morgan Freeman plays his rather typical background role admirably. 

Overall, Million Dollar Baby's performances cannot save the uninspired material that failed to engage me.[/font][/b]
[b][font=Tahoma][/font][/b] 
[center][img]http://media.movieweb.com/galleries/2744/1899/lo/cou.jpg[/img][/center]","",False,False,False,False,2.0,"2006-07-23","","RT","900674838"
