4.5,"I don't like boxing.  I think it's horrible, brutal, and barbaric.  It's savagery, driven by the same instincts that created gladiatorial combat.  I see no beauty and no dignity in it.

The dignity in this movie is after the boxing is over.  This means that this entire review, save one paragraph at the end, will be full of spoilers.  O Six Readers, if you haven't seen it yet, stand by.  There'll probably be another review later today.  (Our movie pile from the library's getting too high again.)

The right to die is one near and dear to my heart.  When my godmother's sister died, she was very old and in very poor health--80-odd, I think, and terminal, inoperable stomach cancer.  My initial comment was, ""Thank God!""  But she [i]was[/i] old, and she was Catholic--Mexican Catholic, at that--and she held on in great pain and loneliness.  (My godmother died years earlier; she'd taken care of her sister all their adult lives.)  It was over, and not a moment too soon.

Physical loss doesn't mean as much to me as mental--heck, at least if I were paralyzed, I wouldn't [i]hurt[/i] all the time anymore.  But I can certainly see that, if your life's goal is to be a boxing champion, the physical loss of near-total paralysis (if you can still talk, it's not total) would be devastating the way loss of mind would be to me.  I can very much see not wanting to live after that.

Hilary Swank is clearly a substitute daughter for Clint Eastwood in this film.  This is what makes [i]his[/i] battle so agonizing.  He must give up the daughter who loves him.  The daughter he loves has long since given him up.  Not to mention the whole ""it's a sin"" thing.

When Alan Alda found out that Morgan Freeman was considered a 3-1 favorite for Best Supporting Actor the year they were both up for the Oscar, he agreed and stopped worrying what he'd write for his own acceptance speech.  True, Morgan Freeman kind of disappears from the movie for a while, but it is he who galvanizes Clint Eastwood's decision to help Hilary Swank die.

Oh, and the bit that isn't a spoiler?  The gas station Clint and Hilary stop in when they're visiting her family is [i]definitely[/i] not in Missouri.  It's in Altadena, CA, about six or eight blocks from my mother's house and next door to the dentist I saw when I was a child.  The same gas station appears in a lot of things; I don't know if they actually sell gas there.  It was a gas station in Mississippi on an episode of [i]Crossing Jordan[/i] and in the Aretha Franklin video ""Pink Cadillac.""","",False,False,False,False,4.5,"2006-10-01","","RT","900810868"
