5.0,"Eastwood just gets better and better.Who would have thought that a fairly average actor in television ( albeit with the matinee idol/hunk looks necessary for getting a foothold on the media career ladder ) would become easily one of the greatest contemporary film directors in the western world. He has not missed a beat since ""Unforgiven"" and this doesn't disappoint. As with ""Mystic River"" the subject matter is bound entirely in the aspirations and agonies, the virtues and the fatalism of life experience. The film is suffused with the most sombre of tones and it is certainly a human tragedy writ large. The emotional resonance of this film is quite extraordinary.If you want rip-roaring entertainment, go elsewhere. If, however, you want totally gripping drama from a master film maker at the height of his creative powers, look no further. One wonders just how many great movies this man has in him, given that time is granted to him. He reminds me of John Houston, knocking out stunners to the last.","",False,False,False,False,5.0,"2007-03-13","","RT","637056739"
