5.0,"Love it, love it, love it. This one caused a lot of bad blood in 2004, because it basically drew a line between supporters of Eastwood and Scorsese (who made The Aviator that year). .I personally see no reason to pick sides, as they are the 2 best filmmakers alive today (Tarantino could be up there if he'd get a little more work ethic). .but, if I have to speak honestly on the 2004 Oscar race, this was the better film by leaps and bounds. Eastwood directed, acted, and composed the score for this picture, and the whole thing feels about as personal and beautiful as movies get. I reflected on this film for days after I saw it. Hilary Swank roared back into the spotlight with her brilliant performance as Maggie, Freeman got his first Oscar, and Eastwood set his own high watermark for acting. Even as a simple sports movie, this film elevates the genre with themes of redemption and rebirth. But I was unprepared for the complex, agonizing emotional wallop of the film's 3rd act. The film is touching, funny, human, and haunting, and one of my favorite films. Its brilliance defies the teachings of film textbooks. It very simply, very unsuspectingly draws you into the lives of its characters, in much the same way that Frankie is drawn into Maggie's life without ever quite realizing it. The film simply exists, and I wouldn't  have it any other way.","",False,False,False,False,5.0,"2007-05-31","","RT","287450315"
2.5,"Good movie! The end was not what I thought it would be.","",False,False,False,False,2.5,"2007-05-31","","RT","579585521"
4.5,"Review from when I first saw it in theaters:
Clint Eastwoodâs Million Dollar Baby is one of the most poignant films this critic has ever seen. Baby is about a man (Eastwood) who, reluctantly, does whatever it takes to help a woman (Oscar-winner Hilary Swank). 
The relationship between the man, Frankie, and the woman, Maggie, is not romantic, but a fill-in father-daughter relationship. Frankie and his daughter donât speak, and Maggieâs father died when she was a young girl. A love of boxing is what brings these two lonely people together. 
Maggie pleads Frankie to be her trainer, but Frankie wants nothing to do with her. Frankieâs former trainee and confidant, played terrifically by Morgan Freeman, gives Maggie a few pointers that seem to impress Frankie, although he doesnât admit it. Finally, Frankie reluctantly agrees to train Maggie. After training Maggie for about a year, she starts to become a successful fighter and gets the chance to fight the champ. Being overly cautious, Frankie at first turns down the offer, but, after Maggie wins another big fight, he gets a good deal for her to fight the champ. 
Eastwood does not only do a great job in directing, but he also gives a gut-wrenchingly powerful performance as the burdened Frankie. Swank, who became famous as The Next Karate Kid and garnered critical praise about 10 years later for her work as the cross-dressing Teena Brandon/Brandon Teena in Boys Donât Cry, for which she won the Best Actress Oscar, is also terrific in Baby and is very deserving of a second Oscar for her emotional performance as the all-but-abandoned Maggie. Freeman gives a cool, calm and collected supporting performance. 
The movie, while it took a little extra time to get into the story, can basically be classified as a surprise hit. Most people, including myself, knew very little or nothing at all about this film until November or December, when the big critics started praising it and its starsâ performances. 
For most of his acting career, Eastwood has been known for playing the extremely macho characters. In Baby, he almost seems to poke fun at the typecast and play a somewhat-macho character that gets over it when he crosses paths with Maggie.","60c04286-3eb9-43bc-8f82-bc57ba95fee5",False,False,False,False,4.5,"2007-05-31","","RT","782082852"
