3.0,"million dollar baby is a strange beast. the opening acts are merely a retooled version of the rocky movies. perseverance, fighting for your dreams.....all stuff weve seen a (pardon the pun) million times before. its the 3rd act that this really turns into a film. i was not particularly impressed with any of the performances, at least not to the degree that everyone else seems to suggest. there is a bit too much archetype in them for my liking. this is especially true of eastwood as he plays the same sad- eyed, world-weary, old codger he has in one way or another since the 70s with the siegel and leone films. does it work? sure, why not? is it tired? absolutely. what is impressive about this film is that eastwood definitely flexes his visual storytelling muscle. there are some really excellent, moody shots that fit the tone of the film and their respective scenes perfectly. for example, the scene in the rehab facility when he walks out at the end. the dark hallway that he walks down with light coming through the glass doors at the end of it and the reflection of the clean floors. perfect symmetry. what also makes this film worth watching is that it takes on an incredibly difficult issue head on. not only that but it does not moralize about it to any recognizable extent.","a8d74039-7e36-43d0-915c-3be809fc4a88",False,False,False,False,3.0,"2007-06-05","","RT","783044860"
