4.0,"Deseptively powerful, Clint Eastwoods second big oscar winner (the first been 1992's haunting ""Unforgiven"") appears to be a ""standard cranky old vet teaches the naiive novice"" cliche ridden sports film, but watching it (and getting through a slow start) reveals something quite surprising and very moving. The three headlining performers deliver what's expected of them (Clint and Morgan reprise their wonderfully grissled chemistry from the previously mentioned film), with Hilary Swank turning in an outstanding lead, really getting us behind Maggie and her relationship with her difficult manager, played with surprising emotion by Eastwood. The characters are all well fleshed out, their histories bleeding through the film constantly, and really add a lot of power to what would have otherwise been a merely adequate drama. Not Clint's best, but one of them at least, well worth watching.","",False,False,False,False,4.0,"2007-06-17","","RT","783821021"
3.5,"There's a lot of stuff going on in this film that I found fascinating.  Very literate.  Good showing from director Eastwood.","",False,False,False,False,3.5,"2007-06-17","","RT","784493741"
4.5,"A film that takes time and patience to appreciate, this film is one of the most subtle and elegant studio films of the decade, and is truly a marvel that was rightly rewarded by the Academy as the best film of 2004. The characters, Maggie, Frankie, and Scrap are outsiders, all having been dealt bad hands in life. The story is ultimately not about boxing, but about the pseudo-family that these three people make, an island of human connection in a world of isolation. The character arcs that are made by these three are remarkable, especially that of Frankie, who goes from a crusty old man to someone who, with a little push here and there, reveals a fighter's heart and courage. The film is deceptive in its approach, and is actually much different than one would glean from the advertising, and as such can feel a little manipulative. However, if one follows the characters' personal journeys, the actions and decisions they make perfectly flow from their human bonds and connections. Some may complain as to why these choices are made, and why other roads aren't pursued, but that's what great cinema is for, debate. Overall a fine piece of art, with unremarkable but appropriate costumes, art direction and editing, with an achingly melancholic score (also by Eastwood), all capped by an assured and masterful sense of direction by a modern master. Definitely a classic of sport, drama, or any genre.","",False,False,False,False,4.5,"2007-06-17","","RT","783343764"
4.5,"A fine example of how brilliant Clint Eastwood can be as a filmmaker. Hilary Swank, Eastwood and Morgan Freeman are all excellent. I don't want to say too much because I don't want to ruin anything for people that haven't seen this yet..but it's great.","",False,False,False,False,4.5,"2007-06-17","","RT","783078101"
