4.5,"Another boxing related film, but unlike 'Rocky Balboa', which was an uplifting piece, this one is quite a downer. 

Frankie Dunn (Eastwood) is an old gruff boxing trainer/manager who has had his share of successes. Sadly, his daughter doesn't' acknowledge him anymore, and boxing essentially is his family. A former boxer, Scrap (Freeman) is the only one who Dunn is relatively close to.  

Along comes Maggie Fitzgerald (Hillary Swank) , a younger 30 something from the poor side of the tracks, looking to catch a break in women's boxing. She's a determined fighter, willing to give everything she's got to get her chance. Unfortunately, Dunn doesn't train women, and he makes that clear.

Relentless, Maggie continues going to the gym sharpening her skills on her own, and with Scrap's prodding,  Dunn steps in telling her what she's doing wrong, and reluctantly takes her on as a trainee. From there, they have their differences, but we see them getting closer, and Dunn witnesses the problematic relationship she has with her family. A rotten bunch that really want nothing to do with her, other than when it suits them. That in itself is tragic, and we see a father/daughter like relationship develop between Frankie and Maggie.

He takes her to the bigtime, and she's a phenom. This is where the downer comes in. 

It's a story that's both heartwarming and tragic, where an ethical issue becomes center stage. And I'm telling you, it can potentially drain the feelings you had about the film prior to this. Many can hate the movie based on this alone. 

Whichever way you fall on that issue, or if you're torn, it's still a powerful story, and one worth visiting.","",False,False,False,False,4.5,"2007-09-10","","RT","793088651"
