5.0,"In Million Dollar Baby, Eastwood plays Frankie Dunn, proprietor of Dunn's Gym and longtime boxing trainer and cut man. He is a man haunted by his past, attending regular mass, despite a near atheist stance on life. He hopes to find a way to one day reconcile with his daughter, with whom he hasn't spoken in years. Dunn also holds onto the blame for the events that led to his friend and former boxer, Scraps (Morgan Freeman), losing an eye in the ring. Scraps now maintains Dunn's Gym, an all around fix-it and clean-up man, and lives in a room in the back. Maggie Fitzgerald (Hilary Swank) is a boxing hopeful who is seeking the proper training to elevate her skills to the next level. She is struggling to get by, working as a waitress and training in gyms every spare moment she has. When she approaches Dunn to assist her training, he has no interest in working with a girl. Through her persistence and some subtle urging from Scrap, Maggie convinces Dunn to give her a shot. To his great surprise, she may well be the most talented boxer he's ever worked with.

There has been a lot of talk that this is not a boxing movie. Warner Brothers has done their best to promote the film as such, likely afraid that a story of the brutal sport would alienate audiences. As a boxing fan myself, I tend to disagree. Million Dollar Baby is very much a boxing film. In fact, it's one of the best boxing films ever made. It's also much more than a boxing film. Rocky is often considered one of the great films of the sport, but many forget that Rocky itself was about much more than what went on in the ring. Heck, Rocky didn't even win in the end! Likewise MDB will appeal to and touch anyone, regardless of an interest level in the sport. It is a boxing movie, but it's not at all reliant on that aspect.

The Paul Haggis-penned script for MDB, based on the short story out of the book Rope Burns by F.X. Toole, is an epic tale of redemption. Maggie is struggling to escape the white trash life she's always hated, to have that one chance at overcoming mediocrity in life. Frankie lives his life in the past, a victim of a Catholic guilt that stems from the mistakes of his past and weighs on his each and every breath. Frankie and Maggie are the unlikeliest of pairings, but their relationship completes the life journey both characters are seeking.

The trick of any story like this is to achieve these extreme dramatic highs and lows without becoming a tear-jerker. When an audience feels as though they are being manipulated, you have lost. The casting of MDB does the justice this story deserves. I wasn't so sure about seeing Eastwood in such an emotionally demanding role. After all, he's almost the ultimate example of a Hollywood tough guy. But even with that preconception, he overcomes. Eastwood is incredibly good here, easily his best performance in many years and possibly his best. Period. There is a moment where Eastwood breaks down and cries, something I don't think I ever expected to see on screen. He pulls it off very gracefully.

Hilary Swank is an actress who burst onto the scene a few years ago with a terrific performance in Boys Don't Cry. In her time since, she's had a few good parts, but nothing that has challenged her or allowed her to really flex her skills the way Boys did. MDB finally gives her that chance, and Swank didn't hesitate to sink her teeth into the part of Maggie. Adding an additional 19 pounds of pure muscle to her already athletic physique, Swank looks chiseled and hard. As she hones her boxing skills in the film and begins to decisively defeat her opponents, you believe that she is knocking these women out.

Morgan Freeman, who previously worked with Eastwood in Unforgiven, serves a supporting role and also narrates the film. Freeman's distinctive voice provides a nice backdrop for the story and weaves everything together nicely. Freeman is one of the best actors out there and his performance here is up to his usual standards. He sports a few extra muscles himself, with some bulging biceps, and even gets the chance to put them to use to teach a young punk, played by Anthony Mackey, a lesson.

If you have read anything about this film so far, you probably know that there is an element to the story that everyone is being careful not to reveal. I urge you to avoid reading too much about this film, because this aspect of the story is something that, if you discover it ahead of time, could potentially ruin what was, for me, one of the most memorable and emotional experiences I've had at the cinema in a long, long time. This is at times a difficult film. If you pardon the pun, Eastwood does not pull punches. If you submit yourself to this film and let down the cynical guard modern film has given many of us, MDB is a very rewarding experience.

Eastwood has crafted one of the most heartfelt and honest works of his career. This is an emotionally naked film with more heart and spirit than just about every other Hollywood film this year put together. It's the kind of work that reminds me why I first fell in love with the art of film in the first place. In the days since seeing this film, the images are still with me. Million Dollar Baby is impossible to forget.","",False,False,False,False,5.0,"2007-09-25","","RT","788454199"
