5.0,"Okay, it IS a boxing movie, but a lot more than that. While there's plenty of ring footage, it's certainly not an ""action"" film, boxing ""tourism"" for fanatics and fans.

It's about people, lives, and why people do sport, the dreams it fulfills, the passions it taps into.

So while it's a movie sports professionals will love, it's just as much for non-athletes, amateurs.

I have never trained seriously for any sport. I learned more of what that is about from this movie--why anyone would want to do this, what drives them, the deep satisfactions.

 So, recommend this to the ""non-believers,"" those toying with the idea of serious sports.

There's more. The gender issue is central--women's lives and women in sports. And the message is not sugar coated, or sentimental--no preaching. Eastwood's move from open refusal to train women, to grudging acceptance, to open admiration and more--this is believable, telling, ""true life,"" transforming. 

That's the core. Around this is built a gritty world of tough love, of failure and recovery, of daily drudgery and moments of hope. The gym is a slice of the world, full of all what's good and bad, dreams in process, arrogant young toughs, gentle ""has beens.""

It's a film everyone can love, and learn from, and feel good about themselves and what everyone is capable of.","d71da81e-fa8b-47a5-af8b-4b28503a5c72",False,False,False,False,5.0,"2007-10-03","","RT","797466352"
