4.5,"It'll be a sad day when illustrious Hollywood allumni Clint Eastwood passes from this world. It will be a sad day indeed as Hollywood will have lost one of their most dedicated and passionate filmmakers of all time. In a day when the latest Hollywood reels spin celluloid of souless remakes of older films that transparently transcend the boundaries of mediocrity in storytelling and where the use of ones imagination comes as a second thought being replaced with gigantuan eyepopping CGI garbage that focuses solely on distracting and fooling the viewer into thinking that the film is more than it actually is. Eastwood is an original. He is someone who directs with heart and passion, weaving stories that reshape the ways we think about ourseleves, the choices we make, and that the ways we at times think of ourseleves as invincible, and showing us that we are not. He never gives his films a direct approach but rather an approach that builds  up slowly peeling back the layers while allowing us the time to explore the identities of the characters in each film. His style is reminiscent of the older days before CG animation when each story relied on a solid story with solid acting that is such a rarity in this day and age. Such as the case with Million Dollar Baby, we identify and care for each and every character this film presents us. From the very start we want to know where this film is going and not a single computer element can be found from start to finish. The environments and props are as real as the world Eastwood places his characters in. There is not a single aspect in Million Dolllar Baby that will convince you that what you are seeing in the film could not be a possibility in the real world, and this hold true not with just this film but with Gran Torino, A Perfect World and Letters From Iwo Jima. It took me awhile to watch this and for that I am shamed, as the only film I had seen by Eastwood before it was A Perfect World which I recall was also an excellent work to Eastwoods credit at the time. Since A Perfect World and before watching Million Dollar Baby I became exposed to The Changling, Letters From Iwo Jima and then the more recent Gran Torino, all of which I have a huge respect for. Since then my ignorance shifted about this film as I was rather hesitant to watch it on the sole reason that it had garnered status as a heavy hitters with the Academy and for me that was always a bad sign. Million Dollar Baby, for me as an uplifting and often tragic powerhouse of emotional storytelling about, rising to the top, to do what you feel is the right thing for you, to not let anyone stand in your way of your dreams no matter what, and that anything can happen at anytime where dreams can come crashing down like a house of cards in any single given moment. I had envisioned a film where the central character rises to the top while overcoming various obstacles and disadvantages but what what I got was a film that wasn't focused on just one single central character, but where all characters were treated equally across the board thus allowing us to fully explore the intricasies and points of view of each throughout. I highly recommend this film and it deserved every bit of hype it received during its run.","49c5ea05-c337-41d4-8641-557f936663ff",False,False,False,False,4.5,"2007-11-04","","RT","630649621"
