4.0,"In a rebuke of I Am Sam's simplistic ""All you need is love"" message, Million Dollar Baby replies with ""Tough ain't enough.""  Eddie (Morgan Freeman) reaffirms this with his line that ""The body tells us when to stop fighting.""  We don't completely make reality by our desires.  Even Cinderella sees midnight, and our subjectivity only bends the truth so much. 

But MDB replaces IAS's blind triumph with a quasi-nihilistic triumph:  We live for our moment of glory... of hearing the crowd chant our name.  The best to be hoped for before death is a shot at the title.  Aside from this acclaim, the question of the meaning of life is meaningless.  If she wants to die, let her die...  We're left with a more honest view of life, and somehow less hopeful.

Even God is hopeless, being rendered a silent actor in the film, lived out through the life of Frankie:  No questions allowed.  ""I'll teach you what you need to know, but you gotta win the title on your own.""  In the end, when Frankie gives up hope on Maggie's life, he can't help but feel that God gave up hope on Frankie too.  Perhaps, the man badgering to understand God his whole life, is really badgering to understand himself.  Perhaps, God really isn't that meaningful after all.

But only the God of Christianity offers the objective triumph we all long for.  If the Greatest approves of us, and - because of His Son - is chanting our name, then the purpose of life doesn't depend on the physical condition of the body, or the acclaim of others, but the metaphysical will of the mind. The triumph of Maggie's life wouldn't need any external token of success.  She could live the highest life as a cripple.  And if hardship is God's tool of refinement, maybe even a more meaningful life than others.

I applaud MDB for attempting to overcome the blatantly false notions of meaning in our society, but with a silent God, Clint Eastwood has merely replaced one handicap for another.","",False,False,False,False,4.0,"2007-11-29","","RT","786233042"
