5.0,"""Million Dollar Baby"" is the story of three different charactersâ¦ Actually, two of them, Frankie Dunn (Eastwood) and Scrap Iron (Freeman) are pretty much mirror images of each otherâ¦

Frankie owns a rundown Los Angeles gym where he trains up-and-comersâ¦ His motto for his fighters is ""protect yourself at all times."" Scrap is an elderly ex-boxer who helps out around the gym and keeps Frankie companyâ¦ Frankie was the 'cut-man' in one of Scraps' fights that turned badâ¦ Frankie still feels he's partly to blameâ¦ 

But 31 year old Maggie (Hilary Swank) is totally differentâ¦ She works as a waitress but aspires to be a boxerâ¦ She is so poor that she has to pick leftover meat off her customers' plates just to have the energy to keep goingâ¦ 

Maggie needs boxing to make something of herself and gain respectâ¦ When she first approaches Frankie, asking to be trained, he told her he never trains girlsâ¦ She continues to hang around and after much harassment he eventually changes his mindâ¦ From there, we begin to feel some natural affinity between the twoâ¦To say more would be to ruin a lovely, heartbreaking film carefully layered with surprisesâ¦

Beneath ""Million Dollar Baby,"" there is a love storyâ¦ A deep love story between two people with scars in their lives... Both are haunted by family problemsâ¦ Maggie's family, led by her opportunistic mother is alternately cruel and exploitativeâ¦ Frankie has an unloving daughter he writes to constantly, but his letters always returned unopened, unread and intactâ¦ We never know what came between themâ¦

Eastwood handles his role with such ease that it's hard to judge whether his acting or directing is the greater accomplishmentâ¦ He stands as a challenge to his conscience when he finds the correct answer in his heartâ¦","",False,False,False,False,5.0,"2008-01-06","","RT","792977507"
