5.0,"Exceptional three-character study.  Prose on film quietly, lovingly and acutely offering insight into its no-bigger than life personalities, each at a crossroad with enough  untapped ambition, some regret , unanswered questions and a lot of fervor.  Even with its dour subject matter it is a startlingly winning film mainly because it smartly trails off from being a movie focusing on its topic of boxing and instead concentrating on its subjects.  Itâs a deeply philosophical film yet accomplished through its accessibility and easy on the eye subject matter.  Without ever skimping on its mainstream appeal (and most probably its big studio intentions), the film richly delves deeper than most in finding its character nuances, idiosyncrasies, inner-workings and flaws.  Freeman is a standout [award wining performance alert] even if the film belongs to Swank â who carries the film from start to end.  Eastwood benefits from having the two shining stars fuel a spark that allows him to outshine most of his past introverted performances.   Here while still working from within he allows moments where he actually comes alive.  All this is thanks to a top-notch script filled with insightful nuggets on life, death and the meaning of no regret.","",False,False,False,False,5.0,"2008-01-27","","RT","350874549"
