4.0,"This is a film people seem to either love or hate. Fight fans don't tend to like it because, expecting a traditional, throwaway boxing movie, they actually get an admirably dark stealth-weepie. I mean, heaven forbid a film should produce an emotional response!

In truth, the fight scenes are the weakest part of ""Million Dollar Baby""--being neither as realistic as we've seen elsewhere or as beautifully stylised as in, for example, Scorsese's ""Raging Bull""--but, then, Eastwood isn't primarily interested in the boxing; it's just a means to an end: a device to put Maggie (Hilary Swank) in harm's way. More than anything, the movie is about the mutually dependent relationship of Swank's boxer and Eastwood's trainer. It's a simple platonic love story and, as such, the film succeeds magnificently.

It's not without its faults, however: Maggie's trailer-trash family, though well played, are one-dimensional stereotypes; though he won an Oscar and contributes a fair amount of screen time, there's always a niggling suspicion that Morgan Freeman is only there to provide one of his peerless voice-overs; and the instant championing of Maggie by the Irish contingent at her London fight, stereotypes again, seems unlikely and silly.

An actor of limited range and ability, Eastwood has always been shrewd enough to play to his strengths and he's magnificent here, turning in perhaps the performance of his career. Hilary Swank is equally wonderful. Is ""Million Dollar Baby"" a great boxing movie, in the tradition of ""Champion"", ""The Set-Up"", ""Fat City"" and ""Raging Bull""? Perhaps not, but it is a great movie.","",False,True,False,False,4.0,"2008-02-05","","RT","795047044"
