4.0,"I do hope you enjoy these reviews. The Departed IS coming pretty soon, and so is a Munich and United 93 review.
[center][size=7]Million Dollar Baby
***+
[/size][url=""http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Image:Million_Dollar_Baby_poster.jpg""][img]http://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/d/d3/Million_Dollar_Baby_poster.jpg/200px-Million_Dollar_Baby_poster.jpg[/img][/url][size=7]
[/size][left][size=3]I'm going to give this film 3 and a half stars because of many reasons: one being that this film is truly a masterpiece. Every part of it is superb, from the producing to the directing to the acting to the writing. All of those elements make for a Best Picture winner, which this film is. I don't feel a bit bitter or upset that this beat out Finding Neverland, instead I celebrate FN's nomination and pat Million Dollar Baby on the back.

What makes Million Dollar Baby ultimately work is the way it grabs the audience and never lets go emotionally. There isn't a single moment of disconnect, where I realize ""Oh, wait, this is a movie"". No. Hillary gives one of the most powerful and outstanding female performances of all time. She deserved the Oscar. Yes, I said it. She deserved it more than Kate Winslet. [img]http://images.rottentomatoes.com/images/user/smilies/eek.gif[/img]

That being said, I must have an excuse for my eight. Alas, this film may be a masterpiece, but it isn't the most entertaining thing ever invented. It was a little tedious at times, but it pulled itself up.

Excuse me, but why haven't most of my friends seen this?[/size]
[center][size=7]Sideways
***
[/size][url=""http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Image:Sideways_poster.JPG""][img]http://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/f/ff/Sideways_poster.JPG/200px-Sideways_poster.JPG[/img][/url][size=7]
[/size][left][size=3]Um, no. Sorry, guys, but this film never really held my attention that much, or made me care. It was kind of like watching a charade between these four actors that, every now and then, seemed way too over-the-top or demanding of me. I was entertained all the time, that's for sure, but to call this the best film of 2004 is such a stretch I can't even imagine it. Fair enough as a story, and fair enough to receive a seven, but I can't give it any more. This film had potential, folks, but I saw so little of it exerted. It was constantly constipated by pointless conversations about Pinot Noir and Merlot or whatever the hell kind of wine they said.

However, I did like hearing my town name said in this. :D Paso Robles. Sandra Oh is sooooo fancy.
