5.0,"[font=Arial]I should be mad at the likes of Michael Medved, who ""accidently"" let the cat out of the bag with the ending of Million Dollar Baby, a movie that is by far Clint Eastwood's best film. This film is dark and moody, yet thrilling and exciting in parts. With wonderful performances and Eastwood's brilliant direction, this film earns every accolade it gets.

The film follows 31-year-old boxer-hopeful Maggie Fitzgerald (Hilary Swank) and trainer Frankie Dunn (Eastwood). Maggie corners Frankie after a fight, needing a trainer. Frankie rejects her on account of her gender and partially of her age (""Do you start training to be a ballerina at 30?""). But her persistance slowly warms him up (not to mention that he loses a great fighter to his timidness about going for Title bouts). And over the course of a year, he trains her to be a better boxer. But Maggie is head-strong, only taking the advice she wants and leaving the rest behind. She becomes a success by taking her rivals out fast and hard. But belive it or not, this is NOT what the movie is really about. This is just the machinery to keep the story moving while we see the true beauty in characters who are worn thin by life and expectations. Great scenes that show this is when Frankie watches as Maggie allows the verbal beating by her mother and family. We see she has the heart of a warrior and Frankie sees her not as just another fighter, but almost like a daughter. And what happens to bring out the third act (and my disgust with Limbaugh and Medved), brings us to the heart of this tale.

The acting in this movie is nothing less of superb. Especially this year, it would almost be hard to distinguish great acting from one movie to another (too many great performances). Swank has a tendency not to be in many movies at any given time. That's because she doesn't have the graceful beauty of many Hollywood leading ladies. But that's not a bad thing at all. She makes her career in doing roles that you wouldn't want to see anyone else filling. Maggie is perfect for her. This is a woman who is very independent, bound and determined to walk by the beat of her own drum that she refuses to listen even to good advice. She is matched almost step-for-step by Eastwood who shows pure emotion from Frankie, even when he is supressing it. When the stakes raise over his head, we see the delimahs he faces and the pain inflicted upon him. And then there is Morgan Freeman. He is the greek chorus (or you could even say Ringmaster) to these events. His scratchy voice is perfect in revealing where this story goes. And his comraderie with Frankie is so pure, you know there is a history between these two that you will never truely understand. And then there are the other fighters in the gym Frankie runs. I'd almost believe I could find that gym nearby and meet these people as they worked out, trained and sparred with each other. And there there is one crucial role needed to be talked about. The priest who Frankie torments both by his demeanor and his presence is played by Brian F. O'Byrne as a man who is so tired of Frankie's presence, that even when he comes to him with a serious problem, he only takes him half-seriously and doesn't give him the guidance he really needs. 

Eastwood's direction in this movie easily outdoes anything he's done in the last 10 years. Mystic River was amazing, yes. But this film slaps it in the face. Brilliant setting of moods and a wonderful use of shadows lament what he expects these characters to be in naturally. Shadows are very important in this film because most of these characters want to come to the light, but find themselves hiding because of demons that haunt them or fears only half-realized. The fight sequences are amazing to watch (and even more amazing to hear. Trust me, sound plays a crucial part in this). Joel Cox's editing is impressive to say the least. And this film shows just how far you can go on a small budget of $25 million dollars, baby (sorry, couldn't help myself).

Is there any more that can be said about this film? Volumes. I will say that my only dispute comes from the ending that doesn't perfectly blend with the nature of Maggie, but I can see why she feels the way she does. I won't Medved on you or anything. But I think it needed to be worked out a little more at the end.

Which brings me back to Medved and Limbaugh and anyone else who thinks that they should spoil endings because of a beef they have with a filmmaker's choice. It's your right as Americans to say what you want. But don't you DARE give away something that people might want to find out for themselves. Perhaps you don't like the ending. Then at least allow people to see for themselves exactly what you disagree on. If they weren't going to see the film to begin with, they aren't going to care. If they were, then let them make up their own minds. Or perhaps you don't trust them to be able to think for themselves.

All in all, this film is a brilliant masterpiece by an amazing filmmaking cast and crew. I would urge you to see this film no matter what your beliefs are. You are not going to find many other films more worthy of your money.[/font]","",False,False,False,False,5.0,"2008-03-20","","RT","901072503"
