4.0,"Clint Eastwood marks another directing triumph in the
new boxing drama âMillion Dollar Baby.â

Eastwood also stars in the film as Frankie Dunn, a
strict yet gifted boxing trainer, who has yet to take
one of his fighters to a title win. 

Frankie also has a fractured relationship with his
family, which clearly pains him, and sends him to
church daily seeking forgiveness.

Hilary Swank co-stars as Maggie Fitzgerald, a woman
trying to overcome her cruel family and follow her
dream of being a boxing champion.

Maggie is determined to be trained and managed by
Frankie, knowing he is the best.

Frankie, however, refuses to take Maggie on because
she is a girl, and too old.

Maggie signs up for a six month membership at
Frankieâs training gym, hoping to prove herself.

Frankie still doesnât respond, however his right hand
man, best friend, and live-in janitor Eddie (Morgan
Freeman- who also narrates the film) sees potential in
Maggie and begins to help her in secret.

Maggieâs persistence finally pays off, when Frankie
notices her improvement (and its source) and agrees to
take her on, for a trial period.

After many months of training, Maggie is finally ready
to fight, and Frankie tries to send her off to another
manager, however, he proves to be far too attached to
Maggie to let her go, and takes her on permanently.

In her first prize fight, Frankie gives Maggie a
nickname in Gaelic ( a language Frankie avidly
studies,) which immediately catches on with fans, and
sticks in every fight from then on.

Frankie will not however, tell Maggie what it means,
that is until her first title match. Frankie promises
if she wins, he will tell her.

The fight proves to be life changing for all involved.

âMillion Dollar Babyâ is not just a sports movie, nor
is it typical of a movie centered on an athlete.

The characters are all developed thoroughly and
painstakingly, and the audience deeply cares for each
of them, particularly the two principles.

Freemanâs character, though more removed from the main
plot, is also surprisingly affecting. 

It is one of his lines that is one of the most  moving
quotes in a film this year, and also sums up the tone
of the film, as well as being an unspoken truth for
both of the leading characters, and one of the reasons
they form such a strong bond.

""There's a magic of fighting battles beyond endurance.
It's the magic of risking everything for a dream that
nobody sees but you.""

The acting is exceptional all around, and very worthy
of the various award nominations and wins.

There are obvious but moving parallels of a father and
daughter relationship between Frankie and Maggie, and
this is one of the major elements that does make the
film more than the journey of an athlete. 

It is likely that Frankie didnât take on girls,
because he knew he would become too attached due to
his estrangement from his own daughter.

The filmâs core really is at the journey of two people
emotionally, and how they enrich and change each
others lives.

âBabyâ does speak that people who are âall heartâ will
fall, and the others will stay standing, but the good
will have gone out on top, which is far better than
going out with nothing.

Eastwood continues to prove he has a genuine
directorâs eye, with brilliant use of symbolism, light
and shadow, and object placement, that tell the story
all on their own. âBabyâ is his best work to date.

Eastwood also composed the unique and effective score,
as he has done for most of the films he has directed.

You donât have to care about boxing at all to love
this movie, and root for its characters.

Almost any audience will relate in some way, due to
how human the story is.

âMillion Dollar Babyâ truly is one of the best films
of the year, and is unexpectedly emotional and
engaging.  The story and characters will stay with you
long after you leave the theater.","",False,False,False,False,4.0,"2008-05-27","","RT","824113500"
