5.0,"One of the instant classics of this decade. Eastwood's best directorial effort and his greatest performance, as well. Hilary Swank outdoes her Oscar-winning work in Boys Don't Cry with an emotive, impressively physical and immersive performance. Her character has the most extreme arc and you believe her every inch of the way. Morgan Freeman offers a more compelling variation on his popular persona, and I bristle at the notion that his Oscar was a sympathy win -- he is absolutely instrumental to MDB's success. The three leads work beautifully off of each other to the point where it's impossible to see anyone else in their roles. Also, the Paul Haggis screenplay is basically perfect and the film's technical aspects -- especially the lighting -- almost become another character. This was easily the best movie of 2004, and if it turns out to be Clint's last screen role then he couldn't've picked a better exit. MDB might be sad, but no good movie is depressing. Actually, the film is extremely moving and inspiring. This is a love story wrapped up in sports film's clothing. What greater expression of love can there be than the sacrifice Frankie Dunn makes for Maggie at the end of this movie -- giving up the thing he loves most in his life for her happiness? God, I'm getting goosebumps just writing about it. If you don't appreciate Million Dollar Baby, you probably don't like movies to begin with. And the poster is beautiful.","",False,False,False,False,5.0,"2008-07-28","","RT","783512332"
