3.5,"Just one in a string of amazing films Clint Eastwood churned out in the last decade, starting with the incredible Mystic River, and continuing with this extraordinary follow-up from 2004, Million Dollar Baby. The cinematic poetry Eastwood poured into Mystic River also seeps into this film, a delicate story of emotion and heart disguised as a boxing flick. Million Dollar Baby is adapted from a short story from the collection Rope Burns by F.X. Toole, by screenwriter Paul Haggis, and focuses on Maggie Fitzgerald, a waitress from the Ozarks who wants to be a fighter. She makes her way to Los Angeles and into the gym of Frankie Dunn (Eastwood himself in a remarkably bruising performance), a former fighter and cut man (they're charged with staunching the flow of blood during a bout) who has no patience for the female fighters ('girlie-tough isn't enough' he tells her with his signature growl). He also tells her she's much too old at 31 to begin fighting, but Frankie's longtime friend Scrap (Morgan Freeman, magnificent in an Oscar-winning turn, thinks differently.Scrap too is an ex-fighter who lost an eye in a fight in which Frankie was cut man. It all sounds like an easy set-up for cheap tearjerking, but hold on, Eastwood is hunting bigger game. Even disaster strikes Maggie in the ring and the script has run-ins with sentimental cliches (Maggie's exploitative family, Frankie's estranged daughter) Eastwood keeps things nice and grounded, the mark of a true film artist. Eastwood also enjoys an easy and palpable rapport with Freeman, they're just perfect together. Eastwood's performance too is incredible, stripped down and devoid of any fat, and Swank delivers a rip-roaring, emotionally raw performance as Maggie. Million Dollar Baby is a KO, and a damn good one.","c1ee9fbc-0ff8-424d-b36e-24c658d5e859",False,False,False,False,3.5,"2008-08-20","","RT","783684766"
