4.5,"Million Dollar Baby is a crowning achievement. There really is no point in starting this write up without saying that right off the bat. It is uplifting, yet heartbreaking. It's funny, yet sad. It's awe inspiring, yet humbling. It's a magnificent film in nearly every way.

Frankie (Clint Eastwood) is a boxing trainer who claims that he doesn't train girls. They're weaker, smaller, and more fragile. But Maggie (Hilary Swank) is persistent in her refusal to leave his gym. After Frankie's prize fighter leaves him for another manager, he decides to give Maggie a go, at least until they can find a good manager for her. He trains her, learns all about her, and becomes attached to her. But on their way to the title match, something happens that will change their lives forever.

Clint Eastwood is a master of his craft. He's a man who has made a living playing iconic, bad ass characters in films that are still enjoyed today. But he's also a terrific director. Somebody give this guy something he can't do. I don't think it's possible. This movie oozed with stylish camera work, which is surprising seeing as how it's the type of film that doesn't require it. He knows where the line is at, reaches it, but never crosses it. He goes just as far as needed to make an effective piece of work. Every frame was simply beautiful to look at.

The acting was just as terrific as the directing in most respects. It's pretty apparent that Clint Eastwood's old age has taken a bit of a toll on him. He's not like Morgan Freeman or Jack Nicholson who just seem to get better and better as time goes by. However, he was still really good. And Hilary Swank was amazing. She received the best actress award for her role in the film and she wholeheartedly deserved it. She was riveting.

Now here's the hard part. How can I possibly discuss the intricacies of the plot without somehow divulging what happens in the process? I suppose I'll do my best.

This picture is a multifaceted wave of emotion. Frankie goes to church nearly every day, but isn't sure why. He has no family besides a daughter who, for unknown reasons, does not speak with him anymore. He writes her every week, but always receives the letter back with ""return to sender"" on it. Maggie is a person who grew up in a less than privileged home. Her father is dead and her family is unappreciative. She buys them a house with her fight money and they just scoff at her and tell her she's stupid. He's a miserable old man who gets by solely on his fighters and she's an optimistic woman who just wants to make a name for herself. It's saddening to see Frankie this way and heartbreaking to see Maggie get treated unfairly. But it truly is uplifting to see their unlikely relationship spawn from the gutters of unhappiness, despair, and poverty. He thinks he is helping her, when in reality she's helping him. It's an emotional movie for at least an hour and a half, in a good way.

But then something happens. Something that wasn't supposed to and their relationship drastically changes. Of course, to go any further would be to ruin the experience, so I'll stop here. Still, I want to say that after this event, the film should have begun to wrap itself up. But it went on for another 30 minutes, if not more. Throughout the first hour and a half, you take this terrific journey with the characters and really get to know them, but then tragedy befalls and that should have been that. It should have lasted another 10 minutes or so and ended. It simply carried on for longer than necessary. The film would easily have received five stars had it been trimmed up a bit.

Nevertheless, Million Dollar Baby is still amazing. It's a cinematic tour de force which ranks up there among Hilary Swank and Clint Eastwood's strongest work. It's remarkable in the way it takes a somewhat mundane subject and creates a riveting experience out of it. Did it deserve to win the Best Picture Oscar in 2004? Considering that it was the year of Eternal Sunshine of the Spotless Mind which inexplicably wasn't even nominated, probably not. However, it still ranks among one of the best of that year. Million Dollar Baby is a triumphant film.","",False,False,False,False,4.5,"2008-09-19","","RT","786910301"
5.0,"Million Dollar Baby is a rare modern movie:  no special effects (computer or otherwise); no major action or set pieces; no sex for sex's sake--just a straightforward story of three people's lives that happen to intersect around the sport of boxing and the meaning of love and loyalty.  Brilliant performances by the three leads. Absolutely breathtaking in its intensity and unsympathetic, raw emotion, this classic brooding (almost noir) film is Clint Eastwood's masterpiece.","",False,False,False,False,5.0,"2008-09-19","","RT","836398024"
