4.0,"The Only reason I did not give it 5 stars was due to the tragic ending. What a tear jerker, I was crying so much by the time I left the theater...Excellent job by cast (especially hilary Swank) and director (Clint Eastwood). A hardened trainer/manager works with a determined woman in her attempt to establish herself as a boxer. This film won 4 OSCARS- Best pic of the year for 2005, Best Director (Clint Eastwood) Best Actress in Leading Role (Hilary Swank) and Best Actor in Leading role (Clint Eastwood). Excellent movie that will break your heart!","",False,False,False,False,4.0,"2008-09-30","","RT","837439637"
4.0,"Don't let someone give you any synopsis of this film, as knowing the direction of the ending takes away a lot of its power.  I'll avoid spoilers here.

Million Dollar Baby is like two films sandwiched into one, both of which on their own, wouldn't be anything remarkable: boxing underdog story meets ""large complications"" tale (to veil a spoiler).  I have to admit that setting the first up so solidly and then dropping the other one in makes for a really interesting plot; you really never see the second one coming.

Both of these plots also have the potential for heavy-handedness or getting caught up in their own metaphors, yet MDB somehow manages to steer clear (though just barely during the last half).  The focus here is so clearly on characterization, even the moral dilemma that comes into play during the ending is still wrapped up enough in the story and its players to deflect the risk of eclipsing them.

There is a subtle minimalism at work here as well, which sets a calming and deep tone that I really enjoy.  Everything from the simple dialogue, the music, the lighting, etc. sets a mood that helped keep the story outside of the flash, bang, and over-zealousness that permeates much of Hollywood.  With subject material like this, one would expect the training montages set to 80's power ballads, the slow-motion punch sequences, the sweeping musical score near the ending, but MDB displays a commendable maturity by keeping quietly to itself.

This is the kind of cinema I've grown to expect from Eastwood who continues to impress me with the material he puts out.  Million Dollar Baby may not be the kind of film that shakes down mountains and changes lives, but it tells a solid story, plain and simple.  Well done Eastwood.","",False,False,False,False,4.0,"2008-09-30","","RT","782626675"
