4.5,"'Million Dollar Baby' is a tale of finding something to fight for when you have nothing left - and giving it all you've got. 

We are introduced to the film through a narration by Eddie ""Scrap Iron"" Dupris (Morgan Freeman), and this narration continues throughout most of the non-dialogue scenes of the film. His lovely voice weaves an intricate picture of Frank Dunn's (Clint Eastwood) life, as well as the lives of his fighters. Dunn is a boxing coach, but has been lacking a front-running fighter for a long time. Enter Maggie Fitzgerald (Hilary Swank) - a budding boxer who won't leave Dunn alone until he trains her. Even though she's too old (32) and is a girl, Dunn still takes her under his wing. But the bond they form knows no bounds.

In making this film, Eastwood has chosen to develop character relationships and plot rather than technical brilliance, and it has paid off extraordinarily well. Though the film comes off as more of a play rather than a film, it works perfectly. The fluidity of the film ran like golden silk through a spool. Every scene led into something more profound and more captivating than the last. It also veered away from previously laid-out conceptions of the boxing world, mainly because of the differentiation in character and background of Maggie from any other boxing film star. 

As a part of the intricate character that is Maggie Fitzgerald, we see many different parts of her childhood through her own narration, through Scrap's narration, and through her family's involvement in and attitude towards her lifestyle.  Her family becomes a large part of her, even though, when we finally meet them, we see them to be (to quote the film) ""white trash"". The audience cannot help but sympathise with her situation - she is going out into the world and working in a ""mans job"" when she ""should"" be finding a husband and being a stay-at-home mum. Conflicting views and beliefs make her exceedingly unhappy - all she has is her relationship with Frank. 

Through fate's twisted hand (and a certain someone's uncalled-for fist), Maggie becomes paralyzed from the neck down. It is from here on that the film really starts to connect. We see the struggle that Frank goes through as he is threatened with the loss of his dearest friend and companion. We also see him battle with his own morals as Maggie tries to tell him that she has nothing left to live for - her life was boxing, and now it was gone. Maggie asks him not to let them take the fight out of her. Hence a topical issue is raised - euthanasia. Eastwood was very brave in incorporating this into the film, as it could have, and did, create a lot of controversy. But it poetically painted a perfect end to the emotional and rocky journey Maggie and Frank had come together for, and was a beautiful end to the film.

In short: the emotional ride this boxer and trainer go through together will touch your heart and cause a re-thinking of your soul.

9-","",False,False,False,False,4.5,"2008-11-29","","RT","901095772"
