4.5,"A million dollar movie.  Hilary Swank and Morgan Freeman in well-deserved Osacr-winning roles, with Clint Eastwood in his nomination-worthy lead.  Eastwood at his best.  He plays a pro boxing coach secretly fearing major title matches, and it comes back to haunt him when his star boxer leaves him for a more ambitious coach.  Swank, a considered-too-old broke Southern woman boxer urges Eastwood and Freeman, his old former boxer friend, to coach her.  Reluctantly, Eastwood agrees to train her, with Freeman right by his side.  Paul Haggis' screenplay is just as Oscar-worthy as Swank and Freeman's acting performances.  Each and every character is rounded out, given fantastic personalities, characteristics, and backgrounds.  Eastwood often tries to hide his rapidly growing affection for Swank, though often fails to do so.  Personally, he is also dealing with a daughter who refuses to even open his weekly letters, and he tries to find comfort in Church, with no help from the mouthy priest there.  Swank comes from a greedy hillbilly family in the South who never cared much about her, and it shows later on.  She makes her meals out of leftovers from the diner she works as a waitress, making her money solely off tips.  Freeman is a boxer who quit after he lost his eye to his 109th match.  With some great cinematography and film editing to complete the excellent story of bonding, love, and pain. A","8d4c62cb-dc40-4a05-9443-e3b094b54830",False,False,False,False,4.5,"2009-03-07","","RT","807859753"
3.0,"While this is a good movie, something about it is a bit off. I just cannot pinpoint what exactly that something is. It isn't the actors, they're great - well, what did you expect from Clint Eastwood and Morgan Freeman - and in Hilary Swank's case, surprisingly so. Some of the plot twists are a bit rough around the edges, but the story itself isn't that bad - though at some points it feels a bit rushed. The undertones are okay, the side characters are pretty standard - meaning not bad - and the settings are okay as well. So really, what is there not to like? Well, I don't know, but for some reason I really didn't enjoy this as much as I could have had that something not been there bothering me. Perhaps it's just that the movie tries to be so realistically and cleanly told that it loses something along the way. Actually, the word clinical is perfect to describe this film. It's a good film and you should watch it, but it's still a bit clinical.","d8a45e8c-9ba6-4f02-95d6-66830f3833a2",False,False,False,False,3.0,"2009-03-07","","RT","844191803"
