4.5,"One of the things that I've always admired about Clint Eastwood is how willing and determined he is to challenge the macho, uber-manly tough guy image he is known and adored for in the films he directs.  Sure, he will always be The Man With No Name, the Outlaw Josey Wales, The Pale Rider, and Dirty Harry, the manliest of men, but look at the characters he embodies in his own pictures.  His first film, Play Misty for Me, he did the unthinkable and played a man who is endangered by an obsessive woman, not a typical role for an action hero.  In Unforgiven, he played a man trying to escape the same type of existence he embodied in his earlier westerns.  And in last years Gran Torino, he examined the flaws in an old-school value system while also showing it had a level of decency to it on some tier.

In his Best Picture-winning Million Dollar Baby, Eastwood once again challenges his image, albeit likely not intentionally, by showing the relationship between a grizzled older man, and a determined younger woman.  The unlikely pairing makes for a very strong film, and it can be attributed to a couple of factors.  First of all, the simplest reason is that Eastwood is a masterful director.  There are some who would likely challenge his merits simply because he was an actor before he got behind the camera, but the performances that Eastwood can pull out of an actor is nothing short of astounding, and he can elevate mood and build drama as well as some of our most revered and applauded auteurs.  Eastwood is known as a man of many talents, having gained recognition as both an actor, an accomplished musician, and he can add virtuoso filmmaker to that classification as well.  

The other element that drives his films, especially this one, is in his ability to pick great talents for his films.  Hilary Swank can be really inconsistent at times, often picking very lackluster films and giving subpar performances at moments, but when she's at her best she is incredible, and her performance as Maggie Fitzgerald is one of her most endearing roles.  There is a determination in Maggie that doesn't feel forced or overly sentimental, but real and you can't help but wish for her success in the ring.  Her interactions with Eastwood were the highlights in the film for me, and the progression from tense and reluctant trainer and trainee to loving and supportive partnership doesn't feels very authentic.  And Morgan Freeman, a great character actor in his own right, really seems to add a different dimension to his characters under Eastwood's direction.  Even his narration in this film has a more weathered, darker, and stronger delivery than his usual quality narration.  

Much of the opinions harbored toward this film hinged upon where people stood politically pertaining to the film's ending.  That is unfortunate because whether or not you agree with actions the characters take, it doesn't subtract from the fact that this is a great, powerful film.","c0cd4e52-6748-4ec4-aac5-5aac9c57af18",False,False,False,False,4.5,"2009-08-05","","RT","784883003"
