4.5,"4.5: A masterpiece.  Mesmerizing.  I'll never forget the first time I saw this movie at night in my Hogan dorm room during my senior year.  It's one of those films you absolutely cannot look away from.  One is completely transported into another world.  This is one of Eastwood's greatest achievements and that is certainly saying something.  each and every character and performance is perfect.  This is up to and including the more minor characters of the weakling at the gym, the mother, the lawyer, the title-ready boxer at the beginning, the fight promoters, etc.  Each piece fits just so right into its proper place.  The three leads obviously carry the film though.  Swank, Eastwood, and Freeman all perfectly personify their characters.  I can't think of a single actor that could have played any of the three roles better.  Certainly no one could duplicate Freeman's voice.  It is perfectly suited to this extensive voiceover, just as it was with The Shawshank Redemption.  I'd have to say I probably tend to underrate Eastwood as an actor too as I now primarily think of him as a director.  He is spectacular here though.  I can honestly say that I see relatively little of his other characters, which is certainly saying something since he's tended to play similar roles over and over and built up a certain movie star personality that is hard to avoid on screen.  The real genius of the film is the abrupt turn that it takes after the first two acts.  It works amazingly well as what seems to be a pretty simple and straightforward boxing/sports film over the first two acts.  We are engrossed in the story and the characters and look forward to everyone growing together and achieving their dreams.  The seemingly abrupt change still manages to flow though.  It isn't two separate stories, moods, or tones.  It all works together as a cohesive whole despite the radical departure in terms of genre.  Few other than Eastwood could have managed this minor miracle.  Perhaps no one could have put the team together that could make this particular script play so well.  It could easily depart into the sappy, sentimental, and sickening, but Eastwood works with poise, restraint, and simplicity throughout.  The photography and music are absolutely integral to the overall effectiveness.  Rarely has a film dared to be so dark, but rarely has it felt more alive.  One can also imagine this world being just about anywhere as well, despite the fact that it has a definite setting, the same as many of Eastwood's films (think Gran Torino).  It is both specific and general, just as much of America is.  The closing act is so poignant and moving it strains belief.  It is enough to restore anybodies faith in studio cinema.  There are few artists of such skill and integrity working in the cinema today, but I for one am happy we at least have this one.","38ef9632-6ac6-449f-9e91-6d3afd486e97",False,False,False,False,4.5,"2009-09-08","","RT","784904745"
