5.0,"I usually rate films based on how well I feel they are executed, but don't always take into account the way they make me feel. Whilst watching 'Million Dollar Baby', I wasn't having the greatest viewing experience. I mean, the film was gripping me enough, but I felt it was a bit too 'bland', or maybe I wasn't enthusiastic about the boxing element of the film. As the film progressed, I found that it was transforming, almost into a work of art. In the film's final minute, I shed a tear. This movie had obviously moved me; it had tapped into my deepest emotions, something a film rarely does. Any film that has such an impact on me deserves five stars, because it takes great craftsmanship to move an audience. You wanna know the last film that made me shed a tear? It was 'Gran Torino', that's right, another film by Clint Eastwood, which I also rated five stars. I have loved every movie I've seen that Eastwood has either directed or starred in; there's just something majestic about him. He is a legend of cinema. But don't think my five-star rating is ONLY because I shed a tear. 'Million Dollar Baby' is an excellent film. Don't worry about the 'blandness' I mentioned earlier, I'm often too naive (at times too stupid) to appreciate great cinema. To sum it up, 'Million Dollar Baby' is one of those movies where you find yourself awestruck as the end credits roll, where you sit in silence and ponder on what you just viewed.","1cff568a-88cf-4bee-9af8-2e46cf90df1b",False,False,False,False,5.0,"2009-10-11","","RT","855765396"
