4.5,"So this film was really all I expected. For the first hour and fifteen minutes or so I thought I had this story all figured out, then the big twist comes out of nowhere.
And what a friggin' twist it was! I have never been more surprised by an ending. I don't want to give away too much because there are people out there who still have not seen it. But the movie essentially starts out very Rocky-esque in that there is an older trainer Frankie (Eastwood) and the thought to be over the hill, maybe contender Maggie (Swank). Maggie works her way up the ranks and eventually builds an amazing bond with Frankie. A bond that's rarely seen on film these days.
Clint Eastwood plays the familiar role of the take no shit from anyone character in Frankie. But in this role, he actually lets his guard down just enough to build an enduring and powerful relationship with his co-star. Hilary Swank did a great job portraying the down and out, broke and lonely fighter. She knew she had nothing and she was nothing and she did a wonderful job portraying that to the crowd.
Eastwood makes the crowd believe they're watching a powerful boxing film. Then in a matter of one scene he changes the entire plot and it turns into a film about choices, consequences, family, friendship and love. What a timeless and unpredictable movie this has turned out to be.
The entire cast did a spectacular job from Eastwood to Swank. Morgan Freeman also plays the familiar role of the older and wiser sidekick which is as brilliant as ever. There was even a noticeable face from Knocked Up in Jay Baruchel in which he plays a young, southern hillbilly who just wants to fight the best boxing has to offer.
In all this was an unforgettable movie about all important things in life. As clever a plot as I've ever seen and yet another spectacular performance as director/ actor for Clint Eastwood.","db9e9777-fd95-4a66-af09-52f872af459c",False,False,False,False,4.5,"2009-10-21","","RT","901319864"
