5.0,"Maggie Fitzgerald (Hilary Swank) is a waitress with a dream of becoming a female boxer. She wishes to be trained by Frankie Dunn (Clint Eastwood); however Frankie is reluctant and refuses to train females. Frankie is a brilliant boxing trainer whose had some bad luck over the years and has a daughter that refuses to speak to him, sending every letter that he sends to her back to him. Maggie doesn't give up and after much hesitation, Frankie then decides to take on Maggie and help her achieve her dream of becoming a female boxer. 

The characters in this film are used to their fullest extent and we witness how they develop over the course of events that take place. We are given an idea of who the characters are deep down and why. Even some of the less important characters in the film, which are put to good use, are given a decent description and explanation. For example, Danger Barch (Jay Baruchel) who may seem like one of the characters that have no real meaning in the story, brought a sense of innocence in the film during those dark moments and made the film feel real. Danger has a dream just like Maggie's but he simply doesn't have the talent to accomplish it. Although everyone is well aware of this fact, no one other than another boxer at Frankie's gym is cruel enough to mock him. This boxer is known as Shawrelle Barry (Anthony Mackie) who is described as having ""a left hook that could move a tank"" but a heart the size of a split pea. Even though he is also seen as perhaps an unnecessary character in the film, his actions help characters like Danger develop.

Maggie Fitzgerald comes from a poor, stereotypically redneck family and holds onto the dream of becoming a successful boxer one day and is determined to do what it takes to make her dream come true. Maggie is a cheerful, yet stubborn character. She is repeatedly seen making light of bad situations and frequently ignoring the advice Frankie gives her during her fights, much to Frankie's dismay. Frankie is predominantly pessimistic and unwilling to take risks early on in the film, refusing to let his one of the boxers he's training to fight in a title match because he believes he only gets one shot at the title. It's because of Maggie that we see Frankie's character develops and changes into someone more likeable than what he originally was in the beginning of the film. Maggie holds onto her dream of becoming a boxer because boxing is the only thing she ever felt good doing. Without it, she believes she has nothing. Even after Frankie tells her she has no hope of becoming a champion boxer one day, she still refuses to give up and continues to pursue her dream. Although Frankie may come off as unsympathetic or bad-mannered at times, his character is revealed to have a reason for everything. 

During some of the non-dialogue scenes the film is narrated by one of Frankie's former boxers, Eddie Dupris (Morgan Freeman) also known 'as Scrap Iron'. Million Dollar Baby is just one of few films that Morgan Freeman has narrated. It's rather obvious as to why he was chosen to narrate this film as his voice is articulate and his expression certainly appropriate. His narration is simple. It doesn't go into great detail about the events that take place, it simply tells us what is happening and in some cases why. Eddie Dupris has a history with Frankie. Frankie was once his boxing trainer, giving him advice during his fights until Dupris was unable to continue boxing. He is frequently seen lecturing Frankie which would appear to be a somewhat reverse situation of when Frankie was his trainer. They share an obligatory friendship. Frankie feels guilty over something that happened with Dupris back when he was boxing and feels as if he could have somehow changed the outcome of his boxing career. Frankie is also estranged from his daughter. While reasons for this are left unknown, we see Frankie is tormented with guilt. He regularly attends mass almost every day for 23 years, much to the annoyance of his priest, Father Horvak (Brian F. O'Byrne). He tells Frankie the only person who comes to church that much is the kind of person who can' forgive himself for something. Frankie' life is run by the mistakes he' made in the past. He has a tendency to avoid taking risks. After constant hesitation to take one of his fighters to title fight, his fighter finally leaves him, and after Dupris encourages Maggie, Frankie finally agrees to train her. He takes a risk and whether it was the right decision or not, it's up to the audience to watch and decide.

Hilary Swank portrays Maggie flawlessly. In every scene involving her, not once does she slightly step out of character. Even her facial expressions, body language and dialogue are flawless. Sometimes she may be hard to understand because of her accent, but changing her accent for it to be understood a little better wouldn't exactly be in character. Clint Eastwood's performance was also amazing. Some may disagree with his actions towards the end of the film but he's doing what the character would do in that situation, not what the audience expects him to do. The score was composed by Clint Eastwood. It adds emotion to the scenes in the movie. It does not distract you from what is happening on screen, instead, it pulls the audience even further into the film. Even the very last scene in the film, and when the credits begin to roll, the music has a way of adding that extra bit of emotion on screen. 

This film is undoubtedly a masterpiece. Every last second is made to perfection. The characters are portrayed flawlessly and without a doubt the audience will find themselves emotionally involved. If the audience disagrees with how the characters act throughout the film, then they should be reminded that characters do what they would do, not what you want them to do.","d77ef9a4-0fe3-4eb7-9dc3-f8004ca5785f",False,False,False,False,5.0,"2009-12-25","","RT","895675076"
