4.0,"I am starting this review by admitting a crime. I first saw this movie in a bar somewhere in Asia. With time to kill, I was watching a copy. It was grainy, out of sync and featured the odd unscheduled head. Full of remorse on a number of counts, I bought the DVD - honestly Clint, I did.
Of course watching the fake ruined it for me, as I had a rough idea what happened, so was denied the pleasure of viewing a film as intended - with a clean slate. Essentially, before the credits rolled, I was sceptical as I know a bit about boxing - been in the ring a few times, not always to my advantage - and believe women and boxing don't belong in the same sentence.
Clint Eastwood won me over and made me snivel. There, two confessions in one piece! Hilary Swank is an excellent actress that gives it her all, but I felt she was slightly miscast as a boxer. To be honest, I reckon most of her opponents could have blown her over. But then, had she been less feminine, we may not have felt so devastated by what happened to her. So, here again, Clint Eastwood was ahead of the game. I loved the ripple of plot that concerned his daughter and the chocolate tones of Morgan Freeman's narration. He was outstanding as always. This film deserved its four Academy awards and to hell with that bar in Asia!","30a1a6cd-3440-4990-81a2-50c96c704cc1",False,False,False,False,4.0,"2010-02-14","peter a","RT","584910fb-3d8f-418a-94db-e69f839dbf9f"
