5.0,"Â¤Â¤Â¤Â¤Â¤Â¤Â¤Â¤  Hilary Swank - Such a Thrilling Boxing Girl  Â¤Â¤Â¤Â¤Â¤Â¤Â¤Â¤","f3a0b6ea-8a2b-473c-b2bc-463721d44ed8",False,False,False,False,5.0,"2010-02-19","","RT","891625850"
5.0,"Directed by Clint Eastwood, Million Dollar Baby revolves around Maggie Fitzgerald (Hilary Swank) a waitress with a dream of becoming a female boxer. She wishes to be trained by Frankie Dunn (Clint Eastwood); however Frankie is reluctant and refuses to train females. Frankie is a brilliant boxing trainer whose had some bad luck over the years and has a daughter that refuses to speak to him, sending every letter that he sends to her back to him. Maggie doesn't give up and after much hesitation, Frankie decides to take on Maggie and help her achieve her dream of becoming a female boxer. 

Every story revolves around its characters. They are a medium for the audience to become emotionally involved with what's happening in a film. To put it simply, the characters are the most significant aspect of this film. While it may not sound like much, it is actually everything. The story on its own isn't overly captivating, but to have characters as interesting as the ones portrayed in this film entwined with everything that's taking place are what makes it the emotionally gripping journey that it is. 

The characters in this film are used to their fullest extent and we witness how they develop over the course of events that take place. A precise amount of their story is revealed giving the audience the ability to understand them better. Never is too much or too little revealed, with more detail given to the more significant characters, and less given to the minor ones. This isn't a bad thing, as being minor characters it is important to only reveal what is neccessary. Danger Barch (Jay Baruchel), who may seem like one of the characters that have no real meaning in the story, brought a sense of humour to the film and prevented it from taking itself too seriously. Danger has a dream just like Maggie's but he simply doesn't have the talent to accomplish it. Although everyone is well aware of this fact, no one other than another boxer at Frankie's gym is cruel enough to mock him. This boxer is known as Shawrelle Barry (Anthony Mackie) who is described as having ""a left hook that could move a tank"" but a heart the size of a split pea. Even though he is also seen as perhaps an unnecessary character in the film, his actions help characters like Danger develop.

Maggie Fitzgerald comes from a poor, stereotypically redneck family and holds onto the dream of becoming a successful boxer one day and is determined to do what it takes to make her dream come true. Maggie is a cheerful, yet stubborn character. She is repeatedly seen making light of bad situations and frequently ignoring the advice Frankie gives her during her fights, much to Frankie's dismay. Frankie is predominantly pessimistic and unwilling to take risks early on in the film, refusing to let his one of the boxers he's training to fight in a title match because he believes he only gets one shot at the title. It's because of Maggie that we see Frankie's character develops and changes into someone more likeable than what he originally was in the beginning of the film. Maggie holds onto her dream of becoming a boxer because boxing is the only thing she ever felt good doing. Without it, she believes she has nothing. Even after Frankie tells her she has no hope of becoming a champion boxer one day, she still refuses to give up and continues to pursue her dream. Although Frankie may come off as unsympathetic or bad-mannered at times, his character is revealed to have a reason for everything. 

During some of the non-dialogue scenes the film is narrated by one of Frankie's former boxers, Eddie Dupris (Morgan Freeman) also known 'as Scrap Iron'. Million Dollar Baby is just one of few films that Morgan Freeman has narrated. It's rather obvious as to why he was chosen to narrate this film as his voice is articulate and his expression is certainly appropriate. His narration is simple. It doesn't go into great detail about the events that take place, it simply tells us what is happening and in some cases why. Eddie Dupris has a history with Frankie. Frankie was once his boxing trainer, giving him advice during his fights until Dupris was unable to continue boxing. He is frequently seen lecturing Frankie which would appear to be a somewhat reverse situation of when Frankie was his trainer. They share an obligatory friendship. Frankie feels guilty over something that happened with Dupris back when he was boxing and feels as if he could have somehow changed the outcome of his boxing career. Frankie is also estranged from his daughter. While reasons for this are left unknown, we see Frankie is tormented with guilt. He regularly attends mass almost every day for 23 years, much to the annoyance of his priest, Father Horvak (Brian F. O'Byrne). He tells Frankie the only person who comes to church that much is the kind of person who can' forgive himself for something. Frankie' life is run by the mistakes he' made in the past. He has a tendency to avoid taking risks. After constant hesitation to take one of his fighters to title fight, his fighter finally leaves him, and after Dupris encourages Maggie, Frankie finally agrees to train her. 

Hilary Swank portrays Maggie flawlessly. In every scene involving her, not once does she slightly step out of character. Even her facial expressions, body language and dialogue are flawless. Sometimes she may be hard to understand because of her accent, but changing her accent for it to be understood a little better wouldn't exactly be in character. The moments shared between Maggie and Frankie are touching. When they become friends, they immediately share a close bond. It's more than just a student teacher relationship; the two care about eachother deeply but it's nothing intimate. 

The film has a way of bringing everything together in it's own special way. At one point, the film may feel as if it's heading in a direction that doesn't quite fit, however this isn't true; if you've paid close attention to everything that's been said and done up until the end, you will see everything fits together. It's a beautiful film, and the struggles of each character will draw you in emotionally. By the end, the audience has experienced something very profound.

Clint Eastwood also composed the films score. It adds emotion and depth to what is happening on screen. It does not distract the audience from what is happening, instead, it pulls the audience even further into the film. Even the very last scene in the film, and when the credits begin to roll, the music has a way of adding that extra bit of emotion on screen. 

This film is undoubtedly a masterpiece with every last second made to perfection. Films like this remind us just how important characters are. They are everything in this film and evoke great emotion because of how they are portrayed. This is Clint Eastwood at his best.","529a1f5e-2f2a-48ca-a501-bfbd62e3d598",False,False,False,False,5.0,"2010-02-20","","RT","901380485"
