5.0,"""Beyond his silence, there is a past. Beyond her dreams, there is a feeling. Beyond hope, there is a memory. Beyond their journey, there is a love.""

 As an emotionally out-washed boxing trainer, Frankie Dunn doesn't see a light of hope in his life. Frankie's emotional misery, caused by the painful estrangement from his daughter, has caused him to be close-minded with himself.  His only friend is the other boxing trainer that helps him run his boxing gym, Eddie Scrap-Iron Dupris (Morgan Freeman--Academy Award Winner at the 2004 Oscars). Life, for Frankie, is a downward spiral into a cautrostrphic mid-life crisis.  All of that changes after a poor waitress by the name of Maggie Fitzgerald walks into his gym and asks for her shot to compete in a national heavyweight challenge.  Frankie, however, refuses to train girls in boxing.  But, after Frankie thinks it over and Maggie over-pleases, he decides he will train her in boxing.  They form a special bond and take off on a boxing fleet like no other team.  

 Million Dollar Baby, not to be modest, is a knock-out emotionally challenging drama of a masterpiece.  Rising to the challenge like he has done so many times, Clint Eastwood cleverly exploits the sadness and hardships that exist in the working-class city, along with the sad reality that exists in sporting injuries.  What makes the audience feel the sadness of the actors is the emotionally gripping performances done by the leading cast members.  
    You watch Maggie, struggling to make ends meet on a small paid waitress salary. You watch her have to steal the leftover food from her customers  because she can barely afford to buy food for herself.  Then we see Maggie go onto become a strong and resilient boxer, who works her way to the top of the heavyweight.  We are cheering for Maggie, and we are happy for Maggie. But for Maggie, her on-going climb to the top of the heavyweight only ends with a sad and depressing downfall.  (Hilary Swank got the Oscar for Best Leading Actress as Maggie Fitzgerald)
     Then you watch Frankie Dunn, a severely saddened and unforgiving boxing trainer, who resorts to attending mass every day because he cannot forgive himself for what he has done with the relationship with his daughter.  He writes a card to his daughter everyday, only to not mail it and lay on his office desk.  
  The bonding that Maggie and Frankie have formed is something special and poignant, which will be remembered forever by many people. This is Clint Eastwood's best movie to date, and there's no doubt that a lot of other people will think the same thing.  Go and watch the best picture winner at the Academy Awards in 2004","5cecbfb6-86cb-490e-bf2c-f37e7a1aa034",False,False,False,False,5.0,"2010-04-04","","RT","863150375"
4.0,"Emotional and deep. Difficult to watch, but very good.","22185084-bfa9-43a1-983c-7e5e49110261",False,False,False,False,4.0,"2010-04-05","","RT","861719315"
4.0,"***1/2 (out of four)

Few directors work so well with actors in getting just the right performance.  Clint Eastwood knows both sides of the camera well and he uses them wonderfully in ""Million Dollar Baby"".

Eastwood plays Frankie a former boxing manager and current gym owner.  He has become soured by the past and now bitterly oversees things at his rundown gym.  At first unrelenting, he agrees to represent a young female boxer (Hilary Swank) who has heart and promise.

Excellent performances from everyone involved, especially Eastwood, Swank, and Morgan Freeman.  The first half of the story runs a mostly familiar and predicatble course, but what sets the film apart is that it dares to go in directions that aren't expected.  Those paths make for a very good and moving film.","4de8766e-f22f-4a93-aaf4-c7c9c7308241",False,False,False,False,4.0,"2010-04-07","","RT","fdb6b74a-780f-46a7-b9e2-db6f9bf5b6ff"
