5.0,"Million Dollar Baby, Great Movies
I thought films like ""Rocky"", ""Raging Bull"", ""Cinderella Man"", and ""The Fighter"" are the best boxing films ever made, but after seeing ""Million Dollar Baby"", it's worth a million dollar on it's greatness and truthness to itself. Unlike those 4 boxing films I've mentioned, this film is not that kind of you would expect a last fight at the end, instead you would see rather not a boxing match here, but you would see three people who are related to boxing but has some stories about courage and determination in life. We start with Frankie, played and directed of course by Clint Eastwood. You know if a movie is directed by Eastwood, you're gonna expect boredom, NO! You're gonna expect more drama and more lessons about his characters. He is a veteran retired trainer, and his famous line, ""I don't train girls"" becomes a memorable one. He goes to the church every morning and prays every night, so he's a religious and a faithful man. He also reads a book about poetry while he trains other boxers on his gym. He has a daughter but we never met her, nor him because she hates him so much. And I gotta say, this is the only film that I know where he stars and directs the film, that made him cry and become emotional, and I'll explain that later on. 
Next character is Scrap, played by Morgan Freeman which took him an Oscar as well. He is also a retired old man who is also once a boxer, and now lives on the side of the gym. His role here is very important to Eastwood's character and to the film itself. He is more of a friend than a trainer to Frankie, and once for all, Scrap is more of the undertaker to Frankie. And also, he narrates the whole film, where he tells a story about Frankie and then our next character which is Maggie who goes to the gym and about their story until to the end. 
Hillary Swank, plays Maggie in her second Oscar winning performance. She is a waitress and goes to the gym regularly to forget about her job for the day. One day Frankie notices her, and at first he refuses to train her but by the help of Scrap, he takes her. Maggie is more of a woman than what she is outside, she is a strong and a courageous woman inside who never fears or cries at anything until to that last scenes. We met her and we know she is not rich or poor, her mother is very selfish and ignorant, and her other family members doesn't love her much. At those moments, you would be very sad and feel emotional to Maggie. She never gives up hope and determination, and that's why Frankie says she is more of a daughter to me and one of the persons that I get so touched by it, I gotta also say the same thing!
Why is it a great movie? Unlike many other films out there, whether it is a sports film or not, this movie is very simple and beyond that, very subtle and quiet just to understand and feel what these three characters say to us and to themselves. That's why these 3 characters are the main key subject to this film. Probably like other Clint Eastwood films, ""Million Dollar Baby"" is worth watching, sometimes it's funny, and sometimes it makes you cry or even applaud at the end because we know it is great and left a lot lesson to us. 
I couldn't think of any other film that has the same story of a woman who goes on a boxing, except a film called ""Girlfight"", that is about this time a woman who gets in trouble all the time and wants to go boxing. But this film, is very standard and reliable to it's characters, why Maggie wants to go boxing? Why does Frankie doesn't train woman in boxing? And why Scrap didn't leave or hated Frankie? Those questions are answered more depth and more confident, that it became so powerful and real intellectual. And once again they all deserve those Oscars that they took home in 2004, best picture, director, actress, supporting actor, and I think there's more, but still it really is one of the best films of 2004, NO! One of the best films of the last decade, and also the best and honest boxing film ever made.
Warning: please bring a tissue box, because it would make you cry at the end? Why? You're gonna find out!","9118bfc6-25a5-404d-ba53-09801c380546",False,False,False,False,5.0,"2010-06-16","","RT","890853313"
5.0,"An amazing drama about achieving ur goal in life and about love.","680e2a68-5cdf-4bd5-962b-96d22d8a0e93",False,False,False,False,5.0,"2010-06-17","","RT","336414336"
4.5,"Maybe a little too depressing for some people's taste, but there's no denying that this movie was a well told story through very strong performances from great actors.","1b780f16-6bc8-4f14-b768-5530693c2a85",False,False,False,False,4.5,"2010-06-17","","RT","901321721"
5.0,"""Million Dollar Baby"" has to be one of the most brilliantly executed movies in recent times. It's execution is so powerful, and so heart reaching. You probably have to not have a pulse to not be emotionally moved by this film. It's without a doubt one of the best movies ever made...period. 

It seems that Clint Eastwood has made every possible movie ever. He's made war films, cop movies, westerns, and yes, even boxing movies. What Eastwood does here, is he effectively mixes in sports with tear-jerking drama. It's something that we have never seen before, and it's so brilliantly done. He just knows how to move an audience, oh and he knows it too. 

Basically Hillary Swank plays a girl who just wants to be recognized. She wants to become the best boxer around, and she wants Franky (Clint Eastwood) to train her. Originally, Franky says no, mainly because he has a good male boxer, and secondly because he doesn't like to train girls. But after his guy leaves him, Franky decides to take her in. What follows, besides  Maggie (Swank) winning every fight, is an emotional bond that forms between Franky and Maggy, not to mention Morgan Freeman's narration to go with it, as well as his words of advice to both Franky and Maggie. 

Everything about this movie is fantastic, and the acting is no slouch. Everyone in this movie is brilliant. Clint Eastwood gives one of the best performances of his career in this movie, and he was in the director's chair! How does he do it! Hillary Swank is wonderful from start to finish, she's just so believable. And Morgan Freeman dominates the screen as well. These three actors give such moving and touching performances. I just love to see this kind of stuff in movies. A+ in this department without a doubt. 

The cinematography in this movie is brilliant. The colors during specific scenes are all lighted perfectly, whether it be a touching scene, a happy scene, or a gritty scene, each frame is polished beautifully. I love the work that was put into this. 

The score is something to right home about as well. That one theme that constantly plays in the movie is so touching and heart-felt. Some other compositions during the film just make you want to sob for hours. I love it...

But the thing that sticks out the most is obviously the direction by Eastwood. He just did a fantastic job from start to finish. Not only did he give a wonderful/powerful, and tear jerking performance, but he gives some of the best direction that I have seen in a film before. EVERYTHING is perfect, everything is fantastic....every single shot leaves you wanting more. He completely knocked this out of the park...I don't know how he does it. 

""Million Dollar Baby"" is one of the best movies ever made for so many reasons. It's touching without a doubt, the actors all give brilliant performances, the style to it's execution creates various different sub-genres in one film. It allows the audience to feel all kinds of different emotions, allowing us to become attached to characters. It's like I said a million times already...brilliant. No other word can describe it. ""Million Dollar Baby"" is simply brilliant.","bbeff5a8-800b-4472-b860-fe37e9b54c06",False,False,False,False,5.0,"2010-06-20","","RT","829355661"
4.0,"Works tough wrought word parlays among weathered characters.  The trio of big name actors have those big names for good reason.  Each eases into their role without a ripple of falsity.  Each delivers on their unique gifts.  Through the first half of the film the script, actors, directing and story move in synch edging into superb cinematic grace.

Something false in the action emerges when the boxer and  trainer meet her mom.  For the tale the mom is cartoonishly beastly.  Her and other family come back to torment us late in the film and again this feels cheaply manipulative.  If these scenes were edited out it would vastly improve this picture.

You know this bends tragic.  This maudlin turn searches for meaning, and gets somewhere, but the first half of the reel outscores the last round.","f2528950-0a80-450b-9d9d-4a5167c53e54",False,False,False,False,4.0,"2010-06-23","","RT","901380900"
