5.0,"A touching, if not overly depressing, film filled with great moments and a tight story Hell it won Best Picture.","b0f44dd7-95e9-43fb-baa6-6a5172acfe07",False,False,False,False,5.0,"2010-06-27","","RT","897952520"
4.5,"There are some movies that win best picture but dont deserve it, Million Dollar Baby deserves every bit of its award. The film is an emotional roller coaster that takes you for a rough trip without mercy but just before you come to the end of the ride and the safety bars go up you find yourself speechless at what you just experienced. 

As an indie film, Million Dollar Baby lacks any spectacular visual effects or music performed by some top symphony orchestra. Instead it fills all these gaps with raw grit and drama and this is made all the more effective because they found the perfect cast to do it.

Clint Eastwood is absolutely flawless in the movie. In fact Eastwood gets so into character in some scenes that you almost forget you are watching a movie and not an actual man going through deep inner turmoil. 

Hillary Swank somehow manages to perfectly play a character that requires more feeling and emotion than any single human being should have. The movie does a good job of getting across the point that one should not be so quick to discard someone who isn't willing to quit. While this point is subtly made by the character of a more than somewhat stereotypical Texan (speaking as a real Texan here I wasn't offended but I felt it was inaccurate) the point is hammered home with ever punch by Swank's character as we come to learn what is behind her determination.

If there were an award for best narration at the Oscars, Morgan Freeman could build himself a house out of his trophies. His role in Million Dollar Baby is Morgan Freeman doing what Morgan Freeman does best. His best scene is when he punches out a mouthy gangster boxer who crosses a line by distracting Freeman so he can beat up on a less experienced boxer.

The story is inconsistent in pace but overall the mix between slow and fast paced scenes are handled well. There are also a few pointers about boxing thrown into the movie for the uninitiated that fit in with the story. In essence the plot follows Swank's character from a wannabe boxer who wont take no for an answer to a title fighter who still wont take no for an answer. The chemistry between her and the Eastwood character is one of the best things about the movie and most every scene deals with this interaction.

The only reason I dont give this movie 100% is because somehow I just dont think it is a perfect movie. I dont know why not but its one of those things where I will know it when I see it. There are a few nitpicks I can make like about how the sub story about Eastwood's daughter could have been developed more as not that much is revealed about her and her relationship with Eastwood's character by the end of the movie. Also the ending, without spoiling anything, is somewhat bittersweet and leaves you feeling a bit empty but that is probably what the movie intended. Million Dollar Baby is not a film that you should watch if you want to feel good after seeing it or if you want a happy ending. Nevertheless it is a film which anyone who claims to love drama should see at least once in their lifetime because it comes very very close to a perfect movie.","1ba998e7-7aab-4e8f-9992-73018669f883",False,False,False,False,4.5,"2010-06-27","","RT","901672045"
