5.0,"""Beyond his silence, there is a past. Beyond her dreams, there is a feeling. Beyond hope, there is a memory. Beyond their journey, there is a love."" 

 As an emotionally out-washed boxing trainer, Frankie Dunn doesn't see a light of hope in his life. Frankie's emotional misery, caused by the painful estrangement from his daughter, has caused him to be close-minded with himself. His only friend is the other boxing trainer that helps him run his boxing gym, Eddie Scrap-Iron Dupris (Morgan Freeman--Academy Award Winner at the 2004 Oscars). Life, for Frankie, is a downward spiral into a catastrophic mid-life crisis. All of that changes after a poverty-leveled waitress by the name of Maggie Fitzgerald walks into his gym and asks for her shot to compete in a national heavyweight challenge. Frankie, however, refuses to train girls in boxing. But, after Frankie thinks it over and Maggie over-pleases, he decides he will train her in boxing. They form a special bond and take off on a boxing fleet like no other team. Million Dollar Baby, not to be modest, is a knock-out emotionally challenging drama of a masterpiece. Rising to the challenge like he has done so many times, Clint Eastwood cleverly exploits the sadness and hardships that exist in the working-class city, along with the sad reality that exists in sporting injuries. What makes the audience feel the sadness of the actors is the emotionally gripping performances done by the leading cast members. You watch Maggie, struggling to make ends meet on a small paid waitress salary. You watch her have to steal the leftover food from her customers because she can barely afford to buy food for herself. Then we see Maggie go onto become a strong and resilient boxer, who works her way to the top of the heavyweight. We are cheering for Maggie, and we are happy for Maggie. But for Maggie, her on-going climb to the top of the heavyweight only ends with a sad and depressing downfall. (Hilary Swank got the Oscar for Best Leading Actress as Maggie Fitzgerald) Then you watch Frankie Dunn, a severely saddened and unforgiving boxing trainer, who resorts to attending mass every day because he cannot forgive himself for what he has done with the relationship with his daughter. He writes a card to his daughter everyday, only to not mail it and lay on his office desk. The bonding that Maggie and Frankie have formed is something special and poignant, which will be remembered forever by many people. This is Clint Eastwood's best movie to date, and will be touted by many as a modern-day version of ""Raging Bull""","70f7cdce-2bc6-4cc7-a1ca-c52e7d80e240",False,False,False,False,5.0,"2010-07-11","","RT","904387507"
5.0,"Wow. Clint Eastwood's Million Dollar Baby works as classic cinema. It does little more than tell a story. But, what a story it is. It leaves you with a sense of wonder. It dazzles you with a perfectly constructed narrative with character arcs and relationships that linger on our brain long after the film. Clint Eastwood masters the look and feel of the film. His camera takes a moody, low, yet un-opinionated look into the story of the characters presented in Million Dollar Baby. It feels like a classic piece of cinema. The lighting, the moody resonance the film carries, the perfectly constructed dialogue. It manages to be brilliant and entertaining at the same time.
Million Dollar Baby is the story of Frankie Dunn, a very old and grumpy boxing trainer who owns a gym with his equally grumpy friend Eddie Scrap Iron Dupris played by Morgan Freeman. Dunn takes little interest in anything besides boxing. He has one final student left and he's almost ready to be a champion, only Frankie holds him back keeping him away from a title shot. He says he's not ready. The kid leaves Frankie to go for a title shot. This does little more than to further Frankie's aggravation with life and with those around him, especially a pestering girl who hangs around the gym annoyingly asking Frankie to train her. Frankie declines again and again claiming, ""I don't train girls."" It may seem like a harsh response, but she doesn't let up. Eventually, Frankie agrees to give her pointers, only before you know it, he's training her and she's the best damn boxer out there. I will give little more of the plot away for those who haven't seen it. All I can say, is that Paul Haggis's screenplay is nothing short of brilliant. It classicly constructs the story. It doesn't fall into the normal cliches that you expect to see coming around every corner. Rather, it takes a fresh look at these characters and who they are and the decisions they make.
Eastwood's direction is...perfect. The camera manages to observe the events happening never forcing audiences to be jerked around one way or another. Rather Eastwood wants to present a story. Nothing more. Nothing less. This is why Eastwood is one of the best storytellers around. He knows how to grab an audience and create the perfect mood and tone for you to follow, never attaching yourself back to everyday thoughts until the end credits finish. This is his specialty. And he performs it perfectly here.
All the performances are oscar worthy. Every one if these actors deserves an oscar. The bantering between Eastwood and Freeman is hilarious, but at the same time emotionally relevant. You not only feel the chemistry between these two, but you feel that there is a history. Many unspoken stories in their heads that have become little more than memory. This doesn't need to be explained to the audience. Haggis and Eastwood wisely know this. The brilliant performances simply inform us of this withough amaterously telling us. 
Hilary Swank has never been a great actress. Everything about her from her demeanor to her looks requires the right role and, let me tell you, this is the right role. She perfectly embodies the ""tom-boyness"" and dreaminess of her character. We believe every word that comes out of every character's mouth. 
Eastwood is always a master at overall themes. This film does the same, but it is mainly a character piece. The ending does not in any way try to give us an overall life lesson. It is simply a decision made by a character. Was it right or wrong? Eastwood doesn't know and we feel Frankie Dunn doesn't quite know either.
Every character here has a story arc, a past, behaviors that we buy into because they feel genuine. Even a minor character played by Jay Baruchel leaves us with something of value. Not a lesson. But a sense of a character. A sense of a real genuine character who changes and whose story we can contemplate. Eastwood and Haggies construct each and every character perfectly and each and every actor brings them to life genuinely.
One would think this is the story of Swank's character, but it's not. It's the story of Frankie Dunn. He is the one changed by the events of the film. He may seem like a grumpy old man with little more than boxing in his life, but the film slowly peels back the layers with each scene not only in an emotionally mindful and brilliantly told manner, but in an entertaining and deeply resonant manner. Million Dollar Baby is a film that genuinely  deserves the rare compliment of being called a masterpiece.","a071ebf6-fb15-461f-a221-708f069c9a08",False,False,False,False,5.0,"2010-07-12","","RT","901081980"
