3.0,"I'm a fan of much of Clint Eastwood's late career work, but despite all the accolades it received this one doesn't really do anything for me.  It's basically a conventional underdog sports movie with the ending of a conventional melodrama grafted on to it, and the good performances and competently staged boxing scenes don't make up for the fact that neither half is all that great. The three leads are all fine, but the roles they play are neither particularly novel nor anything more than two-dimensionally written, and the minor characters around them have even less depth.  Despite the credentials of everyone involved it basically adds up to nothing more than classic Oscar bait, and if you want to watch a dark, brooding boxing movie, you're much better off renting ""Raging Bull"".","ba394b06-8524-4dc5-8510-7948538aed6e",False,False,False,False,3.0,"2011-01-10","","RT","789721899"
5.0,"*Films Owned On DVD*

Million Dollar Baby is one of the few movies that can still make me cry regardless of how many times I watch it. There's no denying though that this film is an absolute masterpiece and one of Clint Eastwood's best movies as well. One of the most heartfelt movies I've ever seen, but also a breath-taking experience. The film also strongly benefits from the knockout performances by Hilary Swank and Clint Eastwood. (excuse the pun). Eastwood's direction is brilliant, the cinematography is excellent, but it's the story and the script that really steals the show here. Complete with a brilliant although heart-breaking ending. Overall, I think calling this one of the best sports movies ever made is actually a major understatement. This is without a doubt one of the best films ever made.","acade21e-9b01-40bb-bc82-4051646e3b29",False,False,False,False,5.0,"2011-01-12","","RT","906344592"
