2.5,"Are films, by nature, manipulative? Perhaps. Good theatre engages the senses, plays on the emotions, stimulates dormant thought processes, challenges  preconceptions and misconceptions. We expect, even invite, as much from filmmakers. Done legitimately, it is acceptable and entertaining. Done well, it is art. Done the way Clint Eastwood directs Million Dollar Baby, it is propaganda. 

Million Dollar Baby is about life, death and choice. Without agreeing or disagreeing with the moral and political statements he ultimately attempts to make, I find the employed methods in this film facile, little more than disinformation pamphlets. 

For the first two thirds of the film we are engaged, rather skillfully, in an against-all-odds story of determination and second-chances with top caliber performances from Eastwood, Swank and Freeman. Then comes the turning of the tide. Disaster strikes in a series of sucker punches disguised as movie scenes. A blatant and heinous crime is simply unaddressed, a platform from which Mr. Eastwood begins to preach his social gospel. Characters suddenly begin acting out of character. Determination crumbles into capitulation. All is lost. It's as though we are awash in a blitzkrieg of assisted suicide pamphlets dropped from a squadron of invading Nazi Junkers. 

I've already stated that this isn't about agreeing or disagreeing with Mr. Eastwood's position or his right to espouse it. It's about the overly contrived means in which he goes about addressing a matter of great complexity. I won't necessarily deny that surrender may at times be warranted, but the events and circumstances surrounding such a serious matter are never quite so facile, so one-sided. In his headlong, tunnel-vision rush to deliver his statement, Eastwood tramples insensitively and somewhat arrogantly on those who are living reduced quality lives. The film's abrupt philosophical shift completely undermines the basis for the first part of the film.  

I suppose that Mr. Eastwood did successfully engage my senses and emotions, and even stimulated some thought processes. But he did little to challenge any personal conceptions, pre or mis, with his carelessly wielded filmmaking omnipotence.

Million Dollar Baby is a film about giving up. It is a heavyweight match featuring Hope versus Despair. In it, Mr. Eastwood pays off Hope to throw the bout and elevates Despair to the winner's circle without letting them go a full twelve. 

Million Dollar Baby is a fixed fight.

Shame on that.","0b4fae72-7502-4f3d-9ef0-cc76848e6e4a",False,False,False,False,2.5,"2011-01-25","","RT","906482436"
4.0,"Remarkable! Clint Eastwood really succeeds at making old material new again with Million Dollar Baby .The film runs at such a perfect,fluid, and intelligent pace its hard not to get absorbed in its beautifully crafted,wonderfully acted and emotional story. 

