4.5,"Clint Eastwood's graceful, spare direction has been compared to the prose of Ernest Hemingway and in this boxing drama his material has the muscularity and emotional resonance to make those comparisons apt. Adapted by Paul Haggis from stories in FX Toole's ""Rope Burns,"" this religious parable is about the hard scrabble lives of the lower-class world of boxers in a Los Angeles gym. Clint Eastwood is the guilt-ridden gym owner; Hilary Swank is the trailer-trash, 31-year-old waitress who views boxing as her only hope for survival; Morgan Freeman is the ex-boxer turned janitor who supplies the narration. The three lead performances are first-rate. The surrogate father-daughter relationship between Eastwood and Swank is the heart of the movie and there is a feeling of loss and desperation among these emotionally wounded characters that is touchingly poignant. One flaw is the portrayal of Swank's welfare family which is a gross stereotype. The tragic ending created a great deal of controversy. Won Oscars for Best Picture, Director, Actress (Swank), Supporting Actor (Freeman). Low-key cinematography by Tom Stern; Editing by Joel Cox; Music by Clint Eastwood. With Anthony Mackie, Jay Baruchel, Mike Colter, Lucia Rijker, Brian F. O'Byrne.","4ee2fe24-79b2-49bb-b39a-1c9b7c2e4fab",False,False,False,False,4.5,"2011-02-23","","RT","33797494-edaf-4ca2-bded-f5c3414a2810"
3.5,"This movie had you in it's grasp from beginning to end. The main characters performances were just mind blowing and it was also interesting to see Morgan Freeman play a somewhat different role cause I am use to this ""nice guy"" or ""the wise one"" but in this movie he was a old boxer who had a gambling problem I am not sure if it just me but I saw a different side of him. Eastwood just did a good job all around when it came to directing and acting, it is was neat to see him play this dad figure to Hilary Swank's character who is this young girl who wants to be a boxer. cause every time I see him in a movie he is like a totally bad-ass.  I am trying to say this in the nicest possible but I could not help but to laugh at Hilary Swank's southern/mid america accent but in the end had a very convincing performance thought the entire movie. This movie just left you cheering and in tears, out of the other boxing movies I have seen this was a really good one and can see why this movie got 4 Oscars which included best director and best supporting for Freeman.","bdcb98c9-8542-4c67-96b3-fdd57c417834",False,False,False,False,3.5,"2011-02-23","","RT","788727216"
4.0,"This movie really reached to my emotions. At times I found myself responding to the characters. Truly a great movie.","16b3ce2a-a872-4176-b3c7-628d77daae04",False,False,False,False,4.0,"2011-02-23","","RT","885358690"
5.0,"Best Eastwood movie ever!! You hear me? EVER!","9b381ee1-af69-4580-89eb-aa109e240fa5",False,False,False,False,5.0,"2011-02-26","","RT","905839942"
4.5,"The marketing of ""Million Dollar Baby"" leads you to believe that the film about to unfold is the inspirational tale of a female Rocky and that description does this remarkable film very little justice.   ""Baby"" is Eastwood's most sentimentally-driven film with raw displays of emotion that punches you right in the heart.

Young Maggie Fitzgerald (Hilary Swank) walks into a boxing training facility run by crabby Frankie Dunn (Eastwood).  She pesters him long enough to get him to train her so that she can pursue her dream of becoming a female boxer and eventually he agrees.  This partnership not only borns an incredible boxing champion but it creates a father/daughter bond that is central to the film's success.

Eastwood masterfully shifts gears - going from real humor to riotous action to heart-tugging emotion - with ease.  Swank is outstanding as Maggie Fitzgerald playing her with a tough innocence that makes her easy to root for and understand and Morgan Freeman doesn't disappoint.

Be prepared to go an emotional roller coaster with ""Million Dollar Baby""; you'll walk out of the theater shaken and emotionally stirred.","d3c8b469-611d-4f50-83d6-ca5f5134ed97",False,True,False,False,4.5,"2011-02-27","","RT","900792241"
5.0,"One the greatest and most dramatic films I have ever seen.  Clint Eastwood, Hillary Swan, and Morgan Freeman lead possible the best cast of actors in a film I have ever seen, I mean how many films get nominated for all those Oscars in Acting and win most, not many thats for sure but this film did.   The story is about a old boxing coach who runs a gym, when his main boxer leaves him he is left with only his friend (Morgan Freeman) and his gym.  A woman (Hillary Swank) wants to get his training and is a big fan, but he refuses to coach her for being a girl.  But after seeing her determination and work, he decides to train her.  But their realationship becomes stronger and is put to the ultimate test.  Such an amazing film and cast make this the best boxing movie since Rocky and Raging Bull.","661fa88d-03ee-4479-b30a-13478a7cecb6",False,True,False,False,5.0,"2011-02-27","","RT","906713304"
