4.0,"And in this corner, Hilary Swank: The Hardest Working Anti-Woman in Showbiz! Man, she is trying to destroy every bit of feminism in her, and that's a shame, because she seems like she'd be reasonably attractive were it not for those set of chompers that made me want to pause the movie and go grab a bag of oats every time she put in a mouth piece. More like ""Mule-Jawed Angels"". Seriously though, Swank clearly realizes her disadvantage in the mouth department, so all of these very masculine roles seem to be her finishing the job. So, I guess that I can assume that after ""Boys Don't Cry"", her second hit in the one-two punch to her femininity is, well, a one-to punch. I know it's a Clint Eastwood film about women professionally beating each other into submission, but I don't know if this was exactly the best choice for a ""manly"" film for Swank, because by the end, I was choking up like a little baby girl. Still, for every hit of raw emotion - complimented by some knockout acting -, there's slowness right around the corner to all but knock your lights out.

Okay, the film isn't terribly boring, which is surprising, because it's so pin-drop atmospherically quiet with some section without, not just score, but dialogue. Of course, just because it's not tedious, that doesn't mean that the film is completely out of the dull waters, and sure enough, while it's not too much of a challenge to sit through this film, things are bound to wear on you, and right when you're on the ground, the film decides to give you a good clean kick, or at least the writer does. Now, the dialogue isn't Liftime sports movie of the week abysmal, but it is snappy to a fault, being rather melodramatic, sometimes to a sappy degree. Listening to it is not a painful experience, but it certainly intensifies the conventional tone of the film. The film falls into many a cliche, plagued by the conventions of your run-of-the-mill underdog tale, and while you don't see ""exactly"" what ends up happening coming, it's no hard task to figure out that a certain something's gonna hit the fan. The dialogue takes a blow, the atmosphere is broken to a limp and originality is weary, leaving only one more pressure point to take a hit to send this fighter crashing: Execution, but it's there where this film truly keeps up its guard. The film is with all the slowness of Oscar bait and all the conventions of a manipulator, yet with none of the pretense; only the assurance that leaves the film to raise to the occasion, with Clint Eastwood at its back, pushing it the whole way through.

Yes, I'll be brutally honest and proclaim Eastwood the very reason why this film isn't terribly impressive, because his intensely slow atmosphere slows things down way too much, yet, were he not here, that wouldn't be the only unforgivable problem, because, at the end of the day, it's better to have Eastwood on board than not, because when it comes to atmosphere, outside of the dullness, he knows what he's doing. A film this slow tends to consistently get very disengaging, but Eastwood pumps so much spirit into the aura and keeps it frequently enthralling, boasting charm and essence to truly catch your eye, and the handsome cinematography is certainly helping. The cheesiness of the writing is fought back by Eastwood's soulfull execution of the script, with the film's highest point being the final act, which is not simply excellent in quality, but in emotion, as it is with so much powerful, riveting emotional resonance, yet not with an aura of manipulation, and the result is a deeply satisfying, deeply provokative and deeply moving capper to the film. True, that high point comes in a little too little too late for the film itself to be entirely excellent, but the whole film is still consistently engaging, and is made so, not just by Clint Eastwood, but by... well, Clint Eastwood, who's performance as this old know-it-all that's still learning his share of life lessons that will change ""his"" life all together is a charmingly subtle one and is matched by Morgan Freeman, who's playing Morgan Freeman: A charming, engaging force of reason with plenty of pain in his time and plenty of things to say... through narration. On top of that, Eastwood and Freeman give us an extra dose of charm by having some electric chemistry, not just with each other, but with every other cast member, and it's the other end of one of those chemistry formulas that really standsout above everyone else: ...Jay Baruchel. No, that big goofball is good, but not that good, yet, I must admit that manipulating that mega nerd accent to produce an awesome mega redneck accent was so genius that it just had to be concieved by a nerd, so I guess Baruchel really can't escape that role, afterall. No, but seriously though, the web of electric chemistry all leads back the real standout of the film: Hilary Swank, who delivers a moving, charismatic portrayal of such a conventional character, leaving her to actually transcend the genericism in the role and evoke only the genuine humanity, and she does with an emotionally riveting presence that will win your heart, shake it up and then break it, thus resulting in a truly enthralling performance to fit a truly enthralling picture.

At the end of this film, you limps away a little bit tired after all the dullness brought by extreme quietness, and it's a recovery made harder by the additional blow of conventional writing, but it's a risk worth taking to see how Clint Eastwood delivers on a charming, when not emotionally riveting aura - both behind and before the camera - and to see Hilary Swant deliver the graceful blast of true acting skill that helps raise the fists of ""Million Dollar Baby"" as it succeeds in standing as a thoroughly enjoyable, deeply moving drama.

3/5 - Good","eb7bfd16-84d1-4147-81c1-174dd5874d64",False,True,False,False,4.0,"2011-05-22","","RT","dccfc5fe-3174-4729-8c03-aa9db76b8819"
