3.0,"Million Dollar Baby is the story of a woman in her early thirties (Hillary Swank) who takes up boxing. Her coach reluctantly takes her under his wing and teaches her what she needs to become an exceptional boxer. 
It was a good start, getting our hopes up and making Maggie a star. What was not preferable is that Maggie has a large amount of matches, but many are not shown for very long or not at all. It is just shown that she wins but does not give us the full story.
When Maggie makes it up to her final match, she gets paralyzed from the neck down and is from then on living her life in a home. After much protest, Frankie finally cooperates with Maggie -- and mercy-kills her because she hates living life that way.
The acting was superb, however the story was missing certain aspects.","b49461a5-699c-4fe4-99d7-1c10351a64e2",False,False,False,False,3.0,"2013-05-30","","RT","917227659"
4.0,"A good movie but a bit formulaic for my taste. That's forgiveable with a boxing movie though and the strong performances allow it stand out from the crowd a little. It's not my favourite boxing movie but its far from a bad one.

The Morgan Freeman voiceover technique is brought out again for this one and the script is good enough that that works despite being overused as hell. The performances are good- Swank is excellent, Freeman is good and Eastwood is himself (what I can say? I like the movies but the acting does little for me.)

Its a pretty simple movie and I think that's where the emotion lies in it- if this attempted to be flashy and epic then it would fail but this one knows to keep it with the story. That's where the strength of the film is and the 2+ hours fly by because of it.

Its still a bit quiet for my liking though, maybe too much of the butterfly and not enough of the bee. (I HATE MYSELF) Even though the approach grates on me a little, it only makes the film more ambitious and more of a success for pulling it all off.

While it tells a good story, the story doesn't seem to mean anything and its a real failing here- we have a real underdog story that isn't converted into more- its not about overcoming prejudice, its not about realising who you are, its not even about boxing after a while. It ends up feeling like a bit of an ego-trip in that way, it ends up being more about Eastwood showing that he could make another good movie as opposed to it being about making the best movie possible.

The fact that Dunn is so prevalent in the film and yet, to me anyway, is so unappealing is another fault. Some elements are so overdone also- the family is just annoyingly bad, we get the idea and its hammered in far too heavy, one scene was almost laughable because of it. These things along with all of the clichés ruin some of the genuineness and makes it difficult for this one to live up to the hype for me. 

Still, films are about telling stories and this one is well put together and well delivered and it manages to have its own character within a pretty restricted genre. A good film but not the best by anyone involved.","1dd8fc77-5b0a-4e2d-8034-91512c68ab5a",False,False,False,False,4.0,"2013-05-31","","RT","945668697"
